1 CHAN
2 DATE 30 AUG 2020
1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT I said B. H. Reeves was well known both in Kentucky and Missouri.  Befo
2 CONC re the death of Aunt Sallie Ewin, (of whom I shall speak more particula
2 CONC rly) she requested him, (then about eighteen or nineteen years old) as t
2 CONC he oldest and most experienced of the little band of cousins in the far
2 CONC , far west, to carry her little daughter Jeannetta to her sister Margar
2 CONC et Smith, that she might be a mother to her orphan child.  Faithfully d
2 CONC id Ben Reeves perform the task, but Jeannetta was never to see her Aunt S
2 CONC mith.  The two sisters were sick about the same time and neither knew o
2 CONC f the death of the other till they met on the Shining Shore.  [Daniel] S
2 CONC mith, then a practicing lawyer, gladly assumed the duty of educating hi
2 CONC s young cousin.  On his way to Virginia, B. H. Reeves visited his Aunt D
2 CONC only, and made such progress in winning the affections of her daughter, h
2 CONC is cousin Martha, that on his way back they were married, and he surpri
2 CONC sed his mother, bringing a wife home with him.  He was not twenty years o
2 CONC ld; his wife a year or two older.  This was in 1806; and in 1812 the wa
2 CONC r with Great Britain was commenced and a company was speedily raised in C
2 CONC hristian County, notwithstanding its sparse population.  For the captai
2 CONC ncy there were two candidates, B. H. Reeves and Ben Patten, a young law
2 CONC yer of great promise and remarkably fine personal appearance, residing i
2 CONC n Hopkinsville, the county seat.  Before the close of the campaign, he a
2 CONC ttained to a majority.  Before the termination, his services were calle
2 CONC d for in the Legislature of the state, and again Mr. Patten was his com
2 CONC petitor, and again Reeves was the successful candidate.  From this time u
2 CONC ntil 1818 (when he removed to Missouri) he represented Christian County i
2 CONC n the Legislature.  Emigrating to Missouri in 1818, he was a member of t
2 CONC he convention which enacted the first constitution of the State, and re
2 CONC presented the County of Howard, (in which he settled) as long as it rem
2 CONC ained a Whig County, until after the election of J. Q. Adams to the Pre
2 CONC sidency of the United States, when Howard County, being a strong Jackso
2 CONC n man county, he was left in the minority.  His wife, Martha Donly, die
2 CONC d in the spring of 1835.  They had five children, who lived to maturity
2 CONC ; William Long, Clark Davis, Jeannetta, Mary Elizabeth and Benjamin; an
2 CONC d some three or four who died in infancy.  Clark married, but died soon a
2 CONC fter his mother, leaving one daughter, Ellen.
2 CONT ...
2 CONT After the death of his wife, B. H. Reeves went back to Kentucky, leavin
2 CONC g his mother with his daughter Jeannetta, then the wife of the late Jud
2 CONC ge Leonard.  He married and resided there until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some time after Col. Reeves’ return to Kentucky, he married Virginia Cr
2 CONC oss, widow of George Cross.  They had three children, two daughters and o
2 CONC ne son.  Col. Reeves died April 16, 1849; his wife not long afterwards.
2 CONC   He was at the time of his death a member of the Cumberland Presbyteri
2 CONC an Church. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "History of Todd County, Kentucky", ed. J. H. Battle, F. A. Battey Publ
2 CONC ishing Co., 1884, pp. 328-30:
2 CONT
2 CONT [From the biography of Crittenden Reeves] Of his father, an obituary no
2 CONC tice says: "Benjamin H. Reeves, the eldest son of Brewer and Martha Ree
2 CONC ves, was born in Augusta County, Va., March 21, 1787, and moved with hi
2 CONC s parents to Christian County, Ky., about the commencement of the prese
2 CONC nt century, and settled on the West Fork of Red River.  Shortly after t
2 CONC heir arrival in Kentucky his father died, leaving his widow and infant c
2 CONC hildren in comparatively a wilderness, surrounded by the red men of the f
2 CONC orest.  His mother, a lady of uncommon energy, firmness, and fine power
2 CONC s of mind, richly stored with the truths of the Gospel, in the absence o
2 CONC f anything like good schools, laid the foundation for his future elevat
2 CONC ion in life by her industry, and, with his assistance, they managed to s
2 CONC upport the younger members of the family.  To them he was both a parent a
2 CONC nd an elder brother; his heart seemed to be entwined around them during l
2 CONC ife.  By his own generous worth and energy of character he soon acquire
2 CONC d the esteem and confidence of his countrymen.  On the declaration of w
2 CONC ar by the United States against Great Britain in 1812, he took up arms i
2 CONC n defense of his country's rights, and was, on the fourth day of July i
2 CONC n that year, elected Captain of the first company of volunteers from Ch
2 CONC ristian County, and in a short time joined the army in the then Territo
2 CONC ry, now State, of Indiana, stationed at Vincennes; was shortly after pr
2 CONC omoted to the rank of Major; commanded an escort to the relief of Zacha
2 CONC ry Taylor, the present President of the United States.  In November, 18
2 CONC 12, he returned to Kentucky, having been, at the August election previo
2 CONC us, elected a member of the Legislature of Kentucky from Christian, and t
2 CONC ook his seat as a member of that body on the first Monday in December t
2 CONC hereafter, and continued a member of that body, with the exception of o
2 CONC ne or two years, until 1818, when he moved to the then Territory of Mis
2 CONC souri.  In 1821 he was elected a Delegate from the county of Howard to a
2 CONC ssist in framing a Constitution for that State, and was, a few years th
2 CONC ereafter, elected Lieutenant-Governor of Missouri. In 1826 [should be 1
2 CONC 825] he was appointed by the Government of the United States a Commissi
2 CONC oner to survey and mark out a road from Missouri to the Spanish provinc
2 CONC es in a direction to Santa Fe.  In the recent Indian wars on the fronti
2 CONC ers of Missouri, he again took up arms in defense of his country.  In 1
2 CONC 836 he returned from Missouri, and settled in Todd County, Ky.  The par
2 CONC tiality of his countrymen soon called him to represent them in Legislat
2 CONC ure in several successive years.  He filled many civil and military off
2 CONC ices in Missouri and Kentucky.  In private life his virtues shone most c
2 CONC onspicuous--a dutiful child, a kind husband, a fond parent, a devoted f
2 CONC riend. Warm-hearted, generous, and devoted in his sentiments, he had ma
2 CONC ny personal and devoted friends.  About the first of January last his h
2 CONC ealth began to decline rapidly, and on Monday, the 16th day of April, 1
2 CONC 849, at his residence in Todd County, having, as his friends fondly hop
2 CONC e, made his peace with God, with a smile on his countenance, and withou
2 CONC t a struggle or a groan, fell asleep in Jesus, universally lamented by h
2 CONC is family, relations and friends."  Brewer's sons were: Benjamin H., Wi
2 CONC llis L., Ottaway and Archibald.  Subject's mother was Virginia T. (Gart
2 CONC h) Reeves, who died in 1850.  Col. Benjamin's children are: William L., J
2 CONC ennette (Leonard), Mary (Wilson), Benjamin, Missouri (Ainslie), Eugenia (
2 CONC Griffin) and our subject.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT A letter from W.L. Reeves to J. Leonard, dated 24 Apr 1849, Todd County
2 CONC , KY (from the Abiel Leonard Papers in the State Historical Society of M
2 CONC issouri Manuscript Collection, obtained from John Wanamaker):
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Sister
2 CONT  
2 CONT Your letter of date not recollected came to hand some days ago, which I o
2 CONC mitted answering till I would see the final result of our dear old fath
2 CONC er, which has terminated in death.  He has gone to try the realities of a
2 CONC nother World.  He drew his last breath on Monday the 16th of this month
2 CONC , twenty minutes before twelve o’clock of that day.  His remains were t
2 CONC aken the next day to Uncle Willis L. Reeves’s and there consigned to hi
2 CONC s narrow dwelling.  Oh, my dear distant Sister, can you imagine the wei
2 CONC ght of my feelings in recording to you the death of a dear father.  He i
2 CONC s gone.  Gone, I hope, to rest, he expressed a hope, beyond the grave.  I h
2 CONC ave lost all now.  I have no ties in Kentucky now.  His talk while on h
2 CONC is bed of affliction was Missouri.  The last conversation he had with m
2 CONC e was, he wanted to sell his land and go to Missouri, which he no doubt w
2 CONC ould have done could he only have got up again.  Three days after he di
2 CONC ed they lost a bitchy young Negro girl.  He left a will.  Uncle Willis a
2 CONC nd P. L. Garth his executors.  The [most of a line obliterated here].  I c
2 CONC annot at this time say where my destiny will be.  I am without a home h
2 CONC ere.  I would be glad, my dear Sister, if you would write to Ma soon an
2 CONC d solicit her strongly to move to Missouri.  It was Pa’s wish for her t
2 CONC o do so.  Brother Ben is anxious to move to Missouri.  We have lately h
2 CONC ad a spell of cold, frosty weather; killed all the fruit, bit the corn d
2 CONC own, injured the wheat crops very much.  H. C. Ewing is quite sick at t
2 CONC his time.  The balance of the relations, so far as I know, are well.  M
2 CONC a and the children are well.  We are all on foot here [?].  I must draw t
2 CONC o a close.  My heart is too full to write.  Give my love to all the fri
2 CONC ends in Mo.  When did you see sister Mary?
2 CONT  
2 CONT For yourself, receive a brother’s love.
2 CONT W.L. Reeves
2 CONT
2 CONT [This letter was written by B.H.'s son, William Long Reeves, to sister J
2 CONC eanette Reeves Leonard.  William was living in Kentucky at that time.  H
2 CONC e was still there the following year at the time of the 1850 census, bu
2 CONC t he moved back to Missouri soon after that.  William spends part of th
2 CONC e letter talking about getting Virginia ("Ma") to move to Missouri, but s
2 CONC he would die not too long after this.  William took all the younger chi
2 CONC ldren to Missouri after she died.  The two girls (Missouri and Eugenia) w
2 CONC ere already living with the Leonards in Missouri in the 1850 census.  C
2 CONC rittenden and Ben Jr. were with William in Kentucky.  Crittenden and Be
2 CONC n Jr. went to Missouri with William and his family, but Crittenden went b
2 CONC ack to Kentucky after a few years.  Crittenden might have joined his si
2 CONC sters with the Leonards while he was in Missouri; Ben Jr. would have be
2 CONC en out on his own soon, though he might have stayed with William until h
2 CONC e married in 1855.  Uncle Willis, B.H.’s younger brother, is mentioned i
2 CONC n a couple of places.  He lived in Todd County.  B.H.’s other brothers w
2 CONC ere already dead by this time.  I’m not sure who some of the other name
2 CONC s were.  A Garth is mentioned; that was Virginia’s maiden name, so it m
2 CONC ight be her father or brother.  H. C. Ewing is probably Henry Clayton E
2 CONC win.  At the end of the letter, William mentions “sister Mary”, who wou
2 CONC ld be Mary Reeves Wilson.  More letters by and about Ben can be found a
2 CONC t https://archive.org/details/Reevesletters_rev .]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From the McGrew letter:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ben was appointed by the President of the United States to survey the S
2 CONC anta Fe Trail from Independence, Missouri, to Santa Fe, New Mexico, whe
2 CONC n it was built.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT WFT 22, 1690:
2 CONT He surprised his mother by marrying her niece and namesake.
2 CONT During the war of 1812 he became the Captain of a volunteer company rom C
2 CONC hristian Co., KY.  Before the war ended he attained the rank of Major. H
2 CONC e ran for the Legislature of KY and won.  He represented Christian Co. i
2 CONC n the legislature until 1818 when he moved to Howard Co., MO.  He was a m
2 CONC ember of the committee that enacted the first Constitution of MO.  He s
2 CONC erved as representative of Howard Co. in the MO legislature.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Missouri State Archives Web site (http://www.sos.state.mo.us/a
2 CONC rchives/history/historicallistings/ltgov.html & governors.html), he was t
2 CONC he second lieutenant governor of Missouri, from 1824 to 1825.  His term w
2 CONC as supposed to run until 1828, but he resigned in July 1825 (to become o
2 CONC ne of the three commissioners tasked with surveying the Santa Fe Trail)
2 CONC .  The governor died in office in August 1825, so Benjamin would have s
2 CONC ucceeded to the governorship if he had not resigned.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Bob Reeves, he was named for Benjamin Harrison, the father o
2 CONC f President William Henry Harrison.  Benjamin Harrison was a friend, ne
2 CONC ighbor, and sometime business associate of Brewer Reeves.  They were co
2 CONC -founders of the Presbyterian Church in Harrisonburg, VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a nice biography of him in:  "Benjamin H. Reeves" by F. A. Cul
2 CONC mer (Missouri Historical Review, Vol. 25, No. 4, pp. 556-562, July 1931
2 CONC ).  Available from the web site of the State Historical Society of Miss
2 CONC ouri, http://statehistoricalsocietyofmissouri.org/ .  The Wanamaker man
2 CONC uscript has a great deal of information about him as well.
2 CONT
2 CONT A transcription of Ben's journal of the expedition to survey the Santa F
2 CONC e Trail can be found in "The Road to Santa Fe: The Journal and Diaries o
2 CONC f George Champlin Sibley" by Kate L. Gregg (University of New Mexico Pr
2 CONC ess, 1952).  Note the following lines from "The Santa Fe Trail: Its His
2 CONC tory, Legends and Lore" by David Dary (2000), p. 93:
2 CONT  
2 CONT Missourians were not surprised when Benjamin H. Reeves was appointed on
2 CONC e of the three commissioners to mark the road to Santa Fe.  After all, h
2 CONC e was the most influential citizen in Howard County, the center of Sant
2 CONC a Fe trade in Missouri.  Reeves arrived in Missouri from Kentucky in 18
2 CONC 19; was a member of the first constitutional convention; and was electe
2 CONC d state auditor, state senator, and in 1824 lieutenant governor, a posi
2 CONC tion he resigned when appointed commissioner.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that Benjamin Harrison Reeves and Martha Donley were first cousins
2 CONC .
