2 DATE 1 MAY 2019
1 NOTE He is the author of the Adair Wilson family record.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Portrait and Biographical Record of Denver and Vicinity, Colorado", Ch
2 CONC apman Publishing Co., Chicago, 1898, pp. 321-325 (obtained from http://
2 CONC www.memoriallibrary.com/CO/1898DenverPB/pages/pbrd0319.htm):
2 CONT
2 CONT HON. ADAIR WILSON, associate judge of the Colorado State Court of Appea
2 CONC ls, was born in 1841 in what is now Cambridge, Saline County, Mo., and i
2 CONC s of Scotch-Irish lineage.  His paternal great-grandfather emigrated fr
2 CONC om Ireland to the United States and after a short sojourn in Pennsylvan
2 CONC ia went to the Shenandoah Valley, of Virginia, where he was engaged as a p
2 CONC lanter until his death.  He had a brother, James, who was a signer of t
2 CONC he Declaration of Independence, also of the Constitution, by profession a
2 CONC n attorney, and under appointment by President Washington chosen to fil
2 CONC l the position of justice of the supreme court of the United States.
2 CONT
2 CONT The grandfather of our subject, William Wilson, was born in Virginia, a
2 CONC nd took part in the Revolution when a young man.  Many years later, in 1
2 CONC 824, he removed to Missouri and settled upon a farm near Glasgow, Howar
2 CONC d County, where he lived retired until his death. The youngest of his l
2 CONC arge family was William A., a native of Augusta County, Va., and in ear
2 CONC ly life a merchant, but later a student of law with his brother, Gen. J
2 CONC ohn Wilson, who had preceded the family to Missouri and had served in t
2 CONC he war of 1812.  William was admitted to the bar in Saline County and o
2 CONC pened an office in Marshall, where he was a pioneer and prominent attor
2 CONC ney.  For years he was clerk of all the courts there. When the Civil wa
2 CONC r broke out he was somewhat advanced in years, but enlisted in the stat
2 CONC e militia and was made lieutenant-colonel of a regiment, serving until t
2 CONC he close of the war, but the exposure of camp life caused his death soo
2 CONC n afterwards.  He was then about fifty-seven years of age. Fraternally h
2 CONC e was a Mason.
2 CONT
2 CONT Our subject's mother was Mary E. Reeves, a native of Todd County, Ky., a
2 CONC nd now living in Marshall, Mo.  She is the descendant of English and Sc
2 CONC otch-Irish ancestors, who early settled in Virginia.  Her father, Col. B
2 CONC enjamin H. Reeves, was born in Augusta County, Va., but about the close o
2 CONC f the eighteenth century, when in infancy, he removed to Kentucky with h
2 CONC is parents.  His father had served in the Revolution and he took part, a
2 CONC s a captain, in the war of 1812, being of the greatest assistance to th
2 CONC e cause in Indiana and Kentucky and relieving Zachary Taylor when the l
2 CONC atter was besieged near Lafayette.  During his residence in Kentucky he w
2 CONC as for many years a member of the legislature.  In 1818 he removed to M
2 CONC issouri, where he was a member of the constitutional convention, later s
2 CONC tate senator from his district, and afterward lieutenant-governor of th
2 CONC e state for one term.  He was one of the commissioners appointed by the p
2 CONC resident of the United States to locate the Santa Fe trail. Both while i
2 CONC n Kentucky and Missouri he was active in the skirmishes with the Indian
2 CONC s, and during the Iowa Indian war he was colonel of a regiment.  He die
2 CONC d in 1849, at the age of sixty-two years.  Politically he had been an a
2 CONC rdent supporter of Henry Clay and the Whig party.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family of which our subject is a member consisted of seven children
2 CONC , he being third in order of birth.  One brother, Benjamin H., was a ca
2 CONC ptain in a Missouri regiment during the Civil war and is now a resident o
2 CONC f Denver.  Our subject was reared in Marshall and received his educatio
2 CONC n in the Masonic College, from which he graduated with the degree of A. B
2 CONC ., in 1858, when less than seventeen years of age, being the youngest m
2 CONC ember of his class.  He studied law under an uncle, Judge Abiel Leonard
2 CONC , who was at one time judge of the supreme court of Missouri.  In 1860 h
2 CONC e was admitted to the bar at Marshall and in the spring of the followin
2 CONC g year came to Denver, making the trip overland with teams.  After a fe
2 CONC w weeks he proceeded westward to California and located in San Francisc
2 CONC o, where his uncle, Gen. John Wilson, was a prominent attorney.  The un
2 CONC cle and nephew practiced together for two years, then the latter went t
2 CONC o Virginia City, Nev., and embarked in the newspaper business as city e
2 CONC ditor of the Virginia City Union, at the same time that Mark Twain was c
2 CONC ity editor of the Enterprise. After one year he went to Austin, Nev., w
2 CONC here he was the first editor of the Reese River Reveille, a paper that i
2 CONC s still being published. Resigning his position a year later, he went b
2 CONC ack to San Francisco and resumed the practice of law.  His father dying i
2 CONC n 1867 [actually in 1865], he returned to Missouri to look after the es
2 CONC tate, and opened an office in Marshall, where he practiced until 1872.
2 CONT
2 CONT Coming again to Colorado in 1872, our subject located in Pueblo, where h
2 CONC e practiced for a year.  He was among the earliest settlers in the San J
2 CONC uan mining region and located at Del Norte, which became the county sea
2 CONC t.  In 1875 he was elected the first member of the territorial council f
2 CONC rom the San Juan country, comprising five or six counties, and served d
2 CONC uring the last session of the legislature of the territory, being chose
2 CONC n as president of the body.  In 1876 he was a delegate to the national D
2 CONC emocratic convention at St. Louis that nominated Samuel J. Tilden for p
2 CONC resident, and during the ensuing election was one of the Democratic can
2 CONC didates for presidential elector voted for by the legislature of Colora
2 CONC do.  During the same year he was nominated for judge of the fourth judi
2 CONC cial district, but declined the nomination.  In 1880 he was tendered th
2 CONC e Democratic nomination for governor, in the convention held at Leadvil
2 CONC le, but refused to accept. Six years later he was nominated on the Demo
2 CONC cratic ticket for state senator from the San Juan district and was the o
2 CONC nly one on the Democratic ticket elected, the district being Republican
2 CONC .  His term of service covered the years 1887-90, during which time he i
2 CONC ntroduced many bills of importance.  In 1887 he opened an office in Dur
2 CONC ango, where he has since resided.  At the convention in Chicago in 1896 h
2 CONC e was elected a member of the Democratic national committee.  In April, 1
2 CONC 897, Governor Adams appointed him to the position he now holds, that of a
2 CONC ssociate judge of the court of appeals.  He is a member of the Pioneers
2 CONC ' Association of San Juan, and fraternally is connected with the Masoni
2 CONC c lodge of Del Norte.  In Arrow Rock, Saline County, Mo., he married Mi
2 CONC ss Margaret E. Edwards, who was born in Pettis County, that state, bein
2 CONC g the daughter of Philip W. Edwards, who was born in Kentucky in 1800, r
2 CONC emoved to Missouri in an early day and engaged in business there until h
2 CONC is death.  This union was blessed with the following children: Katharin
2 CONC e W., who married Austin H. Brown; Edwards Adair, Alva Adams and Margar
2 CONC etta.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT I'm afraid that some of the earlier information in this biographical sk
2 CONC etch is not correct.  Adair’s paternal great-grandfather was Robert Wil
2 CONC son, and Page Smith's biography of James Wilson does not list a brother n
2 CONC amed Robert.  Possibly Robert was some sort of cousin to James, but I d
2 CONC on't think he was a brother (the Foster/Reid Family Tree says a half-br
2 CONC other, which might be possible).  Also, the biography says that the unn
2 CONC amed brother of James came from Ireland, but James was from Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT The bio above says that Adair moved to Colorado in 1872 and it mentions e
2 CONC lected positions in 1875 and 1876.  Interesting, then, that Edwards and A
2 CONC lva were both born in Marshall, MO, in 1874 and 1879 (and this is accor
2 CONC ding to Adair's own bible and family record).  Was Bettie going home to M
2 CONC arshall each time for the births?
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From one of his obituaries:  "Adair Wilson, former judge of the Colorad
2 CONC o court of appeals, is dead.  In Berkeley, Cal., at the home of his dau
2 CONC ghter [presumably Kate Wilson Brown], where he went three years ago on a
2 CONC ccount of illness, he passed away at noon Sunday and there he will be b
2 CONC uried on Tuesday.  Mr. Wilson had not abandoned Colorado as his home an
2 CONC d only recently talked of returning to Denver this spring.  He suffered a p
2 CONC aralytic stroke soon after going to California and since that time has b
2 CONC een an invalid."
2 CONT
2 CONT The obituary also says, about his time as editor of the Virginia City U
2 CONC nion:  "At that time Mark Twain was editing the Virginia City Enterpris
2 CONC e, and while the two were business rivals they were fast personal frien
2 CONC ds.  This friendship endured until Mr. Twain’s death two years ago."
2 CONT
2 CONT A copy of a 1907 letter from Adair to Mark Twain was sent to me by Todd A
2 CONC dair Wilson, a great-great grandson of Adair.  The original letter is b
2 CONC elieved to be in the Mark Twain Papers at the Bancroft Library of the U
2 CONC niversity of California at Berkeley.  Adair’s letter was typed, but a h
2 CONC andwritten note on the letter, presumably by Mark Twain, says that Adai
2 CONC r Wilson "was a man who justified Horace Greeley’s remark go west, youn
2 CONC g man, go west.  He was a reporter & then student of law with a shyster l
2 CONC awyer & then practiced & finally became a judge."  I wonder if the "shy
2 CONC ster lawyer" comment refers to John Wilson?
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In the bio of William Adair Wilson in "History of Saline County, Missou
2 CONC ri", Adair is mentioned as "W. A."  I wonder if he was really William A
2 CONC dair Wilson Jr.?
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a cute anecdote in "Pioneers of the San Juan Country" by Sarah P
2 CONC latt Decker Chapter, N.S.D.A.R., Durango, Colorado (Durango Printing Co
2 CONC ., Durango, CO, 1952, Vol. III, pp. 168–169):
2 CONT
2 CONT ... In 1873 their residence was established in Del Norte, then southwes
2 CONC tern Colorado’s most thriving town.  In December of that year Mr. Wilso
2 CONC n became the first editor of the <i>Del Norte Prospector</i>.  The firs
2 CONC t issue of the new paper made its appearance in December of that year.  I
2 CONC n this capacity his marked political sagacity first manifested itself.
2 CONT
2 CONT Apparently some of his time must have been devoted to his law practice, b
2 CONC ecause one of his favorite stories centered around a certain term of di
2 CONC strict court held in Del Norte, which was attended by all the legal lig
2 CONC hts of the San Juan country.
2 CONT
2 CONT One evening all participated in a party where some refreshments were se
2 CONC rved but more were poured.  When court convened next morning not an att
2 CONC orney had completely recovered from the liquid intake.  Together they d
2 CONC ecided it would add to the merriment to march into the court room in a b
2 CONC ody singing songs that had been a part of the previous evening’s entert
2 CONC ainment.
2 CONT
2 CONT One by one the legal culprits were called before the presiding judge an
2 CONC d fined $10.00.  Sternly “His Honor” stated, “Gentlemen, I am not finin
2 CONC g you because you entered the court room singing, BUT because you can’t s
2 CONC ing!”
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Pioneers of the San Juan Country" also says this:
2 CONT  
2 CONT After the family moved to California he spent much of the time during h
2 CONC is declining years writing a history of his experiences in Southwestern C
2 CONC olorado; only to have the manuscript burned in the fire that followed t
2 CONC he San Francisco earthquake of 1906.  Repeated attempts on the part of h
2 CONC is friends in the San Juan to get him to re-write the history were with
2 CONC out success.  He felt he was too far advanced in life to repeat his eff
2 CONC orts.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There is quite a bit about Adair in the Hotchkiss manuscript (James Hot
2 CONC chkiss Jr. was Adair's grandson).
2 CONT
2 CONT
