1 SEX M
1 _UID 9625A186BD7E2B43B5ACF2694EEB9B5B7615
1 CHAN
2 DATE 25 AUG 2020
1 NOTE Horace Everett Wilson was apparently named for Horace Everett, who marr
2 CONC ied into the extended family (he was married to Mary Leonard, Horace's m
2 CONC uch older first cousin).  Perhaps the Wilsons had a close relationship w
2 CONC ith Horace Everett, though maybe another possibility is that they were e
2 CONC specially close to Mary Leonard Everett.  In a letter from Mary Reeves W
2 CONC ilson to Jeanette Reeves Leonard dated 12 Feb 1856, Mary wrote "... I d
2 CONC ont believe I have a child that I love any better than I did little Mar
2 CONC y Leonard."  In fact, the child before Horace, Mary L. Wilson (who died y
2 CONC oung), was probably Mary Leonard Wilson.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From Find A Grave (listing for MAJ Horace Everett Wilson):
2 CONT
2 CONT 1900 Serving in Philippines. Army paymaster.
2 CONT 1910 - In New York City with wife May
2 CONT 1920 - In Philadelphia
2 CONT 1930 and 1940 - In Berkeley, Alameda Co., California
2 CONT
2 CONT [Wife May is also with him in the 1920, 1930, and 1940 censuses.]
2 CONT [It is perhaps worth noting that Horace's occupation is given as paymas
2 CONC ter, U.S. Army, in the 1910 census, and he is described as a paymaster i
2 CONC n the U.S. Army in obituaries for his brother Adair, who died in 1912.]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Horace’s probable listing in the 1880 census (H. E. Wilson in St. Josep
2 CONC h, MO) lists his occupation as traveling salesman (actually, it appears t
2 CONC hat he is listed twice in the 1880 census, at two different addresses i
2 CONC n St. Joseph).  Note that the biography of William Adair Wilson in the 1
2 CONC 881 "History of Saline County" said that son Horace E. Wilson was livin
2 CONC g in St. Joseph.  A brief obituary of Horace's brother Leonard, in the F
2 CONC ind A Grave listing for Leonard W. [sic] Wilson, says that Leonard was i
2 CONC n business with his brother H. E. Wilson in Nance Co., NE, for a number o
2 CONC f years (see the notes for Leonard Wilson).  Perhaps this was in the ea
2 CONC rly 1880s.  Leonard moved to Denver by 1884 or 1885, but Horace must ha
2 CONC ve stayed longer since he was married in Nance County in 1886.  He must h
2 CONC ave joined the army relatively late in life, since he was apparently in b
2 CONC usiness in Nance County till at least 1886, when he was 31 years old, b
2 CONC ut then he was a paymaster in the army in 1900, when he was 45 years ol
2 CONC d.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Army Register of 1940, available on Fold3.com, shows that he was a p
2 CONC aymaster’s clerk from 6 Jun 1898 to 23 Aug 1912 and then a pay clerk in t
2 CONC he quartermaster corps from 24 Aug 1912 to 17 Jul 1916.  After that, he w
2 CONC as promoted to second lieutenant in the quartermaster corps.  He reache
2 CONC d his final rank of major in the quartermaster corps on 7 Apr 1919.  Th
2 CONC e final entries are confusing.  It says he was honorably discharged on 1
2 CONC 3 Oct 1919, but there are additional entries after that, ending with be
2 CONC ing relieved from active duty on 30 Jun 1921.  In the 1920 census, U.S. A
2 CONC rmy is listed for his occupation.  Being in the Philippines in 1900 pro
2 CONC bably implies that he went there for the Spanish–American War in 1898; s
2 CONC ince he joined the army in 1898, it looks like he probably joined becau
2 CONC se of the war.  The “U.S. Register of Civil, Military, and Naval Servic
2 CONC e, 1863-1959” on Ancestry.com has an entry from 1905 indicating that he w
2 CONC as still in Manila at that time.  It also indicates that he was appoint
2 CONC ed in Nance County, Nebraska, which presumably means that is where he j
2 CONC oined the army.  He was in the army during World War I, but it is not k
2 CONC nown when or where he was deployed.  However, there is a passenger list i
2 CONC n “U.S. Army Transport Service Passenger Lists 1910-1939” on Ancestry.c
2 CONC om indicating that he left New York for France on 30 Sep 1918 as a capt
2 CONC ain.  His entry in the 1930 census indicates that he was a veteran of t
2 CONC he U.S. military mobilized for a war or expedition, and for “What war o
2 CONC r expedition” it indicates WW SA, which presumably means he was mobiliz
2 CONC ed for the World War and the Spanish–American war.  It is interesting t
2 CONC o note that Horace’s first cousin once removed, Abiel Leonard Smith, wa
2 CONC s a brigadier general in the quartermaster corps (see the newspaper art
2 CONC icle in the notes for him).  It raises the question of whether Horace s
2 CONC erved under his cousin.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Berkeley Daily Gazette, 9 Sep 1940, p. 11 (obtained from the Berkeley P
2 CONC ublic Library):
2 CONT
2 CONT WILSON--In Berkeley, September 8, 1940, Major Horace Everett Wilson, hu
2 CONC sband of May Mulford Wilson; a native of Missouri; aged 84 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Private family services will be held Tuesday morning, September 10, at 1
2 CONC 1:30 o'clock from "The Little Chapel of the Flowers" (Hull & Sons), Ade
2 CONC line Street at Ashby Station, Berkeley.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1860 census
2 CONT Listed as Horace Wilson, age 4, born in Missouri.  Living with his pare
2 CONC nts.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1870 census
2 CONT Listed as Horace E. Willson, age 14, born in Missouri, living with brot
2 CONC her B.H. Willson and family.  Attended school within the year.
