2 DATE 28 AUG 2020
1 NOTE Several sources list his birth place as Lincoln County, KY.  Christian C
2 CONC ounty seems more likely, but it is possible that Martha went to be with h
2 CONC er mother in Lincoln County.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is something of a tradition of using the mother's maiden last nam
2 CONC e for first names in this extended family -- Davis Reeves, Stockton Don
2 CONC ley, Donley Ewin, Reeves Leonard, Leonard Everett, Edwards Wilson, and m
2 CONC any more.  (For Davis and Edwards, it is pretty common to find records m
2 CONC istakenly show their names as David and Edward.)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT A letter from D.C. Reeves to Mrs. J.E. Leonard, dated 20 Mar 1837, Memp
2 CONC his, TN (from the Abiel Leonard Papers in the State Historical Society o
2 CONC f Missouri Manuscript Collection, obtained from John Wanamaker):
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Sister
2 CONT  
2 CONT Yesterday, when passing by the mouth of the Ohio River, my mind was bro
2 CONC ught to bear on years that are past, when we were all under the protect
2 CONC ion of a father and mother, and to contrast this period of our existenc
2 CONC e with that exhibits how great the changes a few fleeting seasons have m
2 CONC ade in our family.  I feel my dear sister that I have given you good gr
2 CONC ounds to consider me unkind and unbrotherly in not writing to you, but w
2 CONC ere you to ask me the cause of my long silence I am sure that I would b
2 CONC ut speak the true feelings of my nature when I would say that it is owi
2 CONC ng only to our never being in the habit of writing to each other.  But w
2 CONC hen I reflect upon the great changes which our Creator has wrought amon
2 CONC gst us, I feel that my plea for my silence is very unsatisfactory even t
2 CONC o myself.  In the month of December, I went to Mississippi with the int
2 CONC ention to settle there, but the sickness of that climate and the dissip
2 CONC ation of the community were so great that I have abandoned all idea of l
2 CONC iving there.  In the month of February, Mrs. Phillips and myself left C
2 CONC linton, Miss., for Texas, but I was attacked with sickness in Vicksburg
2 CONC , which prevented me from taking the trip.  I got on a boat and returne
2 CONC d immediately to Kentucky.  I am now returning to Clinton, but do not i
2 CONC ntend to remain in south but a week or two.  Mrs. Phillips and Ellen ar
2 CONC e in Mississippi and I presume they will return with me.  I have not se
2 CONC en my father or any of his family since December, but understood that t
2 CONC hey were all well, and that himself and Lady intended to visit you in M
2 CONC ay.  I am pleased with the companion our father has chosen.  She is sen
2 CONC sible, prudent, and affectionate.  Mary has been unwell the most of thi
2 CONC s winter.  Her constitution is very frail.  When I left, her health was s
2 CONC omething better than it had been.  It is probable that I will move out t
2 CONC o Missouri in the course of this season.  The motion of the boat is so g
2 CONC reat that I have written you a rough letter; you must excuse it.  Tende
2 CONC r my love to Grandmother, and to all my connection.
2 CONT  
2 CONT Your brother truly and affectionately,
2 CONT D.C. Reeves
2 CONT
2 CONT [Mary, who was unwell, would be Clark's wife, Mary Jane Phillips Reeves
2 CONC .  Mrs. Phillips was presumably Mary’s mother.  Mary Jane had a younger s
2 CONC ister named Ellen, who was still young at the time of the letter (in fa
2 CONC ct, she would later die when she was only about 15), and who was presum
2 CONC ably the Ellen traveling with Mrs. Phillips.  Davis Clark and Mary Jane a
2 CONC lso had a daughter named Ellen, but she wasn’t born yet at the time of t
2 CONC his letter.  She was probably named for Mary’s sister Ellen after she d
2 CONC ied.  Their father’s new companion would have been Virginia Garth Cross R
2 CONC eeves, whom B.H. Reeves had married about half a year earlier.  Grandmo
2 CONC ther, mentioned at the end, would be Martha Davis Reeves, who stayed in M
2 CONC issouri with the Leonards when B.H. moved back to Kentucky.]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT A letter from B.H. Reeves to A. [Abiel] Leonard, dated 30 Sep 1841, Tod
2 CONC d Co., KY (from the Abiel Leonard Papers in the State Historical Societ
2 CONC y of Missouri Manuscript Collection, obtained from John Wanamaker):
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Sir,
2 CONT
2 CONT It is with the deepest feelings of sorrow that I now write you.  Last n
2 CONC ight I returned home from paying the last sad melancholy duties to my c
2 CONC hild.  Clark Reeves departed this life just before 5 in the morning of t
2 CONC he 28th Inst.  He was taken sick in July, was quite ill for some weeks - r
2 CONC ecovered so far as to be able with his family to visit me.  About four w
2 CONC eeks ago, himself and friends thought he was in a fair way to recover.  A f
2 CONC ew days after his return home, I was sent for.  His malady increased.  N
2 CONC o human skill could stay or arrest it.  He died easy, like a child goin
2 CONC g to sleep, but rejoicing in the confident hope of a happy immortality.  O
2 CONC h that my life, but above all that my death could be like his.  Yea [?] o
2 CONC f certainty of the future.  But he is gone, gone from me.  The stroke y
2 CONC ou can not feel as I feel it, & I pray God that you may never feel such p
2 CONC angs as now wring my heart.  But why should I mourn, 'tis the lot of ma
2 CONC n to die & altho, one of the props of my declining years have been wres
2 CONC ted from under me, 'tis his gain.  An eternity of glory to him, but a w
2 CONC orld of pain to me.  I write to you to communicate the sad intelligence t
2 CONC o his Sister and Brother as prudence may direct.  Heaven bless you & th
2 CONC em.
2 CONT
2 CONT B. H. Reeves
