1 NAME Jeanette E. /Reeves/
2 GIVN Jeanette E.
2 SURN Reeves
1 SEX F
1 _UID 56AEC109A8830340974DACB064B673CAD321
1 CHAN
2 DATE 1 JUN 2020
1 NOTE Middle initial E is from Reeves letters.  Some are addressed to J. E. L
2 CONC eonard; also the introductory information for the Abiel Leonard collect
2 CONC ion at the State Historical Society of Missouri Manuscript Collection m
2 CONC entions that Abiel married Jeanette E. Reeves.  Bob Reeves thinks it mi
2 CONC ght stand for Ewin, which would make sense: named for Jennetta Ewin.  H
2 CONC er name is spelled Jeanetta in the Parks manuscript, Jennette in the Cr
2 CONC ittenden Reeves biography.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Howard County Leader, 19 Dec 1895 (obtained from John Wanamaker):
2 CONT
2 CONT MRS. JEANETTE LEONARD
2 CONT
2 CONT A Venerable and Noble Woman Passes From Earth
2 CONT
2 CONT The news of the death of Mrs. Jeanette Leonard, which reached here Satu
2 CONC rday morning was received with profound sorrow.  Her death was not unex
2 CONC pected however, for she had been in failing health for some months past
2 CONC .  She was a grand, noble woman.  Her funeral was preached by Rev. Pend
2 CONC elton Brooke of Clinton, Tuesday afternoon at St. Mary's Episcopal chur
2 CONC ch, after which her remains were laid to rest in the city cemetery, the
2 CONC re to await a glorious resurrection.
2 CONT
2 CONT the following extended and appropriate notice of her life and death is t
2 CONC aken from the St. Joseph Herald of Saturday.
2 CONT
2 CONT There passed from this earth yesterday in this city one of the most nob
2 CONC le and venerable women of Missouri, Mrs. Jeanette Leonard, of Fayette, H
2 CONC oward county.  She died at the residence of her son-in-law, Dr. J.D. Sm
2 CONC ith, where she had been ill for some months.  Mrs. Leonard was the wido
2 CONC w of Judge Abiel Leonard, formerly of the supreme bench of Missouri, a d
2 CONC istinguished citizen and able lawyer during his life.  Mrs. Leonard was 8
2 CONC 3 years of age.  She was born in Todd county, Kentucky, and was the dau
2 CONC ghter of Col. Benjamin H. Reeves.  She came to Missouri with her parent
2 CONC s in 1817.  Her father served in the war of 1812 as an officer under Ge
2 CONC n. Harrison, afterward president of the United States and grandfather o
2 CONC f ex. President Benjamin Harrison.  He was president of the territorial c
2 CONC onvention which formed the constitution under which Missouri was admitt
2 CONC ed to the union of states.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Leonard was married in 1830 to Judge Abiel Leonard at New Franklin
2 CONC .  Two years later they removed to Fayette, Howard county, where Judge L
2 CONC eonard erected a palatial home in which Mrs. Leonard has lived ever sin
2 CONC ce.  She was a frequent visitor to St. Joseph, and a couple of months a
2 CONC go was taken ill here, her illness terminating fatally yesterday.  She l
2 CONC eaves two sons, Bishop Abiel Leonard, of Utah and Nevada, and Nathaniel W
2 CONC . Leonard who resides at Fayette.  Four daughters survive her, Mrs. Hor
2 CONC ace Everett, of Council Bluffs, Iowa; Mrs. General Odon Guitar, of Colu
2 CONC mbia, and Mrs. Dr. J. D. Smith and Mrs. Dr. C. H. Darby, of this city.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Leonard was one of the brilliant women of Missouri.  She was a gre
2 CONC at reader and profound thinker.  She was an encyclopedia of history.  S
2 CONC he was intimately acquainted with all the distinguished men of the Stat
2 CONC e, and in her home she entertained all the prominent people that ever v
2 CONC isited Fayette.  She was a woman of strictly commanding appearance and a b
2 CONC rilliant conversationalist.  At her great age up to the time of her las
2 CONC t illness it was esteemed a great pleasure to be in her company and con
2 CONC verse with her.  She possessed a love for all human kind, and her manne
2 CONC rs were as free from guile as those of a child.  Her whole life was spe
2 CONC nt in doing good deeds.  Hundreds of people in need were the recipients o
2 CONC f her bounty, and a great number consulted her with great profit to the
2 CONC mselves.  Here was one of the great hearts that alone understand how mu
2 CONC ch glory there is in doing good, and if "the actions of the just smell s
2 CONC weet and blossom in the dust" her resting place will surely be a bed of p
2 CONC erpetual flowers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Surrounding her death bed were her three daughters, Mrs. Everett, Mrs. S
2 CONC mith and Mrs. Darby, grandchildren and other relatives.  She passed so p
2 CONC eacefully that they-
2 CONT
2 CONT "Thought her dying when she slept,
2 CONT And sleeping when she died".
2 CONT
2 CONT A special car furnished by General Manager W. C. Brown of the "Burlingt
2 CONC on" last night conveyed the remains of Mrs. Leonard to Fayette.  They w
2 CONC ere accompanied by her grandson, Capt. Abiel Leonard Smith, of the regu
2 CONC lar army, her daughters, Mrs. Everett, Mrs Smith and Mrs. Darby.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT I have seen in other places that Jeanette's and Abiel's wedding was in F
2 CONC ayette, MO, but the obituary above says New Franklin.
