2 DATE 16 AUG 2020
1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT The third child of Walter and Martha Davis was Euphemia, who married Ch
2 CONC arles Donly.  Of her early life I have not much information, except tha
2 CONC t she was a cheerful, amiable, and merry girl.  She married when my mot
2 CONC her was quite a child and of course she could tell but little of the gi
2 CONC rl-hood of her sister Euphy.  I think, however, that she was one of the f
2 CONC ew of the family that had taste and talent for song.  At an early perio
2 CONC d in the settlement of Kentucky, Uncle Donly and Uncle James Davis and p
2 CONC erhaps Uncle Reeves removed from Virginia to Lincoln County, Kentucky.  U
2 CONC ncle Donly settled in that county on the Hanging fork of Dick River, ab
2 CONC out four miles from the town of Stanford.  There he died some years aft
2 CONC erwards, leaving his wife and seven children, four daughters and three s
2 CONC ons, and these she brought up to be men and women.  Lincoln County was i
2 CONC nhabited by those who had spent their early life in the wilderness, for t
2 CONC he most part, consequently educational advantages were slender and mann
2 CONC ers rude.  Of course their circumstances had their influence on the Don
2 CONC lys, yet by the prudence and energy of their mother, they grew up to be r
2 CONC espectable men and women, with such education as the common schools of t
2 CONC hat rude country could give.  Her sons she put out with respectable men t
2 CONC o learn trades.  James and Charles were carpenters, William a tanner.  T
2 CONC he third daughter, Sallie, died when young....
2 CONT
2 CONT Some four or five years after my cousin’s marriage [referring to Polly D
2 CONC onley’s marriage to Benjamin Givens], my father moved to Kentucky, and s
2 CONC pent the first winter in Lincoln County.  I then became acquainted with m
2 CONC y Aunt Euphemia.  I found her a most genial and affectionate old lady n
2 CONC ear sixty years old, and from that time till near the close of her life
2 CONC , I was often with her, sometimes for months in the same house.  She wa
2 CONC s a most amiable woman, cheerful and happy, fond of reading, and her te
2 CONC mper uniformly good.  I don’t think that in my acquaintance with her I e
2 CONC ver saw the least manifestation of fretfulness or anger.  In the year o
2 CONC f 1819, Mr. Givens moved to Missouri.  Aunt started with them they stop
2 CONC ped for a few days at my father’s, who lived in Christian County, Kentu
2 CONC cky.  She being not well and very much fatigued concluded to remain wit
2 CONC h my mother until Mr. Givens would be settled.  She remained there that w
2 CONC inter, in which her son, William Donly, residing in Russellville, was m
2 CONC arried.  He took her to live with him; she continued to remain until he
2 CONC r death.  She died in Russellville, I think about 1821, at the age of s
2 CONC ixty-six.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Cormode info lists her as Euphemia Davis?  WFT 5 # 1914 spells her n
2 CONC ame Euphema.
