1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT Willis Long Reeves, the youngest son of Brewer and Martha Reeves, was p
2 CONC laced in the clerk’s office at Hopkinsville, Kentucky, with James McLau
2 CONC ghlin, an experienced clerk and a good lawyer, when a youth, remained w
2 CONC ith him for several years, acquiring a fine character for business habi
2 CONC ts and faithfulness in office, and when Todd County was organized from p
2 CONC arts of Christian and Logan, (1820) he received the appointment of Cler
2 CONC k of the Circuit Court, which he held for many years, and was highly es
2 CONC teemed for his integrity and accuracy in the discharge of the duties of h
2 CONC is office.  He was married three times, first to Susan Phillips, with w
2 CONC hom he lived happily for some years and had children (1).  After her de
2 CONC ath, he married Mary Harrison, widow of Carter Harrison and daughter of H
2 CONC aden Edwards of Texas; and after her death, Mrs. Conly, widow of Dr. Co
2 CONC nly and daughter of William Wilson near Russellville.  He died about 18
2 CONC 67 or ’68, aged about seventy-three years.  Years before his death, he p
2 CONC rofessed faith in the Redeemer and became an active member of the C.P. C
2 CONC hurch.  I believe his brother Otway had also united with the Church pre
2 CONC vious to his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Biographical Encyclopedia of Kentucky", J. M. Armstrong & Company, Cin
2 CONC cinnati, Ohio, 1878 (obtained from http://boards.ancestry.com/mbexec/me
2 CONC ssage/an/localities.northam.usa.states.kentucky.counties.woodford/772):
2 CONT
2 CONT Willis Long Reeves Sr., Lawyer, was born in Woodford County, Ky., while h
2 CONC is parents were enroute from Virginia to Kentucky on the 9th of Februar
2 CONC y 1796.  His educational attainments were necessarily limited, but, by c
2 CONC lose application he succeeded in acquiring an English education in adva
2 CONC nce of the ordinary facilities offered in his times. Owing to a pecunia
2 CONC ry misfortune with which his father was stricken, he was, at an early a
2 CONC ge, thrown upon his own resources and succeeded, at the age of 16 years i
2 CONC n obtaining employment in the clerk's office at Hopkinsville, notwithst
2 CONC anding his residence in Trenton, Todd County. In this position he remai
2 CONC ned until 1822 when he was made first Circuit Clerk of the County, and, i
2 CONC n a few years, County Clerk, holding and filling both offices until the a
2 CONC doption of the new State Constitution in 1850.  After leaving his publi
2 CONC c offices he gave his attention to his farm and other large property in
2 CONC terests, of which he had at this time possessed himself.  He early unti
2 CONC ed [sic] with the Cumberland Presbyterian Church, of which he was an ac
2 CONC tive and leading member and transacted all the legal business of the co
2 CONC ngregation gratuitously, besides donating largely from his means toward t
2 CONC he support and encouragement of the cause.  In politics, he was a stead
2 CONC fast Henry Clay Whig, both from conviction of the correctness of the pr
2 CONC inciples and a strong personal attachment to the leader of the party.  H
2 CONC e was a man of great energy and most indomitable will, who knew not fai
2 CONC lure. He turned a great deal of his time and attention, besides his mea
2 CONC ns, to the education and elevation of the masses.  His death, which occ
2 CONC urred on the 29th of April 1866 was universally regret[t]ed and the com
2 CONC munity sustained a loss not easily repaired.  He was married, in 1856, t
2 CONC o Mrs. Emily Carr, daughter of James H. Davidson, Esq., of Logan County
2 CONC , and left five children.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The above biography says that he was born in 1796, but some other sourc
2 CONC es say he was born in 1793, which would fit with Parks' statement that h
2 CONC e was about 73 years old when he died.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The dates in the Ancestral File don't make sense (as well as those of s
2 CONC ome of the other brothers).
2 CONT
2 CONT WFT 22, 1690 lists his name as William Long Reeves.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT I don't know who wrote this, but it was obtained from the Trewolla Mosk
2 CONC op Haas Mansfield Tree web site:
2 CONT
2 CONT This narrative properly begins with the statements of Dr. Thomas Davis P
2 CONC arks, page 11 of his family history.  This paper should be attached to t
2 CONC his effort.  Dr. Parks states that Willis Long Reeves was the youngest s
2 CONC on of Brewer and Martha Davis Reeves.  He was placed in the office of J
2 CONC ames McLaughlin who was Clerk of the Circuit Court in Hopkinsville.  Wh
2 CONC en Todd County was formed in 1820, Willis Long Reeves was appointed Cle
2 CONC rk of the Circuit Court in this newly formed county.  Dr. Parks states t
2 CONC hat he was married three times but he seems not to know of the fourth a
2 CONC nd last marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Willis Long Reeves married Susan E. Phillips (1804-1829) on November 13
2 CONC , 1823.  Susan was from Paris in Bourbon County.  To this union was bor
2 CONC n three children: William Reeves (1824-1852), Albert Reeves (1826-1847) a
2 CONC nd Douglas Reeves (1828-after 1850).  Susan died in 1829 after an illne
2 CONC ss of six weeks.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second marriage of Willis Long Reeves was to Mrs. Margaret Henry Ed
2 CONC wards Harrison (1803-1840) on November 18, 1830.  Mrs. Harrison was the w
2 CONC idow of Carter Henry Harrison of Russellville, Kentucky.  [Section abou
2 CONC t her previous children in notes for Margaret Henry Edwards.]
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Margret Henry <u>Edwards</u> Harrison and Willis Long R
2 CONC eeves were; Martha Park Reeves (1831-1833), Willis Brewer Reeves (1835-
2 CONC 1921), Haden Edwards Reeves(1838-1909), and an infant son born on Febru
2 CONC ary 4,1840 and died on the same date.  The mother died three days later F
2 CONC ebruary 7, 1840.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 12, 1840, Willis Long Reeves remarried and this time the br
2 CONC ide was the daughter of James Wilson, one of the prominent citizens of R
2 CONC ussellville.  She was the widow of James B. Connelly and had at least t
2 CONC wo children- Erasmus and James H. Connelly.  The third wife, Caroline M
2 CONC . <u>Wilson</u> Connelly, was born August 12, 1806 and died March 29, 1
2 CONC 848.  Four children were born to this union but we know about only two.  T
2 CONC he oldest was Willis Long Reeves Jr. who graduated from Yale and was a d
2 CONC istinguished jurist.  [Section on Willis Long Reeves Jr. in notes for h
2 CONC im.  The following sentence was in that section but is moved here becau
2 CONC se it names the second child of Willis and Caroline.]  We have a letter f
2 CONC rom J.C. Reeves to "Ma" (Emily <u>Davidson</u> Carr Reeves) in "Letters t
2 CONC o Priscilla".
2 CONT
2 CONT The fourth wife of Willis Long Reeves was Emily <u>Davidson</u> Carr (1
2 CONC 811-1876).  [Continued in notes for Emily Davidson.]
