1 NAME Walter /Davis/
2 GIVN Walter
2 SURN Davis
1 SEX M
1 _UID 42608BEFA9ABFA42BAF4CD1F2A9D39CE147C
1 CHAN
2 DATE 18 MAY 2020
1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter Davis came to America between 1740 and 1750, and spent some time i
2 CONC n trading with the Indians northwest of the Ohio.  He was of respectabl
2 CONC e family of the north of Ireland, which traced its ancestry to Wales.  I b
2 CONC elieve he was the youngest son of the family, but am not able to state t
2 CONC he Christian name of his father nor the maiden name of his mother, or i
2 CONC n what country he was born, but he was a small man with sparkling black e
2 CONC yes, and most genial of spirit.  In or about the year of 1750, he marri
2 CONC ed Martha Cunningham and settled at Rock Spring.  Whether great-grandfa
2 CONC ther [Robert Cunningham] died at this time, I cannot say.  But I have a
2 CONC lways understood from my mother and other members of the family that my g
2 CONC randfather Davis became the possessor of Rock Spring soon after his mar
2 CONC riage, and that all of his children were born on that place.  Walter Da
2 CONC vis, as I said, was a man of genial spirit.  So far as I know, he never h
2 CONC eld a civil office, but was an elder in the Presbyterian Church, and ex
2 CONC ercised almost boundless influence throughout his neighborhood.  There w
2 CONC as but little use for law or lawyers where he lived, for he managed to s
2 CONC ettle all difficulties by the law of love.  In short, I doubt not, he i
2 CONC s in the enjoyment of the blessings promised to the peacemaker.  I thin
2 CONC k he was not an actual participant in the Revolutionary struggle, altho
2 CONC ugh an ardent patriot, and heart and soul for the cause of Liberty.  He w
2 CONC as then advanced in life, had a large family, and was bound to provide f
2 CONC or it and protect it.  But he, no doubt, gave freely of his substance f
2 CONC or the good cause; and his oldest son went into the army.  The others w
2 CONC ere too young.  All who personally knew Walter Davis regarded him with t
2 CONC he utmost veneration and affection.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Parks manuscript lists eight children of Walter and Martha Davis. T
2 CONC ogether, the wills of Walter and Martha list nine children; the one not l
2 CONC isted by Parks is John.  I would have guessed from this that John was t
2 CONC he youngest child, but the Ancestral File shows him as being born befor
2 CONC e 1770, which would make him older than Elizabeth.  The Ancestral File a
2 CONC lso lists a daughter named Euphonia, who was born before 1762.  I do no
2 CONC t include her since she is not listed anywhere else.  Parks says that E
2 CONC uphemia was the third child and James was fourth, but if the birth date
2 CONC s for Martha and James are correct, there is no way that Euphemia could h
2 CONC ave been born between them.  On the other hand, several sources say she w
2 CONC as born about 1756.  Also, her mother's will appears to list her childr
2 CONC en in order of birth, and Euphemia (transcribed as Euphora) is listed a
2 CONC fter James (actually, the heirs of James).  This theory also fits with J
2 CONC ohn being the youngest child, because he is listed last in Martha Cunni
2 CONC ngham Davis's will.
2 CONT
2 CONT Several sources say that Walter was born in 1733, but that seems too la
2 CONC te.  Birth in 1733 would mean he was only about 17 when he married Mart
2 CONC ha, and according to Dr. Parks he had spent time trading with the India
2 CONC ns before marrying Martha and settling at Rock Spring.  Perhaps it woul
2 CONC d work if the marriage was actually later than 1750.  It might make sen
2 CONC se for Robert Cunningham's sale of land to Walter in June 1753 to have b
2 CONC een at the time of the wedding.  That would not be consistent with daug
2 CONC hter Margaret being born in or about 1751, though, or even with daughte
2 CONC r Martha being born in January 1754.  For what it's worth, Find A Grave (
2 CONC listing for Walter Davis) says the inscription on his gravestone is 70 y
2 CONC r.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The names of Walter’s parents come from the Dryden manuscript and WFT 5 # 1
2 CONC 914.  Further research on them is needed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter Davis's will, from the Dryden manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God, Amen, I Walter Davis of Augusta County & Commonweal
2 CONC th of Virginia being weak in body but of sound mind & memory Blessed be t
2 CONC he almighty God for the same, do make and sign ordain this my last Will & T
2 CONC estament, as for the worldly Estate with which God has blessed me, I de
2 CONC vise & bequeath in the manner & form following to wit;
2 CONT
2 CONT It is my will that my just debts be first paid.  It is my will that all m
2 CONC y lands shall be sold, & also my entry that I made joining the Seder Sp
2 CONC ring Survey.
2 CONT
2 CONT I allow my Executors hereafter named to sell & convey my right of it to t
2 CONC he best advantage as it now stands, all which lands I allow my said Exe
2 CONC cutors to sell & convey as soon as they can have a good opportunity & I d
2 CONC o hereby empower them to make a sufficient title for the said land as I m
2 CONC yself could do and the money they receive for it they are to keep the o
2 CONC ne half of each payment & put it to interest & if that interest is not s
2 CONC ufficient I do allow them to make use of the principle for the support o
2 CONC f my wife & <u>daughter Agnes</u> & the other half of the price of the l
2 CONC ands, I give unto my <u>son John Davis</u>, him to get one of each paym
2 CONC ent, as soon as it is paid.
2 CONT
2 CONT I devise to my said John Davis the Waggon & geers & the farming utensil
2 CONC s except the four axes & two of he is to have, which ever two he choses a
2 CONC nd do allow him to live on the land, untill it is sold, if be think fit
2 CONC , and give his mother the one third of the produce, & to put her part o
2 CONC f both grain & hay up in good order in the lower end of the barn or wha
2 CONC tever place she choses to have it put, and my wife to hold possession o
2 CONC f the dwelling house & That part of the barn until the land is sold, bu
2 CONC t if my son will not chose to live on the land & pay his mother that re
2 CONC nt, I allow my executors to rent it to the best advantage they can for t
2 CONC he Support of my wife & daughter Agnes for untill the land can be sold t
2 CONC o the most advantage for the Legatees, & my said son John Davis to have t
2 CONC wo thirds of the rent, and
2 CONT
2 CONT I do give devise & bequeath to my beloved <u>wife Martha Davis</u> the w
2 CONC hole of both household and kitchen furniture & my negroe girl Clery & a
2 CONC ll her increase to her & her heirs & assigns forever with all my sheep g
2 CONC ees & her choice of two cows to dispose of as she thinks fit, except on
2 CONC e bed & I give & bequeath to my daughter Agnes forever.  I allow the ho
2 CONC gs to be equal divided between my wife & John, and all the remainder of m
2 CONC y estate not otherwise disposed of, I do allow my executors to sell & c
2 CONC ollect it with all the money due to me at my decease & put the whole wi
2 CONC th what ready I may have to interest, which interest & principle, I do l
2 CONC ikewise allow with the before mentioned interest & principle for afores
2 CONC aid sufficient support my wife & daughter Agnes during their natural li
2 CONC ves & what money is not expended at both of their deaths, I devise to b
2 CONC e divided in the manner following.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give my <u>daughter, Euphema Donelly</u> twenty pounds and my <u>daug
2 CONC hter Elizabeth Parks</u> twenty pounds & all the rest of my children th
2 CONC at is not heretofore mentioned, each of them to have one dollar, I devi
2 CONC se to my grand children, Patsy Donelly, Wat Ewin, Walter Davis, Martha A
2 CONC nn Davis, Patsey Davis & Patsey Davis Herrin, each of them to have ten p
2 CONC ounds.  In case ever my son John Davis should have a son named Walter, o
2 CONC r if my daughter Elizabeth Parks should have a son named Walter, or if e
2 CONC ither have a daughter named Martha, each of them is to have an equal sh
2 CONC are with the rest of my grand children before mentioned & in case <u>Ma
2 CONC rtha Rivers</u> has no child named Walter or Martha she is to have five p
2 CONC ounds herself & in case there should be more money left I allow my daug
2 CONC hters & grand children to have in proportion to what I have allowed the
2 CONC m, & if there is left they are to take in proportion.  I allow Andrew H
2 CONC unter the privilege of the water that runs through his pipes from my sp
2 CONC ring during the life of his present wife, if she lives there if she do n
2 CONC ot more damage that what she do at present, & also during the natural l
2 CONC ife of the said Andrew Hunter under the same restrictions & and I do he
2 CONC reby nominate constitute & appoint my beloved <u>son William Davis</u> & m
2 CONC y trusty friends Joseph Bell & Robert Stuart Executors of this my last W
2 CONC ill & Testament revoking all others.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Testimony hereof I have hereunto set my hand & affixed my seal this 1
2 CONC 5th day March in ye year of our Lord eighteen hundred & three.
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter Davis  (Seal)
2 CONT [Augusta County, Virginia, Superior Court Will Book 1-A, page 51]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT WFT 137 # 299:
2 CONT Supplement to the Annals of Augusta County, VA, pages 442-443
2 CONT Davis Family Records, February 21, 1878
2 CONT Family History of Christian County, KY 1797-1986, page 149
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT WFT 22 # 1690:
2 CONT He came to America between 1740 and 1745.  He spent some time trading w
2 CONC ith the Indians northwest of OH.  After marriage he settled at Rock Spr
2 CONC ing.  all his children were born at Rock Spring Farm, Augusta Co., VA. H
2 CONC e was a member of the House of Burgesses.  He was an Elder in the Presb
2 CONC yterian Church.  As an older man he joined the Colonial Army.
2 CONT Rock spring Farm was near Tinkling Spring Church.
2 CONT His daughters were called the five smartest women in Augusta Co., VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that most of the notes from WFT 22 # 1690 are clearly based on Par
2 CONC ks.  However, the Parks manuscript says that Walter Davis never held ci
2 CONC vil office, so the part about him being a member of the House of Burges
2 CONC ses may not be correct (available lists of members of the House of Burg
2 CONC esses say they are not complete, so it is hard to be sure).  That Walte
2 CONC r was a church elder is confirmed in "The Tinkling Spring: Headwater of F
2 CONC reedom", by Howard McKnight Wilson, which has a list of Elders of the T
2 CONC inkling Spring Meeting House in Appendix C.  It says that his service b
2 CONC egan before 1765 and presumably continued until his death on 20 Mar 180
2 CONC 3.  The part about him joining the Colonial Army disagrees with Parks, b
2 CONC ut according to Cynthia McBride (email dated 18 May 2020) Walter is con
2 CONC sidered a patriot by the DAR because of his service as a surveyor durin
2 CONC g the war.  This is confirmed by an entry in Augusta County Order Book 1
2 CONC 6, p. 378 (17 Nov 1778), which says, "Ordered that Walter Davis be cont
2 CONC inued Surveyor of the road from Love's ford to the road that leads from L
2 CONC ong's Mill to Staunton and ordered that he with the usual tithables cle
2 CONC ar and keep the same in repair as the law directs."  ("Augusta County, V
2 CONC irginia Court Orders 1774-1785" by Bevin J. Creel, self-published, Alex
2 CONC andria, VA, 2010.)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Tinkling Spring church was very important in the early history of A
2 CONC ugusta County and to the Cunningham/Davis family.  Since Walter was a c
2 CONC hurch elder for 40 years, this seems like a good place to include some d
2 CONC iscussion of the church.  This brief discussion is based on the book “T
2 CONC heTinkling Spring: Headwater of Freedom”, which is a history of the chu
2 CONC rch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Religion was an important driver in the settlement of the Shenandoah Va
2 CONC lley.  Many Scots-Irish Presbyterians came to America seeking religious f
2 CONC reedom, but they had trouble practicing their religion even here.  Many o
2 CONC f the Scots-Irish initially came to Pennsylvania, but conflicts with th
2 CONC e Quakers and others there led them to head up the Shenandoah River int
2 CONC o the wilderness.  The settled part of Virginia, east of the Blue Ridge
2 CONC , was dominated by the Church of England, but in 1738 Virginia explicit
2 CONC ly established a new religious policy for the settlers beyond the mount
2 CONC ains.  “Tinkling Spring”, on p. 44, says “It was in this condition of f
2 CONC reedom of conscience and enjoyment of limited civil and religious liber
2 CONC ties that the Presbyterian faith was first to take root and grow vigoro
2 CONC usly in the Virginia Colony.”
2 CONT
2 CONT The first regular Presbyterian pastor in the area was John Craig, who w
2 CONC as ordained in Pennsylvania in 1740, with the intent to practice in the s
2 CONC outhern part of the Beverley Manor.  In 1740 and subsequent years, seve
2 CONC ral “meeting houses” (only the official Church of England could legally u
2 CONC se the term “church”) were established for church services.  At first t
2 CONC he meetings were in homes, but on 14 Aug 1741 a resolution was recorded f
2 CONC or the south side of Rev. Craig’s congregation to purchase land and bui
2 CONC ld an actual meeting house.  Robert Cunningham was one of the signers.  I
2 CONC t was decided on 28 Sep 1742 that the meeting house would be built at t
2 CONC he Tinkling Spring.  ("Tinkling Spring", p. 3: “The earliest pioneers i
2 CONC n the Valley of Virginia found a bold spring, whose emerging waters mad
2 CONC e a musical sound upon the cavernous rocks, and they called it the tink
2 CONC ling spring.”)  It was to be a log structure, 50 ft. by 24 ft., with do
2 CONC ors on the two shorter sides, which were on the north and the south.  T
2 CONC he “Tinkling Spring” book has a drawing and a floor-plan layout. There w
2 CONC as no chimney and no heating.  There was a note in Rev. Craig’s record o
2 CONC f baptisms that Sunday, 14 Apr 1745, was the first day meeting at the “
2 CONC Contentious Meeting house about half built”.  A 110-acre tract of land w
2 CONC as purchased from William Thompson for £15 on 19 Nov 1747.  There were s
2 CONC ome difficulties in raising the money to finish the work and then to pa
2 CONC y the minister’s salary, so starting on 26 Apr 1748 a policy was establ
2 CONC ished of renting out the seats in the church!  The rents ranged from £1
2 CONC .12.6 down to £0.12.0 per year (the numbers are pounds.shillings.pence)
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1764, the congregation of the Triple Forks of the Shenandoah was spl
2 CONC it up and Rev. Craig went to the northern part, which met at the Stone M
2 CONC eeting House (several miles north of Staunton, at present-day Fort Defi
2 CONC ance).  Tinkling Spring did not have a regular pastor for the next seve
2 CONC ral years, until James Waddel took over in May 1776.  This development w
2 CONC as important to Walter Davis’s family, especially daughter Sallie, as r
2 CONC ecounted in the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT  
2 CONT In the year of 1776, that remarkable man and devoted minister of God, J
2 CONC ames Waddell, came to Augusta County, Virginia, and was settled as past
2 CONC or over Tinkling Spring Church.  Of this church our grandfather [Walter D
2 CONC avis] was a ruling elder.  Mr. Waddell lived within about three miles o
2 CONC f him.  Of course intimate intercourse immediately sprang up between th
2 CONC e families.  At this time my Aunts Smith, Reeves, and Donly were all ma
2 CONC rried.  My Uncle James was probably absent in the army, but Aunt Sallie w
2 CONC as just budding into early womanhood with the warm affections and sprig
2 CONC htly intellect.  It is easy to imagine the influence of his rare eloque
2 CONC nce and of the society of a refined intelligent family on a girl of tha
2 CONC t age and character.  [From the notes for Sarah (Sallie) Davis.]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Rev. Waddel was also one of the founding trustees of Liberty Hall in Le
2 CONC xington, VA, which later became Washington and Lee University.  He was p
2 CONC astor at Tinkling Spring until 1784.  According to "Tinkling Spring" (p
2 CONC . 208), the congregation was unable to support a minister in their post
2 CONC -war condition, so Rev. Waddel established a new church in Staunton, th
2 CONC ough he did not stay there very long.  After Waddel, there was another g
2 CONC ap without a regular pastor, until John McCue became pastor in 1791.  H
2 CONC e served until 1818, well after Walter Davis’s time as church elder.
2 CONT  
2 CONT It was resolved on 20 Jun 1777 to build a new meeting house of stone or b
2 CONC rick.  Walter Davis pledged £10 to the effort.  Five others also pledge
2 CONC d £10; the other pledges were smaller, so we see that Walter was prospe
2 CONC rous and deeply committed to the church.  However, this effort was disr
2 CONC upted by the war and the new stone meeting house (60 ft. by 30 ft.) was n
2 CONC ot built until about 1792, under Rev. McCue.  Walter Davis was one of t
2 CONC he trustees appointed to direct the building.  They sold 107 acres of t
2 CONC he church’s land on 15 Jun 1794 to help cover the expenses.
