2 DATE 30 MAY 2020
1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT As before remarked, Martha Cunningham was nine years old when her fathe
2 CONC r settled at Rock Spring, and at Rock Spring she resided until her deat
2 CONC h at about eighty years of age.  She was a woman of culture, so far as m
2 CONC ental culture could be obtained in an almost uncultivated wilderness.  
2 CONC But although the early inhabitants of Augusta County were poor, they ha
2 CONC d Scotch perseverance and Irish sprightliness, and although they had bu
2 CONC t few books and no newspapers, they carried the Bible with them, and th
2 CONC is was the basis of their philosophy, and very successfully did Martha C
2 CONC unningham study that best of books.  I have often heard my mother say t
2 CONC hat if any verse or passage of the Bible was repeated to her, my grandm
2 CONC other, she would instantly name book, chapter, and verse, so perfect wa
2 CONC s her memory, and so diversified that the neighbors relied on her for t
2 CONC he ages of their children.  She lived on the same spot from childhood t
2 CONC o old age, beloved and respected, and a blessing to all within the sphe
2 CONC re of her influence, and died lamented by all who knew her.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In a letter from Katherine Bushman to J.H. Johnson in the Katherine Gen
2 CONC try Bushman Papers, 1961-1997 (Accession 35743, Personal Papers Collect
2 CONC ion, The Library of Virginia, Richmond, VA), she says that Martha's gra
2 CONC vestone gives age 75 at death.  This makes her birth at about 1731, and i
2 CONC f she was nine years old when they settled at Rock Spring that would ha
2 CONC ve been about 1740.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Martha Cunningham Davis's will, from the Dryden manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Name of God Amen, I Martha Davis of Augusta County and State of V
2 CONC irginia Being weak in Body but of sound mind and memory do make and ord
2 CONC ain this my last will and Testament as for the worldly Estate with whic
2 CONC h God has Blessed me I devise and bequeath in the manner and form follo
2 CONC wing Viz.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is my will that my Property that I have not disposed of myself befor
2 CONC e my deceas shall be equally divided by my Executors without making a s
2 CONC ale between my <u>Daughter Margret Smith</u> my <u>Daughter Martha Reiv
2 CONC es</u> the Heirs of my <u>Son James Davis</u> my <u>Daughter Euphora Do
2 CONC nley</u> the Heirs of my <u>Daughter Sally Ewin</u> my <u>Son William D
2 CONC avis</u> my <u>Daughter Elizabeth Parks</u> and my <u>Son John Davis</u
2 CONC > and I do hereby nominate constitute and appoint my Beloved Sons Willi
2 CONC am Davis and John Davis Executors of this my Last will and Testament Re
2 CONC voking all others by me heretofore made in Testimony hereof I have here
2 CONC unto set my hand and affixed my Seal this 26th day of December in the Y
2 CONC ear of our Lord one Thousand Eight hundred and Five.
2 CONT
2 CONT Martha Davis  (Seal)
2 CONT [Augusta County, Virginia, Will Book 10, page 38]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT WFT 22 # 1690:
2 CONT Martha was about 9 when her parents settled at Rock Spring.  She lived t
2 CONC here until her death.  She studied the Bible faithfully and could quote c
2 CONC hapter and verse for any scripture.
2 CONT
2 CONT WFT 22 # 1690 had her death as about 1749 (Rock Spring Farm, Augusta Co
2 CONC ., VA), which makes no sense since her children were all born after tha
2 CONC t.  Possibly confused with her mother.
