1 _UID 6757020150A76947A43AA1646FC7F7F56D9C
1 CHAN
2 DATE 11 JUN 2020
1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Cunningham emigrated from Ireland to America early in the eighte
2 CONC enth century, probably about 1720.  From what county I cannot say, but c
2 CONC ertainly from the northern part of the island, as he and all of his fam
2 CONC ily were Presbyterians and are believed to be descendants from the Cunn
2 CONC inghams of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT He settled at Rock Spring, in Augusta County, Virginia, about 1735, at w
2 CONC hich time his daughter Martha, our grandmother, was nine years old.  He h
2 CONC ad two other daughters and I believe no son.
2 CONT ...
2 CONT I will now go back and say a few words in regard to the character and s
2 CONC tanding of Robert Cunningham as derived from conversations with his gra
2 CONC nddaughters.  When he settled in Augusta County, it was almost a wilder
2 CONC ness extending from the Blue Ridge as far west as white people dwelt, a
2 CONC nd the savages roamed and hunted over the greater part of it at will.  I
2 CONC t therefore required a man of nerve to settle down on a farm with a fam
2 CONC ily, and cultivate it.  Such a man was Robert Cunningham; that he was a
2 CONC mong the most intelligent of the frontier men is evinced by the fact th
2 CONC at he was their representative in the House of Burgesses in the Colony o
2 CONC f Virginia.  And there is a tradition in the family that at least on on
2 CONC e occasion he was engaged in debate with the <u>Randolph</u> of that da
2 CONC y, and did not come off second best.  I presume that he was a true Scot
2 CONC ch Irishman, rough but sure.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Settlers by the Long Grey Trail" says that Robert Cunningham was one o
2 CONC f the first Burgesses of Augusta County, along with John Willson (no kn
2 CONC own relationship between this Willson and my Wilsons, but there is some D
2 CONC NA evidence that there might be a relationship).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Annals of Augusta County, Virginia, from 1726 to 1871", by Joseph A. W
2 CONC addell (section on The Cunninghams obtained from online edition at http
2 CONC ://www.roanetnhistory.org/waddellsannals.html):
2 CONT
2 CONT THE CUNNINGHAMS.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Cunningham, a native of north Ireland, settled on a farm called R
2 CONC ock Spring, in Augusta county, about the year 1735.  He was one of the f
2 CONC irst set of justices of the peace appointed in 1745, and afterward a me
2 CONC mber of the House of Burgesses.  His wife was a widow Hamilton, and the m
2 CONC other of several children at the time of her second marriage.  One of h
2 CONC er daughters, Mary Hamilton, married David Campbell, and was the mother o
2 CONC f John and Arthur Campbell, and others.  Two of the daughters of Robert C
2 CONC unningham also married Campbells.  He had no son.  His daughter, Martha
2 CONC , about the year 1750, married Walter Davis, who became the owner of Ro
2 CONC ck Spring farm.  Mr. Davis never held civil office, but was an elder of T
2 CONC inkling Spring church, and a man of much influence.  His daughter, Marg
2 CONC aret, married John Smith, and was the mother of Judge Daniel Smith, of R
2 CONC ockingham.  His son, William Davis, born in 1765, married Annie Caldwel
2 CONC l, and died about 1851, aged eighty-six.  He was a man of high standing i
2 CONC n the community, a justice of the peace, high sheriff, etc.  Walter Dav
2 CONC is, Jr., son of William, born in 1791, was for many years one of the tw
2 CONC o commissioners of the revenue in Augusta county, and noted for his fai
2 CONC thful and intelligent discharge of the duties of his office.  His wife w
2 CONC as Rebecca Van Lear.  William C. Davis, a brother of Walter Davis, Jr., r
2 CONC emoved to Missouri in 1836 or 1837.  Dr. Thomas Parks, of Missouri, is t
2 CONC he only surviving grandchild of Walter Davis, Sr.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Cunningham, believed to have been a brother of Robert, lived in St
2 CONC aunton, his residence being on Lot No. 1, southwest corner of Augusta s
2 CONC treet and Spring Lane.  He had three daughters and one son.  His oldest d
2 CONC aughter was Mrs. Margaret Reed, who was baptized by Mr. Craig in 1747, a
2 CONC nd died in 1827.  Another daughter, Isabella, married Major Robert Burn
2 CONC s, and was the mother of Mrs. Waterman and Mrs. Gambill, of Rockingham.  T
2 CONC he third daughter of John Cunningham, Elizabeth, married Captain Thomas S
2 CONC mith.  The daughters of Capt. Smith were Mrs. Michael Garber, Mrs. Mose
2 CONC s McCue, and Mrs. John Jones.  Captain Walter Cunningham, only son of J
2 CONC ohn, removed to Kentucky in 1788, and thus the name disappeared from th
2 CONC e county.
2 CONT
2 CONT We are indebted to Major James B. Dorman, a grandson of Mrs. Moses McCu
2 CONC e, for most of the above facts.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1735 date for Robert settling at Rock Spring in Augusta County migh
2 CONC t be too early.  Daughter Martha was probably nine years old around 174
2 CONC 0 rather than 1735 (see notes for her).  Note also that Waddell might n
2 CONC ot be an independent source for 1735; it says he got his information fr
2 CONC om James B. Dorman, but it is possible that James got his information f
2 CONC rom Parks.  "The Tinkling Spring: Headwater of Freedom" by Howard McKni
2 CONC ght Wilson has a list of the original landowners in the "Beverley Manor P
2 CONC atent" in Appendix A.  Robert Cunningham is listed as having purchased 4
2 CONC 82 acres for £14.10 on 5 Feb 1742.  However, this is not necessarily in
2 CONC consistent with the above statements that he settled there about 1735 (
2 CONC or 1740); indeed we know that Robert was in the area before 1742 becaus
2 CONC e he signed a resolution dated 14 Aug 1741 (see the notes for Walter Da
2 CONC vis).  It says in Chapter 1 of "Tinkling Spring" that many early settle
2 CONC rs were "squatters" with no title to the land, but were able to deal wi
2 CONC th William Beverley because "Beverley needed settlers and the settlers n
2 CONC eeded legal titles to their homesteads, so he and the early settlers tr
2 CONC aded for an average of one English pound for forty acres."  (Beverley n
2 CONC eeded settlers because the government wanted settlers in this wildernes
2 CONC s area to provide a buffer for the already-settled areas against the In
2 CONC dians.)  The first settlements in the area were in about 1730-32, so 17
2 CONC 35 or even 1740 would have been very early on.
2 CONT
2 CONT Augusta County was only organized in 1745, so the deed for Robert’s pur
2 CONC chase would be in Orange County records.  Augusta County was huge at th
2 CONC e time it was organized, in theory stretching from the Blue Ridge all t
2 CONC he way to the Mississippi River.  However, Robert’s land was in the cor
2 CONC e part of the county, a little south and east of Staunton, which is the c
2 CONC ounty seat (though it was not called Staunton until 1761).
2 CONT
2 CONT This area was not safe at the time.  According to “Annals of Augusta Co
2 CONC unty”, the Shenandoah Valley was not inhabited at the time European set
2 CONC tlers started arriving there in the 1720s.  Quoting an earlier author, W
2 CONC addell says “This delightful region of country was then only used as a h
2 CONC unting ground, and as a highway for belligerent parties of different na
2 CONC tions, in their military expeditions against each other.  In consequenc
2 CONC e of the almost continuous hostilities between the northern and souther
2 CONC n Indians, these expeditions were very frequent, and tended somewhat to r
2 CONC etard the settlement of the Valley, and render a residence in it, for s
2 CONC ome time, insecure and unpleasant.”
2 CONT  
2 CONT A major pathway for immigration to the Shenandoah Valley and beyond was c
2 CONC alled the Great Wagon Road or the Great Valley Road.  From “Map Guide t
2 CONC o American Migration Routes, 1735-1815” by William Dollarhide:
2 CONT  
2 CONT Before 1744, the “Great Warrior Path” was an Indian trading path from N
2 CONC ew York to the Carolinas.  This trail marked the western frontier of th
2 CONC e colonies, and no white man ventured across that line without fear of a
2 CONC ttacks from hostile Indians.  In 1744, when a treaty with the Indians g
2 CONC ave whites total control of the area east of the Great Warrior Path in V
2 CONC irginia, the way was clear for the path to evolve into the most heavily t
2 CONC raveled road in colonial America.  By the end of the 1740s, the Great V
2 CONC alley Road was the scene of large migrations into the wilderness of wes
2 CONC tern Virginia, beginning at the Shenandoah Valley.
2 CONT  
2 CONT During the period 1745-1775, thousands of immigrants used this road.  M
2 CONC any of them were Scotch-Irish families who had sailed from Ireland to P
2 CONC hiladelphia or Alexandria….
2 CONT  
2 CONT The general route of the Great Valley Road today is called U.S. Highway 1
2 CONC 1 (or I-81) and is very easy to locate on a modern map….
2 CONT  
2 CONT  
2 CONT As noted above, beginning in the 1730s, the government of the Colony of V
2 CONC irginia encouraged settlement in the area that would become Augusta Cou
2 CONC nty, to form a buffer between the Native Americans (and the French) and t
2 CONC he more settled parts of Virginia east of the Blue Ridge Mountains.  Th
2 CONC e danger increased quite a bit after the French and Indian War started, a
2 CONC nd especially after the defeat of British General Edward Braddock at wh
2 CONC at is now Pittsburgh, PA, on 9 Jul 1755.  Most of the action in the Fre
2 CONC nch and Indian War was farther north, but there were a number of raids a
2 CONC nd even massacres in Augusta County.  “Annals of Augusta County” has a l
2 CONC ong discussion of events in the “Indian Wars” from 1753 to 1764.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many of the early settlers of Augusta County were from Scots-Irish fami
2 CONC lies who had initially settled in Pennsylvania (as noted above), and it i
2 CONC s likely that Robert lived in Pennsylvania between the time he immigrat
2 CONC ed to America and his move down to Virginia.  (The Scots-Irish, or Scot
2 CONC ch-Irish, were people who had settled in Northern Ireland from Scotland a
2 CONC nd northern England, primarily of Presbyterian faith; see https://en.wi
2 CONC kipedia.org/wiki/Scotch-Irish_Americans.)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert sold his land to son-in-law Walter Davis in two transactions:
2 CONT Augusta County Deed Book 5, pp. 448–450 (9 Jun 1753): 322 acres from Ro
2 CONC bert Coningham to Walter Davis for £75.
2 CONT Augusta County Deed Book 9, pp. 331–334 (17 Apr 1761): 160 acres from R
2 CONC obert Cuningham to Walter Davies for 5 shillings.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Death after 1767 comes from the Ancestral File and WFT 5 # 1914.  I do n
2 CONC ot know the basis for this.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT WFT 22 # 1690:
2 CONT His family is descended from the Cunninghams of Scotland.  They were Pr
2 CONC esbyterians.
2 CONT He emigrated to America from Northern Ireland about 1720.
2 CONT He settled at Rock springs in Augusta Co., VA about 1735.
2 CONT When he settled in Augusta Co. it was almost a wilderness.
2 CONT He was a member of the House of Burgesses in the Colony of VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The DAR database on Ancestry.com has entries showing that Robert was bo
2 CONC rn in 1735 in Augusta County, VA, and was a Lieutenant of Artillery in t
2 CONC he Revolutionary War.  However, he is apparently being confused with a d
2 CONC ifferent Robert Cunningham because this doesn’t agree at all with the P
2 CONC arks manuscript; being born in 1735 would be far too young to fit with o
2 CONC ther dates here.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT WFT 5 # 1914 gives his father's name as John, and lists several ancesto
2 CONC rs before that.  I'm not sure how this fits in with the brother John li
2 CONC sted by Waddell, so I'm not including them at this time.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT WFT 137 # 299:
2 CONT
2 CONT Supplement to the Annals of Augusta County, Virginia, pages 442-443
2 CONT Family History of Christian County, Kentucky 1797-1986, page 149
2 CONT
2 CONT Davis Family Record written by Dr. Thomas Davis Parks, Elmood, MO on Fe
2 CONC bruary 2, 1878:
2 CONT Robert Cunningham emigrated from Ireland to America early in the 18th c
2 CONC entury probably about 1720.  From what county I cannot say, but certain
2 CONC ly from the Northern part of the island, as he and all his familiy were P
2 CONC resbyterian and are believed to be descended from the Cunninghams of Sc
2 CONC otland.
2 CONT He settled at Rock Spring in Augusta County, Virginia, about 1735 at wh
2 CONC ich time his daughter, Martha, our grandmother, was nine year old.  He h
2 CONC ad two other daughters and I believe no son.
