1 CHAN
2 DATE 5 JUN 2020
1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT I am not able to give the maiden name of his wife, but previous to her m
2 CONC arriage with Robert Cunningham, she was the widow Hamilton, and the mot
2 CONC her of several children, and if I am not mistaken, one of her daughters m
2 CONC arried a William Campbell and was the mother of Gen. William Campbell o
2 CONC f King’s Mountain fame.  Two of her daughters by Robert Cunningham also m
2 CONC arried Campbells of the same family.  One of these was Arthur Campbell o
2 CONC f Campbell Station East Tennessee, and the father of Gen. John Campbell a s
2 CONC oldier in the War of 1812, and the grandfather of William C. Campbell s
2 CONC ome twenty years ago Governor of Tennessee.  The other daughter of our a
2 CONC ncestor Robert Cunningham also married a Campbell, some of whose Descen
2 CONC dants I knew in Muhlenberg County, Kentucky, worthy and respectable men
2 CONC .  From them and from General John Campbell of Arkansas, I received a l
2 CONC arge portion of my information in regard to the Campbell family.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The fact that Parks remembered or had heard family stories about being r
2 CONC elated to those Campbells surely has some basis, but the specific infor
2 CONC mation he gives does not seem to be correct.  Such references as I coul
2 CONC d find on Gen. William Campbell of King’s Mountain fame give no indicat
2 CONC ion that he was descended from a Hamilton or a Cunningham.  His father w
2 CONC as not William Campbell, but Charles Campbell, and his mother’s maiden n
2 CONC ame was Buchanan.  The other parts of Parks’ story, while not quite fit
2 CONC ting with information I have found, are at least closer.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the section on The Cunninghams, Waddell’s “Annals of Augusta County” g
2 CONC ives the following version:
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife was a widow Hamilton, and the mother of several children at th
2 CONC e time of her second marriage.  One of her daughters, Mary Hamilton, ma
2 CONC rried David Campbell, and was the mother of John and Arthur Campbell, a
2 CONC nd others.  Two of the daughters of Robert Cunningham also married Camp
2 CONC bells.  He had no son.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Then, in Waddell’s section on The Campbells, there is much information a
2 CONC bout descendants of David Campbell and Mary Hamilton.  Arthur Campbell h
2 CONC ad a son, Col. John B. Campbell, who died in the War of 1812.  Margaret
2 CONC , one of the daughters of Mary Hamilton and David Campbell, married ano
2 CONC ther David Campbell, who was one of the pioneers of Campbell’s Station, n
2 CONC ear Knoxville, TN.  One of Margaret’s grandchildren was William B. Camp
2 CONC bell, who was a governor of Tennessee (this isn’t in Waddell, but in Fo
2 CONC ote’s “Sketches of Virginia, Historical and Biographical”, among other p
2 CONC laces).  So, Parks’ account fits, in spirit if not in detail, with Wadd
2 CONC ell’s account of the Cunninghams, Hamiltons, and Campbells.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, there is a well-known genealogy of these Campbells by Margaret C
2 CONC ampbell Pilcher (“Historical Sketches of the Campbell, Pilcher and Kind
2 CONC red Families”), which has a different story.  She says that Mary Hamilt
2 CONC on’s mother was Martha Conyngham, whose second husband was Walter Conyn
2 CONC gham, a cousin (as opposed to Robert Cunningham in the version above).  M
2 CONC artha and Walter Conyngham had a daughter named Jane Conyngham (therefo
2 CONC re, a half-sister to Mary Hamilton), who married yet another David Camp
2 CONC bell, whom Margaret Campbell Pilcher calls “Black David” Campbell.  (Sh
2 CONC e calls Mary Hamilton’s husband “White David” Campbell to differentiate t
2 CONC hem.)  “Black David” and Jane were the parents of David Campbell of Cam
2 CONC pbell’s Station, who married Margaret Campbell and had Gov. William B. C
2 CONC ampbell as a grandson.  (William B. Campbell was Margaret Campbell Pilc
2 CONC her’s father, by the way.)
2 CONT
2 CONT I don’t know what to think of all this, since I don’t know what evidenc
2 CONC e either Waddell or Pilcher had for their conclusions about the parenta
2 CONC ge of Mary Hamilton.  It seems wisest for now not to include the Campbe
2 CONC lls, since there is considerable question about whether or how they are r
2 CONC elated to the Cunninghams and Davises, but on the other hand I’m inclin
2 CONC ed to believe Waddell’s version since Parks was closer to these events t
2 CONC han Pilcher (he was born in 1799, she was born in 1843).  He got the de
2 CONC tails wrong, but it’s hard to believe he would be completely wrong abou
2 CONC t being related to the Campbells.  In addition, the court judgment disc
2 CONC ussed below could be evidence for Waddell's version since it implies so
2 CONC me sort of relationship with David Campbell.  (It is also possible that t
2 CONC here is a more-distant relationship, with Robert Cunningham being relat
2 CONC ed to Walter and Martha Conyngham, but married to someone else.  This w
2 CONC ay, Robert’s daughter Martha Cunningham would not be Mary Hamilton’s ha
2 CONC lf-sister, but they might be cousins of some sort.)
2 CONT
2 CONT My compromise for now is to include Mary Hamilton as a half-sister of M
2 CONC artha Cunningham, with Mary's husband and children, but not put any fur
2 CONC ther effort into that line.  Also, since I don't know of any reliable i
2 CONC nformation on Robert Cunningham's other daughters, I am not including t
2 CONC hem.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ancestral File says that Martha was born in 1713, but this cannot b
2 CONC e true if her daughter Martha was born about 1726.  It would probably n
2 CONC ot work even if daughter Martha was born about 1731 since Robert was he
2 CONC r second husband and she already had several children with her first hu
2 CONC sband.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Some sources, like Parks, just call her widow Hamilton, but some give h
2 CONC er first name as Martha.  There is some confirmation of this in a deed i
2 CONC n Augusta County Deed Book 5, pp. 448–450, from Robert Coningham to Wal
2 CONC ter Davis on 9 Jun 1753, which was signed by Robert Coningham and Marth
2 CONC a Coningham.  There is also an Augusta County court judgment from 1759 (
2 CONC originally discovered through an entry in Chalkley’s Chronicles, Vol. 1 p
2 CONC . 323 for County Court Judgments from May and November 1759), which ref
2 CONC ers to Martha, wife of Robert Cuningham.
2 CONT
2 CONT The papers for this court judgment were obtained from the Augusta Count
2 CONC y Genealogical Society.  They do provide some useful information, but t
2 CONC hey are frustratingly difficult to read and don't give desired details.  A
2 CONC pparently David Campbell sued Robert Cuningham for expenses incurred by D
2 CONC avid for Robert's wife, Martha, "she being turned out of doors by you".  I s
2 CONC uppose that David Campbell was probably the husband of Martha's daughte
2 CONC r Mary Hamilton (see above).  This cries out for an answer to what happ
2 CONC ened between Robert and Martha that she was "turned out", but unfortuna
2 CONC tely the court papers do not say.  There is an accounting page dated Fe
2 CONC bruary 1759 that refers to funeral expenses for Martha.  It would be ni
2 CONC ce if a death date were given, but at least we know that she died in or b
2 CONC efore February 1759.
2 CONT
2 CONT The accounting page refers to:
2 CONT
2 CONT - "3 months Boarding of yr wife Martha"
2 CONT - "5 months attendence when She was lying sick"
2 CONT - "Funeral Expences Trouble & attendence"
2 CONT - "2 Bushels of malt"
2 CONT - "1 Bottle of Abeatment [?] Drops"
2 CONT
2 CONT The listed expenses are totaled to 10 pounds 7 shillings 6 pence, but t
2 CONC he latter two numbers are crossed out, both on the accounting page and o
2 CONC n a discussion page.  It appears that Robert was required to pay ten po
2 CONC unds to David, but the discussion is difficult to read.  The final page o
2 CONC f the papers was signed by John Madison as representative of King Georg
2 CONC e II, on 20 Aug 1759.
