1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT The fourth child of Walter and Martha Davis was a son, James.  He was a g
2 CONC reat favorite with his sisters, and I believe with the whole family.  H
2 CONC e was a noble, generous, and enterprising man, successful in acquiring p
2 CONC roperty, and free to spend it on deserving objects, – particularly gene
2 CONC rous towards his sisters and brothers.  His father at one time gave him o
2 CONC r sold him the Rock Spring Farm, at that time not much improved.  After
2 CONC wards when he saw prospects of success in the west he returned it to hi
2 CONC s father without price, that he might have it for settlement of another o
2 CONC f his family.  He married Deborah Miller, whose father at an early peri
2 CONC od had gone to Kentucky, acquired titles to considerable quantities of l
2 CONC and, and died there, leaving his wife with two children, this daughter a
2 CONC nd son.  These circumstances drew the attention of Uncle James to the t
2 CONC hen far west.  I should have stated that he had served one campaign at l
2 CONC east in the War of the Revolution, and had acquired a taste for adventu
2 CONC re.  Soon after his marriage, he went to Kentucky accompanied by his br
2 CONC other-in-law Donly and their families, also his wife’s brother Abram Mi
2 CONC ller, then a boy.  Whether Brewer Reeves went this time or later, I can
2 CONC not say.  However, James Davis and Charles Donly with their families em
2 CONC igrated at an early period to Lincoln County, Kentucky, in which the la
2 CONC nd located by Mr. Miller lay.  Donly [I think this means James -- mlw]  a
2 CONC s I stated, settled by Mr. Miller, and as I have understood, turned the l
2 CONC arger portion of them over to Abram Miller, and soon afterwards proceed
2 CONC ed farther west to Christian County, where he settled.  I believe he re
2 CONC presented that county in Legislature, and at the time of his death, whi
2 CONC ch occurred not many years subsequent, was surveyor of the county, at t
2 CONC hat time a very profitable office, but his premature death deprived him o
2 CONC f the advantages to be expected from it.  He left a widow and five chil
2 CONC dren, the oldest not much if any beyond ten.  William Cunningham, Eliza
2 CONC beth, Sallie, Martha, and Abram.  Abram died when a youth of eighteen o
2 CONC r twenty.  Martha married a man named Elliot and died soon afterwards, – c
2 CONC hildless.  The other three lived to have families.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT WFT 22 # 1690:
2 CONT He served in the Revolutionary War.  He later moved to Lincoln Co., KY. H
2 CONC e settled in Hanging Fork of Dick River.  He later moved to Christian c
2 CONC ounty.  He became surveryor for the county.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The histories of Christian Co., KY, by Perrin (1884) and Meacham (1930) h
2 CONC ave a bit of a mix-up about James Davis.  They say that James Davis was o
2 CONC ne of the first two settlers of Christian Co., along with John Montgome
2 CONC ry, who was his brother-in-law.  They also say that his is the earliest k
2 CONC nown grave in Christian Co., and give his birth and death dates (from t
2 CONC he gravestone) as 17 Apr 1755 and 29 Mar 1797.  However, apparently the
2 CONC re were two James Davises who were early settlers of Christian Co., KY.  "
2 CONC Family Histories: Christian County, Kentucky, 1797-1986" (by the Christ
2 CONC ian County Genealogical Society) sorts them out -- they differentiate J
2 CONC ames Davis #1, who was the brother-in-law of John Montgomery and maybe o
2 CONC ne of the first settlers in Christian County, and James Davis #2, who w
2 CONC as the son of Walter Davis and Martha Cunningham and husband of Deborah M
2 CONC iller.  The gravestone and birth and death dates above are for James Da
2 CONC vis #2, not #1.  Another interesting fact about James Davis #2 is that h
2 CONC is last will and testament was presented at the second meeting of the C
2 CONC hristian County court (18 Jul 1797), and Brewer Reeves was one of the m
2 CONC en appointed to appraise his estate.  This is mentioned by Perrin and M
2 CONC eacham, though once again they confuse him with James Davis #1.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meacham:  http://www.westernkyhistory.org/christian/meacham/chap3.html
2 CONT Perrin:  http://westernkyhistory.org/christian/perrin/chap2.html
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There are some interesting stories about James Davis in Perrin's and Me
2 CONC acham's books, but I don't include any of them because who knows which J
2 CONC ames Davis they apply to (or if they even apply to either of them)?  In p
2 CONC articular, there is a story about him being killed by Indians, but I am s
2 CONC keptical that it applies to "our" James Davis because you would think t
2 CONC hat Parks would have mentioned something like that.  The following exce
2 CONC rpt from Meacham, however, gives information about where James Davis se
2 CONC ttled, which should be correct because of the other names mentioned:
2 CONT
2 CONT One of his daughters, Sallie Davis, married Robert Harrison and Naomi H
2 CONC arrison, her daughter, in turn became the wife of William H. Fortson.  T
2 CONC he Fortsons inherited the part of the original grant upon which Davis l
2 CONC ived and it is now known as the Fortson place.  It is about one mile fr
2 CONC om the Dixie Bee Line, about half way between Pembroke and Trenton, in t
2 CONC he edge of Christian County, near the Todd County line.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The will of James Davis, from Christian County Will Book A, p. 1 (obtai
2 CONC ned 30 May 2020 from Diane's Genealogy web site; there is a note that i
2 CONC t was transcribed by Laura Willis, that it was the first will in the bo
2 CONC ok, and that it was a single paragraph but has been broken up for reada
2 CONC bility):
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God, Amen.  I, James Davis, being weak in body but of so
2 CONC und mind and memory, do make and ordain this my last will and testament
2 CONC .  As for the worldly estate with which God has blessed me, I bequeath i
2 CONC n the manner following.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is my will that all my just debts be first paid.
2 CONT
2 CONT I give and bequeath to my beloved wife Debora Davis, the whole tract of l
2 CONC and on which I live, when at home, on the west fork of Red River, to my s
2 CONC on, William C. Davis, arrives to the age of twenty-one years, and after t
2 CONC hat time.  I leave her, my said beloved wife, the one third of said tra
2 CONC ct of land, enduring her life laid of any part she chooses.  I also lea
2 CONC ve her, my said wife, my negroes, Chloe and David, forever, to dispose o
2 CONC f as she thinks fit.  I also leave her all my other negroes, to my son A
2 CONC braham Miller Davis, arrives to the age of twnty-one.
2 CONT
2 CONT And for the purpose of raising and scooling my children, I leave her th
2 CONC e whole remainder of my moveable estate after my debts is paid, but sho
2 CONC uld it be more convenient fo her to sell a negro or negroes to pay my d
2 CONC ebts, it is my will she shall sell any of them she chooses for the purp
2 CONC ose.  It is my will for her to keep the property or sell it at her plea
2 CONC sure without being accountable to any person for any of it.
2 CONT
2 CONT I further will to my two sons, William C. Davis and Abraham Miller Davi
2 CONC s, the above mentioned tract of land, to be equally divided between the
2 CONC m, quantity and quality, each to bear their proportion of their mother'
2 CONC s third, to them, their heirs and assigns.  It is my will that in conse
2 CONC quence of getting the land, my two sons shall each of them pay to each o
2 CONC f their sisters, viz, Betsy, Salley, and Patty, forty pounds before the
2 CONC y arrive at the age of twenty-four.
2 CONT
2 CONT I will to my said three daughters, Betsy, Salley and Patty, my negroes t
2 CONC hat is not otherwise disposed of, to be equally divided between them, a
2 CONC s soon as my son Abraham Miller Davis arrives to the age of twenty-one y
2 CONC ears of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT I do hereby appoint my beloved wife Debora Davis, the sole Executrix of t
2 CONC his my last will and testament, and I do hereby impower her to made a d
2 CONC eed of conveyance to Brewer Reeves for three hundred acres of land whic
2 CONC h I sold him on which he now lives.
2 CONT
2 CONT I do hereby revoke any former will by me heretofore made, and declares t
2 CONC his my last will and testament.  In witness whereof, I have hereunto se
2 CONC t my hand and seal this 9th day of February 1797.
2 CONT
2 CONT Test:
2 CONT Thomas Murry
2 CONT Abraham Miller
2 CONT
2 CONT                                                 J. Davis
2 CONT
2 CONT Produced in open court and duly recorded this Jul term of 1797.
2 CONT John Clark [County Clerk]
