1 _UID 1F85BFFEDF397C4A84887196D97622D497B6
1 CHAN
2 DATE 31 MAY 2020
1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT The fourth daughter and fifth child of our grandparents was Sallie, bor
2 CONC n about the year of 1760.  She was somewhat distinguished in the family
2 CONC , and I believe, in the circle in which she moved, for intellectuality a
2 CONC nd intelligence.  I presume that she was gifted with conversational pow
2 CONC ers.  I have heard Alexander Herring, the husband of my Cousin Margaret S
2 CONC mith, say that he considered Aunt Reeves her equal in good sense, but n
2 CONC ot able to express her ideas so clearly.  This I consider high praise f
2 CONC or I was permitted to enjoy the society of Aunt Reeves in her old age, a
2 CONC nd even then I esteemed her a fine talker, and so she was esteemed by i
2 CONC ntellectual and literary persons who knew her.  Aunt Sallie certainly e
2 CONC njoyed opportunities of mental culture which were not attainable, at le
2 CONC ast to the same extent by any other members of the family.  In the year o
2 CONC f 1776, that remarkable man and devoted minister of God, James Waddell, c
2 CONC ame to Augusta County, Virginia, and was settled as pastor over Tinklin
2 CONC g Spring Church.  Of this church our grandfather [Walter Davis] was a r
2 CONC uling elder.  Mr. Waddell lived within about three miles of him.  Of co
2 CONC urse intimate intercourse immediately sprang up between the families.  A
2 CONC t this time my Aunts Smith, Reeves, and Donly were all married.  My Unc
2 CONC le James was probably absent in the army, but Aunt Sallie was just budd
2 CONC ing into early womanhood with the warm affections and sprightly intelle
2 CONC ct.  It is easy to imagine the influence of his rare eloquence and of t
2 CONC he society of a refined intelligent family on a girl of that age and ch
2 CONC aracter.  Her life and character evince that she improved those rare ad
2 CONC vantages.  She acquired a reputation for general intelligence and conve
2 CONC rsational powers far above the common standard of the ladies of that ti
2 CONC me.  She married John Ewin of Rockingham County.  They removed to Kentu
2 CONC cky some years afterwards, probably intending to settle in Christian Co
2 CONC unty where James Davis and Brewer Reeves had previously gone.  But Mr. E
2 CONC win sickened and died in Hardin County, leaving his wife and three chil
2 CONC dren; Henry Clayton, Watts Davis, and Jeanetta.  I am not able to say m
2 CONC uch about Uncle Ewin.  He was of respectable family, and was I think em
2 CONC ployed in the clerk’s office in Rockingham County.  Aunt Ewin after the d
2 CONC eath of her husband managed (I suppose by the assistance of her brother
2 CONC ) to reach the neighborhood of her brother and sister, and secure a hom
2 CONC e for herself and children.  First that brother, so dear to them all, f
2 CONC ell a victim, then the remaining brother-in-law, and the two sisters an
2 CONC d sister-in-law were left with their children, the oldest scarcely more t
2 CONC han twelve years, in that rude uncultivated country.  But although the s
2 CONC cattered inhabitants were rough in manner, many of them had large heart
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT I will put the name of one in this brief sketch, Edward Davis, the fath
2 CONC er of the late Col. Joe Davis of Howard County, who proved a true and u
2 CONC nfailing friend to those bereaved and lonely widows.  Aunt Ewin continu
2 CONC ed to live on her farm with her three children until the year of 1806, w
2 CONC hen she was taken from them.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ancestral file listed two daughters, one named Sarah and one named "
2 CONC Sally or Sarah".  It looks to me like these are the same person.  The C
2 CONC ormode info listed her as Sarah (Sally) Davis?  Also Sarah (Sallie) Dav
2 CONC is.  Most sources call her Sallie.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that her mother’s will, dated 26 Dec 1805, refers to “the Heirs of m
2 CONC y Daughter Sally Ewin”, so Sallie died enough before then for word of h
2 CONC er death to have reached Virginia.  By odd coincidence, Diane's Genealo
2 CONC gy web site has the following item from Christian County Court Order Bo
2 CONC ok A, p. 195, 14 Feb 1804 (thanks to Daniel Cormode for the pointer):
2 CONT
2 CONT The Court doth appoint Edward Shanklin guardian of Watts Davis Ewin and J
2 CONC ennetta Ewin who entered into bond with Peter Ferguson and Sam'l Davis h
2 CONC is securities in the penalty of 4000 dollars conditioned as the law Dir
2 CONC ects.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT This makes it sound like Sallie may have already died by February 1804, b
2 CONC ut that doesn't seem to fit with other evidence.  If she died that earl
2 CONC y, why was Jennetta not sent to Virginia until 1806?  Is this possibly j
2 CONC ust an indication that Sallie was already very sick by 1804?  Unless th
2 CONC ere was another Peter Ferguson in the area, he was the second husband o
2 CONC f James Davis’s wife, Deborah Miller Davis Ferguson.  No relationship i
2 CONC s known to Samuel Davis.
