2 DATE 16 AUG 2020
1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT The second child of Walter and Martha Davis was Martha, born about 1754
2 CONC .  At about seventeen years old, she married William Long, who in littl
2 CONC e more than a year after his marriage died, leaving her a very young wi
2 CONC dow with one child, a boy, William Long, who died at about twenty years o
2 CONC f age.  Some years after the death of William Long, his widow was marri
2 CONC ed to Daniel Smith, brother of John Smith, her sister’s husband.  With h
2 CONC im she lived a number of years, and was the mother of three children, w
2 CONC ho all, with their father, Uncle John, and a large portion of the Smith f
2 CONC amily, died of an epidemic fever then prevailing, and Martha was a seco
2 CONC nd time left a widow.  In the year 1782 she was married to Brewer Reeve
2 CONC s.  The children of this marriage were six, – Richard, who died in infa
2 CONC ncy, Margaret who died at fifteen or sixteen, Benjamin Harrison, Archib
2 CONC ald Stuart, Otway Curry, and Willis Long, and all but Archibald married a
2 CONC nd raised families.  B. H. Reeves was well known both in Kentucky and M
2 CONC issouri.  At the death of her husband, about 1798, Martha Reeves was le
2 CONC ft a widow in a wild country, for they had a few years before emigrated t
2 CONC o Christian County in Kentucky, at that time almost a wilderness, with h
2 CONC ere and there an inhabitant.  Often they were for weeks if not months w
2 CONC ithout bread.  I have heard her say sometimes they had to send to Nashv
2 CONC ille, sixty miles off, for meal; flour was a luxury rarely enjoyed in t
2 CONC hose days.  I have heard her say it was not uncommon to use venison for b
2 CONC read and the flesh of the bear for meat.  Brewer Reeves did not leave h
2 CONC is wife destitute of means.  She had lands, some slaves, and home comfo
2 CONC rts, such as a wild country could afford, but an unfortunate security f
2 CONC or a friend who proved recreant, left her at the mercy of a relentless a
2 CONC nd cruel creditor, who took from her all the slaves and personal proper
2 CONC ty, leaving her destitute.  The land could not be sold for debt at that t
2 CONC ime.  But she did not sink under the strike; her energies were aroused, a
2 CONC nd with what help she could obtain from her rude but kind neighbors, sh
2 CONC e managed to raise and educate so far as that rude country would allow, h
2 CONC er four boys. – her daughter died not long after her husband.  I may he
2 CONC re remark that but a short time previous to the death of Mr. Reeves, he
2 CONC r brother James Davis (her only brother in Kentucky) had died; and that b
2 CONC eing doubly, trebly bereaved, an ordinary woman would have sunk to the l
2 CONC evel of all around her.  Not so with Martha Reeves, although educated s
2 CONC o far as she could be said to be educated in Augusta County, Virginia, w
2 CONC hen it was a frontier, almost a wilderness.  She had studied in the bes
2 CONC t of schools, – from the age of thirteen she had read her Bible as regu
2 CONC larly as the sun set, and was then taught to improve the many severe le
2 CONC ssons of afflictions which she received through her long life.  Had I t
2 CONC he pen of a ready writer, I would give a page to the character of my de
2 CONC ar Aunt Reeves; as it is I can only give a scant summary of her eventfu
2 CONC l life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Augusta County when a frontier, subject to the (1754) invasion o
2 CONC f savages, destitute of schools except of the most ordinary classes, a w
2 CONC idow with an infant boy after a little more than a year of married life
2 CONC , a few years later married again and again a widow, having buried her s
2 CONC econd husband and three children, married again – removed to the southe
2 CONC rn wilderness of Kentucky, where her third husband, her favorite brothe
2 CONC r, (I say, favorite, because he was the favorite of the whole family) a
2 CONC nd her only daughter, just budding into womanhood, were in a few brief m
2 CONC onths buried, – and she bereft of the property which would at least hav
2 CONC e secured personal comfort for her family, by the ruthless creditor of a m
2 CONC an for whom her husband stood surety.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her oldest living (for her oldest, William Long, a favorite in his gran
2 CONC dfather’s family, and of all who knew him, had died years before at abo
2 CONC ut the age of twenty) son was not more than ten or twelve years old, an
2 CONC d the oldest son of her brother was still younger, her sister-in-law no
2 CONC t able to give her any assistance or even judicious advice, yet under a
2 CONC ll these difficulties, afflictions, and privations her courage and trus
2 CONC t in God never forsook her.  She managed to raise her family in a respe
2 CONC ctable manner and to have them at least as well educated as any of thos
2 CONC e around them; so that when they grew to manhood they took their stand o
2 CONC n a level with the most cultivated of their company.  When Aunt Reeves w
2 CONC as about sixty-three years old, I sixteen, I was privileged to make her a
2 CONC cquaintance.  I had been in the society of ladies before and often sinc
2 CONC e; and now I can look back on the varied scenes that have passed before m
2 CONC y vision, and contemplate the characters of the many excellent and refi
2 CONC ned ladies whom I have known, I can truly say that she who had passed h
2 CONC er life on the frontier and in the wilderness, was in all that constitu
2 CONC tes the true woman and the refined lady, the peer of the best.  The pri
2 CONC vilege of intercourse with that dear old lady continued at that time bu
2 CONC t two years, for in 1818, before the first land sales in Boon’s Lick co
2 CONC untry, she and her son Benjamin removed to Howard County Missouri, agai
2 CONC n on the frontier.  Some nine years afterwards, I came to Missouri, and h
2 CONC ad the privilege of spending much time in her company, and continued to v
2 CONC isit her, I believe every year till the close of her life, at the ripe a
2 CONC ge of eighty-six, and still found her the same cheerful, trusting, lovi
2 CONC ng, intelligent Christian woman, respected and beloved by all who knew h
2 CONC er.  One fact more I will state, Clergymen of different denominations, o
2 CONC f fine mental culture who visited her, admired her for her general inte
2 CONC lligence, and were even astonished at her for the amount of her scriptu
2 CONC ral knowledge.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Martha Davis was in two places in the Ancestral File.  As wife of Brewe
2 CONC r Reeves, her birthdate was listed as about 1765 and her death date was l
2 CONC isted as about 1840.  As daughter of Walter and Martha Davis, her birth
2 CONC date was listed as about 1754 and her death date was listed as 1840.  T
2 CONC he actual birth date is from Bob Reeves.  The Ancestral File charts had t
2 CONC he parents of Martha Davis (wife of Brewer Reeves) as Thomas Reeves and S
2 CONC arah, so it was really messed up somehow.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT WFT 22 #1690:
2 CONT
2 CONT Her first child William Long died at 20 years of age.  After marrying D
2 CONC aniel Smith she had three children.  They all died, along with their fa
2 CONC ther, their Uncle John Smith and a large portion of the Smith family of a
2 CONC n epidemic.  Once when asked how she endured all her sorrows she replie
2 CONC d "It Sems to me that my first sorrow made a hole through my heart and a
2 CONC ll that came after went through that hole".  She moved with Brewer Reev
2 CONC es to the wilderness in KY.  They often went for weeks and months witho
2 CONC ut bread.  They sometimes had to send to Nashville, 60 miles away, for m
2 CONC eal.  They did have plenty of venison and bear meat. When Brewer died s
2 CONC he was left in this wilderness to raise her children.  He left her land a
2 CONC nd slaves.  A cruel creditor took many of her slaves and personal prope
2 CONC rty.  The creditor was a brother of Mr. Reeves.  They were not able to t
2 CONC ake her land.  With assistance of her neighbors she managed to raise an
2 CONC d educate her four remaining sons. [H]er daughter died after Mr. Reeves a
2 CONC nd her brother, James Davis, also died in KY.
2 CONT
2 CONT She had a good education and had attended the best schools in VA. From t
2 CONC he time she was 13 she read her Bible daily.  Her courage and trust in G
2 CONC od never failed.  In 1818 she moved with her son, Benjamin Harrison Ree
2 CONC ves to Howard Co., MO again on the frontier.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Bob Reeves, she is buried at Oak Hill, Christian, KY? or G
2 CONC reene site MO.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT WFT 137 # 299:
2 CONT Davis Family Records
2 CONT Family History Christian County, Kentucky, 1797-1986, page 149
