1 NOTE "History of Todd County, Kentucky", ed. J. H. Battle, F. A. Battey Publ
2 CONC ishing Co., 1884, pp. 328-30:
2 CONT
2 CONT HON. CRITTENDEN REEVES was born in this county December 28, 1842. In 18
2 CONC 49, he moved with his brothers to Missouri; remained there until 1856, a
2 CONC nd then returned to Todd County.  Of his father, an obituary notice say
2 CONC s: "Benjamin H. Reeves, the eldest son of Brewer and Martha Reeves, was b
2 CONC orn in Augusta County, Va., March 21, 1787, and moved with his parents t
2 CONC o Christian County, Ky., about the commencement of the present century, a
2 CONC nd settled on the West Fork of Red River. Shortly after their arrival i
2 CONC n Kentucky his father died, leaving his widow and infant children in co
2 CONC mparatively a wilderness, surrounded by the red men of the forest.  His m
2 CONC other, a lady of uncommon energy, firmness, and fine powers of mind, ri
2 CONC chly stored with the truths of the Gospel, in the absence of anything l
2 CONC ike good schools, laid the foundation for his future elevation in life b
2 CONC y her industry, and, with his assistance, they managed to support the y
2 CONC ounger members of the family.  To them he was both a parent and an elde
2 CONC r brother; his heart seemed to be entwined around them during life.  By h
2 CONC is own generous worth and energy of character he soon acquired the este
2 CONC em and confidence of his countrymen.  On the declaration of war by the U
2 CONC nited States against Great Britain in 1812, he took up arms in defense o
2 CONC f his country's rights, and was, on the fourth day of July in that year
2 CONC , elected Captain of the first company of volunteers from Christian Cou
2 CONC nty, and in a short time joined the army in the then Territory, now Sta
2 CONC te, of Indiana, stationed at Vincennes; was shortly after promoted to t
2 CONC he rank of Major; commanded an escort to the relief of Zachary Taylor, t
2 CONC he present President of the United States.  In November, 1812, he retur
2 CONC ned to Kentucky, having been, at the August election previous, elected a m
2 CONC ember of the Legislature of Kentucky from Christian, and took his seat a
2 CONC s a member of that body on the first Monday in December thereafter, and c
2 CONC ontinued a member of that body, with the exception of one or two years, u
2 CONC ntil 1818, when he moved to the then Territory of Missouri.  In 1821 he w
2 CONC as elected a Delegate from the county of Howard to assist in framing a C
2 CONC onstitution for that State, and was, a few years thereafter, elected Li
2 CONC eutenant-Governor of Missouri. In 1826 he was appointed by the Governme
2 CONC nt of the United States a Commissioner to survey and mark out a road fr
2 CONC om Missouri to the Spanish provinces in a direction to Santa Fe.  In th
2 CONC e recent Indian wars on the frontiers of Missouri, he again took up arm
2 CONC s in defense of his country.  In 1836 he returned from Missouri, and se
2 CONC ttled in Todd County, Ky.  The partiality of his countrymen soon called h
2 CONC im to represent them in Legislature in several successive years.  He fi
2 CONC lled many civil and military offices in Missouri and Kentucky. In priva
2 CONC te life his virtues shone most conspicuous--a dutiful child, a kind hus
2 CONC band, a fond parent, a devoted friend. Warm-hearted, generous, and devo
2 CONC ted in his sentiments, he had many personal and devoted friends.  About t
2 CONC he first of January last his health began to decline rapidly, and on Mo
2 CONC nday, the 16th day of April, 1849, at his residence in Todd County, hav
2 CONC ing, as his friends fondly hope, made his peace with God, with a smile o
2 CONC n his countenance, and without a struggle or a groan, fell asleep in Je
2 CONC sus, universally lamented by his family, relations and friends".  Brewe
2 CONC r's sons were: Benjamin H., Willis L., Ottaway and Archibald.  Subject'
2 CONC s mother was Virginia T. (Garth) Reeves, who died in 1850 [actually 184
2 CONC 9].
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. Benjamin's children are: William L., Jennette (Leonard), Mary (Wil
2 CONC son), Benjamin, Missouri (Ainslie), Eugenia (Griffin) and our subject, w
2 CONC ho procured a good education at the common schools in the county and at t
2 CONC he Central College of Missouri.  At the commencement of the late war, h
2 CONC e enlisted in Company I, First Kentucky Cavalry, in which he remained t
2 CONC wo years.  December 16, 1863, he was married to Miss Louisa V., daughte
2 CONC r of Squire Roscoe C. and Emily (Harris) Dickinson, of Todd County, who d
2 CONC ied June 16, 1874.  To them were born: Elijah S., Eugenia A., Benjamin H
2 CONC . and John C.  Subject was next married October 14, 1875, to Miss Marth
2 CONC a, daughter of John W. and Martha D. (Barclay) McElwain, of Todd County
2 CONC , Ky., and this union has been blessed with: Robert D., Luke P. B., Mar
2 CONC tha V. and Belle C.  In 1879, Mr. Reeves was chosen a member of the Leg
2 CONC islature of his State; and his public career, while in the discharge of h
2 CONC is duties, was endorsed by his constituents in his return to the same h
2 CONC onorable position in 1881.  He is a prominent and high-toned gentleman, a
2 CONC nd certainly has a bright future before him. He is by profession a farm
2 CONC er, owning 187 acres of valuable and productive land, which he is succe
2 CONC ssfully engaged in cultivating, in the profitable staples of this count
2 CONC ry.  He is a Granger, and also an active member of the Democratic party
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT [The biography says that he moved to MO with his brothers in 1849 and r
2 CONC eturned to Todd Co. in 1856.  The first year is apparently incorrect, a
2 CONC s he is listed as being in Todd Co. in the 1850 census.]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Dickinson descendants list, he moved from his farm to E
2 CONC lkton, KY, after being elected County Court Clerk in the early 1890s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bob Reeves says that he was probably named for Sen. John Crittenden of K
2 CONC Y, or one of his two sons, who were both generals.
