1 _UID D82F9F12D27B0C45B62B130034CBEAC3F6FA
1 CHAN
2 DATE 11 APR 2019
1 NOTE Biographical sketch from the introduction to Abiel Leonard Papers (C101
2 CONC 3) at the State Historical Society of Missouri Manuscript Collection (h
2 CONC ttp://shsmo.org/manuscripts/columbia/c1013):
2 CONT
2 CONT Abiel Leonard was born May 16, 1797, in Windsor, Vermont, to Nathaniel a
2 CONC nd Mary Leverett Leonard.  His family moved to Lewiston, New York, shor
2 CONC tly afterward, where he spent his boyhood.  He began his studies at Dar
2 CONC tmouth College, Hanover, New Hampshire, in 1813, but left in 1815, poss
2 CONC ibly because of a scandal involving his father, a military officer in c
2 CONC ommand of Fort Niagara, which was captured by British forces in the War o
2 CONC f 1812.  Abiel would always regret not having more education and kept a
2 CONC n extensive personal library all his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Leonard made his way to Missouri by 1819, and began to practice law in F
2 CONC ranklin and Boonville by 1820, slowly building his practice and his rep
2 CONC utation over the next decade.  His reputation seems to have been impecc
2 CONC able except for one incident.  In 1824 Leonard fought a duel, which was i
2 CONC llegal in the new state of Missouri, and killed his opponent Taylor Ber
2 CONC ry.  For this he received a fine of $150, was disbarred and disfranchis
2 CONC ed.  A few months later, however, he was fully reinstated, though the f
2 CONC ine was still paid.
2 CONT
2 CONT He remained in touch with his family back east.  Because they were havi
2 CONC ng a difficult time financially, Abiel's growing success encouraged his y
2 CONC ounger brother, Nathaniel Leonard, Jr., to follow him west.  After a fe
2 CONC w stops along the way, Nat settled in Cooper County, Missouri, where he e
2 CONC stablished a small plantation, Ravenswood, and lived his entire life.  O
2 CONC lder sister Margaret, and her husband Jonathan Bailey, also migrated to M
2 CONC issouri, settling in St. Louis after some earlier business failures in m
2 CONC id-Missouri.  Although brother Benjamin never moved farther west than O
2 CONC hio, he purchased large amounts of land in Missouri for speculation.  S
2 CONC ister Mary Bell who died in childbirth in 1836, never moved to Missouri
2 CONC , though Abiel kept in close contact with her children throughout their l
2 CONC ives.  In 1840, several months after his mother's death, Abiel brought h
2 CONC is father to Missouri to live with him until his death in 1844.  The su
2 CONC pport and education of his entire family, including nieces, nephews, an
2 CONC d in-laws, would remain a primary focus of Abiel Leonard's entire life.
2 CONT
2 CONT On October 31, 1830, Leonard married Jeanette Reeves, daughter of Benja
2 CONC min H. Reeves, who was one of three commissioners appointed by Presiden
2 CONC t Andrew Jackson to survey the Santa Fe Trail.  Oakwood, the home the L
2 CONC eonards built in Fayette in 1833, was one of the loveliest in the mid-M
2 CONC issouri area and they entertained extended family and friends there on a r
2 CONC egular basis.  Abiel and Jeanette had seven children.  Mary, born in 18
2 CONC 33, married distant cousin, Horace Everett, in 1851; Martha, born in 18
2 CONC 36, married Dr. Joseph Smith in 1854; Reeves, born in 1838, was educate
2 CONC d at Dartmouth and in Germany and married Alice Gardenhire in 1868; Ada
2 CONC , born in 1841, married Episcopal Bishop Cicero Hawks in 1864; Kate, bo
2 CONC rn in 1844, married General Odon Guitar in 1865; Abiel, Jr., born in 18
2 CONC 48, became a minister and married Flora; and Nathaniel William (Willie)
2 CONC , born in 1851, completed the family.
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1831 Leonard's legal skills and personal integrity were bringing him c
2 CONC onsiderable prestige within the mid-Missouri legal, business, social, a
2 CONC nd political communities.  Several prominent business and political fig
2 CONC ures urged him to run for political office in that year and several oth
2 CONC er times throughout his career.  Although Leonard did attempt a Senate b
2 CONC id in 1838, most of the time he chose to support his chosen party, the W
2 CONC higs, through speeches and behind-the-scenes help.  He decided that his f
2 CONC amily obligations and his legal career, which involved a great deal of t
2 CONC raveling from court to court around the state, took up too much of his t
2 CONC ime to allow for political office.
2 CONT
2 CONT In late 1854, however, the opportunity to serve as a judge in the Misso
2 CONC uri Supreme Court proved to be too intriguing to pass up.  The position a
2 CONC gain required much time away from home, usually in Jefferson City or St
2 CONC . Louis.  Within only a few months of accepting the office, Leonard wro
2 CONC te, "I doubt whether the honor of the office will compensate for the sa
2 CONC crifice on the part of both myself and my family," and a short time lat
2 CONC er, ". . .as soon as my present term of service is over, I will accept n
2 CONC o office -- nor engage in any business that shall hereafter separate me f
2 CONC rom my family."  In addition to the loneliness and the health problems t
2 CONC hat were aggravated by his travels, the "sacrifice" he mentioned also r
2 CONC eferred to the fact that accepting the judgeship meant a loss of revenu
2 CONC e from the considerable fees he could receive for his well-respected le
2 CONC gal opinions.
2 CONT
2 CONT Throughout the 1840s and early 1850s, Abiel Leonard, like many other me
2 CONC n of means of the period, engaged in land speculation.  They assumed th
2 CONC at the west, as Missouri and Iowa were considered in the mid-nineteenth c
2 CONC entury, had plenty of land that could be bought up and later sold to im
2 CONC migrants at substantial profit.  Leonard and many of his family and fri
2 CONC ends bought heavily and, unable to sell as profitably as they wished, f
2 CONC ound that the taxes on the land became quite a burden.  Leonard, in dis
2 CONC cussing his financial difficulties after leaving the Supreme Court in 1
2 CONC 857, stated that, "all my means are locked up in unproductive property.
2 CONC "
2 CONT
2 CONT One reason that Leonard required more money than he could make as a jud
2 CONC ge was that he placed great importance on the education of his children
2 CONC .  When he stepped down from the court, he had the considerable expense o
2 CONC f educating daughter Ada at St. Mary's Hall in New Jersey, and Reeves a
2 CONC t Dartmouth College in New Hampshire.  Reeves was expected to continue h
2 CONC is education for two years in Germany, while Kate, Abiel, Jr., and Will
2 CONC ie were still to be educated at their various schools.  In addition, th
2 CONC e family home had to be maintained, including the maintenance of severa
2 CONC l slaves.
2 CONT
2 CONT At 61 years old, in failing health, and in spite of his promise not to b
2 CONC e separated from his family again, Leonard had little choice but to ret
2 CONC urn to the profession of law to earn the money needed to support his fa
2 CONC mily in the manner to which they were accustomed.  He wrote, "I am prov
2 CONC oked with myself -- that I have so conducted my affairs as to place thi
2 CONC s necessity upon me."  His reputation was such that he could command ra
2 CONC ther large fees practicing as a counselor, "leaving the attorney's busi
2 CONC ness to younger members of the profession."  He decided that he could e
2 CONC arn more money in St. Louis, so he spent most of his time there for the n
2 CONC ext couple of years, until his health precluded his working any longer.
2 CONT
2 CONT In spite of the fact that they had owned slaves most of the time they l
2 CONC ived in Missouri, the Leonards were strong supporters of the Union in t
2 CONC he Civil War, with Reeves serving as an officer in the Missouri Militia u
2 CONC pon his return from Germany in 1861.  Leonard continued to correspond w
2 CONC ith professional acquaintances, family, and friends, maintaining his pr
2 CONC ominence in the community and in the state until his death on March 28, 1
2 CONC 863.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his father's death Reeves Leonard took responsibility as head of t
2 CONC he family until his own early demise in 1878.  The estate was in deep d
2 CONC ebt, partly due to Leonard's penchant for land speculation, but also be
2 CONC cause sons-in-law Horace Everett and Joseph Smith owed huge amounts to t
2 CONC he estate.  Much of Reeves' time was spent selling off the land that hi
2 CONC s father had accumulated in order to support his mother and provide for t
2 CONC he education of his younger brothers.  Upon Reeves' death, Willie heade
2 CONC d the family, taking care of his father's estate and eventually marryin
2 CONC g Reeves' widow.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Hammond Darby's obituary listed a middle initial of "J", but I h
2 CONC ave not seen that anyplace else (including the book-length biography by B
2 CONC oman), so I'm not including it.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Web site of the Missouri Secretary of State (http://www.sos.state.m
2 CONC o.us/archives/history/historicallistings/molegw.asp) shows Abiel Leonar
2 CONC d elected to the state house of representatives in 1834.
2 CONT
2 CONT A detailed biography is available:
2 CONT Dennis K. Boman, "Abiel Leonard, Yankee Slaveholder, Eminent Jurist, an
2 CONC d Passionate Unionist" (Edwin Mellen Press, Lewiston, NY, 2002).
