1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT William C. Davis was married when very young to Sallie Van Lear, sister t
2 CONC o Walter’s wife.  They were married before Walter.  William was an ente
2 CONC rprising business man; and having an excellent partner in his wife for a c
2 CONC onsiderable number of years he prospered greatly in his native state; b
2 CONC ut meeting with serious reverses about the year of 1836 or ’37, he reso
2 CONC lved to try the far west, and in ’38 he came to Missouri with very limi
2 CONC ted means and a large family.  A few years afterwards he settled in Sal
2 CONC ine County, where by skillful industry and skillful farming, he acquire
2 CONC d a handsome competency.  And when called away in April 1857, at the ag
2 CONC e of sixty-three, he was free from debt and left considerable property t
2 CONC o his children.  His wife survived him some eighteen or twenty years.  T
2 CONC heir family consisted of seven sons, James Robinson, Jacob Van Lear, Wi
2 CONC lliam, Daniel Smith, Andrew Alexander Arbuckle, Burrus Kincaid, and Joh
2 CONC n Boyden, and two daughters, Martha Jane and Margaret Annie.  These all s
2 CONC urvived him except the last named, who died unmarried at about the age o
2 CONC f twenty years.  Of the survivors and their families, as they are as we
2 CONC ll known to their relations generally as to me, I shall say nothing.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "History of Saline County, Missouri", Missouri Historical Co., St. Loui
2 CONC s, 1881, p. 854:
2 CONT
2 CONT From biography of John B. Davis:  Col. Wm. C. Davis, was born in August
2 CONC a county, Virginia, in 1794, and lived there until 1838.  In the war of 1
2 CONC 812 he commanded the 70th Virginia regiment.  He was the father of nine c
2 CONC hildren, most of whom are now living in Missouri.  [Later on, it also s
2 CONC ays that he was a member of the county court of Saline county.]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Waddell says that he moved to Missouri in 1836 or 1837.
