1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT Martha Ann Davis, about twenty-one, was married to Dr. Andrew Alexander A
2 CONC rbuckle, a young physician of great promise, but was left a widow withi
2 CONC n a few years.  She afterwards married Robert Steele, a merchant in the v
2 CONC illage of Greenville, Augusta County, who after years of prosperity fai
2 CONC led and came to Missouri.  He was many years older than she, and when h
2 CONC e came to Missouri too far advanced in life to recuperate his fortunes, a
2 CONC lthough a man of untiring energy and industry.  He settled in Johnson C
2 CONC ounty, in the fall of 1837; rented for a year or two, then entered land a
2 CONC nd improved a farm for himself.  After coming to Missouri and engaging i
2 CONC n more laborious business than he had been accustomed to, Mr. Steele wa
2 CONC s subject to frequent attacks of intermitting fever, and his family gro
2 CONC wing, he felt the necessity of better schools than Johnson County affor
2 CONC ded, as well as a business better adapted to his advanced age than farm
2 CONC ing.  Accordingly, he sold out in Johnson and removed to Lexington, whe
2 CONC re he died in 1844.  Martha Ann Steele was then left a widow with four c
2 CONC hildren, she had lost one in Johnson County, and I believe one in Virgi
2 CONC nia.  Her surviving children were Annie, William T., Martha A., and Ros
2 CONC a.  She remained in Lexington until the education of her children was w
2 CONC ell advanced.  She then removed to Warrensburg, William engaging in the p
2 CONC ractice of law.  After the war, she removed to Otterville, Cooper Count
2 CONC y, subsequently to Texas, and died in or near Fort Worth, where I belie
2 CONC ve her three surviving children still live.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Martha Ann Davis is listed twice in the Ancestral File, for some reason
