1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT James the oldest son married Mary B. Harland July 24, 1821, in Christia
2 CONC n County, Kentucky, where he resided till the fall of 1833 or ’34 when h
2 CONC e removed to Macoupin County, Illinois, where he died July 13, 1835, le
2 CONC aving a widow and seven children, the oldest but thirteen years of age, S
2 CONC arah Elizabeth, still unmarried, Ann Rebecca, who married Wm. H. Edward
2 CONC s; John Vance, who died aged about twenty-one months; Augusta, married t
2 CONC o Joseph Trabue, Mary Jane, who married Robert D. Quigley; Thomas Walte
2 CONC r, who died aged between twenty-one and twenty-two, and Letitia James, w
2 CONC ho married E. G. W. Quigley, Mrs. Anne Edwards children are James Wm. E
2 CONC dwards, Marly L. married Gurton, Bessie J. Married Randolph, Susan Eudo
2 CONC ra, Kate Augusta, and Charles Ernest.  Mrs. M. L. Trabue’s children are M
2 CONC ary Lizzie, Bennie, James, Annie Letitia, Edward Martha, Agnes and Jose
2 CONC phint [sic] Augusta.  Mrs. Mary J. Quigley’s children are Walter Somers
2 CONC , Robert Parks, Jessie Eadelia, and Minnie (Mary).  Mrs. L. J. Quigley’
2 CONC s children are Thomas, James Malcolm, Ella E. Willie, and John.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Dryden manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1833, James and Mary Porterfield Harlan Parks and their family came t
2 CONC o Illinois, probably with Mary's father, George Harlan, travelling over
2 CONC land with teams to Gillespie Township, Macoupin County, where James pur
2 CONC chased a small farm of 240 acres in Sections 19 and 20.  He also bought 1
2 CONC 60 acres in Sections 22 and 24 of Township 8 North, Range 8 West.  His w
2 CONC ere original purchases from the United States Government at $1.25 per a
2 CONC cre.  In total, he bought 400 acres between November 22, 1833 and March 2
2 CONC 7, 1835....
