1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Parks studied medicine under Dr. Gall, and Attended lectures at t
2 CONC he Transylvania University, Lexington, Kentucky.  In 1828 he came to Mi
2 CONC ssouri, and in 1830 (April 22) he married S. L. Hartt, daughter of Dr. C
2 CONC . C. Hartt, of Boonville, Mo.  Their children were Elizabeth D. Joseph, M
2 CONC aria, H. C., George H., Martha C., James, Thomas D., Walter D., and Sar
2 CONC ah L.  In 1855, Elizabeth D. married W. W. Kennedy, and lived till Marc
2 CONC h 12th, 1864, when she died, leaving four children, Mary Louisa, Sudie B
2 CONC . David P., and Mattie J. H.  Ten years afterwards, W. W. Kennedy died l
2 CONC eaving the children with their grandfather Parks, where they had lived s
2 CONC ince a year before their mother’s death.  In June Sudie B. Kennedy marr
2 CONC ied Samuel T. Dysart, and died November 20th of the same year.  Joseph, o
2 CONC ldest son of Thomas Parks, died at the age of fourteen, though so young h
2 CONC ad established a character for punctuality and thoughtful attention to b
2 CONC usiness, was studious and intelligent, unusual at his age.  George Hart
2 CONC , the second son, died at eight years of age, having given evidence of s
2 CONC incere piety during his year’s illness.  Thomas Davis Parks entered the a
2 CONC rmy from a sense of duty in defense of his country’s laws, at the age o
2 CONC f nineteen, and died the following spring, and now sleeps in Pisgah Chu
2 CONC rchyard.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Thomas Davis Parks is the author of the Parks manuscript.  In the s
2 CONC ection on his brother Walter, Thomas mentions that he moved to Illinois f
2 CONC or a time, desiring to free his slaves and live in a free state, but af
2 CONC ter an outbreak of illness there he moved back to Missouri in 1838.
