1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT Walter Davis, fourth son of Joseph and Elizabeth Parks, married Mary Pa
2 CONC rk in Elkton, Kentucky, September, 1829.  He was then assistant in the c
2 CONC lerk’s office, Willis Reeves being clerk of both the circuit and county c
2 CONC ourts.  Their children were Joseph Reeves, Letitia, Martha Jane, John H
2 CONC amilton, James Thomas, Lizzie Davis, Walter Davis, Mary, David Henry, S
2 CONC amuel Park, and Wm. Harvey Parks.  Shortly after his marriage he moved t
2 CONC o Hopkinsville, Kentucky, and went into the mercantile business remaine
2 CONC d there till June 1835, when he moved to Illinois, where his two brothe
2 CONC rs James and Thomas had settled, all desiring to free their slaves and s
2 CONC ettle in a free state.  Illness in all of the families and throughout t
2 CONC he country caused Dr. Thomas Parks and Walter Parks to leave the state, t
2 CONC he former returning to Missouri in 1838, the latter to Nashville, Tenne
2 CONC ssee, in the spring or summer of 1836, residing there two or three year
2 CONC s, then settled in Memphis, and family moved to Washington County, Arka
2 CONC nsas, in the autumn of 1849, still engaged in mercantile business, live
2 CONC d there till his death, January 1850, leaving a widow and seven survivi
2 CONC ng children – Letitia dying in infancy, also James Thomas, and David Ha
2 CONC rvey, Joseph R. Parks married, and was killed by the Indians in the ear
2 CONC ly part of the Civil War, sacrificing his life in compliance with his m
2 CONC other-in-law’s imperative entreaties to go for a physician for her son, h
2 CONC aving been paroled within the bounds of his farm and neighborhood.  Yea
2 CONC rs elapsed before his remains were found buried under rock and brush in a d
2 CONC eep ravine.  He left two girls, Mary B. and Martha Jane, with their wid
2 CONC owed mother.  Martha Jane Parks married Wm. Spicy of Dardewell, Arkansa
2 CONC s, he dying during the Civil War, and she a few years later leaving fou
