1 NOTE WFT 137 # 299 says that John died about 1793, but that doesn't agree wi
2 CONC th the Parks manuscript, which says that Daniel and Martha (Davis) Smit
2 CONC h (John’s brother and Margaret’s sister) had three children, and Daniel a
2 CONC nd all three children died in an epidemic along with their “Uncle John, a
2 CONC nd a large portion of the Smith family”.  John dying relatively young i
2 CONC n an epidemic could also explain why he and Margaret had only two child
2 CONC ren.  Martha remarried to Brewer Reeves in April 1782, so if John and D
2 CONC aniel Smith Jr. died at about the same time in an epidemic it would hav
2 CONC e to be before that.  If John was really present at the surrender of Yo
2 CONC rktown in October 1781, as it says in the excerpt below (and the sectio
2 CONC n of the book on Daniel Smith Sr. seems to say that Daniel Jr. was also a
2 CONC t Yorktown), there isn’t much of a window between then and Martha’s rem
2 CONC arriage.  It seems like everyone wanted to claim to have been present a
2 CONC t Yorktown, though, so we should maybe take that constraint with a grai
2 CONC n of salt.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage date is from WFT 137 # 299.  Note that it is the same date t
2 CONC hat the excerpt below says John enlisted in the militia.  Not sure if t
2 CONC his date is reliable, but sources agree that the marriage was around 17
2 CONC 76 anyway.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Gleanings of Virginia History, An Historical and Genealogical Collecti
2 CONC on, Largely from Original Sources", by William Fletcher Boogher (Washin
2 CONC gton, DC, 1903), p. 338:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Smith, Jr., son of Daniel and Jane (Harrison) Smith, born Nov. 30, 1
2 CONC 752, in Augusta county; was commissioned March 14, 1776, ensign in the 4
2 CONC th Virginia Regiment; August, 1776, 2d lieutenant; Feb. 21, 1777, 1st l
2 CONC ieutenant; resigned May 26, 1778.  He was again commissioned 1st lieute
2 CONC nant Sept. 12, 1778, and was present at the surrender of Yorktown, Oct. 1
2 CONC 9, 1781.  Married, ------, 1776, Margaret Davis.
