1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT William was a most estimable man, beloved and respected by all who knew h
2 CONC im.  He was nearly of the age with his cousin B. H. Reeves, but he had n
2 CONC ot the turn for public business or the attractive address which the lat
2 CONC ter possessed, but was a man of good business capacity, enterprising an
2 CONC d deeply interested in the general welfare of the country.  He was a ma
2 CONC n whose influence was felt in the community in which he lived.  When ab
2 CONC out twenty-five years of age, he married Miss Anne North, a lady much y
2 CONC ounger than himself.  They had six daughters, the youngest an infant at t
2 CONC he time of his death, which occurred in the spring of 1826 from pneumon
2 CONC ia.  William Cunningham Davis was a warm-hearted, sincere, generous fri
2 CONC end and an affectionate relative.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Parks (above) says that he was about the same age as B.H. Reeves, b
2 CONC ut also says that he was younger than Ben, which would imply that he wa
2 CONC s probably born around 1787 or '88.  Diane's Genealogy web site (http:/
2 CONC /dgmweb.net/genealogy/FGS/D/DavisJames-DeborahMiller.shtml, downloaded 2
2 CONC 0 Feb 2007) and the Trewolla Moskop Haas Mansfield Tree give his birth d
2 CONC ate as 13 Oct 1796, which would be ten years younger than B.H. Reeves.  T
2 CONC he earlier birth also fits better with Parks’ statements that William w
2 CONC as about 25 when he married and that William’s wife was “much younger t
2 CONC han himself” (she was born about 1799).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He had six daughters.    -- WFT 22 # 1690
