1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT Watts Davis Ewin, about a year after the death of his mother, went to V
2 CONC irginia and lived with Uncle William Davis, except the time necessary t
2 CONC o acquire a knowledge of the saddling business, which he did in the vil
2 CONC lage of Greenville with a Mr. Beard.  After the termination of his appr
2 CONC enticeship, he returned to Kentucky and commenced business at the place w
2 CONC here the village of Trenton now is.  He did not however continue long i
2 CONC n this business, but married his cousin Margaret Donly and settled on a f
2 CONC arm.  In the spring of 1818, in company with B. H. Reeves, he removed t
2 CONC o Missouri and made a farm two or three miles from the place where the t
2 CONC own of Fayette now is, on which he resided the remainder of his life.  H
2 CONC is oldest son Charles, died when ten or twelve years old, his daughter E
2 CONC uphemia at about fifteen.  His surviving sons, William the eldest and W
2 CONC atts the youngest, have families and live in Pettis County; they are re
2 CONC spectable men.  John, James, and Henry live in Howard County; they and t
2 CONC heir families are respectable.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT His name was probably Walter Davis Ewin, with Watts as a nickname.  (Se
2 CONC e the will of his grandfather, Walter Davis, in which he lists Watts—sp
2 CONC elled Wat—with other grandchildren named Walter.)
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel Cormode gave his birth date as 24 Jan 1794, but other sources gi
2 CONC ve the year as 1791.  The death date comes from the SpenReeve family tr
2 CONC ee.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that Watts Davis Ewin and Margaret Donley were first cousins.
