2 CONC rn who in early infancy was subject to epilepsy, by which her reasoning p
2 CONC owers were dethroned, and she became a charge to her family.  By his wi
2 CONC ll, her father set apart a fund to be put at interest for her support.  T
2 CONC he fund was ample, and her brother William, who was executor of his fat
2 CONC her’s will, took charge of her until her death; which did not occur unt
2 CONC il the year of 1825.
2 CONT
2 CONT
