1 NOTE "History of Saline County, Missouri", Missouri Historical Co., St. Loui
2 CONC s, 1881, pp. 539-540:
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIAM DAVIS, P. O., Marshall.  Was born in Augusta county, Virginia, M
2 CONC ay 21, 1821.  When he was still quite young, his parents moved to Howar
2 CONC d county, Missouri, where he was raised and educated.  At the age of 21
2 CONC , he studied law with Judge Napton, studying at home, and the judge exa
2 CONC mining him two or three times a week.  At the age of 23, he was admitte
2 CONC d to the bar in Marshall, and practiced his profession in this county f
2 CONC or about five years.  February 1, 1844, he was married to Miss Nancy H. B
2 CONC rown, daughter of Judge Bernis Brown.  They had seven children, of whom s
2 CONC ix are living--Bernis B., Wycliff, Walton, Mrs. Mary J. Harvey, Mrs. Wi
2 CONC llie Odell, and Mrs. Lizzie M. Gregory.  William Davis and his wife wer
2 CONC e divorced in 1864, Mrs. Davis bringing the suit, habitual drunkenness b
2 CONC eing the alleged cause of action.  Mrs. Davis and her children are livi
2 CONC ng on a fine farm of 200 acres of choice land.  Her father came to Miss
2 CONC ouri in 1828, and settled in Saline on what is now known as the E. W. B
2 CONC rown farm.  He was a practical surveyor.  For several years he was coun
2 CONC ty surveyor, and laid off the town of Marshall.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Middle initial is from the Hillis/Abney/Franklin/Smith web site; also f
2 CONC rom FindAGrave.com.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT A letter from William Davis Jr. to William Van Lear, dated 30 Nov 1838, H
2 CONC oward County, MO (from the Missouri History Collection, Missouri Histor
2 CONC y Museum, St. Louis, MO; transcription obtained from Russell Reed):
2 CONT  
2 CONT Dear Sir
2 CONT
2 CONT I now take up my pen to write you a few lines so that you may know wher
2 CONC e I am.  I tell you I thought Missouri was the last place in the world b
2 CONC ut I now begin to think this is the finest place in the known world but y
2 CONC ou now say I am like Frank as I am here I want more here.  I do want yo
2 CONC u here that is the fact but I do not want to force you here but I wish y
2 CONC ou would come.  The land here is very rich.  I never saw such fine corn a
2 CONC s is here  your gardens would not bring such corn.  They leave 4 & 5 st
2 CONC ocks in a hill the weeds grow tall as saplins.  Tell Shelton I think th
2 CONC is country will suit him.  I helped these people to get up their corn a
2 CONC nd got 242 bushels per day.  But corn sells at 1$ per Barrel Beef 3 and 3 c
2 CONC ents Pork 3$.  Game is plenty I went into the woods about 50 yards the o
2 CONC ther day to shoot at a rabbit and up jumped a deer.  I raised up my gun t
2 CONC o shoot at him but he was to quick for me.  I oucht to have shot becaus
2 CONC e I had in one of Fathers loads which he put in for a deere some thing l
2 CONC ess than 4gr of powder 2 or 3 dozen buck shot set this down and lie.  F
2 CONC ather received a letter from Shelton this evening the firs letter that w
2 CONC e have received from Virginia.  The girls here are very handsome.  Lone
2 CONC ly likely they would sem well sorrowful powerful dreadful.  [MS illegib
2 CONC le] got killed [MS illegible] was in one of it a hay stack blew down on t
2 CONC hem  the stack was set up on [MS illegible] when it fell  one of the ra
2 CONC ils fell across [MSillegible] back  the same rail fell across [MS illeg
2 CONC ible] neck but [MS illegible] beings under the same rail it saved [MS i
2 CONC llegible]  it fell on Sunday night and they lay there until Tuesday.  I m
2 CONC issed them but thought they were out hunting as they hunt a great deal a
2 CONC s game is plenty.  But if I had saw it I could not have saved [MS illeg
2 CONC ible] because he was killed dead  Jis let me commensce another side thi
2 CONC s is a very find country.  Apples are very fine.  I have a many as I wa
2 CONC nt and will have all the winter.  They do not pretend to keep their app
2 CONC les from frosting but they do not show they have no trouble fatning the
2 CONC ir stock.  They get fat in the range.  Tell Shelton that everythings co
2 CONC me out safe.  Tell him that I want to see him here this time next year s
2 CONC o that when we move he can preach for us again.  He must come out this s
2 CONC pring and look at this country again.  I think he will be pleased.  You w
2 CONC ould laugh out to see our little hut strung around pictures.  Laugh out a
2 CONC nyhow at the thought of the thing this is the land of promise.  You mus
2 CONC t come here no don’t come on stay there run here don’t yes do what a fo
2 CONC ol this a fact you ccan get anything you want here particularly this th
2 CONC at and the other and you know what I suppose you would be surprised to h
2 CONC ear there was beauties here like myself so would I  But there is some m
2 CONC uch handsomer the people here believe in fun they run horse races  I ha
2 CONC ve seen one since I have been here the bet $500 they always tole me tha
2 CONC t there was no hills in Missouri but however is as rolling as some part
2 CONC s of Augusta.  It is all woods but rich.  I am a hand to describe a cou
2 CONC nty.  I am here but you are not you must come  I don’t care come on tel
2 CONC l Elizabeth to.  To me [MS illegible] is the way to describe a county c
2 CONC urs a fool look you here you must write to me tell Sam to do the same R
2 CONC eeves James Caldwell and direct you letters to Fayette Howard County Mi
2 CONC ssouri.  Write so soon as you received this letter.  I am here tis dark a t
2 CONC ime now but you do not know that you only have my word for that.  You k
2 CONC now I am such a lyar you cannot believe me but I don’t care  Believe me i
2 CONC f you choose and soon and soon come here to Missouri the promise land t
2 CONC he bushy land the [MS illegible] pond the dull knife the mean kin  Moth
2 CONC er has not a partridge net.  I did not tell you what sort of times we h
2 CONC ad comeing out.  We had very fine times if I had had that false face I c
2 CONC ould have had very fine times.  Times are hard and money scarce.  Money d
2 CONC on’t quite grow on trees it will wait till more people get here to get i
2 CONC t.  I expect you will not get this letter for some time they say the ri
2 CONC ver is frozen.  I expect to receive an answer immediately  excuse bad w
2 CONC riting and mistakes you must put on your specks to read this I must clo
2 CONC se as it is getting late in the night.  Be sure and answer this immedia
2 CONC tely.  Tell Martha Janie that I am expecting a letter from her every da
2 CONC y  you cannot say that I change my subject suddenly  Excuse bad writing a
2 CONC nd mistakes.
2 CONT  
2 CONT Your cousin most affectionately,
2 CONT Wm Davis Jr.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Shelton is presumably Shelton Spillsbury Abney, husband of William's s
2 CONC ister Martha Jane (Davis) Abney.  William Van Lear was William’s cousin o
2 CONC n his mother’s side.  The notes in square brackets are from Russell Ree
2 CONC d.]
