1 NOTE The Parks manuscript gives his name as Burrus Kincaid; the Hillis/Abney
2 CONC /Franklin/Smith web site spells it Burris Kinkaid.  Robert Davis and St
2 CONC ephen Landuyt have Burris Kincaid.  His name is spelled Burris Kinkaid (
2 CONC actually, it looks like Burris Kinkiad) in the record of his marriage w
2 CONC ith Mary Amelia Haight on Ancestry.com.
2 CONT
2 CONT His date of birth of 23 Nov 1826 comes from the Hillis/Abney/Franklin/S
2 CONC mith web site.  Some sources have the same date, but with 1827 rather t
2 CONC han 1826.  The 1880 census mortality schedule gives his age at death as 5
2 CONC 3, which fits with the 1826 date. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From Stephen Landuyt:
2 CONT
2 CONT Burris Kincaid Davis was born in 1826 to William Caldwell Davis and Sar
2 CONC ah “Sallie” Van Lear.  Burris Kincaid, often referred to as “BK”, was t
2 CONC heir fifth child and all were born in Augusta County, Virginia.  In the 1
2 CONC 830s, William Caldwell moved his family to Saline County, Missouri, due t
2 CONC o widespread farm failures in Virginia.  It would have been while livin
2 CONC g in Missouri that BK attended high school and perhaps college; however
2 CONC , his life in Missouri remains largely unknown to me.  I am BK’s great-
2 CONC great-grandson, and a lifelong Californian thanks to BK’s decision to c
2 CONC ome West during the Gold Rush.
2 CONT  
2 CONT It is not known precisely when, or how, BK travelled from Missouri to C
2 CONC alifornia.  In contemporary local newspaper articles BK is referred to a
2 CONC s being a gold miner who arrived during the 1849 gold rush.  In 1853, B
2 CONC K was living near the town of Shasta, which is today a California State P
2 CONC ark and during the gold rush was a supply center for gold hunters and a r
2 CONC oute to the northern gold fields.  A January 1854 notice in the <i>Shas
2 CONC ta Courier</i> informed BK that he had mail waiting for him at the Shas
2 CONC ta Post Office.
2 CONT  
2 CONT By June 1854, BK had relocated from Shasta County to Placer County, whe
2 CONC re he lived in the town of Ophirville.  Soon after moving to Ophirville
2 CONC , BK became very active in Placer County Democratic Party politics.  He g
2 CONC ave speeches in support of Democratic candidates, became a member of th
2 CONC e Central Committee of the Placer County Democratic Party, and became a c
2 CONC andidate for the California Assembly in the 1855 general election.  He n
2 CONC arrowly lost that election but remained active and became President of t
2 CONC he Ophirville Democratic Club.
2 CONT  
2 CONT Democratic Party politics in California during the 1850s reflected the l
2 CONC ong-festering schism that led to the Civil War.  The Democratic Party i
2 CONC n California was split between Union Democrats and Southern Democrats (
2 CONC i.e., Breckenridge Democrats and secessionists).  BK was a proud Southe
2 CONC rn Democrat and he was not alone.  Southerners comprised a significant p
2 CONC art of the state’s population and held important positions in federal, s
2 CONC tate, and local governments during the period before the start of the w
2 CONC ar.
2 CONT
2 CONT BK left Ophirville at the end of 1859 and moved to newly formed Mono Co
2 CONC unty, where he lived in the town of Monoville in 1860 and 1861.  There h
2 CONC is business was supplying equipment to gold and silver prospectors in t
2 CONC he areas that soon become Bodie, California, and Aurora, Nevada.  BK al
2 CONC so ran a second time for the California Assembly—this time out of Mono C
2 CONC ounty in the general election of September 1861.
2 CONT  
2 CONT Mono County had three Assembly seats to fill in the 1861 election.  Thr
2 CONC ee Democrats ran in a field of ten candidates.  The Democrats were C.W. K
2 CONC endall, a Union Democrat, and A.H. Mitchell and B.K. Davis, both Southe
2 CONC rn Democrats.  In the initial vote tally C.W. Kendall and B.K. won thei
2 CONC r races and A.H. Mitchell lost his.
2 CONT  
2 CONT Election fraud perpetrated by supporters of A.H. Mitchell was uncovered i
2 CONC mmediately.  Ironically, Mitchell lost his race despite receiving all 5
2 CONC 10 fraudulent votes from a non-existent polling location named “Big Spr
2 CONC ings”.  The conspirators needed to make that fraudulent polling place l
2 CONC ook legitimate, so they also included votes for the other two Democrati
2 CONC c candidates.  C.W. Kendall received 359 fake votes, and BK received 29
2 CONC 8 fake votes.  Suspiciously, there were exactly zero votes for any of t
2 CONC he Republican Assembly candidates.  When the Assembly convened in Janua
2 CONC ry 1862, the election results from Mono County were successfully challe
2 CONC nged and the fake votes removed.  As a result, BK again narrowly lost h
2 CONC is Assembly race; however, the Assembly expressly exonerated BK of any w
2 CONC rongdoing and stated clearly that BK had played no role in the election f
2 CONC raud.
2 CONT  
2 CONT The 1862 Assembly met in San Francisco rather than Sacramento, due to h
2 CONC istoric rains and resulting floods that had turned the Sacramento stree
2 CONC ts into Venetian canals.  The rains during the winter of 1861–62 lasted s
2 CONC o long, and created such extensive flooding, that California’s economy w
2 CONC as devastated.  In San Francisco, the Assembly met in the Merchants Exc
2 CONC hange Building located on the corner of Battery and Washington Streets.
2 CONC   A short walk from that corner is Grant Avenue (then named DuPont), an
2 CONC d crossing Grant is a very short street named Harlan Place.  In an apar
2 CONC tment on Harlan Place lived 16-year-old Mary Amelia Haight with her aun
2 CONC t and uncle.  It is not known exactly how BK and Mary Amelia met, but i
2 CONC t is known that their paths crossed in San Francisco in 1862.  On 9 Jul 1
2 CONC 863, Mary Amelia, then 17, and BK, then 36, were married in Dayton, Nev
2 CONC ada.  Mary Amelia had moved to Dayton after leaving San Francisco in la
2 CONC te 1862, and BK followed her to Dayton.
2 CONT  
2 CONT BK and Mary Amelia continued to live in the Dayton area until 1868.  La
2 CONC ter that year they moved a few hundred miles east to the White Pine Mou
2 CONC ntains near present-day Ely, Nevada.  A silver strike resulted in the f
2 CONC ormation of White Pine County, and shortly after BK and Mary Amelia arr
2 CONC ived, the town of Hamilton became the capital of White Pine County.  BK a
2 CONC nd Mary Amelia raised their family in Hamilton.  They arrived in Hamilt
2 CONC on with one child, my great-grandmother, who had been born four years b
2 CONC efore their move to Hamilton.  In Hamilton, BK and Mary Amelia had four m
2 CONC ore children.  All five children survived early childhood except for th
2 CONC eir third daughter, Maybelle, who died shortly after her second birthda
2 CONC y.
2 CONT
2 CONT BK became the justice of the peace in Hamilton in 1868 and held that el
2 CONC ected position for six years running.  Although Hamilton was a rough-an
2 CONC d-tumble mining town, Hamilton had its eye on becoming a respectable ci
2 CONC ty.  It had the most elaborate hotel in the state, a county courthouse, s
2 CONC chool, churches, Masonic Lodge, theatre, and expectations of someday ri
2 CONC valing San Francisco in size.  As the justice of the peace in Hamilton, B
2 CONC K handled the crimes common to mining towns, which ranged from domestic d
2 CONC isputes, to bar fights, to killings over disputed claims.
2 CONT  
2 CONT BK became a member of the Nevada bar and practiced law in Hamilton.  In 1
2 CONC 875 he was elected the district attorney for White Pine County.  With h
2 CONC is deputy DA, Horace D. Beene, who was a young Alabama lawyer who had m
2 CONC oved west following the Civil War, they prosecuted a number of high-pro
2 CONC file cases.  Judge Davis, as he was professionally known, enjoyed a rep
2 CONC utation for honesty and integrity.
2 CONT  
2 CONT BK died 25 Mar 1880 in Hamilton of “dropsy” (congestive heart failure).  
2 CONC He had been in failing health for some time.  Mary Amelia was carrying t
2 CONC heir fifth child at the time of his death.  The <i>Eureka Sentinel</i> o
2 CONC f Eureka, Nevada, published the following obituary on 26 Mar 1880:
2 CONT  
2 CONT “<b>DEATH OF B.K. DAVIS. </b> Mr. B.K. Davis, District Attorney of Whit
2 CONC e Pine County, died at his home in Hamilton yesterday morning.  The dec
2 CONC eased had been in feeble health for a long time, and his death was not, t
2 CONC herefore, unexpected.  He was a pioneer of the Pacific coast, and an ol
2 CONC d resident of Nevada.  He came to White Pine County with the rush in 18
2 CONC 68, or 1869, from Dayton, Lyon County.  While a resident of White Pine h
2 CONC e filled various positions of public trust, and always with fidelity.  H
2 CONC e was serving his second term as District Attorney at the time of his d
2 CONC eath.  He leaves a wife and a family of children, besides a large numbe
2 CONC r of friends to mourn his loss.  *** The SENTINEL is pained to learn of t
2 CONC he death of Mr. Davis, and extends its sincerest condolences to the str
2 CONC icken family.”
2 CONT
2 CONT BK was buried at Mourner’s Point, the Hamilton cemetery where his daugh
2 CONC ter Maybelle had been buried five years earlier.  They were joined at M
2 CONC ourner’s Point by Horace Beene following his unexpected death in Hamilt
2 CONC on in 1881.
2 CONT  
2 CONT Mary Amelia continued to live in Hamilton after BK’s death.  In 1883 sh
2 CONC e married Stephen E. Starrett, who was a miner originally from Maine.  
2 CONC Together they had three children in Hamilton.  In 1888 they moved from H
2 CONC amilton to the Mission District of San Francisco, where Mary Amelia liv
2 CONC ed until her death in 1918.
