2 DATE 13 AUG 2020
1 NOTE "History of Saline County, Missouri", Missouri Historical Co., St. Loui
2 CONC s, 1881, p. 854:
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN B. DAVIS.  The father of the subject of this sketch, Col. Wm. C. D
2 CONC avis, was born in Augusta county, Virginia, in 1794, and lived there un
2 CONC til 1838.  In the war of 1812 he commanded the 70th Virginia regiment.  H
2 CONC e was the father of nine children, most of whom are now living in Misso
2 CONC uri.  Jacob C. has filled several important offices in Illinois; was a m
2 CONC ember of congress, and also in the state legislature, while quite a you
2 CONC ng man.  James R. is a member of the present county court of Saline cou
2 CONC nty, a position he has held before, and which his father held before hi
2 CONC m.  William, formerly a lawyer of Marshall.  Dr. D. S. Davis located so
2 CONC uth of Marshall, on the old homestead.  Martha J. Abney, wife of Col. S
2 CONC . S. Abney, of Morgan county, Missouri, and member of the late legislat
2 CONC ure.  Dr. A. A. Davis, an extensive practitioner in Pettis county, Miss
2 CONC ouri.  Judge B. K. Davis, of California.  Was elected to the legislatur
2 CONC e in 1860, and was expelled from the same in 1861, for rebel sentiments
2 CONC .  Had to leave the state, and was afterward elected judge in Nevada, a
2 CONC nd afterwards prosecuting-attorney.  Returning to the practice of law, h
2 CONC e died in 1880.  John B., the youngest son, was born in Augusta county, V
2 CONC irginia, in 1834.  Came to Missouri when a child, with his father, and w
2 CONC as raised mostly in Saline county.  In 1855 he was married to Miss Sall
2 CONC ie E. Smith, daughter of Gervis S. Smith, by whom he has had twelve chi
2 CONC ldren, nine of whom are living: Gervas W., Mrs. Mary V. Gilbreath, of S
2 CONC aline county, Lizzie H., S. Mattie, Lavinia A., Charles B., Joseph B., Z
2 CONC eleka, and John R.  Mr. Davis has been a member of the Methodist Church
2 CONC , South, since his eighteenth year.  In 1859 he sold his farm and went t
2 CONC o merchandising in Longwood, but in 1861 his store was robbed by United S
2 CONC tates troops, which broke him up.  In 1863 he went into the tobacco bus
2 CONC iness, and in 1864 into the Confederate army, in Price's last raid.  In t
2 CONC he battles of the retreat he took no part, as he was unarmed.  Surrende
2 CONC red as lieutenant at Shreveport, 1865.  In 1867 he moved to his present r
2 CONC esidence in Salt Fork township.  Suffered considerable loss of property d
2 CONC uring the war, but now has 190 acres of land, mostly in cultivation, go
2 CONC od house and good improvements.  All his family, except one son, are at h
2 CONC ome.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From Robert Davis (e-mail dated 26 Oct 2011):
2 CONT
2 CONT John Boyden Davis entered the Confederate Army in Prices last raid into M
2 CONC issouri in 1864.  He was in H Woods Battalion Drivers Company Shelby Di
2 CONC vision.  He was a teamster. He was a commissioned Lieutenant after the c
2 CONC aptain of his company deserted.  He had a store in Longwood Missouri be
2 CONC tween Marshall and Sedalia Missouri.  It was burned by Federal Troops i
2 CONC n 1863.  He was at the battle of Westport and Prices subsequent retreat b
2 CONC ack to Arkansas.  He broke his sword rather than surrender in Shrevepor
2 CONC t LA in 1865.
