1 NOTE "History of Chariton and Howard Counties, Missouri", T. B. Smith and P. S
2 CONC . Gehrig, Historical Publishing Co., 1923, pp. 454-55:
2 CONT
2 CONT From biography of C. E. Givens:  Dr. H. K. Givens was born on a farm ne
2 CONC ar Fayette, July 17, 1856, the son of C. E. and Mary Ann (Kring) Givens
2 CONC .  C. E. Givens was a native of Kentucky and came to Missouri with his p
2 CONC arents, Joseph W. and Mary Givens.  C. E. Givens became a prominent far
2 CONC mer and stockman, as was his father, and owned large tracts of land.  H
2 CONC e died in 1907, at the age of 75 years[.]   He was a member of the Meth
2 CONC odist Church.  Mary Ann (Kring) Givens was born in Fayette and died in 1
2 CONC 912 at the age of 70 years.  She was the daughter of Henry W. Kring, we
2 CONC ll known jeweler of Fayette and among the first merchants of the town.  D
2 CONC r. H. K. Givens was reared on his father's farm and received his educat
2 CONC ion in the public schools and Central College.  He was graduated from t
2 CONC he St. Louis Medical College in 1878, and then began the practice of me
2 CONC dicine, which he followed until 1889, at that time becoming cashier of T
2 CONC he Farmers and Merchants Bank of Fayette.  In 1906 Dr. Givens was elect
2 CONC ed president of the bank, which office he now holds.  He also follows f
2 CONC arming and stock raising and now operates 1340 acres of well improved l
2 CONC and in Howard County, and 320 acres in New Mexico.  Since 1920, Dr. Giv
2 CONC ens has lived in Fayette.  He is a Democrat and a member of the Methodi
2 CONC st Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1881 Dr. H. K. Givens married Miss Nannie D. Duncan, and to this uni
2 CONC on the following children were born:  Mary L., teacher, Rockford, Ill.; C
2 CONC . E., the subject of this sketch; Donly, lives in Kansas City, Mo.; Flo
2 CONC rence, married James S. Prosser, St. Louis; Fannie, married W. G. Lockr
2 CONC idge, Richmond township, Howard County; and Willie, married W. L. Talbo
2 CONC t, assistant cashier of The Farmers and Merchants Bank of Fayette.   Dr
2 CONC . Givens has served as curator of Howard-Payne College for 42 years and s
2 CONC ecretary of the board for 32 years and served as president of the board f
2 CONC or 20 years, resigning that office in 1922.  Dr. Givens is an influenti
2 CONC al and highly respected citizen of Howard County.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT WFT 22, 1690:
2 CONT
2 CONT He studied medicine and became a doctor in Fayette, Mo.  After a short t
2 CONC ime he left the medical profession and became a banker.
2 CONT During the depression he shot himself at home and died.
2 CONT He became a partner in the medical practice of Dr. Wright in Fayette, M
2 CONC O.  He and Nancy Price Duncan lived upstairs in the Wright Building in F
2 CONC ayette (the oldest building in Fayette) while their house (the Joe Dene
2 CONC y-Sarah Dow house) was being built on Main St (abt 1881).
2 CONT He was still practicing medicine by 1889.
2 CONT In 1890 he bought the farm north of Fayette, MO, 100 acres, for $6700.
