2 CONT
2 CONT BENJAMIN F. GIVENS,
2 CONT farmer.  One of the youngest but, at the same time, most prominent and p
2 CONC rogressive farmers of Howard county is the gentleman whose name heads t
2 CONC his sketch.  His farm contains 1,100 acres, and is devoted to both grai
2 CONC n growing and stock raising.  He has 200 acres of wheat, 140 of meadow, a
2 CONC nd other products in proportion; and his stock of cattle numbers about 2
2 CONC 00 head, his sheep flock 150, besides large numbers of horses, mules, h
2 CONC ogs, etc.  He was born near where he now lives January 30th, 1859, and w
2 CONC as educated at Central college.  He was the second of a family of seven c
2 CONC hildren of E. C. Givens and Mary A. (Kring) Givens.  October 4, 1882, h
2 CONC e was married to Miss India K. Swinney, daughter of Mrs. Salvia Swinney
2 CONC , of Fayette, and came to his farm the same month, where he has since l
2 CONC ived.  He and his wife are both members of the Methodist Episcopal chur
2 CONC ch in Fayette.
