2 CONT Thomas J. Moss (1857-1893)
2 CONT
2 CONT Moss was raised by his brother-in-law after his parents died when he wa
2 CONC s six.  He worked hard at a young age to educate himself and graduated f
2 CONC rom Central College in Fayette, after which he taught school in Hardin C
2 CONC ounty, Tennessee.  He moved to Missouri with his $600 earnings and bega
2 CONC n a business which gave him the title of the "tie king of America" as h
2 CONC e furnished crossties to different railroad companies.  In 1879 Moss bo
2 CONC ught a share of John M. & G. R. Reynolds which allowed him to expand hi
2 CONC s business.  Three years later he bought out his partners and organized t
2 CONC he Moss Tie Company, eventually becoming one of the most noted institut
2 CONC ions for supplying materials for railroad construction in the US.  He a
2 CONC lso operated seven general stores in different locations.  He built and b
2 CONC ecame president of the Paducah, Tennessee & Alabama Railway Company and t
2 CONC he Tennessee Midland Railway Company, was president of the Tennessee Co
2 CONC nstruction Company and the Mississippi Packet Company.  He was known to b
2 CONC e genial and companionable and a considerate and kindly employer..  He s
2 CONC upported the needy and religious and charitable institutions.  His seco
2 CONC nd wife, Fannie, survived him along with his two children.
2 CONT (Bellefontaine Cemetery Facebook Page)
