1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret was married when quite young to Alexander Herring, and was the m
2 CONC other of a large and interesting family, John S., Martha D., William, A
2 CONC lexander, Daniel Smith, Margaret Stevenson, Rebecca, and Ann.  Margaret H
2 CONC erring was a most excellent woman, like her mother was a favorite among h
2 CONC er relations.  Having lived in her family, I can speak of her from pers
2 CONC onal knowledge.  Although a cousin, she was to me a mother, her daughte
2 CONC rs, sisterly and her sons, brotherly.  But they have almost all passed a
2 CONC way.  In its first visitation to this country, cholera cut off the grea
2 CONC ter part of the family.  John had previously died of malaria fever, con
2 CONC tracted in the then marshy country west of the Tennessee, when he was e
2 CONC ngaged as commissioner of the State of Virginia in locating the lands o
2 CONC f her continental soldiers.  Rebecca had died with some disease inciden
2 CONC tal to children.  Daniel entered West Point Academy in 1822, and in due t
2 CONC ime graduated.  I think third in his class.  He entered the army as Lie
2 CONC utenant, and died from disease contracted in the swamps of Florida duri
2 CONC ng the Seminole War.  All of the Herring family in Ohio except the two y
2 CONC oungest, Margaret Stevenson and Annie, were cut off by cholera in 1832 o
2 CONC r ’33.  Martha married George Chrisman and remained in Virginia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Parks manuscript, as well as WFT 22 # 1690 and WFT 137 # 299, spell h
2 CONC er middle name Read.  However, Larry Herrin pointed me to Find A Grave, w
2 CONC here her grave is shown and her middle name is spelled Reed.  Her middl
2 CONC e name is spelled Reed in Herring and Smith genealogies.  Also, note th
2 CONC at she might have been named for Margaret (Cunningham) Reed, who might h
2 CONC ave been a relative (see the notes for John Cunningham).  Larry Herrin a
2 CONC lso mentioned that “Alexander Herring and Margaret Reed Smith moved fro
2 CONC m Virginia to Green Co., KY then to Clermont Co., OH where they died an
2 CONC d are buried.”
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT One source said she was born in Rockingham County, but Rockingham Count
2 CONC y didn't exist till 1778, so presumably she was born in the part of Aug
2 CONC usta County that became Rockingham County.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT WFT 22 # 1690:
2 CONT She was an excellent woman and a favorite among her relatives.  A great m
2 CONC any of her family died from cholera.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The list of children comes from WFT 22 # 1690 and from Gleanings of Vir
2 CONC ginia History.  The Parks manuscript also lists all of them except Eliz
2 CONC a.  However, Parks indicates Margaret Stevenson as one person, not two (
2 CONC possibly this was a transcription error when the manuscript was typed).  T
2 CONC he children are also listed in the article from Hardesty's Encyclopedia t
2 CONC hat is reproduced in the notes for grandson George Chrisman.  The birth y
2 CONC ears and even the birth order are different between Gleanings of Virgin
2 CONC ia History and Hardesty's Encyclopedia.  The information from Hardesty s
2 CONC eems better, giving full birth dates rather than just years, so I am go
2 CONC ing to use his birth dates.
