1 NAME Edward Stuart /Hall/
2 GIVN Edward Stuart
2 SURN Hall
1 SEX M
1 _UID E1418FE84C6CFA469E41561FA83594156BB9
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 AUG 2020
1 NOTE "Todd County, Kentucky, Family Histories", by the Todd County Historica
2 CONC l Society, Turner Publishing Co., 1995, pp. 198-99 (obtained from Googl
2 CONC e Books):
2 CONT
2 CONT EDWARD STUART HALL - Edward Stuart Hall (1788-1870) came to that part o
2 CONC f Christian County that later became part of Todd in 1817.  His bride, J
2 CONC ennetta (Jennettae) Ewing (1792-1845), accompanied him over the Wildern
2 CONC ess Road from Augusta County, VA.  Jenetta was the daughter of John Ewi
2 CONC ng (1761-1796) and Sallie Davis (daughter of Walter Davis and Martha Cu
2 CONC nningham), all natives of Augusta County, VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jennetta Ewing and her parents had started to Christian County in 1795; h
2 CONC owever, they stopped in Hardin County, KY, where her father, John Ewing
2 CONC , planted a corn crop.  Unfortunately he died there.  Her mother, Salli
2 CONC e Davis Ewing, resumed the journey with her four children and joined he
2 CONC r brother, James Davis, a pioneer settler in Christian County.  (James' w
2 CONC ill is the first on record in Christian County.)  Unfortunately in 1805 S
2 CONC allie Davis Ewing became ill and realized she would soon die.  She sent h
2 CONC er daughter Jennetta back to Virginia by horseback over the Wilderness R
2 CONC oad, accompanied by her 18 year old nephew, Benjamin Harrison Reeves (1
2 CONC 787-1849), to be reared by a nephew.  When Jennetta was mature, she mar
2 CONC ried in Augusta County, VA, and with her groom, Edward S. Hall, emigrat
2 CONC ed back to Christian County and took up the old Sallie Ewing farm.  Her
2 CONC e, about 1 1/2 miles south of Bell's Chapel, they lived until Jennetta d
2 CONC ied in 1845.  Following her death, Edward S. Hall continued to live the
2 CONC re until 1857 when he sold his farm and along with all of his children, e
2 CONC migrated to Tarrant County, TX, where he died in 1870.  Edward S. Hall a
2 CONC nd Jennetta had four children that reached adulthood.  Among these chil
2 CONC dren, the oldest son was John E. Hall, about whom more will be said lat
2 CONC er.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Stuart Hall was a descendant of Dr. Isaac Hall, a native of Coun
2 CONC ty Armagh, Ulster, North Ireland, who arrived in Virginia before the ye
2 CONC ar 1740.  His wife was Sarah (?) Jane Allison.  Dr. Isaac Hall had at l
2 CONC east four children, among whom was Edward Hall (c1719-1796), an early s
2 CONC ettler in Augusta County, VA.  Edward married Eleanor Stuart (1724-1802
2 CONC ), daughter of Archibald Stuart (1700-1759) and his wife, Janet Brown.  E
2 CONC dward Hall's son, Major Alexander Hall (1759-1831) married Mary Patters
2 CONC on Stuart (b 1763), niece of Eleanor Stuart.  Like his father, Major Al
2 CONC exander Stuart [sic] was an Augusta County, VA, farmer.  He and Mary Pa
2 CONC tterson Stuart became the parents of 11 children, among whom was Edward S
2 CONC tuart Hall (1788-1870), who became the groom of Jennetta Ewing on Oct. 9
2 CONC , 1816 in Augusta County, VA.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Stuarts were a large family, descending from Archibald Stuart and J
2 CONC anet Brown.  Archibald's ancestors removed from Scotland to the north o
2 CONC f Ireland during the reign of King James I of England.  Archibald fled I
2 CONC reland after an unsuccessful religious uprising in 1727.  It was severa
2 CONC l years later before he was successful in bringing his wife and childre
2 CONC n to Pennsylvania.  They later emigrated to Augusta County and settled o
2 CONC n a farm adjacent to what is now Waynesboro, VA.  It was here that his f
2 CONC amily intermarried with the Hall family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Perrin's History of Todd County, published about 1885, contains some in
2 CONC formation about Edward S. Hall and notes his father was Major Alexander H
2 CONC all.  Edward S. Hall did not enter into political life in Todd County.  H
2 CONC e seems to have been a farmer who was more intent on rearing his childr
2 CONC en and making a living than he was in seeking public office.  John E. H
2 CONC all (1824, Todd County - 1879, Tarrant County, TX), son of Edward Stuar
2 CONC t Hall, was reared in the Bell's Chapel neighborhood.  He married Mary A
2 CONC nn Elizabeth Tandy (1828-1849), daughter of Henry Tandy (1800-1848) and S
2 CONC arah Ann Davis (1809-1887), in 1847 in Todd County.  Their son, James H
2 CONC enry Hall, was born on Sept. 28, 1848.  James Henry's mother died on Ma
2 CONC y 18, 1849, leaving an eight months old infant.  He was taken by his gr
2 CONC andmother, Sarah Ann Davis Tandy (1809-1887), and reared on the farm no
2 CONC w known as the Tandy-Hall Farm.  The nucleus of this farm, now consisti
2 CONC ng of 298 acres, was acquired in 1825 by John Davis (1778-1826) and his w
2 CONC ife, Mary Pendleton (1775-1844), parents of Sarah Ann Davis Tandy (1809
2 CONC -1887).  This land has been owned by some member of the family since.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Henry Hall (1848-1911) and his wife, Henrietta Darnall (1850-1904
2 CONC ), became the parents of James Darnall Hall (1889-1972).  James Darnall a
2 CONC nd his wife, Fay McLain Petrie (1887-1971), daughter of William Sobiesk
2 CONC i Petrie, MD, (1856-1918) and Susie Lenora McLain (1866-1925), had thre
2 CONC e children: Marjorie Darnall Hall (Mrs Wm. Archie Cowherd), William Pet
2 CONC rie Hall, M.D. (wife, Jane Vivian Jones), and Suzanne Hall (Mrs Robert G
2 CONC arland "Bob" Foster).  Marjorie and Suzanne have spent most of their li
2 CONC ves in Todd County.  <i>Submitted by Wm P. Hall MD.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT </i>The Parks manuscript describes Edward Stuart Hall as a nephew of Ju
2 CONC dge Archibald Stuart and it also describes Mary Stuart (wife of William D
2 CONC onley) as a niece of Judge Archibald Stuart.  That might mean that Edwa
2 CONC rd and Mary were first cousins, but I have not so far been able to find a f
2 CONC amily tree that shows this relationship.
