1 _UID 911D10C493679344BB53C7ACF6E6EB30CBBD
1 CHAN
2 DATE 4 JUN 2020
1 NOTE From the Parks manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel Smith studied law, and though not brilliant, he stood high as a l
2 CONC awyer.  At about thirty years of age, he ran as a Republican against Ja
2 CONC cob Swope, a Federalist, and though a very popular man, suffered defeat a
2 CONC s a consequence of the strength of the Federal party in the district.  H
2 CONC e was a great favorite among his relations on account of his ardent att
2 CONC achment to all who partook of his blood.  Indeed, so strong was his fam
2 CONC ily attachment that it extended even to the inanimate objects; so that, i
2 CONC f he found any utensil, however homely, that had belonged to Grandfathe
2 CONC r or Grandmother, he would buy it at any price and lay it up as a souve
2 CONC nir.  Not long after his defeat as a candidate for Congress, he was app
2 CONC ointed Judge of the Supreme Court in Virginia, and continued on the ben
2 CONC ch until late in life, highly respected for his profound legal knowledg
2 CONC e and clearness of judgment.  He lived to the age of 77 or 78.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Gleanings of Virginia History, An Historical and Genealogical Collecti
2 CONC on, Largely from Original Sources", by William Fletcher Boogher (Washin
2 CONC gton, DC, 1903), pp. 339-40:
2 CONT
2 CONT Daniel Smith, 2d of John and Margaret (Davis) Smith, born 1779 ; at the a
2 CONC ge of 21 years came into possession of his interest in his grandfather'
2 CONC s estate, inherited through his father; studied law under Judge Bushrod W
2 CONC ashington; began the practice of his profession at his old home in Harr
2 CONC isonburg; was a member of both houses of the Virginia Legislature; firs
2 CONC t a member<i> </i>of<i> </i>the House of<i> </i>Delegates in 1805; pass
2 CONC ed his life on his estate known as "Waverly," where he generously dispe
2 CONC nsed his hospitality; was Judge of<i> </i>the Circuit and Supreme Court
2 CONC s for over forty years; his portrait can be seen in the Court House at H
2 CONC arrisonburg, Va., the only one the county officials will permit in the b
2 CONC uilding; married, June 10, 1809, Frances Strother Duff, b. Feb. 11, 179
2 CONC 2, d. Oct. 4, 1849.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "A History of Rockingham County Virginia" by John W. Wayland (Ruebush-E
2 CONC lkins Company, Dayton, VA, 1912), p. 352:
2 CONT  
2 CONT Daniel Smith, “a learned, pure judge and good man,” was born at or near H
2 CONC arrisonburg, in 1779, son of John and Margaret Davis Smith, grandson of J
2 CONC ustice Daniel Smith (pp. 54, 68); he married Frances Strother Duff, Jun
2 CONC e 10, 1809; children, Margaret, Elizabeth, Lucius, Frances, Marie, John
2 CONC , Daniel; he died Nov. 8, 1850.  In 1805 he was a member of the Virgini
2 CONC a House of Delegates; from 1804 to 1811 he was commonwealth’s attorney f
2 CONC or Rockingham; on April 10, 1811, he was appointed a judge of the Gener
2 CONC al Court, and from the same date till his death (1850) he was judge of t
2 CONC he circuit superior court for Rockingham County.  He succeeded Judge Hu
2 CONC gh Holmes, and was succeeded by Judge Green B. Samuels.  His portrait n
2 CONC ow adorns the Rockingham County courtroom.  Judge John Paul said of him
2 CONC :
2 CONT  
2 CONT "No judge, perhaps, who ever presided on the Circuit Court bench in Vir
2 CONC ginia exerted a better or more lasting influence on the people within h
2 CONC is jurisdiction.  He was not only a great man intellectually, but he wa
2 CONC s great in the moral attributes necessary to the perfection of judicial c
2 CONC haracter."
2 CONT  
2 CONT In the celebrated case of the National Bank against Steinbergen and oth
2 CONC ers, involving over half a million of dollars, he gave a decision on Sa
2 CONC turday in favor of the plaintiff; on Monday morning following he came i
2 CONC nto court and announced from the bench that he had erred in his former c
2 CONC onclusions, and proceeded to reverse his decision.
2 CONT  
2 CONT I have been told that Judge Smith’s residence was a short distance nort
2 CONC heast of Dayton, near the Shrum brick factory.
