1 _UID 829BB04A34198440BBD412536DAD91F9C007
1 CHAN
2 DATE 21 OCT 2019
1 NOTE I have seen him referred to as Benjamin Harrison Reeves Jr. in many pla
2 CONC ces, and took for granted that it was right.  However, John Wanamaker (
2 CONC a great-grandson) says that he has never seen a middle name for him, so i
2 CONC t's possible that this was just an assumption on someone's part.  I wil
2 CONC l continue to list him as BHR Jr., but beware!
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Wanamaker manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1872 Benjamin and Martha Reeves and their children, which by then in
2 CONC cluded a third child – Jessie, and probably a fourth – Kate, removed to F
2 CONC remont County, Iowa.  Two of Martha Kaziah’s sisters and one brother em
2 CONC igrated to Fremont County as well.  Benjamin was known as “Colonel” Ree
2 CONC ves, as his father had been.  We cannot know with certainty what prompt
2 CONC ed the family to choose Fremont County as their destination, but appare
2 CONC ntly some of the Payne family had preceded them.  M.U. Payne sometime a
2 CONC fter the Civil War had acquired large land holdings in the Missouri Riv
2 CONC er bottom of Fremont county and had married Martha Reeves’s sister, Sal
2 CONC lie Patton in 1867.  The Reeves family took up residence in the Missour
2 CONC i River bottom in what was called the Valley Farm community, where he a
2 CONC pparently rented land from his brother-in-law, M.U. Payne....
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. Reeves bought the original 505 acres of what became known as Holly
2 CONC wood Ranch* in 1883 from Mr. Wm. Knappe for $10,000.  He added 160 acre
2 CONC s to the farm in 1891 and 120 acres in 1900.  The farm has the reputati
2 CONC on of being one of the finest in Fremont county and remains in the fami
2 CONC ly at this time (2001).
2 CONT
2 CONT *My aunt Jean Good reluctantly told me that the farm got its name becau
2 CONC se at one time there was considerable “hanky-panky” (her words) going o
2 CONC n among the hired men and some wives, and someone said the place was “j
2 CONC ust like Hollywood”.  She never referred to the farm by that name, alwa
2 CONC ys calling it “Knox” (for the nearby early village).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Also of interest, from the Wanamaker manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT [Joseph C. Brown's] determination of the north border of Missouri becam
2 CONC e the basis for an intense controversy that was ended only by decree of t
2 CONC he U.S. Supreme Court in 1849.  The result was moving the Iowa – Missou
2 CONC ri boundary a few miles to the south of Brown’s line.  This decree assu
2 CONC red the location of Hamburg to be in Iowa and not in Missouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There is more about Ben Jr. in the Wanamaker manuscript.
