1 NOTE John Wanamaker spells her name Martha Kaziah Patton, WFT Volume 22 Tree 1
2 CONC 690 has her name as Keziah Patton, knoxcolorado.com has Martha Keziah P
2 CONC atton.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From http://knoxcolorado.com/b493.html:
2 CONT
2 CONT obituary FREMONT COUNTY HERALD. May 15, 1908.
2 CONT
2 CONT ONE OF THE OLDEST RESIDENTS OF THE COUNTY GONE TO HER REWARD--The news o
2 CONC f the death of Mrs. Benjamin Reeves which occurred at Hamburg, Iowa, Fr
2 CONC iday, May, 8, 1908, at 11 a.m., cast a gloom over the entire community a
2 CONC nd many earnest inquiries were made regarding this noble woman as she w
2 CONC as well and most favorably known.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her sickness was of short duration and death resulted from heart failur
2 CONC e superinduced by dropsy. She desired to retire from the farm and seek r
2 CONC est in more quiet retreats so a fine home was purchased in Hamburg, but s
2 CONC he never visited her new home but once and that was just to superintend s
2 CONC ome matters of appointment about the convenience of the premises. Durin
2 CONC g her sickness she was at the splendid home of her daughter, Mrs. Frank D
2 CONC avis, where she had every care of medical science and that of relatives a
2 CONC nd friends, but her condition was of a malignant form and quite hopeles
2 CONC s from the first.
2 CONT
2 CONT Martha Patton was born in Howard county, Missour, November 21, 1836, an
2 CONC d was united to Benjamin Reeves July 11, 1855. To them six children wer
2 CONC e born, five of whom are living. Their names: Mrs. Frank Davis, Hamburg
2 CONC , Iowa; Mrs. Jessie Birkby and Mrs. George Borchers, of Nebraska City, N
2 CONC ebraska; B. P. and E. S. Reeves, of Washington township, Fremont county
2 CONC , Iowa.
2 CONT
2 CONT About thirty-six years ago, Mr. and Mrs. Reeves came to Fremont county a
2 CONC nd lived for a time on the Valley Farm near Payne Junction, the land be
2 CONC longing to M. U. Payne. Later Mr. Reeves bought a 500 acre tract of lan
2 CONC d one mile south of Knox which was their home for some thirty years. It w
2 CONC as one of the noted farms of that section in several ways and notably o
2 CONC n account of the hospitality of both Mr. and Mrs. Reeves which was char
2 CONC acteristic of their southern tendencies.
2 CONT
2 CONT Deceased was a member of the Methodist church South and very devoted to i
2 CONC ts teachings. The funeral was conducted by her pastor, Rev. J. S. Rooke
2 CONC r, in the presence of a large concourse of people who gave evidence of t
2 CONC heir friendship by assembling to pay the last sad rites to one whom the
2 CONC y so highly respected, although the drenching rain of last Monday foren
2 CONC oon caused the postponement of her burial until the afternoon. The name
2 CONC s of the pall bearers are: William Eaton, A Mann, Hiram Brown, Mack Sno
2 CONC dgrass, F. A. Jones and R. C. Campbell. Interment in the Hamburg cemete
2 CONC ry.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Posted on the Fremont co IA rootsweb.com site by Walter Farwell)
2 CONT
2 CONT obituary, THE FREMONT COUNTY SUN, Sidney, Fremont co Iowa, May 14, 1908
2 CONT
2 CONT "Mrs. Reeves Called Home"
2 CONT
2 CONT The SUN chronicles the death of Mrs. Reeves, beloved wife and companion o
2 CONC f Col. B. Reeves, her death occurring at the home of her daughter, Mrs. F
2 CONC rank Davis, at Hamburg, Iowa, Friday evening at 11 o'clock p.m. May 8th 1
2 CONC 908. Mrs. Reeves had for two years or more been a sufferer from organic h
2 CONC eart disease, which was the cause of her demise after a last sickness o
2 CONC f about two months duration. Col. Reeves had recently purchased a fine h
2 CONC ome in Hamburg in which himself and wife had planned to remove to and w
2 CONC ere moving there when she was taken so badly sick that she was taken to t
2 CONC he home of her daughter, Mrs. Davis. Col Reeves and wife have been resi
2 CONC dents of the county for over 40 years and were among the early pioneers
2 CONC , witnessing the growth and development from a wild unsettled state, an
2 CONC d have hosts of friends who feel with deep sorrow the loss of Mrs. Reev
2 CONC es. All who knew mother Reeves s[eal pf jear as a friend "indeed" to an
2 CONC y friends or sufferers in need, and who always gave larely of her stren
2 CONC th and love to her loved ones. The stricken husband, three daughters, M
2 CONC rs. Davis of Hamburg, Mrs. Borchers and Mrs. Birkby of Nebraska City, N
2 CONC ebraska, two sons, T. B. and B. P. residing hear Hamburg, survive to mo
2 CONC urn the loss of a beloved wife and loving mothers, also two sisters, Mr
2 CONC s. Givens and Mrs. Payne, old residents of the county mourn the loss of a b
2 CONC eloved sister, who with many life-time friends hope again to be reunite
2 CONC d in love, in that better land where there is no more death or partings
2 CONC , but where there is live everlasting and perfect peace and rest. The f
2 CONC uneral services were heald at the Davis residence in Hamburg on Monday, M
2 CONC ay 11, after which interment was made in the Hamburg cemetery. Many old f
2 CONC riends from Sidney desired to attend the funeral, but were prevented by t
2 CONC he storm.
