1 NOTE The Handbook of Texas Online (article on Stockton P. Donley):
2 CONT
2 CONT DONLEY, STOCKTON P. (1821-1871). Stockton P. Donley, attorney, was born i
2 CONC n Howard County, Missouri, on May 27, 1821. He attended Transylvania Un
2 CONC iversity in Lexington, Kentucky, and was admitted to the bar in that st
2 CONC ate before moving to Texas in 1846. He began his law practice at Clarks
2 CONC ville and in 1847 moved to Rusk, where he became the partner of James M
2 CONC . Anderson.qv Donley soon distinguished himself as a skilled criminal l
2 CONC awyer. In 1853 he was elected district attorney of the Sixth Judicial D
2 CONC istrict. His practical arguments and prodigious ability to unravel crim
2 CONC es were said to have been equal to those of such legendary lawyers as J
2 CONC ohn Randolph and Patrick Henry. In 1854 he married Judith Evans of Mars
2 CONC hall. Their son and only child, William S. Donley, later married Anna R
2 CONC eagan, a daughter of John H. Reagan,qv and became a prominent attorney.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1860 Donley moved his law office to Tyler. When the Civil Warqv brok
2 CONC e out the following year he enlisted as a private in Col. John Gregg'sq
2 CONC v Seventh Regiment of Texas Volunteers. Soon after his promotion to a l
2 CONC ieutenancy he was captured, along with the entire regiment, at the sieg
2 CONC e of Fort Donelson, Tennessee, in February 1862. Due to failing health a
2 CONC fter he was exchanged, he was assigned to post duty and continued in th
2 CONC at capacity until the war's end, after which he resumed his practice at T
2 CONC yler. He was elected to the Supreme Court of Texas in 1866 but removed f
2 CONC rom office by the Reconstructionqv military commandant on September 10, 1
2 CONC 867. He then became the law partner of Oran M. Roberts, and later of Jo
2 CONC hn L. Henry.qqv His wife died, and in 1867 Donley married Mrs. Emma Sla
2 CONC ughter, with whom he had a daughter. He died at Kaufman on February 17, 1
2 CONC 871, and was interred at Tyler. Donley County, in the Texas Panhandle, w
2 CONC as named for him.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT WFT 22 # 1690:
2 CONT
2 CONT Since his mother died when he was a baby he was brought up by his aunt, M
2 CONC argaret Donnelly Ewin.  In 1933 [sic] he went to Ky with his father. He r
2 CONC emained in KY until he moved to Rusk, Cherokee Co., TX in 1846. He beca
2 CONC me a noted lawyer and a man of means in Tyler, TX.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The middle name is from WFT 22 # 1690.  Other sources only had middle i
2 CONC nitial P.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The dates are all messed up in WFT 22 # 1690 and Bob Reeves.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He is mentioned in a letter (spelled as Stockden Donnelly) from Benjami
2 CONC n Harrison Reeves to Jeanette (Reeves) Leonard, dated Feb 1848, as bein
2 CONC g in Texas, within a day or two ride of Reuben A. Reeves (probably in R
2 CONC usk and Palestine, which are about 30 miles apart).  There are a lot of p
2 CONC arallels between Stockton Donley and Reuben Reeves:  They both went to t
2 CONC he same part of Texas near the same time in the 1840s.  They were both l
2 CONC awyers and judges.  They both joined the Confederacy in the Civil War.  T
2 CONC hey both served on the Texas Supreme Court, though not at the same time
2 CONC .  They were both removed from judicial positions during Reconstruction
2 CONC .
