1 NOTE From the Willis Long Reeves notes (obtained from the Trewolla Moskop Ha
2 CONC as Mansfield Tree web site):
2 CONT
2 CONT Emily Davidson married first, Norfleet Carr of Logan County who is list
2 CONC ed in the 1850 census as "Gentleman" whatever that meant in those days.  I
2 CONC t is to be assumed that he was wealthy and that he owned several slaves
2 CONC .  Brewer [i.e., Willis Brewer Reeves] mentions in one of his letters t
2 CONC hat "Ma" said that she would "give you a negro or two".  Priscilla Davi
2 CONC dson [the wife of Willis Brewer Reeves] must have visited her aunt Em a
2 CONC s we soon find that the son marries the niece after the father had marr
2 CONC ied the aunt.  There were no children to this union [between Willis and E
2 CONC mily].  Emily is buried in the Elkton cemetery beside John Littlejohn D
2 CONC avidson, Harriet Ann Davidson, and Harriet B. Davidson who was the seco
2 CONC nd wife of James H. Davidson.  She is the mother of Emily and the step g
2 CONC randmother of the other two.  In the "Letters to Priscilla, we have the w
2 CONC ill of Emily in which she leaves most of her modest possessions to Pris
2 CONC cilla and forgives a note owed her by Brewer.  The will is not witnesse
2 CONC d but is written in her own hand and addressed to Priscilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Continued in the notes for Willis Brewer Reeves.]
