1 _UID EF0E6EEE65FC89439641AA9AD6A6BD6943C3
1 CHAN
2 DATE 23 APR 2019
1 NOTE History of Todd County, Kentucky, ed. J. H. Battle, F. A. Battey Publis
2 CONC hing Co., 1884, p. 306:
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIS B. REEVES is a son of Willis L. Reeves and Margaret Edwards. The
2 CONC y had four children: Willis B., Haden E. (now in Texas) and two who die
2 CONC d in childhood.  Willis B. Reeves was born near Elkton, Ky., June 19, 1
2 CONC 835, and was reared and educated here, which has been his permanent hom
2 CONC e since. In early life he began the study of law with Hon. F. M. Bristo
2 CONC w and H. G. Petrie, was admitted to practice, which combined with agric
2 CONC ulture, has occupied his time.  He has served Todd County in the capaci
2 CONC ty of County Attorney, and two terms as Judge of the County Court.  Mr. R
2 CONC eeves was married in Russellville, Ky., in December, 1859, to Miss Pris
2 CONC cilla G. Davidson, daughter of Col. James W. Davidson.  She is a native o
2 CONC f Kentucky, born in 1836.  They have been bless with eight children: vi
2 CONC z:  Ellen T., who died in early childhood; Mary G., John D., Hattie G., W
2 CONC illis, B., Jr., Fannie, Beall E. and Anna McReynolds Reeves.  The famil
2 CONC y are member of the Cumberland Presbyterian Church of Elkton.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Willis Long Reeves notes (obtained from the Trewolla Moskop Ha
2 CONC as Mansfield Tree web site):
2 CONT
2 CONT Willis Brewer Reeves, second child of Willis Long Reeves and Margret He
2 CONC nry Reeves, lived to the ripe old age of 86, dying in Elkton in 1921.  I
2 CONC n the "Letters" we know that he started out in the "store" and in busin
2 CONC ess.  In one letter, Priscilla warns him not to change to the "law" but t
2 CONC o improve his store.  He studied law in offices in Elkton and /or Russe
2 CONC llville where at that time resided some of the great lawyers of the sta
2 CONC te.  Some of these included Ninian Edwards, Crittenden, Broadnax and Bi
2 CONC bb.  He practiced law in Elkton all his life after having begun in the o
2 CONC ffices of Hon. F.M.Bristow and Hon. H.G. Petrie.  He also engaged in fa
2 CONC rming.  Battle's History of Todd County says that Willis Brewer Reeves w
2 CONC as County Attorney and Judge of the County Court for two terms.  The wr
2 CONC ite-up of his death states that he was elected and served one term as C
2 CONC ounty Attorney.  From September 1870 until September 1876, he was Count
2 CONC y Judge and also served as Master Commissioner from November 1886 to Ma
2 CONC rch 1898.  He was one of Elkton's oldest and best known citizens and a m
2 CONC an of sterling character, kind of heart, and possessed of generous impu
2 CONC lses.  It can be truly said of him that he never forgot the hospitable w
2 CONC ays of the pioneer.  He was a faithful member of the Cumberland Presbyt
2 CONC erian Church and a member of the Independent Order of Odd Fellows.  He w
2 CONC as a student of the Bible and his children were trained in its teaching
2 CONC s.  Following dinner on Sunday, after the family had attended Sunday Sc
2 CONC hool and Church services, they went into the parlor and he read from th
2 CONC e Bible, prayed and talked to them and then they could go about their o
2 CONC ther activities, which to the young ladies of the family meant having t
2 CONC he young men of the town or the young men from Vanderbilt Training Scho
2 CONC ol call on them.  (V.T.S. was a "prep" school in Elkton.)  He had a kee
2 CONC n sense of humor and a delightful chuckle.  He had a second wife, Mrs. L
2 CONC aura Hancock, but she preceded him in death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Willis Brewer Reeves married Priscilla Guinn Davidson (1835-1895) on De
2 CONC cember 15, 1858.  [Continued in the notes for Priscilla Guinn Davidson.
2 CONC ]
