1 NOTE From the Willis Long Reeves notes (obtained from the Trewolla Moskop Ha
2 CONC as Mansfield Tree web site):
2 CONT
2 CONT Priscilla was the daughter of Col. James W. Davidson and Priscilla Guin
2 CONC n Jones of Russellville, Kentucky.  Col. Davidson served in the Kentuck
2 CONC y House of Representatives from Logan and possibly Simpson counties at d
2 CONC ifferent times.  He was a prominent member of the Bar in both Franklin a
2 CONC nd Russellville.  Col. Davidson was born on October 14, 1799 and died S
2 CONC eptember 20, 1848 in Greenville, Kentucky.  His wife was born Dec. 18, 1
2 CONC 801 and died Jan. 2, 1838.
2 CONT
2 CONT Col. Davidson remarried on January 14, 1841, this time to Mary C. Keene
2 CONC .  They had four children: Keene Davidson (1841-1844), Constant Sarah D
2 CONC avidson (1843-    ), Mary Ferman Davidson (1845-1848) and Willie Keene D
2 CONC avidson (1847-1848).  Connie or Constance is referred to in several let
2 CONC ters to Priscilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since our family is primarily interested in Priscilla Davidson Reeves b
2 CONC ecause she is the Grandmother of Willis Ward Reeves, Sarah Moore Dodd a
2 CONC nd Virginia Reeves Johnson and others, we will tell what we know of her f
2 CONC amily.  The oldest child of the Col. Davidson family was Richard Jones D
2 CONC avidson (1828-1851).  We know very little about him in his short life.  W
2 CONC e do know that he went to Louisville, but we do not know what he did th
2 CONC ere.  He writes Priscilla, his young sister, that he is unable to grant a s
2 CONC light request to help her, saying that maybe soon he will be "out of th
2 CONC e suds" and will be able to help her.  Evidently he never lived long en
2 CONC ough to settle down.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second child of the Col. Davidson's was John Littlejohn Davidson (1
2 CONC 830-1862).  He was named for the famous Circuit Rider, Rev. John W. Lit
2 CONC tlejohn, a Methodist minister.  Reverend Littlejohn had possession of t
2 CONC he Declaration of Independence and protected it from the British during t
2 CONC he War of 1812 and the burning of the Capital.  Rev. Littlejohn baptize
2 CONC d Richard, John and Frank [Don't have the rest.]
