1 _UID 62B1548EBF86BE44BA05E0887C3BD55F4E58
1 CHAN
2 DATE 10 APR 2019
1 NOTE "Dictionary of American Biography, Supplement 4: 1946-1950", American C
2 CONC ouncil of Learned Societies, 1974
2 CONT (obtained from http://0-www.galenet.com.albuq.cabq.gov:80/servlet/BioRC
2 CONC ):
2 CONT
2 CONT James Clark McReynolds
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: February 3, 1862 in Kentucky, United States
2 CONT Death: August 24, 1946
2 CONT Occupation: Supreme Court Justice
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHICAL ESSAY
2 CONT
2 CONT McReynolds, James Clark (Feb. 3, 1862 - Aug. 24, 1946), justice of the U
2 CONC nited States Supreme Court, was born in Elkton, Ky., a farming region a f
2 CONC ew miles north of the Tennessee border.  The second child and first son o
2 CONC f John Oliver McReynolds and Ellen (Reeves) McReynolds, he was descende
2 CONC d from Scots-Irish Presbyterians who had migrated to Pennsylvania in th
2 CONC e mid-eighteenth century and moved to Virginia before settling in Kentu
2 CONC cky, where in time they joined the Disciples of Christ.  His mother was a d
2 CONC evout, kindly, but dominating woman. His father, a graduate of Jefferso
2 CONC n Medical College in Philadelphia, was a physician.  A narrow autocrat, "
2 CONC Dr. John" was called "The Pope" because of his belief in his own infall
2 CONC ibility.
2 CONT
2 CONT After attending a private school operated by a cousin, young McReynolds m
2 CONC atriculated at Green River Academy in Elkton.  In 1879 he entered Vande
2 CONC rbilt, which granted him the B.S. degree with first honors in his class i
2 CONC n 1882.  Two years later he graduated from the law school of the Univer
2 CONC sity of Virginia; he was influenced there by John B. Minor, who emphasi
2 CONC zed fixed principles of law and the need to restrain government from in
2 CONC fringing on property rights.  Steeped in the ideas of the Old South, Mc
2 CONC Reynolds did not leave the region permanently until he was in his forti
2 CONC es.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following a brief period as secretary to Sen. Howell E. Jackson of Tenn
2 CONC essee, a conservative Democrat, McReynolds went to Nashville, where he d
2 CONC eveloped an extensive law practice and a profitable real estate busines
2 CONC s.  A low-tariff, sound-money, limited-government Cleveland Democrat, h
2 CONC e ran as a "Gold Democrat" nominee for Congress in 1896, but met defeat i
2 CONC n his only bid for elective office.  From 1900 to 1903 he taught at Van
2 CONC derbilt law school.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1903 President Theodore Roosevelt appointed McReynolds assistant to t
2 CONC he attorney general of the United States, an office in which he served d
2 CONC iligently until 1907, when he joined the New York law firm headed by Pa
2 CONC ul D. Cravath.  He returned to Washington in 1910 to take part in the a
2 CONC ntitrust prosecution of the American Tobacco Company, but left in anger t
2 CONC he following year when the attorney general approved a dissolution decr
2 CONC ee that McReynolds thought too favorable to the old "Tobacco Trust."  W
2 CONC hen an attorney for the trust accused him of favoring confiscation, McR
2 CONC eynolds replied: "Confiscation! What if it is? Since when has property i
2 CONC llegally and criminally acquired come to have any rights?" (Hendrick, p
2 CONC . 30).  Once again, he moved to New York City, this time to open his ow
2 CONC n law office.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1913, at the urging of Col. Edward M. House, who had been impressed b
2 CONC y McReynolds' reputation as an antitrust reformer, President Woodrow Wi
2 CONC lson appointed the Kentuckian attorney general of the United States. Mc
2 CONC Reynolds proved to be a zealous foe of the trusts. After winning a diss
2 CONC olution decree that ended the Union Pacific Railroad's control of the S
2 CONC outhern Pacific, he balked the American Telephone and Telegraph Company
2 CONC 's plan to monopolize all telephone and telegraph systems in the United S
2 CONC tates.  The difficult struggle over the New York, New Haven and Hartfor
2 CONC d Railroad Company, which resulted in a consent decree, brought McReyno
2 CONC lds censure as well as praise.  His campaigns against the trusts led so
2 CONC me to regard him as a radical; in fact, he was a tenacious conservative w
2 CONC ho regarded such prosecutions as a logical consequence of his faith in a c
2 CONC ompetitive society.
2 CONT
2 CONT In August 1914 Wilson elevated McReynolds to the Supreme Court.  The Se
2 CONC nate approved the appointment over the strenuous objections of George W
2 CONC . Norris and others.  By then McReynolds had become, as he was to remai
2 CONC n, a storm center of controversy, as much for his temperament as for hi
2 CONC s ideas.  As attorney general, he had antagonized senators who accused h
2 CONC im of maintaining a corps of spies who investigated federal judges to i
2 CONC nfluence their decisions, a charge he vigorously denied.  He also got o
2 CONC n poorly with other members of Wilson's cabinet; a feud with Secretary o
2 CONC f the Treasury William G. McAdoo reached such a point that communicatio
2 CONC ns between their departments had to be carried on through the White Hou
2 CONC se.  On the bench, McReynolds won a reputation for churlishness toward c
2 CONC ounsel and toward his fellow justices.  Chief Justice William Howard Ta
2 CONC ft described him as "one who seems to delight in making others uncomfor
2 CONC table.  He has a continual grouch."  He was especially rude to his Jewi
2 CONC sh colleagues.  On one occasion, when he refused to accompany the court o
2 CONC n a trip to Philadelphia, he explained to Taft: "I am not always to be f
2 CONC ound when there is a Hebrew abroad."
2 CONT
2 CONT While most of Washington thought him a crotchety, acerbic misanthrope, a s
2 CONC mall circle of friends found him gallant, courteous, sentimental, and f
2 CONC ond of children.  A man of few pleasures, he enjoyed duck hunting, golf
2 CONC , walking, and European travel.  Slender, erect, slightly over six feet t
2 CONC all, he had piercing steel-blue eyes, spoke with a high-pitched voice, a
2 CONC nd carried himself like a Roman senator. McReynolds never married.  Onl
2 CONC y after his death did it become known that he had resolved to remain tr
2 CONC ue to the memory of Miss Will Ella Pearson, who in 1885 had died sudden
2 CONC ly at the age of twenty-four.
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after joining the Supreme Court, McReynolds aligned himself with t
2 CONC he conservative wing.  He viewed the Constitution as an immutable body o
2 CONC f principles that should be interpreted chiefly as limitations on the e
2 CONC xercise of governmental power.  A believer in stare decisis, he apparen
2 CONC tly never wrote an opinion that reversed a judgment. McReynolds, who ra
2 CONC rely took a position that could be interpreted as favorable to the caus
2 CONC e of labor, formed part of the minority of four in Wilson v. New (243 U
2 CONC .S. 332 [1917]), which held the Adamson Eight-Hour Act unconstitutional
2 CONC , and he joined the majority of five in the landmark case of Adkins v. C
2 CONC hildren's Hospital (261 U.S. 525 [1923]), which invalidated a District o
2 CONC f Columbia minimum wage law. Despite his determination to curb governme
2 CONC nt, he was willing to sanction a limited range of regulation, especiall
2 CONC y when it aimed at promoting unrestrained competition. In the field of c
2 CONC ivil liberties, McReynolds wrote the opinions in Meyer v. Nebraska (262 U
2 CONC .S. 390 [1923]), which held void a Nebraska law forbidding instruction o
2 CONC f elementary school pupils in modern foreign languages, and Pierce v. S
2 CONC ociety of Sisters (268 U.S. 510 [1925]), which overturned an Oregon sta
2 CONC tute requiring pupils to attend public schools.  More typical of McReyn
2 CONC olds' views was his action in 1932 when he was one of two justices who o
2 CONC pposed granting new trials to the Scottsboro defendants. Rarely assigne
2 CONC d opinions in important cases, McReynolds in his long career on the ben
2 CONC ch averaged only nineteen opinions a year, mostly on minor questions in s
2 CONC uch fields as maritime law.  These opinions were generally brief and pu
2 CONC ngent.
2 CONT
2 CONT To a Cleveland Democrat like McReynolds, the New Deal was anathema. In e
2 CONC very crucial New Deal case, he voted against the administration, usuall
2 CONC y as a member of the conservative bloc of justices, which also included W
2 CONC illis Van Devanter, George Sutherland, and Pierce Butler. Speaking for t
2 CONC his minority of four, McReynolds early in 1935 delivered a blistering o
2 CONC ral dissent in the gold-clause cases:  "The Constitution as many of us h
2 CONC ave understood it, the Constitution that has meant so much, is gone. . . . H
2 CONC orrible dishonesty! . . . Shame and humiliation are upon us."  When Jus
2 CONC tice Owen Roberts joined the conservatives that spring, the anti-New De
2 CONC al bloc secured a majority that persisted in most cases through the end o
2 CONC f 1936.  In Ashwander v. Tennessee Valley Authority (297 U.S. 288 [1936
2 CONC ]), however, McReynolds was the lone dissenter when the Court sustained a T
2 CONC ennessee Valley Authority contract. McReynolds characterized President F
2 CONC ranklin D. Roosevelt as "a fool," "not quite sane," and "bad through an
2 CONC d through."  Roosevelt, for his part, found McReynolds obnoxious.  When i
2 CONC n 1937 the president submitted his scheme to "pack" the Supreme Court, h
2 CONC e took particular pleasure in the fact that it was based on a similar p
2 CONC roposal McReynolds had advanced when he was attorney general.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Roosevelt's court plan met defeat, the president won a substan
2 CONC tial victory when the Supreme Court in the spring of 1937 began to upho
2 CONC ld New Deal legislation and when the first of a series of resignations e
2 CONC nabled him to begin reconstituting its membership.  As the last survivo
2 CONC r on the Court of the conservative "Four Horsemen," McReynolds proteste
2 CONC d in vain against the "Constitutional Revolution" which saw the Court s
2 CONC anction an enormous range of governmental authority over the economy at t
2 CONC he same time that it safeguarded an ever-widening scope of civil libert
2 CONC ies.  From 1937 through 1941 he dissented 119 times; in his twenty-six y
2 CONC ears on the bench, he recorded 310 dissents, a record number.  Some bel
2 CONC ieved that McReynolds remained on the Court only to deny Roosevelt the o
2 CONC pportunity to name a successor.  After 1937 he refused to attend the pr
2 CONC esident's annual state dinner for the Supreme Court, and when the justi
2 CONC ces paid their traditional courtesy call on the president before the op
2 CONC ening of the October term in 1939, McReynolds did not appear.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two days after Roosevelt was inaugurated for a third term, McReynolds w
2 CONC rote out his resignation in two terse sentences.  He left the bench on F
2 CONC ebruary 1, two days before his seventy-eighth birthday.  He spent his f
2 CONC inal years quietly, attracting national attention only when during Worl
2 CONC d War II he "adopted" thirty-three British refugee children.  McReynold
2 CONC s died at the age of eighty-four at Walter Reed Hospital in Washington, w
2 CONC here he had been under treatment for "an acute exacerbation of a chroni
2 CONC c gastro-intestinal condition"; on the day before his death, he develop
2 CONC ed indications of bronchopneumonia and a failing heart.  He was buried i
2 CONC n the family plot at Glenwood Cemetery in Elkton, Ky.  After his death, i
2 CONC t was revealed that beneficiaries of the old bachelor's bequests includ
2 CONC ed a "lovely" lady, the "mother of my lively triplet girl friends," the K
2 CONC entucky Female Orphans School, and Centre College, "to promote instruct
2 CONC ion of girls in domestic affairs."  Born in the second year of the Civi
2 CONC l War, he died in the first year of the cold war, a man who long ago ha
2 CONC d outlived his times.
2 CONT
2 CONT -- William E. Leuchtenburg
