1 NOTE "The National Cyclopaedia of American Biography", James T. White & Co., 1
2 CONC 904, p. 87:
2 CONT
2 CONT LEONARD, Abiel, P. E. bishop, was born at Fayette, Mo., June 26, 1848, s
2 CONC on of Abiel and Jeanette (Reeves) Leonard, grandson of Capt. Nathaniel L
2 CONC eonard who served in the war of 1812, and with his small command was ca
2 CONC ptured by the British forces at Fort Niagara, great-grandson of Abiel L
2 CONC eonard, D.D., pastor of the Congregational Church at Woodstock, Conn., i
2 CONC n 1774, who was appointed by Washington chaplain of his army, and serve
2 CONC d in that capacity until his death, and a descendant of James Leonard, a n
2 CONC ative of England, who settled at Taunton, Mass., in 1652, and is suppos
2 CONC ed to have been the first to engage in the manufacture of iron in this c
2 CONC ountry.  His father (1797-1863), a native of Windsor, Vt., was one of t
2 CONC he most distinguished members of the Missouri bar, and served for a ter
2 CONC m upon the supreme bench.  The bishop was educated in the preparatory d
2 CONC epartment of Washington University, St. Louis, and was graduated at Dar
2 CONC tmouth College in 1870, and at the General Theological Seminary, New Yo
2 CONC rk, in 1873.  He was ordered deacon by Bishop Robertson of Missouri, an
2 CONC d returning to his native state, he took charge of Calvary Church, Seda
2 CONC lia, Mo.  On Nov. 4, 1873, he was ordained priest at Fayette, Mo., and a
2 CONC fter eight years of service in the diocese of Missouri, he became recto
2 CONC r of Trinity Church, Atchison, Kan., where he remained until his elevat
2 CONC ion to the episcopate in 1888.  While residing in Kansas he had a place u
2 CONC pon nearly every important committee in the diocese and was dean of the e
2 CONC astern convocation and deputy to the general convention.  He was consec
2 CONC rated bishop of the missionary district of Nevada and Utah, Jan. 25, 18
2 CONC 88, Bishop Vail of Kansas presiding.  In 1894 he received the degree of S
2 CONC .T.D. from the General Theological Seminary.  At the general convention o
2 CONC f 1895 his already large field of labor was increased by the addition o
2 CONC f the missionary district of western Colorado but at the general conven
2 CONC tion of 1898 the area of his jurisdiction was lessened, his title becom
2 CONC ing the bishop of Salt Lake.  Bishop Leonard was married Oct. 21, 1875, t
2 CONC o Flora Terry, daughter of Alexander H. Thompson of Sedalia, Mo., and h
2 CONC e has five children, Ada Cameron, Sallie Thompson, Robert Leverett, Dor
2 CONC othy, and Margaret.
