1 NOTE St. Joseph News Press, 15 Aug 1936 (obtained from http://boards.rootswe
2 CONC b.com/localities.northam.usa.states.missouri.counties.buchanan/1556/mb.
2 CONC ashx):
2 CONT
2 CONT DIES AT THE AGE OF 95, Mrs. Ada Leonard Darby was a Missouri Pioneer: P
2 CONC rominent for Many Years in St. Joseph's Social And Club Activities!
2 CONT
2 CONT The death of Mrs. Ada Leonard Darby last night at the age of ninety-fiv
2 CONC e years marked the passing of one of unusual importance in St. Joseph's c
2 CONC lub and social activities of one, whose life furnished a rare link with t
2 CONC he early history of Missouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs Darby's daughter, Ada Claire Darby, well-known as the author of chi
2 CONC ldren's historical books, found in her mother a rich store of reminisce
2 CONC nces of another day and this allied with research work, formed the back
2 CONC ground for her writings.  Miss Darby's first book "Pinafores and Pantal
2 CONC ettes" was the true story of Mrs. Darby's childhood on a pre-bellum pla
2 CONC ntation at Fayette, Mo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dies at Niece's Home:  Mrs. Darby died at 9:30 o'clock last evening at t
2 CONC he home of her niece, Mrs. R.A. Brown, 519 North Eighth Street, after t
2 CONC hree weeks illness.  Mrs. Darby and her two daughters, Mrs. George Dunb
2 CONC ar and Miss Darby, had been spending the summer with Mrs. Brown. Their h
2 CONC ome is at 2602 Jule street.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Darby's first husband, the Rt. Rev. Cicero Stephen Hawks, was the f
2 CONC irst Episcopal Bishop of St. Joseph and they had two children, Mary, de
2 CONC ceased, and Jean, who is Mrs. Dunbar.  They were married at Fayette and w
2 CONC ent to live in St. Louis, Bishop Hawks dying fie years later. His widow c
2 CONC ame to live in St. Joseph, where her sister, the late Mrs. J.D. Smith, r
2 CONC esided.  Ten years later she was married to Dr. Charles H. Darby, who h
2 CONC ad come from New York to St. Jospeh.  He was a president of the Missour
2 CONC i State Dental Association and died in 1910. He and Mrs. Darby lived in a h
2 CONC ouse near Twelfth and Francis streets for twenty-nine years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Runcie Club Charter Member:  Mrs. Darby was a charter member of the Run
2 CONC cie Club.  Its founder and president, the late Mrs. Constance Fauntlero
2 CONC y Runcie had been so active in the conduct of the club that it was fear
2 CONC ed it would disintegrate after her death.  But Mr. Darby, as its second p
2 CONC resident welded it into a permanent organization. She was its treasurer f
2 CONC or many year.  So keen was her sense of humor that even weekly financia
2 CONC l reports, ordinarily considered dull, became amusing when she read the
2 CONC m.
2 CONT
2 CONT Twenty years ago, Mrs. Darby suffered a broken hip, and because this cu
2 CONC rtailed her Runcie Club activities twelve of her friends organized the D
2 CONC rama Club, which met at her home every two weeks to read plays. The reg
2 CONC ular sessions were continued for eighteen years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her partial invalidism did not make her any the less a gracious hostess
2 CONC , nor did it lessen the charm evident to all who came in contact with h
2 CONC er.  She was never heard even by her family to utter one word of compla
2 CONC int concerning her hampering lameness, though she had been a person of u
2 CONC nusual activity.  She was always serene and cheerful. Mrs. Darby had a d
2 CONC eeply religious nature.  She had always been active in church work, esp
2 CONC ecially in the Dorcas Society of which she was once president.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Fayette:  Mrs. Darby was Daughter of Abiel and Jeanette Reeves L
2 CONC eonard, and her family was associated with Missouri's history for a cen
2 CONC tury.  Her father came from Vermont to Fayette in 1819 and he built, 10
2 CONC 0 years ago, the home where she was born, Oakwood.  Her her younger bro
2 CONC ther, N. William Leonard, still resides, and this summer there is an in
2 CONC fant in the home of the fifth generation.  Mrs. Darby's father was a ju
2 CONC dge of the supreme court of Missouri and his portrait was painted by th
2 CONC e famous Bingham.  He was a member of the convention that drew up Misso
2 CONC uri's constitution and he was lieutenant governor in 1826 [this sentenc
2 CONC e applies to Benjamin Reeves, not Abiel Leonard].  His strong Whig symp
2 CONC athies are said to have had their weight in keeping the stat in the Uni
2 CONC on during the Civil War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Judge Leonard was a stickler for the lawn and he was horrified when he f
2 CONC ound that his daughter, Ada, was violating a state law, according to a r
2 CONC eport in the town of Fayette.  She had been teaching his slaves to read a
2 CONC nd write.  It was against the law to teach them reading and writing.  S
2 CONC he didn't not [sic] know this and had organized a little school among t
2 CONC he Negro cabins on her father's plantation, but the little girl disband
2 CONC ed it when her father explained the law to her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral will be Monday:  Mrs Darby's maternal Grandfather, Benjamin Ree
2 CONC ves, surveyed the Santa Fe trail under President Monroe.  Letters were f
2 CONC ound in the attic at Oakwood from Alexander Hamilton and George Washing
2 CONC ton.  The older Abiel Leonard, Mrs Darby's Great-Grandfather, was for a t
2 CONC ime Washington's chaplain during The Revolutionary War.  In the family i
2 CONC s a Copley portrait of this President.  Mrs. Darby's funeral will be he
2 CONC ld Monday in Christ Episcopal Church.
1 BIRT
2 DATE 6 MAY 1841
2 PLAC Fayette, Howard Co., MO
