1 NOTE From Wikipedia (http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cicero_Stephens_Hawks):
2 CONT
2 CONT Cicero Stephens Hawks (May 26, 1812–April 19, 1868) was the first Episc
2 CONC opal bishop of Missouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hawks was born in New Bern, North Carolina. After an education at the U
2 CONC niversity of North Carolina, Hawks began to study the law, but abandone
2 CONC d it in favor of a clerical occupation. He was ordained deacon in 1834 a
2 CONC nd priest in 1836. His first parishes were in Saugerties and Buffalo, N
2 CONC ew York. His brothers, Francis Lister Hawks and William Nassau Hawks, w
2 CONC ere also Episcopal priests. Hawks married Ann Jones in 1835.
2 CONT
2 CONT He became rector of Christ Church, St. Louis, Missouri in 1844, in what w
2 CONC as then the Diocese of Missouri and Indiana. After the diocese was divi
2 CONC ded, Hawks became the bishop of Missouri. He was the 44th bishop in the E
2 CONC CUSA, and was consecrated on October 20, 1844 by Bishops Philander Chas
2 CONC e, Jackson Kemper, and Samuel Allen McCoskry. He received a D.D. from t
2 CONC he University of Missouri in 1847. During his service as bishop, he con
2 CONC tinued to be the rector of Christ Church, serving in both positions unt
2 CONC il 1854, when he relinquished the rectorship. During a cholera epidemic i
2 CONC n 1849, Hawks ministered to the sick of the city. He became ill in 1867
2 CONC , and asked Bishop Thomas H. Vail of Kansas to assist in his parish vis
2 CONC itations. Hawks died the following year, on April 19, 1868, and was bur
2 CONC ied at Bellefontaine Cemetery in St. Louis.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There is much information about his career and especially his tenure as b
2 CONC ishop in "The Episcopate of Bishop Hawks" by Charles F. Rehkopf (Bullet
2 CONC in of the Missouri Historical Society, Vol. 13, pp. 367-380, July 1957)
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In an e-mail from Sue Rehkopf of the Diocese of Missouri:
2 CONT
2 CONT The Rt. Rev. Cicero Stephens Hawks was born in New Berne, North Carolin
2 CONC a 5-26-1812, and died 4-19-1868 in St. Louis.  He is buried at Bellefou
2 CONC ntaine Cemetery in St. Louis along with his first wife, Ann Jones Hawks (
2 CONC died 7-1-1855) and their daughter Isabella, age 13 (born 1/4/1851 - die
2 CONC d 6-17-1864). Also buried in this lot is Abiel Leonard Hawks, his son w
2 CONC ith Ada Leonard (died 3-6-1870).
