1 NOTE St. Joseph News Press, 14 May 1910 (obtained from http://boards.rootswe
2 CONC b.com/localities.northam.usa.states.missouri.counties.buchanan/1555/mb.
2 CONC ashx):
2 CONT
2 CONT DR. C. H. DARBY DIES AFTER A BRIEF ILLNESS:
2 CONT
2 CONT He had been a  Practicing Dentist in St. Joseph for many years! PROMINE
2 CONC NT IN THIS STATE  Natives of New York State and Son of A Baptist clergy
2 CONC man - Was a member of many Dental Associations during life!
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the city's pioneer dentists was lost to St. Joseph today by the d
2 CONC eath at his home, 1205 Francis Street, of Dr. Charles Hammond Darby, af
2 CONC ter an illness of only a week.  He would have been sixty-seven years ol
2 CONC d July 3, next.  He practiced his profession in St. Joseph since 1866.  H
2 CONC e always had been a physician of high ethical standards and his one hob
2 CONC by was the indulgence of his taste for keeping in the advance guard of m
2 CONC odern dentistry, and introducing new modes of procedure.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first dental engine in Missouri was used by Doctor Darby, the first r
2 CONC ubber dam utilized in a patients mouth in Missouri, was in his office, a
2 CONC nd  nitrous oxide gas for extraction had its first Missouri use by him.  J
2 CONC enkins porcelain now in general use by the profession was demonstrated f
2 CONC or the first time in America by Doctor Darby at the thirty-first annive
2 CONC rsary of the Chicago Dental Society.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Odontographic Society of Kansas City, at a meeting Jan 8, 1909, ren
2 CONC dered Doctor Darby, one of the membership and unique testimonial by giv
2 CONC ing him a fine drinking set, cut glass, sliver encrusted, comprising a d
2 CONC ecanter and glasses.  There was no occasion for the presentation other t
2 CONC han the society's regard for a member, and Doctor Darby often referred t
2 CONC o the gift among his friends, as being one of his most prized possessio
2 CONC ns.  The Odontographic Society's exclusive membership includes the lead
2 CONC ing dental practitioners of Kansas City, St. Joseph, and Topeka.
2 CONT
2 CONT HIS DEATH A SURPRISE:  Those who saw the familiar figure of the pioneer d
2 CONC entist in St. Joseph will be surprised at the news of his death.  He wa
2 CONC s about his office as usual a week ago, but when he became ill, he sank r
2 CONC apidly.  His death, at 4:50 o'clock this morning, was due to a complica
2 CONC tion of diseases, including grip, pneumonia, and a constitutional kidne
2 CONC y trouble.  He is survived by his second wife, to whom he was married i
2 CONC n 1880.  She was Mrs. C.S. Hawx, widow of the first Episcopal bishop of M
2 CONC issouri, and daughter of Judge Abiel J. Leonard of Fayette, a former me
2 CONC mber of the state supreme court.   He is survived by the widow, a daugh
2 CONC ter, Miss Ada C. Darby, and two stepdaughters, Miss Mary Hawks & Mrs. G
2 CONC eorge A. Dunbar.  He has a brother in Philadelphia, Dr. Edwin T. Darby a
2 CONC nd a cousin, Dr. Frank B. Darby, also a dentist, in Elmira, N.Y.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Darby was born in Greene, Chenango County, N.Y. in 1844.  His fathe
2 CONC r, the Rev. Chauncey Darby was a Baptist minister, and his mother was a Q
2 CONC uaker.  He was educated at Courtland Academy, Homer, N.Y. and when the C
2 CONC ivil War began, he worked two years at the Springfield United States Ar
2 CONC mory, manufacturing Springfield rifles.  He studied dentistry a year wi
2 CONC th Dr. Ransome Walker, at Owego, N.Y, practiced a year in Fairfield, Io
2 CONC wa, and in June, 1866, he came to St. Joseph. His office was over Garli
2 CONC chs' pharmacy, at the southwest corner of Third and Felix streets, and D
2 CONC r. Robert Gunn shared it with him. While retaining his residence in St. J
2 CONC ospeh, Doctor Darby returned east and was graduated from the Pennsylvan
2 CONC ia Dental College, Philadelphia, with the class of 1867.  About the tim
2 CONC e he returned to St. Joseph, he removed his office to the southeast cor
2 CONC ner of Third and Francis streets, where the Postal Telegraph building j
2 CONC ust had been finished.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. J. M. Austin was Doctor Darby's partner for nearly twenty years, 18
2 CONC 77 to 1896 to be exact.  For the last seventeen or eighteen years, sinc
2 CONC e the completion of the German American National Bank Building, Doctor D
2 CONC arby's office was there.  He was one of the original tenants. In late y
2 CONC ears, Dr. J. G. Morgan has occupied a suite with him.  Doctor Darby had l
2 CONC ived for thirty-five years in the two story brick house where he died.  D
2 CONC octor Darby's connection with general dental affairs extended to member
2 CONC ship in the American Dental Association, the Missouri State Dental Asso
2 CONC ciation and The Odontographic Society of Kansas City. He was president o
2 CONC f the state society for a year.  the Odontographic Society presentation a y
2 CONC ear ago was typical of the favor with which his associates long had reg
2 CONC arded him.  Dr. C. L. Hungerford of Kansas City made the presentation s
2 CONC peech during which he said:
2 CONT
2 CONT " But, especially, I want to call your attention to the fact that we al
2 CONC so have other men at whose feet we have stooped for wisdom, men whose l
2 CONC ong lives have been devoted to the practice of their profession, who ha
2 CONC ve made progress, and with whom we have been associated for many, many y
2 CONC ears;  one, especially, among them, who is thought of and known in fami
2 CONC liar parlance as "The Old Aristocrat". When that term is mentioned, we t
2 CONC hink of Dr. Charles H. Darby of St. Joseph.  A synonym of all that is c
2 CONC omprised in the word "gentleman". All that a gentleman can be Synonymou
2 CONC s with all the good and gracious forces that flow through men's hearts a
2 CONC nd minds, and make their lives profitable to themselves and a benedicti
2 CONC on to these with whom they are associated".  It is announced that the f
2 CONC uneral  of Doctor Darby will be Monday at 10:30 A.M., from the residenc
2 CONC e.  Interment will be at Mount More Cemetery.
