1 NOTE Fayette Advertiser, 12 Oct 1937 (obtained from John Wanamaker):
2 CONT
2 CONT N.W. Leonard Died on Sunday At Age of 86
2 CONT
2 CONT Was Descendant of Pioneer Line Prominent in the County's History.
2 CONT
2 CONT FUNERAL IS TODAY
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. R.H. Ruff to Conduct Rites at the Leonard Home.
2 CONT
2 CONT N.W. Leonard, 86 years old, prominent farmer and landholder of Fayette, d
2 CONC ied Sunday morning at Lee Hospital.  Funeral services, conducted by Dr. R
2 CONC obert H. Ruff, will be held at Oakwood, the Leonard Home, this morning a
2 CONC t 10:00 o'clock.  Interment will be made at the City Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Leonard was born here on June 26, 1851.  He was a son of Judge Abie
2 CONC l Leonard and Mrs. Jeanette Reeves Leonard.  The deceased was educated a
2 CONC t Dartmouth College and returned to Howard County to become a leading c
2 CONC itizen of Fayette and the surrounding area.  He was married to his sist
2 CONC er-in-law, Mrs. Alice Gardenhire Leonard who preceded him in death.  Mr
2 CONC . Leonard was the step father of Mrs. R.P. Spencer but is survived by n
2 CONC o direct descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prominent Pioneer Line.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Leonard's father, Judge Abiel Leonard was a native of Vermont.  He c
2 CONC ame to Missouri via skiff down the Ohio River from Pittsburgh to St. Lo
2 CONC uis in 1819.  As a resident of Howard county he was elected to the Miss
2 CONC ouri Legislature in 1834 and later was appointed justice of the Missour
2 CONC i Supreme Court.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brothers and sisters of N.W. Leonard, all of whom preceded him in death w
2 CONC ere the late Mrs. Horace Everett of Council Bluffs, Iowa; the late Mrs. J
2 CONC .B. [sic] Smith and the late Mrs. Charles Darby, both of St. Joseph; th
2 CONC e late Mrs Odon Guitar of Columbia; the late Bishop Abiel Leonard of Sa
2 CONC lt Lake City, Utah; and the late Reeves Leonard.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bishop Leonard attained nationwide prominence in the Episcopal Church
2 CONT
2 CONT In Failing Health.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Leonard had been in failing health for some time.  He was taken to L
2 CONC ee Hospital here Thursday morning and pneumonia developed, resulting in h
2 CONC is death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Surviving him are Mrs. R.P. Spencer of Fayette, a stepdaughter, and her f
2 CONC amily; Miss Helen Spencer of St. Louis, Mrs. George Kuhn, of St. Louis, W
2 CONC illiam Spencer of New York City, Mrs. Kuhn's children, Jeanette and Geo
2 CONC rge Kuhn, also several nephews and nieces from other places.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The birth date in this obituary is different from the date in other sou
2 CONC rces.
