1 _UID 25222C46434FDA45938019FD9333122F9A95
1 CHAN
2 DATE 8 SEP 2019
1 NOTE St. Joseph News Press, 22 Dec 1953 (obtained from http://boards.rootswe
2 CONC b.com/localities.northam.usa.states.missouri.counties.buchanan/1557/mb.
2 CONC ashx):
2 CONT
2 CONT ADA CLAIRE DARBY, AUTHOR OF CHILDREN'S BOOKS, DIES (By Ada Lyon)
2 CONT
2 CONT Miss Ada Claire Darby, widely recognized as an author of children's boo
2 CONC ks, died this morning at 9, at Missouri Methodist Hospital after an ill
2 CONC ness of three months.  A member of a distinguished family, she was prom
2 CONC inent in social and club circles, in addition to pursuing a literary ca
2 CONC reer.  Two of her books were published this year, the last one "Pull Aw
2 CONC ay, Boatman" issued in September, being considered the best of a long s
2 CONC uccession of notable books for girls.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the past president of the Runcie Club, vice president of the St
2 CONC . Joseph Historical Society and custodian for life of the Book of Remem
2 CONC brance of Christ Episcopal Church.  She was an active member of St. Jos
2 CONC eph Historic Homes Foundation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in this city, Dec 31, 1883, to the late Dr. Charles Hammond Darby & A
2 CONC da Leonard Darby, Miss Darby attended the public Schools here and gradu
2 CONC ated in 1901 from Central High School, making her debut at a dance give
2 CONC n in her honor by her parents at the Benton Club in November 1803 [sic]
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT INTERESTED IN HISTORY:  Her parents were both of English descent, Docto
2 CONC r Darby's ancestors having come to this country in 1659.  One ancestor o
2 CONC f Miss Darby was an early governor of Massachusetts Bay Colony, another w
2 CONC as president of Harvard University and a third was a chaplain in the Co
2 CONC ntinental Army under Geroge Washington.  A Great-Grandfather was one of t
2 CONC hree commissioners named by President Monroe in 1823 to survey the Sant
2 CONC a Fe trail in its' Missouri Portions. From his correspondence she glean
2 CONC ed material for the book "On the Trail to Santa Fe".
2 CONT
2 CONT Records of her ancestry and the stories of early days recounted by her m
2 CONC other awakened Miss Darby's interest in Stat and national history. Her M
2 CONC other family home, Oakwood, at Fayette, MO, built in 1836 by Mrs. Darby
2 CONC 's father, Abiel Leonard, is still occupied by his descendants. Her mot
2 CONC her's childhood in the old mansion, constructed by slave labor from bri
2 CONC cks made on the place and native lumber, furnished the setting for Miss D
2 CONC arby's first book "Pinafores and Pantaletts".  It was published by Fred
2 CONC erick A Stokes Co in 1927 and was the first of a series of seven concer
2 CONC ned with the history of Missouri from 1800 through the Civil War, each b
2 CONC ook a decade apart of history.  They were written in a period of 14 yea
2 CONC rs, and included "Skip, Come -a-Lou"; "Hicory Goody", "Sometimes", "Jen
2 CONC ny Wren", "Gay Soeurette", "Peace Pipes at Portage" and "Look Away, Dix
2 CONC ieland".
2 CONT
2 CONT A BRILLIANT SPEAKER:  At the beginning of this month, her latest work, "
2 CONC Pull Away, Boatman" had a sale of 2493 copies, though it was off the pr
2 CONC esses only six weeks.  Her early books continue to sell well, especiall
2 CONC y "Skip Come - A-Lou", and "Columbine Susan".  The 25th anniversary of t
2 CONC he publication of "Skip-Come-A-Lou" was observed in November during boo
2 CONC k week at Central Public Library.
2 CONT
2 CONT Miss Darby's talent was as marked with the spoken as with the written w
2 CONC ord.  Wit and charm combined to make her a brilliant public speaker and h
2 CONC er book reviews were inimitable.  She gave several series of these revi
2 CONC ews.  For years she was engaged to present all the programs of a curren
2 CONC t events club.
2 CONT
2 CONT Much encouragement was given to her in her literary career by her mothe
2 CONC r and her half-sisters, the late Miss Mary Hawks and Mrs. George Dunbar
2 CONC .  But she attributed the demand for her books less in praise than to s
2 CONC harp criticism from a professional critic.  When she submitted an early m
2 CONC anuscript to him, his comment was "The trouble with you is that you do n
2 CONC ot know how to write a story".  She then determined in her own mind "I'
2 CONC ll show you".  She died, and leading publishers have vied for her work.  I
2 CONC n 1948, the Missouri Group Action Council of St. Louis honored her as o
2 CONC ne of the women of Achievement in this state.
2 CONT
2 CONT GRATEFUL TO LIBRARY:  Her books required a vast amount of research. Cen
2 CONC tral Public Library here to which she always expressed her indebtedness c
2 CONC ertified her to the Library of Congress which mailed rare old books for h
2 CONC er perusal.
2 CONT
2 CONT She had definite daily hours of work each morning, and rarely let anyth
2 CONC ing interfere with her regular writing.  Her summers were spent at Oakw
2 CONC ood in her childhood with many cousins.  In recent years she spent vaca
2 CONC tions at Wind-Inwoyen, the ranch owned at Evergreen Colo, by a cousin, M
2 CONC rs. Leonard Everett of Omaha.  She was literary editor of the News Pres
2 CONC s from 1916 to 1927.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Brave Venture", a story of the voyage of the Mayflower and of the firs
2 CONC t Thanksgiving Day was published in April of this year.  "Seally Allen" w
2 CONC ritten in 1929 was the story of a young girl in Columbia, MO, in that y
2 CONC ear.
2 CONT
2 CONT SERVICES THURSDAY:
2 CONT
2 CONT Miss Darby was the last of her immediate family, but there were unusual
2 CONC ly close ties between all the family connections of many cousins.  All h
2 CONC ave been members of the Episcopal Church. The first Episcopal bishop of M
2 CONC issouri, the Right Rev. Stephen Hawks, was the first husband of Miss Da
2 CONC rby's mother.  Her mother's brother, the late Right Rev. Abiel Leonard, w
2 CONC as the Bishop of the Episcopal Diocese of Utah.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of Miss Darby's death, first cousins were her nearest relat
2 CONC ives living.  Two of them are in this city, Mrs. R.A. Brown and Leonard G
2 CONC uitar.
2 CONT
2 CONT The body will be at the home of Mrs. Brown, 519 North 8th street until i
2 CONC t is taken to Christ Episcopal Church for services at 1:30 Thursday aft
2 CONC ernoon.  The rites will be conducted by the rector the Rev. James S. Al
2 CONC len. Interment will be in Mount Mora Cemetery, Heaton Bowman is in char
2 CONC ge of arrangements.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT According to John Wanamaker, she did not marry.
