1 _UID D1C3D5611A785C4A9E2B5D42AB881DF3ADA8
1 CHAN
2 DATE 8 MAY 2020
1 NOTE "Obituary Record of Graduates of Yale University Deceased during the Ac
2 CONC ademical Year ending in June, 1905" (obtained from http://mssa.library.
2 CONC yale.edu/obituary_record/1859_1924/1904-05.pdf):
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIS LONG REEVES, son of Willis Long Reeves, a farmer and for a numbe
2 CONC r of years Clerk of the Circuit and County Court of Todd County, Ky., w
2 CONC as born at Elkton in that county, September 6, 1841.  His mother's name w
2 CONC as Caroline (Wilson) Reeves.  He was prepared for college at Green Rive
2 CONC r Academy, Elkton, but did not join the class until the beginning of Ju
2 CONC nior year.
2 CONT
2 CONT After graduation he returned to his native place, and since then had li
2 CONC ved on a six hundred acre farm near there.  He was also part proprietor o
2 CONC f the flouring mill of Reeves & Bradshaw.  He was a democratic member o
2 CONC f the State Legislature for two terms (1871 to 1873), Attorney of the L
2 CONC ouisville & Nashville Railroad for many years following, Master Commiss
2 CONC ioner of Todd County Circuit Court from 1880 to 1886, Circuit Judge of t
2 CONC he Fifth Judicial District of Kentucky from September of the latter yea
2 CONC r to January, 1893, and held the same office in the Seventh Judicial Di
2 CONC strict for five years thereafter.  He was a member of the Cumberland Pr
2 CONC esbyterian Church, and Treasurer of the General Assembly of this church f
2 CONC or a number of years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Judge Reeves died at Dawson Springs, Hopkins County, Ky., May 25, 1904, i
2 CONC n the 63d year of his age.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married, March 1, 1877, Elizabeth Christine, daughter of Thornton We
2 CONC athers, a farmer residing near Cox's Creek, Nelson County, Ky., and had t
2 CONC wo daughters, the younger of whom graduated at Oxford College, O., in 1
2 CONC 903.  Mrs. Reeves died in August, 1903.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Another obituary (obtained from the Trewolla Moskop Haas Mansfield Tree w
2 CONC eb site):
2 CONT
2 CONT Judge Willis L. Reeves is dead.  This sad message was received at 8:30 o
2 CONC 'clock this morning by relatives in this city and it proved a shock to t
2 CONC he entire community.  Judge Reeves was at Dawson Springs where he passe
2 CONC d away this morning at 7 o'clock, dying suddenly.  His daughter, Carrie a
2 CONC nd relative, S. H. Wells, of this city, were fortunately with the decea
2 CONC sed in his dying hour.  The remains will reach this city tonight and th
2 CONC e funeral at 10:30 A.M. tomorrow; services from the residence, intermen
2 CONC t in Glenwood.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elkton had no more distinguished or cultured citizen than Judge Willis L
2 CONC . Reeves.  His reputation was not only established at home but his life a
2 CONC nd eminent services on the bench and in legal circles, had given him a n
2 CONC ational reputation.  He was the peer of any in legal lore, a student, a
2 CONC lways a genial companion, a kind husband and father and a good citizen.  H
2 CONC e had just rounded out his three score years of useful and hard work.
2 CONT
2 CONT Judge Reeves was the recipient of many honors at the hands of his peopl
2 CONC e, being representative, master commissioner and for twelve years circu
2 CONC it judge of this district where his great legal ability so often made i
2 CONC tself felt and appreciated.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in the suburbs of this city, where he always lived, owning o
2 CONC ne of the prettiest country homes near town.  Only a few months ago his c
2 CONC ompanion, whose maiden name was Lizzie Weathers, left him for the beyon
2 CONC d.  Two children were born to the pair and now survive them, to wit: Mr
2 CONC s. W. H. Rudy and Miss Caroline Reeves.
2 CONT
2 CONT Judge Reeves took an active interest in the affairs of state, and his c
2 CONC ounsels were sought all over his district and state.  He was master of g
2 CONC reat problems, a graduate of Yale, a Mason, modest and plain, kind and t
2 CONC rue.  In religion, he was a staunch Cumberland Presbyterian and had for m
2 CONC any years been a member of Dr. Gill's church.
2 CONT
2 CONT The state and district will join his home city in paying tribute to so e
2 CONC minent a citizen, lawyer, jurist, father, husband and friend.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Willis Long Reeves notes (obtained from the Trewolla Moskop Ha
2 CONC as Mansfield Tree web site):
2 CONT
2 CONT Judge Reeves was born on November 6, 1841 and died at Dawson Springs, K
2 CONC entucky on May 25, 1904.  He was Circuit Judge of his circuit for many y
2 CONC ears.  Judge Reeves ran for the Court of Appeals and was so confident o
2 CONC f election that it is said that he sat "on his front porch and rocked" n
2 CONC ot choosing to electioneer.  He was defeated by a man names [sic] Guffy a
2 CONC nd it is said that he was broken hearted and died soon after.  [One sen
2 CONC tence moved from here to notes for Willis Long Reeves Sr.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Judge Reeves had two daughters; Roxie Gray Reeves (1879-    ) and Carol
2 CONC ine Wilson Reeves.  Roxie Gray married William Rudy while Caroline Wils
2 CONC on never married.  The wife of Judge Reeves was Lizzie Weathers(October 1
2 CONC , 1855- August 7, 1903).
