2 CONT
2 CONT In the Civil War, he served in Company A (L?), 9th regiment of the Miss
2 CONC ouri State Militia Cavalry.  The Civil War pension package for him and O
2 CONC ttaway states that he died at home on 17 Sep 1865 of wounds received in a f
2 CONC ight with Bushwhackers, however he "was not in the US Service at the ti
2 CONC me he recd his wound which caused his death."  His discharge papers (da
2 CONC ted 26 Feb 1864) say that he was disabled because of "a disease of the e
2 CONC yes contracted about the first of July 1863, while in the line of his d
2 CONC uty".  It further states that he was "incapable of performing the dutie
2 CONC s of a soldier because of myopia and chronic ophthalmia.  There is grea
2 CONC t weakness and sensitiveness of the eyes & the impressions of light and c
2 CONC old.  The disability is permanent."  (Parts of this are hard to read an
2 CONC d I might not have them quite right.)  Other places say (if I understan
2 CONC d correctly) that he contracted diseases of the lungs and kidneys becau
2 CONC se of injuries sustained while he was in the service, and died from the e
2 CONC ffects of those diseases.  There are numerous letters and forms in the p
2 CONC ension package, many of them very hard to read, but it appears that the p
2 CONC ension request was rejected because Benjamin was in a state militia, no
2 CONC t federal service.
