1 NOTE Council Bluffs Nonpareil, 4 Jul 1916 (obtained from John Wanamaker):
2 CONT
2 CONT DEATH OF MRS. MARY L. EVERETT
2 CONT
2 CONT PROMINENT PIONEER WOMAN DIES SUDDENLY IN KANSAS CITY.
2 CONT
2 CONT LIVED HERE SIXTY YEARS
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Everett Had Been Resident of Council Bluffs Since 1854. -- Came He
2 CONC re From South With Her Slaves, Whom She Freed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Word was received here Monday of the death of Mrs. Mary Leonard Everett
2 CONC , widow of the late Horace Everett and resident of this city since 1854
2 CONC , which occurred at Kansas City, Mo., where she went a few weeks ago fo
2 CONC r a visit with her only daughter, Mrs. J. A. L. Waddell.  Mrs. Everett h
2 CONC ad been in failing health for several months, but was enjoying a visit w
2 CONC ith her grandchildren and great grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT She is survived by two sons, Leonard Everett of this city and Torrey Ev
2 CONC erett of Pasadena, Calif. and one daughter, Mrs. Waddell, at whose home s
2 CONC he died.  She also leaves five grandchildren and four great grandchildr
2 CONC en.  The grandchildren have always been frequent visitors at the Everet
2 CONC t home and were one and all devoted to Madame Everett, as she was calle
2 CONC d by her friends.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs Everett will be brought here for burial beside her husband in the f
2 CONC amily lot in Fairview cemetery.  The body will arrive Wednesday morning a
2 CONC nd arrangements will be made for the funeral as soon as word can be rec
2 CONC eived from the relatives.  Mr and Mrs Leonard Everett, who were in Colo
2 CONC rado, started at once for home upon receipt of the news.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs Everett was born in Fayette Mo. and was a daughter of Judge Abiel L
2 CONC eonard of the supreme court of Missouri.  Married at the age of 17, she l
2 CONC ived with her husband for several years in Alabama before they came to C
2 CONC ouncil Bluffs.  Coming here in 1854, they made their home at the Pacifi
2 CONC c house, then located on the present site of the Beno [?] store, until M
2 CONC r. Everett could build a home for his family, which stood on the lot no
2 CONC w owned by Leonard Everett's office.  Later they built the large house o
2 CONC n Second avenue, where Mrs. Everett has made her home for more than for
2 CONC ty years.
2 CONT
2 CONT A southern gentlewoman, she came north with her slaves but quickly real
2 CONC ized their freedom and mastered the situation of a frontier town, and n
2 CONC o house in this part of the state was more noted for its gracious hospi
2 CONC tality than was hers.  Through all the long years of her residence here s
2 CONC he was most loyal to the city of her adoption, and devoted to her famil
2 CONC y and her friends.  Although past the age allowed in frail human nature
2 CONC , she was always young in spirit and heart, progressed with the age, an
2 CONC d was greatly beloved by all who came in contact with her sweet womanly n
2 CONC ature.
2 CONT
2 CONT During all the years of her residence here she was actively identified w
2 CONC ith the work of St. Paul's church, of which she was a member.  For many y
2 CONC ears she served as a member of the library board, her husband having be
2 CONC en active in that work before her.  She was one of the founders of the f
2 CONC irst woman's club in Council Bluffs, the Every Thursday club, of which o
2 CONC nly three members now survive.  During the recent campaign for equal su
2 CONC ffrage no one was more interested than Madame Everett, who was keenly d
2 CONC esirous of once voting for president, remarking to a relative that if s
2 CONC he could do that she would feel that she had not lived in vain.
2 CONT
2 CONT While it was known that she was not well this summer, the news of her d
2 CONC eath came as a shock to her many friends, who deeply mourn the loss of a w
2 CONC oman who had so long been identified with the progress of the city.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The square brackets are in the version as received from John Wanamaker.
