2 CONT From the Wanamaker manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT Martha Leonard was born in Fayette, Mo. in the family’s “log cabin” in w
2 CONC hich they were living while waiting for completion of Oakwood. She marr
2 CONC ied Dr. Joseph D. Smith, a native of Virginia and a Fayette physician.  T
2 CONC he marriage was on 15 June 1854.  During the war Dr. Smith was made med
2 CONC ical director of the St. Joseph district and for some time served as su
2 CONC rgeon of the Ninth Cavalry, Missouri State Militia – the unit commanded b
2 CONC y General Odon Guitar, who married Martha Leonard’s sister Kate.  Reeve
2 CONC s Leonard, brother of Martha and Kate, was a Captain, then Major in the u
2 CONC nit. The Smiths moved to St. Joseph immediately after the war and remai
2 CONC ned there until their deaths.  They were a very prominent family in St. J
2 CONC oseph, both professionally and socially, where the doctor was a disting
2 CONC uished physician, educator and gifted linguist.  He died in 1900 at the a
2 CONC ge of 71.
