1 NOTE From the Howard County Advertiser, 17 Jan 1878 (obtained from John Wana
2 CONC maker):
2 CONT
2 CONT Major Leonard died at his mother’s residence near this city last Saturd
2 CONC ay morning at 1 o’clock, in the 40th year of his age, the deceased was a s
2 CONC on of the late Judge Abiel Leonard of this vicinity.  He was a man of u
2 CONC nusally fine literary attainments, having spent several years abroad in s
2 CONC tudy, after completing a full course at Dartmouth.  It is said that he w
2 CONC as the most thoroughly educated cultured man in letters of his age in t
2 CONC he state, having graduated at the Berlin University, Germany.  Maj. Leo
2 CONC nard returned to his home just at the outbreak of the war between the s
2 CONC tates, and at once espoused the union cause, accepting a captain’s comm
2 CONC ission in the ninth Missouri State Militia from which he was rapidly pr
2 CONC omoted to the rank of Major, which he held till the close of the war.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the cessation of hostilities he married Miss Alice, a daughter of th
2 CONC e late J.B. Gardenhire of Jefferson City and removed to Fort Scott, Kan
2 CONC sas, where he entered into the practice of law.
2 CONT
2 CONT On account of failing health he was soon compelled to abandon his profe
2 CONC ssion and returned to his old home, where he has since remained in feeb
2 CONC le health.  A few weeks since he was seized with a dropsical affection w
2 CONC hich resulted in death as above stated.  The funeral obsequies at the E
2 CONC piscopal church which were very solemn and impressive were witnessed by a l
2 CONC arge assemblage of the friends of the family, after which the remains w
2 CONC ere conveyed to the cemetery and deposited in the family lot.  The dece
2 CONC ased leaves a widow and two little children to mourn his loss.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT According to John Wanamaker, in the Civil War he served under General O
2 CONC don Guitar, who later married his sister Kate.
