2 CONC io:
2 CONT
2 CONT BENJAMIN FRANKLIN HUNTER
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin Franklin Hunter owned and cultivated 365 acres of land in Todd C
2 CONC ounty.  He was a very successful farmer, but lost his health and spent a s
2 CONC mall fortune visiting health resorts and consulting noted physicians wh
2 CONC o never seemed to agree as to his trouble.  It is thought that he died f
2 CONC rom ulcer of the stomach.
2 CONT
2 CONT In November of 1893 he bought a house and lot in Trenton, Kentucky, and m
2 CONC oved there, but continued to operate the farm until his death on 16 Nov
2 CONC ember, 1896.  The house where B.F. Hunter was born and raised was a lar
2 CONC ge two-story brick, and stood in Clarksville where the Goodrich Rubber P
2 CONC lant was later located.
2 CONT (From Aunt Mabel Brewer's notes and letters)
