1 NOTE "A Biographical History of Fremont and Mills Counties, Iowa", The Lewis P
2 CONC ublishing Company, Chicago, 1901, pp. 366–367:
2 CONT  
2 CONT B. P. REEVES.
2 CONT  
2 CONT One of the intelligent, enterprising and progressive farmers of Washing
2 CONC ton township, Fremont county, is B. P. Reeves, who has made his home in t
2 CONC his locality since 1872.  He was born in Howard county, Missouri, near F
2 CONC ayette, October 8, 1857, and is a son of Colonel Benjamin Reeves, a lea
2 CONC ding and influential citizen of Fremont county, who has been actively i
2 CONC dentified with the interests here for twenty-eight years.  He married M
2 CONC artha Patton, a lady of intelligence and culture, whose people were lon
2 CONC g residents of Howard county, Missouri.
2 CONT  
2 CONT B. P. Reeves is the eldest son of their family of five children. He was r
2 CONC eared in Howard county, Missouri, until fifteen years of age, when he c
2 CONC ame with his parents to Fremont county, where he has since remained.  H
2 CONC is youthful labors were practical, for he early became familiar with th
2 CONC e work of field and meadow and was taught the value of industry and hon
2 CONC esty in the affairs of life.  He pursued his education both in Missouri a
2 CONC nd in this state.  As a companion and helpmeet on life’s journey he cho
2 CONC se Miss Cora Hatten, a daughter of J. R. Hatten, one of the well-known p
2 CONC ioneer settlers of Fremont county, now living in Hamburg.  Unto Mr. and M
2 CONC rs. Reeves have been born two sons : Bruce H., who was born in June, 18
2 CONC 87, and Myron, whose birth occurred in June, 1889.
2 CONT  
2 CONT In 1898 Mr. Reeves purchased his present farm in Washington township, c
2 CONC omprising one hundred and twenty acres of very productive land, and the f
2 CONC ields yield to him a golden return for the care and labor he bestows up
2 CONC on them.  His home is a pleasant one, and the outbuildings upon the pla
2 CONC ce include a substantial barn, good sheds and cribs : the rich pasturel
2 CONC ands provide excellent feeding places for the stock through the summer a
2 CONC nd the fields give promise of abundant harvests, while the orchard yiel
2 CONC ds its fruits in the season.  Mr. Reeves carries on farming quite exten
2 CONC sively, for in addition to his own land he cultivates a portion of the C
2 CONC arpenter farm.  His political support is given to the Republican party a
2 CONC nd of its principles he is a zealous and earnest advocate.  He belongs t
2 CONC o Hamburg Lodge, No. 217, K. of P., and to Hamburg Lodge, 187, I. O. O. F
2 CONC .  His manner is genial and cordial and he enjoys the respect of all wi
2 CONC th whom he is associated.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Wanamaker manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin Patton (Pat) Reeves served two terms as Fremont Co. supervisor
2 CONC , and was a bridge builder for the county for many years.  He retired f
2 CONC rom the farm “around the bluff” and moved to Hamburg in 1913.  His obit
2 CONC uary stated that he was “a true friend of all and a man who was held in h
2 CONC igh respect by all”, and that “the community has lost a true pioneer, f
2 CONC or he came to Fremont County when a lad of sixteen.”  He died in 1939 a
2 CONC t the age of 81 years.  His wife Cora (Hatten) Reeves died in 1943.
