1 NOTE The Handbook of Texas Online (article on Reuben A. Reeves):
2 CONT
2 CONT REEVES, REUBEN A. (1821-1908).  Reuben A. Reeves, associate justice of t
2 CONC he Texas Supreme Court, was born on August 9, 1821, in Todd County, Ken
2 CONC tucky.  In 1846 he married Sarah Mills in Kentucky and moved with her t
2 CONC o Palestine, Anderson County, Texas, where he practiced law.  In 1850 h
2 CONC is brother-in-law, Roger Quarles Mills,qv moved into his household. At t
2 CONC his time the Reeves family had five slaves and real property valued at $
2 CONC 2,350.  In 1857 Reeves was elected district judge in Palestine.  While l
2 CONC iving in that city, he was instrumental in forming the Palestine school s
2 CONC ystem.  By 1860 Judge and Mrs. Reeves had six children. His real proper
2 CONC ty was valued at $10,000, and his personal property, which included thi
2 CONC rteen slaves, was valued at $18, 000.  Reeves recruited, organized, and b
2 CONC ecame captain of Company E, Terrell's Texas Cavalry (the Thirty-fourth T
2 CONC exas) on April 11, 1863. In August 1864 he was elected associate justic
2 CONC e of the Texas Supreme Court in the place of James H. Bell,qv whose ter
2 CONC m had expired.  Reeves resigned from the Confederate Army on September 2
2 CONC 8, 1864, to take the position.  He served from November 1, 1864, until t
2 CONC he end of the Civil War.qv  He was a member of the Constitutional Conve
2 CONC ntion of 1866.qv On June 25, 1866, he was elected district court judge o
2 CONC f District Nine. He was removed from office on November 30, 1867, as an "
2 CONC obstruction to Reconstruction" and was reappointed associate justice of t
2 CONC he Supreme Court of Texas by Governor Richard Cokeqv on January 30, 187
2 CONC 4. He served in that capacity until the court was reorganized on April 1
2 CONC 8, 1876.  Reeves returned to Palestine to practice law.  In 1880 three o
2 CONC f the Reeves children were living at home; one of his sons, William, la
2 CONC ter was elected district judge.  Reeves was appointed in the mid-1880s b
2 CONC y President Grover Cleveland to the Supreme Court of New Mexico Territo
2 CONC ry and served until 1889.  On the election of President Benjamin Harris
2 CONC on, Reeves moved to Dallas and retired from politics. He was a life-lon
2 CONC g Democrat, a Mason, and a member of the Dallas Bar Association.  He di
2 CONC ed in Dallas at the home of his daughter, Mrs. Jeff Word, on January 30
2 CONC , 1908, and was buried in Greenwood Cemetery, Dallas.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There is some speculation in putting Reuben in Otway Curry's family.  M
2 CONC y guess is based on the following:  The Henry H. Bollinger biography me
2 CONC ntions that his mother, Martha Reeves, was the sister of Judge Reeves, "
2 CONC an eminent jurist of Texas."  It is suggestive that one of the Bollinge
2 CONC r children was named Reuben.  It is also suggestive that one of the chi
2 CONC ldren was named Otway.  Not very solid evidence, but it makes me think t
2 CONC hat Martha and Reuben were children of Otway C. Reeves.  There is also a p
2 CONC osting to the Reeves-L mailing list by Joan Reeves, 17 Dec 1999 (http:/
2 CONC /archiver.rootsweb.com/th/read/REEVES/1999-12/0945481155) that says tha
2 CONC t Reuben was the son of Otway and Mary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bob Reeves lists Reuben as a son of Otway.  He indicates that Reuben an
2 CONC d Sarah had eight children, but has no names.  One daughter is shown as h
2 CONC aving married Jeff Ward.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Middle name of Anderson is from the Pendley Family Tree on Ancestry.com
2 CONC .  Other sources just had middle initial A.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There are questions about some of the children of Reuben and Sarah.  I'
2 CONC m pretty certain (from information in obituaries and elsewhere) that Fl
2 CONC orence Ellen, Mary T., William Quarles, and Ottway Curry were their chi
2 CONC ldren (which then adds to the confidence that Reuben was a son of the o
2 CONC lder Otway Curry Reeves).  It also looks good for Charles Mills to be a t
2 CONC win of Ottway Curry.  Lastly, the 1880 census lists Frank and R. H. as s
2 CONC ons.  Some family trees list Arthur, Bertram, and Benjamin as children i
2 CONC n this family, presumably based on the 1870 census, but that doesn't se
2 CONC em consistent with the other information.  More detail:
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1850 census (Anderson Co., TX) lists Reuben A. and Sarah Reeves wit
2 CONC h Mary T., age 2.  Also living with them was Roger Q. Mills, a brother o
2 CONC f Sarah, who later became well known in Texas.
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1860 census (Palestine, Anderson Co., TX) lists R. A. and Sarah Ree
2 CONC ves with Mary (age 11), C. M. (age 9), O. C. (age 9), William (age 7), a
2 CONC nd Frank (age 3).
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1870 census is questionable.  There is a listing that is hard to re
2 CONC ad (Jefferson, Marion County, TX), which Ancestry.com transcribes as Ru
2 CONC eben Ravas, with a note by an Ancestry user that it is really Rueben Re
2 CONC eves.  The family includes wife Sarah and Arthur (age 21), Charles (age 1
2 CONC 8), William (age 15), Frank (age 12), Bertram (age 10), and Benjamin (a
2 CONC ge 6).  The listing for Arthur doesn’t make sense since he was not list
2 CONC ed with the family in the previous censuses.  Perhaps he was a relative w
2 CONC ho was staying with them, or something like that.  Charles can fit with C
2 CONC .M. in the previous census, and William and Frank fit.  Maybe Ottway ha
2 CONC d already left home.  The listings for Bertram and Benjamin are doubtfu
2 CONC l because they do not appear with the family in the following (1880) ce
2 CONC nsus. They could have died or left home by 1880, or they could possibly b
2 CONC e visiting relatives.  It is also possible that this is simply not the r
2 CONC ight family.  Note that the biography of Reuben A. Reeves from the Hand
2 CONC book of Texas Online did not include information from the 1870 census, s
2 CONC o they must have had concerns about it as well.
2 CONT  
2 CONT The 1880 census (Palestine, Anderson County, TX) lists R.A. and Sarah R
2 CONC eeves with W.Q. (age 26), Frank (age 23), and R.H. (age 18).  W.Q., Fra
2 CONC nk, and R.H. are explicitly identified as sons (previous censuses did n
2 CONC ot include relationships), so R.H. should be included here, but why was
2 CONC n’t he with the family in 1870?  There doesn’t seem to be any reasonabl
2 CONC e way for R.H. to be Bertram or Benjamin unless the R was a mistake.  O
2 CONC nce again, though, it is also possible that the 1870 listing is the wro
2 CONC ng family.
