1 NOTE From a post to the Gathright mailing list by Carolyn Gathright on 23 Ma
2 CONC r 2016:
2 CONT
2 CONT Palestine, Tex., Aug. 14 – The funeral of Judge Willie Quarles Reeves w
2 CONC as largely attended.  The Palestine bar and court officers attended in a b
2 CONC ody.  Judge Reeves was 39 years, 5 months and 27 days old.  He has been a l
2 CONC eading attorney in this county for fifteen years and was elected judge f t
2 CONC he Third Judicial district in 1892.  In 1882-84 he was county attorney a
2 CONC nd in 1890 served in the city council as alderman from the First ward.  H
2 CONC is father, Judge Reuben Reeves, was district judge just after the war a
2 CONC nd during reconstruction was removed as an impediment thereto, being th
2 CONC e only reason assigned by the military government for his dismissal.  H
2 CONC e was afterward elected associate justice of the state supreme court an
2 CONC d served his term with great ability and distinction.  In 1884 he was a
2 CONC ppointed judge of the supreme court of the territory of New Mexico by P
2 CONC resident Cleveland.  In the death of Hon. W.Q. Reeves the bar of Texas h
2 CONC as lost one of its brightest members and Palestine and Anderson county a g
2 CONC ood citizen.  He was always honest, just, charitable and kind and posse
2 CONC ssed many traits of character so admirable that he was honored and belo
2 CONC ved by all who knew him.  He leaves a wife and two children and a host o
2 CONC f friends to mourn his loss.  Galveston Daily News, 15 August 1893, pag
2 CONC e 2
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT I am using the birth and death dates from his gravestone, which can be s
2 CONC een on Find A Grave (listing for William Quarles Reeves).  They give an a
2 CONC ge of 38 years rather than 39 years as in the above obituary.  I don't k
2 CONC now which one is really correct.
