1 NOTE "Biographical History of Pottawattamie County, Iowa", The Lewis Publish
2 CONC ing Company, 1891, pp. 519-521:
2 CONT
2 CONT HON. HORACE EVERETT, of Council Bluffs, Iowa, descended on both the pat
2 CONC ernal and maternal sides from the oldest and best known of the early Pi
2 CONC lgrim families of Massachusetts.  His mother, Mary Leverett, was a daug
2 CONC hter of William Leverett, of Windsor, Vermont.  The founder of the fami
2 CONC ly in America arrived in Boston in 1633, in the ship Griffin, from Engl
2 CONC and.  His son, Sir John Leverett, was Governor of Massachusetts from 16
2 CONC 73 to 1679.  His grandson was Rev. John Leverett, President of Harvard C
2 CONC ollege from 1707 to 1724.  Mr. Everett’s father, the Hon. Horace Everet
2 CONC t, of Windsor, Vermont, came from Foxborough, Massachusetts, and from t
2 CONC he same family as the great and polished orator, Edward Everett.  Richa
2 CONC rd Everett, the forefather, came from England in 1635, and the family f
2 CONC or many generations lived in Dedham and Waltham, Massachusetts.  Hon. H
2 CONC orace Everett was a prominent lawyer of his State and represented the W
2 CONC indsor district in Congress for fourteen years, where he established a r
2 CONC eputation that cannot be overrated and exerted an influence which will l
2 CONC ong be felt.  His labors in the cause of justice to the Indians are in t
2 CONC hemselves a monument to his memory.
2 CONT  
2 CONT Horace Everett, the subject of this sketch, was born at Windsor, Vermon
2 CONC t, February 8, 1819.  With the keen desire that the parents of that day h
2 CONC ad “that their children should be brought up to learning,” he was sent a
2 CONC t the early age of eight years to the Kimball Union Academy in Meriden, N
2 CONC ew Hampshire.  When fourteen years of age he entered the University of V
2 CONC ermont at Burlington, and graduated therefrom in 1837.  After graduatio
2 CONC n he spent two years in the study of law under his distinguished father
2 CONC , and was admitted to the bar.  In 1841 he decided to seek a wider fiel
2 CONC d for his talents, and settled in Gainesville, Alabama, where he practi
2 CONC ced in the courts of that State and Mississippi, for fifteen years.
2 CONT  
2 CONT In 1851 he was married to Mary, daughter of Judge Abiel Leonard of the S
2 CONC upreme Court of Missouri, also a descendant of Sir John Leverett.  In 1
2 CONC 855 Mr. Everett settled in Council Bluffs, where he resided at the time o
2 CONC f his death and with whose best interests he was ever identified.  He w
2 CONC as appointed by President Lincoln to the responsible position of Collec
2 CONC tor of Internal Revenue for the Fifth District of Iowa, embracing all s
2 CONC outhwestern Iowa.  He served one term as member of the city council, bu
2 CONC t so distasteful to him were the petty annoyances of the office he decl
2 CONC ined a re-election.  He was one of the trustees of the Fairview Cemeter
2 CONC y Association, had been its president ever since its organization, and t
2 CONC o his taste and wise forethought was due the selection of the romantic a
2 CONC nd beautiful site it now occupies.  His interest in the cause of educat
2 CONC ion was great.  He was twice elected by the Legislature as one of the R
2 CONC egents of the State University in Iowa City, and was regarded as one of t
2 CONC he most zealous and efficient members of that board.  Mr. Everett had a r
2 CONC emarkable literary ability, his private and business letters show a dec
2 CONC ided genius for composition.  It is to be hoped that a collection of hi
2 CONC s letters may be published.  He repeatedly interested himself to have t
2 CONC he Legislature abolish corporal punishment in the schools of the State, c
2 CONC onsidering the use of the rod on little children as barbarous and cruel
2 CONC .
2 CONT  
2 CONT Mr. Everett was a member of the Episcopal Church, and had been a member o
2 CONC f the vestry of St. Paul’s Church since the organization of the parish.  
2 CONC To his long continued benefactions during all the years of his residenc
2 CONC e in Council Bluffs the church owes much of its present success.  Alway
2 CONC s a liberal giver to every worthy object, he was sadly missed in the tw
2 CONC o institutions in which he took a special interest, the church and the p
2 CONC ublic library: of the latter he was really the founder, and was the pre
2 CONC sident of the board of trustees at the time of his death.  While Mr. Ev
2 CONC erett was a successful and practical man of affairs, yet his tastes and p
2 CONC leasures were those of a scholar.  He enjoyed poetry and literature, an
2 CONC d only those who knew him intimately realized how largely sentiment and i
2 CONC magination characterized his mind.  In every enterprise relating to the w
2 CONC elfare of Council Bluffs he was an active factor.  He was one of the me
2 CONC n to whose zeal the city was indebted for the location of the terminus o
2 CONC f the Union Pacific Railroad.  A life time Whig and Republican and stro
2 CONC ng Union man, always an advocate of the emancipation of the negro, he s
2 CONC acrificed large property interest in the South rather than remain where f
2 CONC ree speech was denied him.  Mr. Everett was a devoted lover of nature; h
2 CONC e never tired of the beautiful scenery of the Missouri River bluffs, an
2 CONC d the prairies bordering on them, and was never happier than when rusti
2 CONC cating on his large “Highland” farm of 4,000 acres near Council Bluffs, w
2 CONC here under his personal supervision were planted 100 acres of forest tr
2 CONC ees and forty acres of apple orchards.  He had the pleasure of gatherin
2 CONC g nuts from his walnut groves and of seeing his orchards red with apple
2 CONC s.  The trees planted by himself in front of his residence are now four f
2 CONC eet in diameter and seventy feet in height.  Mr. Everett retained to th
2 CONC e last a warm place in his heart for his birthplace, “delightful Windso
2 CONC r,” as he called it.  He never looked upon a Vermonter as a stranger, a
2 CONC nd never forgot the hills, brooks or mountains of his native State.  He w
2 CONC as not willing that the old homestead, situated on the banks of the bea
2 CONC utiful Connecticut River, should ever pass into the hands of strangers: s
2 CONC o early in life he purchased the interest of the other members of the f
2 CONC amily, and made provisions in his will for retaining this beloved old h
2 CONC ome in the family.  Mr. Everett’s great interest in everything that rel
2 CONC ated to the early history of the country and his zeal and enthusiasm in t
2 CONC he collection and preservation of manuscript and books led to his appoi
2 CONC ntment as trustee of the Iowa Historical Society.  Mr. Everett’s privat
2 CONC e library was a very large and well selected one.  His collection of ra
2 CONC re old books was complete and interesting.  He took great care of all i
2 CONC nteresting papers and manuscripts which came into his possession and ha
2 CONC s preserved many autograph letters of great value.
2 CONT  
2 CONT Mr. Everett was a man of unfailing courtesy, great dignity and beautifu
2 CONC l refinement, one of the most sympathetic of men, but of a retiring nat
2 CONC ure and wholly unambitious of public life, preferring the quiet comfort o
2 CONC f home and society of his family and books.  Exemplary in all the relat
2 CONC ions of private life, genial, benevolent and hospitable, he was tenderl
2 CONC y beloved by his family and friends, and honored by the esteem of all w
2 CONC ho knew him.  He was in failing health for a year previous to his death a
2 CONC nd was stricken with paralysis on the 30th day of September, rendered a
2 CONC lmost helpless thereby, but lived until the 3rd day of November, 1890, t
2 CONC enderly and assiduously ministered to by his devoted family until the e
2 CONC nd.  When the appointed time came he had passed to that world where the
2 CONC re shall be no more death, neither sorrow, nor crying nor any more pain
2 CONC .  He was buried in the family grounds in Fairview Cemetery, beneath th
2 CONC e beautiful elms he had so carefully planted and cared for.  Mr. Everet
2 CONC t left surviving him of his family his widow, Mrs. Mary L. Everett, his s
2 CONC ons, Leonard, Torrey and Edward, and his daughter Ada, wife of Prof. J. A
2 CONC . L. Waddell, of Kansas City, Missouri.
