1 NOTE "Biographical History of Pottawattamie County, Iowa", The Lewis Publish
2 CONC ing Company, 1891, pp. 615-616:
2 CONT
2 CONT LEONARD EVERETT, a lawyer, and one of the large land-owners of western I
2 CONC owa, was born in Gainesville, Alabama, June 27, 1853.  His parents, Hon
2 CONC . Horace Everett and Mary L. Everett, made their home in Council Bluffs i
2 CONC n the year 1855.  A sketch of the life of Hon. Horace Everett will be f
2 CONC ound in this volume.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his maternal grandfather's side, Mr. Leonard Everett is a direct des
2 CONC cendant of the Leonards of Taunton, Massachusetts, one of the prominent f
2 CONC amilies of the early settlement of New England, and in the Revolutionar
2 CONC y struggle.  His maternal grandmother was a daughter of Colonel Benjami
2 CONC n Reeves, of Howard County, Missouri.  Mr. Everett's early studies were i
2 CONC n the public schools of Council Bluffs.  In 1868 and 1869, for one year h
2 CONC e attended the Cheshire Academy, near New Haven, Connecticut, where he p
2 CONC repared for college.  For one year Mr. Everett was a student of Dartmou
2 CONC th College, New Hampshire.  For three years he studied at Cornell Unive
2 CONC rsity, Ithaca, New York, and graduated in the class of 1873.
2 CONT
2 CONT Returning home Mr. Everett read law in the office of his father and was a
2 CONC dmitted to the bar in 1876.  Mr. Everett has made a specialty of the st
2 CONC udy of titles and real-estate law.  His experience in the management of h
2 CONC is father's large landed estate has been of great practical benefit in h
2 CONC is chosen branch of the law.  Mr. Everett is now recognized as one of t
2 CONC he leading lawyers in western Iowa upon all matters appertaining to lan
2 CONC d or land titles.
2 CONT
2 CONT For many years Mr. Everett has taken an active interest in farming and t
2 CONC he cause of the farmers.  He has always been a sincere and earnest Repu
2 CONC blican, but has not hesitated to oppose and denounce the railroad wing o
2 CONC f the party.  In 1889, without solicitation on his part, and while he w
2 CONC as absent in Washington, Mr. Everett was elected, by a large majority, A
2 CONC lderman from the Fourth Ward.  Although the only Republican in the Coun
2 CONC cil he was elected President of the Board of Aldermen.  Mr. Everett dec
2 CONC lined a renomination.  He is now a member of the Board of Library Trust
2 CONC ees and President of the Fairview Cemetery Association.  He enjoys the c
2 CONC onfidence and respect of the public, and takes a zealous interest in th
2 CONC e welfare of the community.
