1 NOTE J.A.L. Waddell was an important civil/structural engineer, who was espe
2 CONC cially influential in the area of vertical-lift movable bridges.  A nic
2 CONC e recent biography was published in the Feb 2007 issue of Structure Mag
2 CONC azine, "John Alexander Low Waddell: Genius of moveable bridges" by Rich
2 CONC ard G. Weingardt (obtained from http://www.structuremag.org/article.asp
2 CONC x?articleID=469).  A few excerpts giving personal information follow:
2 CONT
2 CONT ...
2 CONT John Alexander Low was born in Port Hope, Ontario, Canada, on January 1
2 CONC 5, 1854.  His father, Robert Needham Waddell, was born in Newry, Irelan
2 CONC d and immigrated to Canada in 1831, when he was 16.  He served as a cap
2 CONC tain in the Canadian Militia in 1856 and was appointed high sheriff of t
2 CONC he United Counties of Northumberland and Durham in 1865.  John’s mother A
2 CONC ngeline Esther Jones was the daughter of Colonel William Jones, the she
2 CONC riff of NYC and a member of the state legislature.
2 CONT ...
2 CONT After studying at a business college in Toronto, John enrolled at Renss
2 CONC elaer Polytechnic Institute (RPI) in Troy, New York, where he graduated a
2 CONC s a civil engineer in 1875.
2 CONT ...
2 CONT Prior to sailing for Japan in 1882, Waddell married Ada Everett, the on
2 CONC ly daughter of Horace Everett, a prominent lawyer in Council Bluffs.  J
2 CONC ohn and Ada would have three children - a daughter and two sons.  One s
2 CONC on, Leonard, would become a well-known Kansas City lawyer/realtor and t
2 CONC he other, N. Everett, would become a civil engineer like his father - a
2 CONC nd eventually a partner in his firm.
2 CONT ...
2 CONT In 1917, naming his son Everett as a partner, Waddell opened a second o
2 CONC ffice in NYC with expectations of making it his headquarters.  Bridge b
2 CONC uilding in the U.S. began reviving right after World War I and both of t
2 CONC he firm’s offices did extremely well for three years, then tragedy sudd
2 CONC enly struck - Everett died unexpectedly in 1920.
2 CONT ...
2 CONT When the flamboyant J.A.L.Waddell died in 1938 in NYC at 84 - four year
2 CONC s after Ada, his devoted wife of 52 years - his company’s fortunes were o
2 CONC n the rise.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT An e-mail from a descendant, Melvin Ford, dated 5 Sep 2011, said the fo
2 CONC llowing:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Alexander Low Waddell married Ada Everett of Council Bluffs, Iowa, o
2 CONC n July 3, 1882.  They had three children, Needham Everett, Leonard, and E
2 CONC thel. His politics were Republican, and he enjoyed reading, hunting and f
2 CONC ishing.  On November15, 1937, two months short of his 84th birthday, Jo
2 CONC hn Waddell suffered a stroke.  Four months later,on March 3, 1938, he d
2 CONC ied in his apartment in the Hotel Earle, Washington Square, New York Ci
2 CONC ty.  His wife had died four years earlier.  He was sutvived by his son, L
2 CONC eonard; two brothers, Robert and Montgomery Waddell; one sister, Ethel H
2 CONC awks; four grandchildren and one great-grandchild.  The grandchildren w
2 CONC ere; Jack, the twins Ada and Fransis Waddell and my mother Ada Everett T
2 CONC albot, Great-grand daughter Yvonne Joy Ford.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Iowa Marriage Records 1880-1940" on Ancestry.com gives his marriage da
2 CONC te as July 13 rather than July 3.
