1 NOTE Adair Wilson family record gives William's first wife's name as Bowen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bob Reeves said that she was born in Howard Co., MO, but note that ther
2 CONC e was no Howard Co., MO, in 1811.  And I don't think her family moved t
2 CONC o what would become Missouri that early, either.  Her younger brother, S
2 CONC amuel, is said to have been born in Madison Co., KY.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Our Amazing Family web site says that she was born on 20 Jan 1811, b
2 CONC ut Find A Grave (listing for Martha Brown Reeves) says 30 Oct 1811.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Missouri Intelligencer, Fayette, MO, 20 Nov 1829, p. 3 (obtained from J
2 CONC ohn Wanamaker 1 Mar 2012):
2 CONT
2 CONT Departed this life, at her father's (Major James Brown) on Saturday the 1
2 CONC 4th inst. in the 18th year of her age, Mrs. MARTHA REEVES, consort of M
2 CONC r. William Reeves, after an acute and obstinate disease of congestive b
2 CONC illious fever, with local inflammation of the lungs and heart.  During t
2 CONC he whole period of her affliction, which was about twelve days, she per
2 CONC sisted in the assertion that she would die; and remonstrated with her a
2 CONC ttending physician, parents and relatives against taking medicine, and r
2 CONC equested to expire in peace.  Her mental faculties continued unimpaired
2 CONC , collected and entire, till near her dissolution -- she earnestly desi
2 CONC red the consolation and prayers of the pious: gave strong manifestation
2 CONC s of interest in the Lord, and his mercy: prayed, and sang to His prais
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT She wished her grand-father and the neighbors to be sent for, to see he
2 CONC r die -- and when she was told that she was dying, she exclaimed to her w
2 CONC eeping mother, "I told you, mother, I would die."  She was asked if she w
2 CONC as willing to die.  She replied, "O, if I cannot live, I am willing to d
2 CONC ie -- the Lord says come, and I must go."  A day or two previous to her u
2 CONC ntimely end, he [sic] was heard to lisp the following consolatory langu
2 CONC age of the inspired poet: "Here, Lord, I give myself away, 'tis all tha
2 CONC t I can do."  And while grasped by the cold hand of death, and when the v
2 CONC ital flame of mortality was almost extinguished, she uttered, in an aud
2 CONC ible and distinct tone of voice, with bloodless lips and a palsied tong
2 CONC ue, "I'm not ashamed to own my Lord, nor to defend his cause." &c -- An
2 CONC d shortly afterwards, with perfect resignation to the will of Him who h
2 CONC olds the issue of life, paid the melancholy debt of mortality.
2 CONT
2 CONT The death of this young and amiable woman, just as she was entering upo
2 CONC n the stage of a settled and domestic life, with the felicitous enjoyme
2 CONC nt of the connubial state in perspective before her; struck with dissap
2 CONC ointment the high and anxious expectations of her interested relations a
2 CONC nd friends: blasted the lively hopes of her fond parents, and is not on
2 CONC ly a sore and afflictive bereavement to the disconsolate and distressed h
2 CONC usband, but also to a circle of numerous relations and friends, by whom s
2 CONC he was esteemed a bright ornament, and a choice member of society.
2 CONT
2 CONT A FRIEND.
