1 NOTE Newspaper article, from the Wanamaker manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT THE HAMBURG REPORTER
2 CONT
2 CONT Hamburg , Iowa Thursday Morning June 3, 1943
2 CONT
2 CONT “Tebe” Reeves Died Sitting in His Car
2 CONT
2 CONT Tebe Reeves, life-long resident of this community, died in his car at h
2 CONC is Hollywood farm Friday afternoon.  He had been talking with his forem
2 CONC an, Reuben Monroe and seemed to be in his usual good health.  Reuben le
2 CONC ft the car to go about his work, heard a slight sound and looking aroun
2 CONC d saw Mr.Reeves slumped over the steering gear of his car.  Mr. Reeves h
2 CONC ad not been in the best of health, but the end was unexpected.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born and reared in this community Mr. Reeves had always followed farmin
2 CONC g and was a large operator, being very successful in his work.  He took a g
2 CONC reat delight in life and had many friends and could always be depended u
2 CONC pon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elijah Sebree Reeves, named after a close friend of his father’s, was b
2 CONC orn in the Valley Farm community on August 10, 1874 and died on May 28, 1
2 CONC 943, aged 68 years, 8 months, and 18 days.  He was united in marriage t
2 CONC o Miss Fannie Morgan at Sidney on December 18, 1902, and to this union f
2 CONC our children were born.  Morgan and Ben preceding their father in death
2 CONC .  He is survived by his wife, two daughters, Mrs. Roy Wanamaker, and M
2 CONC rs. Jack Good and two sister[s], Mrs. Frank Davis of this place and Mrs
2 CONC . George Borchers of Nebraska City, many other relatives and friends.
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services were held at the Mansfield Funeral Home Tuesday aftern
2 CONC oon, conducted by the Rev. Henry Teele, and assisted by the Rev. A.W. T
2 CONC andy and interment was in the Hamburg cemetery.
