1 NAME Odon /Guitar/
2 GIVN Odon
2 SURN Guitar
1 SEX M
1 _UID B35F8788C31CD940A76F41B9EB85276D2D77
1 CHAN
2 DATE 18 NOV 2017
1 NOTE "Boyle Genealogy.  John Boyle of Virginia and Kentucky", by John Boyle,
2 CONC <i> </i>Perrin & Smith Printing Co., St. Louis, MO, 1909, pp. 122-124 (
2 CONC obtained from http://www.archive.org/details/boylegenealogyjo00boyl):
2 CONT
2 CONT Odon Guitar, son of Emily Gordon [and John Guitar], was educated in Col
2 CONC umbia, and entering the State University at its opening session — in 18
2 CONC 42 — was graduated in 1846. War against Mexico being declared, without a
2 CONC waiting the college commencement, and leaving his graduating thesis to b
2 CONC e read by a classmate, he enlisted in Doniphan's First Missouri Volunte
2 CONC ers, participating in its entire unexampled triumphant march. His love o
2 CONC f adventure had previously led him to the plains for buffalo and other l
2 CONC arge game, where he also acquired some knowledge of Indian warfare.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Doniphan personally selected a detail of experienced hunters to su
2 CONC pply the column with fresh meat, Guitar was one of the chosen ten, and t
2 CONC hey performed their special service with such hardihood and success fro
2 CONC m the immense numbers of buffalo found from time to time along the rout
2 CONC e, as secured the high praise of their distinguished leader.
2 CONT
2 CONT Serving throughout the war, he read law with his uncle, John Boyle Gord
2 CONC on, and was admitted to the bar in 1848. The gold excitement in Califor
2 CONC nia allured him temporarily, but he soon returned to Columbia, where hi
2 CONC s character, attainments and manner gave him secure standing in a commu
2 CONC nity famous for eloquence and learning. As a Whig, Boone county sent hi
2 CONC m to the legislature in 1853, and again in 1857, where he served his co
2 CONC nstituency to its entire satisfaction, and with great personal credit. I
2 CONC n 1856 he was the Whig candidate for Attorney General. In 1864 he was e
2 CONC lected to Congress, but by the chicanery of his opponents was defrauded o
2 CONC f the seat. His open advocacy of the Union, and his intense nature rend
2 CONC ered him conspicuous in 1861.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the largest meetings ever held in Boone county was convened at t
2 CONC he court house in Columbia, pursuant to notice, May 6, 1861, to conside
2 CONC r the pending crisis. Among the resolutions offered, was one calling on t
2 CONC he Federal administration to recognize the southern confederacy, as a g
2 CONC overnment de facto; and another pledging the meeting, in case of war, t
2 CONC o stand by, and cooperate with the south. General Guitar spoke at lengt
2 CONC h against these resolutions, and cast the sole negative vote in opposit
2 CONC ion to their adoption. He denounced secession as the most damnable poli
2 CONC tical heresy ever invented by the brain of the vilest political demagog
2 CONC ue, and in concluding, said: He cared nothing for the fate of the resol
2 CONC ution, but hoped the friends of the Union would dare to do, and say, wh
2 CONC at they thought was right. For himself, he had not, and would not, occu
2 CONC py any equivocal position, when the liberties and destiny of his countr
2 CONC y were at stake. He was for his country, and should remain so. He pride
2 CONC d himself in her glory, and was willing, if need be, to participate in h
2 CONC er shame. "If," he said, "the glorious old ship of state shall be disma
2 CONC ntled by the storm, deserted by her crew, and left to flounder and sink a
2 CONC mid the waves of anarchy which will engulf her, it will be glory enough f
2 CONC or me to go down with the wreck."
2 CONT
2 CONT When the civil war began his determined stand was immediately recognize
2 CONC d. Governor Gamble commissioned him to raise a regiment of volunteers. H
2 CONC e commanded the Ninth Cavalry, Missouri State Milita, until the battle o
2 CONC f Moore's Mills, August 11, 1862, when he was appointed a brigadier gen
2 CONC eral for gallant conduct on the field. His chief service was in North M
2 CONC issouri, where the most daring and desperate guerilla forces were opera
2 CONC ting, and no one did more to expel them from that field than General Gu
2 CONC itar.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war he resumed his profession, attaining distinction both as a c
2 CONC ivil and criminal lawyer. For more than a third of a century he continu
2 CONC ed in active practice, and made an unusually brilliant record. He defen
2 CONC ded more than one hundred and forty homicides, only one of whom suffere
2 CONC d capitally.
2 CONT
2 CONT A distinguished judge who filled the circuit bench for many years thus w
2 CONC rote of him:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Hon. O. Guitar has a clear, analytical mind, that grasped at once the s
2 CONC trong points of the case, in which he was employed. He was not what may b
2 CONC e termed a case lawyer, while he had a proper regard for adjudged cases
2 CONC ; he thought for himself, and acted upon his convictions. Being a just m
2 CONC an himself, he was governed in his practice by the general principles o
2 CONC f justice and right, that apply to all the transactions of men, more th
2 CONC an by adjudged cases. In his practice he was earnest and unyielding if h
2 CONC e believed he was right. As a cross-examiner of witnesses, he had few i
2 CONC f any equals, and in his arguments before the court or jury he was logi
2 CONC cal and convincing; he was one of the ablest practitioners in Missouri.
2 CONC "
2 CONT
2 CONT And another writer said of him: "For years, Gen. Guitar was one of the m
2 CONC ost powerful criminal lawyers in Missouri. He has lived a singularly in
2 CONC tense and tempestuous life; he loves his friends, but with a candor tha
2 CONC t is delightful, shows no quarter to an enemy. He is one of those grim, d
2 CONC etermined men of blood and iron, who are fast passing away in this age o
2 CONC f commercialism. Vigorous, with every faculty alert and unimpaired, he i
2 CONC s one of the most interesting of men, not only in Missouri, but in the n
2 CONC ation."
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in Howard county, Missouri, December 26, 1865, Kate Leonard, b
2 CONC orn March 12, 1844, daughter of Abiel Leonard, one of the most eminent J
2 CONC udges of the Supreme Court of Missouri. They lived in Columbia.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT According to an article on the Guitar brothers from the Mid-Missouri Ci
2 CONC vil War Round Table (http://www.mmcwrt.org/2002/BkgrndGuitar.htm), Odon
2 CONC 's brother, David Guitar, was a captain in the Confederate army, and su
2 CONC pposedly once said "A Confederate Captain always outranks a Union Gener
2 CONC al."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Several sources give his birthday as August 31; Boyle Genealogy has Aug
2 CONC ust 30.
