1 NOTE Biographical Directory of the United States Congress
2 CONT (http://bioguide.congress.gov/scripts/biodisplay.pl?index=E000265):
2 CONT
2 CONT EVERETT, Horace, a Representative from Vermont; born in Foxboro, Mass., J
2 CONC uly 17, 1779; was graduated from Brown University, Providence, R.I., in 1
2 CONC 797; studied law; was admitted to the bar in 1801 and commenced practic
2 CONC e in Windsor, Vt.; prosecuting attorney for Windsor County 1813-1818; m
2 CONC ember of the State house of representatives in 1819, 1820, 1822, 1824, a
2 CONC nd again in 1834; delegate to the State constitutional convention in 18
2 CONC 28; elected to the Twenty-first, Twenty-second and Twenty-third Congres
2 CONC ses, and reelected as a Whig to the Twenty-fourth through Twenty-sevent
2 CONC h Congresses (March 4, 1829-March 3, 1843); died in Windsor, Vt., Janua
2 CONC ry 30, 1851; interment in Old South Burying Ground.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Leverett Memorial" by Charles Edward Leverett; Crosby, Nichols & C
2 CONC o., 1856, p. 158 (obtained from Google Books):
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary,(34) b. 1792, m. 1811, Hon. Horace Everett, lawyer at Windsor, and m
2 CONC ember of Congress
2 CONT ...
2 CONT Hon. Horace Everett, M. C., d. 1851, aged 72.  "He was for 14 years a r
2 CONC epresentative in the lower house of the National Legislature.  During t
2 CONC his time he established a reputation that cannot be overrated, and exer
2 CONC ted an influence, the effects of which will be long felt.  His labors t
2 CONC o cause justice to be done to the Indians, are of themselves a monument t
2 CONC o his memory."
