2 DATE 11 APR 2019
1 NOTE Scarsdale Inquirer, 4 May 1945 (obtained from https://news.hrvh.org/ver
2 CONC idian/cgi-bin/senylrc?a=d&d=scarsdaleinquire19450504.2.78):
2 CONT
2 CONT General Abiel Leonard Smith, Soldier of the United States
2 CONT
2 CONT By Grace Pomeroy Fearon
2 CONT
2 CONT Forty years of our military history and dim intimations of a past and g
2 CONC lamorous age, tread the streets of our village daily in the trim figure o
2 CONC f General Abiel Leonard Smith.
2 CONT
2 CONT The General, who will celebrate his eighty-eighth birthday this July, h
2 CONC as still the orderly habits of his life profession.  He emerges, at jus
2 CONC t about the same time each day, from his home on Circle Road where for t
2 CONC he last five years he has lived with his daughter, Mrs. Mason Shoup, an
2 CONC d her family and erect, with disciplined gait, sets forth on his aftern
2 CONC oon constitutional.
2 CONT
2 CONT His greatest hobby, while in Scarsdale, is walking, and aside from conc
2 CONC ern for his grandsons in the service, his greatest worry is that, altho
2 CONC ugh he believes himself past the age for active service, his neighbors m
2 CONC ay feel that he is a “slacker.”  Week-ends and the summer months, he sp
2 CONC ends at his three hundred acre farm in Carmel where, since his retireme
2 CONC nt, the inveterate cavalry officer has kept his horses and where his ha
2 CONC ppiest hours have been spent teaching his grandchildren to ride in his y
2 CONC outh his father taught him.
2 CONT
2 CONT General Smith, to quote from the fine print of an old newspaper, “is th
2 CONC e son of Dr. and Mrs. J. D. Smith of Saint Joseph, Missouri, and the gr
2 CONC andson of Judge Abiel Leonard, one of the ablest and purest men who eve
2 CONC r sat upon the supreme court bench in Missouri, and the nephew of Major R
2 CONC eeves Leonard, as gallant a soldier as ever went forth for the preserva
2 CONC tion of the union.”  From other clippings, we learn that he is also the n
2 CONC ephew of Bishop Abiel Leonard, then of the Episcopal diocese of Utah an
2 CONC d Nevada.  Further pursuit into the past reveals that he is a descendan
2 CONC t of the Reverend Abiel Leonard, the famous “fighting chaplain” of Gene
2 CONC ral Washington’s revolutionary army.
2 CONT
2 CONT West Pointer
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1874, General Smith received his appointment to West Point from Judg
2 CONC e I. C. Parker, then congressman from the Saint Joseph district.
2 CONT
2 CONT “The wisdom exhibited in the selection was apparent to all,” applauds a t
2 CONC ime-yellowed clipping in the family scrap book.  “The boy inherited the p
2 CONC ride, the honor, the ambition of a noble ancestry, and his aim, as a bo
2 CONC y and man has been to prove himself worthy of the stock from which he c
2 CONC ame.”
2 CONT
2 CONT To be sure, this earlier confrere of the present reporter was getting a h
2 CONC itch from hindsight when he wrote that article.  “The boy” had already b
2 CONC een graduated from the Military Academy, which he had entered with the a
2 CONC ssistance—not to say connivance—of an examining officer who knew when t
2 CONC o be a little vague about birthdays, before he had reached the required a
2 CONC ge of seventeen.  Th[e] youngest boy, therefore, in a class that number
2 CONC ed 168 to begin with, he had learned to do “what was required” and had b
2 CONC een graduated fortieth of the forty who finally ran the course.  Moreov
2 CONC er, he had served eleven years in the Cavalry with the honor and ambiti
2 CONC on so approvingly described.
2 CONT
2 CONT Indian Scout
2 CONT
2 CONT “. . . served as subaltern,” continues the fine print of the old newspa
2 CONC per, “and in command of ‘B’ troop, Fourth Cavalry, in scouting against C
2 CONC heyennes and Coma[n]ches in Indian Territory, Kansas, and Texas, until D
2 CONC ecember, 1880. * * * Served with ‘B’ troop, Fourth Cavalry, and as batt
2 CONC alion adjutant in the removal of the Utes from Uncomphagre, in Colorado
2 CONC , to the White River agency, and in the operations against Apaches in A
2 CONC rizona in 1881. * * * Served with troop in the field in south New Mexic
2 CONC o after renegade Apaches in 1882. * * * Served in command of his troop i
2 CONC n the field and in post in 1883, until November 15.  On leave of absenc
2 CONC e for two months from that date. * * * Promoted to First Lieutenant Jun
2 CONC e 30, 1883. * * * Served in command of ‘D’ troop, Fourth Cavalry, at th
2 CONC e Me[s]calero Indian agency from January 2 to September 5, 1884. * * * S
2 CONC erved in command of ‘B’ troop, Fourth Cavalry from September to October 1
2 CONC 884. * * * Then served at Jefferson Barracks until April 1886. * * * He v
2 CONC olunteered to give up his detail at the Barracks when the Apaches in Ar
2 CONC izona broke out in 1885, but was informed that he could not be spared.
2 CONT
2 CONT “Hostilities continuing, however, he was relieved in 1886. * * * Served i
2 CONC n command of ‘B’ troop, in the field in Arizona until June 27, 1886, wh
2 CONC en at his own request (and in spite of illness) he was allowed to join ‘
2 CONC B’ troop, Fourth Cavalry under Captain H. W. Lawton who was operating a
2 CONC gainst Apaches under Chief Geronimo near Nacon, New Mexico. * * * Serve
2 CONC d under Lawton until the surrender of the Indians to that gallant offic
2 CONC er, and afterwards escorted the renegade Apaches to San Antonio, Texas.
2 CONC ”
2 CONT
2 CONT For his part in that wilderness fighting against the wily Geronimo, the y
2 CONC oung Lieutenant was breveted Captain in February, 1890.
2 CONT
2 CONT Geronimo Surrenders
2 CONT
2 CONT It was a good story in 1896 when the Kansas City paper of Sunday, Octob
2 CONC er 18, carried it in full under the heading, “Geronimo Surrenders” and t
2 CONC he subhead, “Captain A. L. Smith, USA, Tells of the Campaign Which Resu
2 CONC lted in the Wily Old Apache Chief’s Suppression.”
2 CONT
2 CONT The story is still good, if the General can be persuaded to tell it—the s
2 CONC tory of that dogged pursuit by the determined young Lieutenant serving u
2 CONC nder his good friend Captain Lawton, of the most cunning of the Indian M
2 CONC edicine Men.  For Geronimo was not a warrior-chief, explains the Genera
2 CONC l.  Natchez was the Chief, a bewildered victim of Geronimo’s vicious sc
2 CONC heming.  There was the ride over strange wild trails in the black of ni
2 CONC ght, to make the connection that would insure the troop’s supply line, w
2 CONC hen the Lieutenant, walking for a while to rest his horse, would be dri
2 CONC ven to the comparative safety of its back again to escape the rattlers h
2 CONC e heard signalling in the brush.
2 CONT
2 CONT In June, 1890, Lieutenant Smith married Miss Florence Compton, daughter o
2 CONC f Colonel Charles E. Compton, “one of the best and bravest soldiers in t
2 CONC he United States service,” says another of the clippings in the Smith s
2 CONC crap book.
2 CONT
2 CONT Headed, “A Military Wedding,” that article in the Walla Walla (Washingt
2 CONC on) Statesman, carried the following subhead: “Bravery and Beauty Joine
2 CONC d in Holy Wedlock.  Beautiful Flower Decorations and a Fashionable Asse
2 CONC mblage.”
2 CONT
2 CONT A gem of reporting, the article described the wedding in astonishing de
2 CONC tail and once more set forth the exploits of the groom in the Indian up
2 CONC risings, and revealed that the young couple’s wedding trip would be com
2 CONC bined with the Lieutenant’s assignment to travel in Germany, France, En
2 CONC gland and Italy for the purpose of studying military science.  The comb
2 CONC ination worked very well indeed, and the young couple remained in Franc
2 CONC e until their first son was born.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Philippines
2 CONT
2 CONT But the Lieutenant’s career had only begun.  He was made Captain of Cav
2 CONC alry in May, 1892 and Captain of Commissary in June of the same year.  O
2 CONC n the eleventh of March, 1898, he became Major of Commissary (Spanish W
2 CONC ar) and Lieutenant Colonel in 1901.  In 1905 he was a Colonel of Commis
2 CONC sary serving in the Philippines.
2 CONT
2 CONT At that time, the General tells of being sent up into the Manchuoko ter
2 CONC ritory incognito, to “observe” the goings-on of the Japs there.  He tra
2 CONC veled as an American merchant with his two sons, driving a cart, inquir
2 CONC ing at strategic moments for certain produce of the country.  He got in
2 CONC to trouble with the Japs, however, quite frequently when his poking abo
2 CONC ut became a little too assiduous, and each time that happened, he found h
2 CONC imself presenting his faked credentials to the same little Jap officer.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jap never seemed to doubt the “merchant’s” word, but the inevitable h
2 CONC appened, and he was finally requested to leave the country.  Indeed—he w
2 CONC as escorted to the ship by the same little Jap officer.  The Jap was co
2 CONC urteous in his farewells, and just as he was about to go ashore, he cli
2 CONC cked his heels, drew himself up in a smart military salute and said, “G
2 CONC oodbye, Colonel.”
2 CONT
2 CONT “I never fooled him for a moment,” chuckled the General.  “And I made a r
2 CONC eport to headquarters saying that it was useless to send a white man on a j
2 CONC ob of that sort.”
2 CONT
2 CONT It was while he was stationed in the Philippines that the General made o
2 CONC ne of his most acceptable contributions to the life of the armies.  He c
2 CONC onceived the idea of buying fresh food for the Commissary and traveled t
2 CONC o Australia to make the necessary arrangements for the shipment of fres
2 CONC h meat and fruits and even vegetables.  That was the first time Commiss
2 CONC ary officers had thought of supplies except in terms of tin cans, says t
2 CONC he General.
2 CONT
2 CONT Back to Carmel
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 21, 1916, he became a Brigadier General in the Quartermast
2 CONC er Corps, serving until January 3, 1918, when at his own request he was r
2 CONC etired after more than forty years of service in five different conflic
2 CONC ts—for he was also in Mexico with General Pershing just before the firs
2 CONC t World War.  His next “station” was his own farm in Carmel where it ha
2 CONC s been his pleasure and his pride to teach each of his grandchildren to r
2 CONC ide a horse as a horse should be ridden.
2 CONT
2 CONT The reporter was interested to find among the clippings in the family s
2 CONC crap book, a long, and in the fashion of the times, flowery account of t
2 CONC he wedding of the General’s sister, Miss Medora Smith, and Wynkoop Kier
2 CONC sted.  Their son, Wynkoop Kiersted, and his family, live in Scarsdale o
2 CONC n Westminster Road.
2 CONT
2 CONT The General Smiths had four children.  The oldest son was A. Leonard Sm
2 CONC ith, Jr., whose widow lives on Rodney Road.  Dorothy, now Mrs. Mason Sh
2 CONC oup, lives on Circle Road.  Lieutenant Colonel Charles Compton Smith, U
2 CONC SA, is retired now because of disability in the line of duty, and Mrs. J
2 CONC ohn C. Hawkins lives in Lake Forest, Illinois.  There are ten grandchil
2 CONC dren, five girls and five boys.  All five of the boys are in the servic
2 CONC e, and Charles Compton Smith, Jr., of the AAF, has given his life for h
2 CONC is country in the present war.
2 CONT
2 CONT Years and events, changing customs and emphases, lie between the Indian f
2 CONC ighter of 1886 and the flyer of 1941 who died in the performance of his d
2 CONC uty, but all along the line, of the General and of his progeny, the wor
2 CONC ds of that reporter of 1880, who has long since written his last story, a
2 CONC re still pertinent.  The Smiths have, indeed, “inherited the pride, the h
2 CONC onor, and ambition of a noble ancestry . . . and their aim has always b
2 CONC een to prove themselves worthy of the stock from which they came.”
