1 NOTE Newspaper article, from the Wanamaker manuscript:
2 CONT
2 CONT YOUNG MAN FALLS TO DEATH OFF MUD ROAD
2 CONT
2 CONT Pinned Under Auto With Face Buried In Very Shallow Water
2 CONT
2 CONT NOT FOUND UNTIL MORNING
2 CONT
2 CONT Morgan Reeves, 25, oldest son of Mr. and Mrs. E.S. Reeves of Hamburg, w
2 CONC as killed Thursday night about 11:30 o’clock when a Model T roadster, i
2 CONC n which he was riding alone, slid off an embankment about a mile south o
2 CONC f Knox and pinned the young man underneath with his head and shoulders s
2 CONC ubmerged in the mud and water.  Death was attributed to drowning and mu
2 CONC d asphyxiation.  When Coroner Ralph Lovelady reached the scene he remov
2 CONC ed a quantity of mud from the dead man’s mouth and nose and water had c
2 CONC ompletely filled his stomach and lungs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Members of the day crew working on the dredgeboat near Cooper creek and o
2 CONC n land belonging to the deceased saw the roadster upturned in the highw
2 CONC ay ditch as they were going to work early Friday morning.  Obtaining a l
2 CONC antern at the dragline, they lifted the car off the body and pulled you
2 CONC ng Reeves out of the ditch, but he had been dead for several hours.  No b
2 CONC ones were broken, but he had been so completely trapped by the car that d
2 CONC eath ensued shortly after the plunge into the ditch.
2 CONT
2 CONT As nearly as can be learned , the accident occurred a little after 11:3
2 CONC 0 as Reeves had spent the evening with Mr. and Mrs. Floyd Birkby, neigh
2 CONC bors, and about 11:15 left there and started home.
2 CONT
2 CONT Coming to the corner where he would ordinarily have turned to go to his h
2 CONC ome, a quarter of a mile east, he turned west on the road leading to wh
2 CONC ere a dragline is in operation, and his car evidently slid off the mudd
2 CONC y road and into the ditch at that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT No inquest was held as no evidence of foul p[l]ay was detected.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Reeves, while only a young man, has for the past few years, lived o
2 CONC n the farm of his deceased uncle, T.A. Morgan, batching and has engaged i
2 CONC n farming on a large scale.  He was well known in Sidney, a friendly, l
2 CONC ikable young man, and had a large circle of friend[s] both here and at H
2 CONC amburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Morgan Reeves, son of Mr. and Mrs. E.S. Reeves, was born near Hamburg, J
2 CONC anuary 6, 1904, being at the time of his death 25 years, 9 months and 5 d
2 CONC ays of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT His education was received in the Hamburg public schools, and following h
2 CONC is graduation he had been associated with his father in the management o
2 CONC f their large holdings of farm land.  He is survived by his parents, on
2 CONC e brother, Ben, two sisters, Azile and Jean.
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services were held at Hamburg Sunday afternoon, very largely at
2 CONC tended by friends from all parts of the county.
