1 NOTE Bob Reeves listed her as Nancy (Robert) Reeves.  He had the following n
2 CONC otes:
2 CONT
2 CONT She graduated in 1935 from Sophie Newcomb College, New Orleans.  She wa
2 CONC s an active volunteer for numerous charities, including being President o
2 CONC f Junior League, President of YWCA, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1992 the Nancy Reeves Dreux Chair in Women’s Studies was endowed Sophie N
2 CONC ewcomb College, Tulane University.
2 CONT
2 CONT THE NANCY REEVES DREUX CHAIR IN POLITICAL SCIENCE was established in 19
2 CONC 99 by the University with the invested earnings from a bequest gift fro
2 CONC m Nancy Reeves Dreux in 1992 which created the original Nancy Reeves Dr
2 CONC eux Chair in Political Science.  Nancy was the wife of the late William B
2 CONC . Dreux, a 1933 Notre Dame graduate.  She was a 1935 graduate of Newcom
2 CONC b College in New Orleans, Louisiana (part of the Tulane University syst
2 CONC em).  In addition to maintaining her ties to Tulane and Newcomb, she wa
2 CONC s a dedicated civic leader in the New Orleans community.  She served as p
2 CONC resident of the Junior League of New Orleans and the Young Women's Chri
2 CONC stian Association, as a board member of the Greater New Orleans Regiona
2 CONC l Foundation and as co-chair for auctions benefiting New Orleans public t
2 CONC elevision.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to the two Nancy Reeves Dreux Professorships in Political S
2 CONC cience, the estate also established the Nancy Reeves Dreux Chair in Psy
2 CONC chology.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nancy was affectionately known as Ti-tante to her nephews and nieces, a
2 CONC nd Tante-ti to her grandnephews and grandnieces.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From the New Orleans Times Picayune, 2 Sep 1992 (obtained from Lynda Mo
2 CONC reau):
2 CONT
2 CONT Dreux - Nancy Reeves Dreux, a civic leader, died Sunday at Touro Infirm
2 CONC ary Hospital.  She was 77.  Mrs. Dreux was a lifelong resident of New O
2 CONC rleans.  She graduated from Louis S. McGee School and Newcomb College.  S
2 CONC he was the student body president of Newcomb College from 1934 To 1935, p
2 CONC resident of the Junior League Of New Orleans, the Young Women's Christi
2 CONC an Association, the Maison Hospitaliere, and the Kappa Kappa Gamma Alum
2 CONC ni Association.  She also was co-chairman of the 1962 WYES-TV and 1985 W
2 CONC LAE-TV auctions, registration chairman of the League Of Women Voters in J
2 CONC efferson Parish, and chairman of the Woman's Division of United Way at J
2 CONC efferson Parish.  She was active in civic and community affairs.  She w
2 CONC as a member of the board of directors of the Greater New Orleans Founda
2 CONC tion from 1964 to 1988 and of the Women's Advisory Council, University O
2 CONC f Notre Dame, from 1963 to 1990.  She received the Junior League's Sust
2 CONC aining Member award, The Volunteer Service Bureau's Volunteer of the Ye
2 CONC ar award, the Louise S. McGee School Alumnae Recognition award.  She wa
2 CONC s a member of the Orleans Club and the New Orleans Town Gardners.  Surv
2 CONC ivors include two nieces, Lynn R. Thayer and Dulaney D. Montgomery; and t
2 CONC wo nephews, Ro[b]ert D. Reeves and John Dart Jr.  A mass will be said t
2 CONC oday at 3 P. M. at St. Francis Xavier Church, 448 Metairie Road.  A vis
2 CONC itation will be held today after 1 P. M. at Bultman Funeral Home, 3338 S
2 CONC t. Charles Ave.  Burial will be in Metairie Cemetery.
