1 NOTE Notes from Bob Reeves:
2 CONT
2 CONT He [was a] graduate of Notre Dame and later served several years on its B
2 CONC oard of Regents.  In WWI [should be WWII] he parachuted behind lines in F
2 CONC rance and much later wrote "No Bridges Blown".  After law school he bec
2 CONC ame a successful attorney in New Orleans.
2 CONT
2 CONT After graduating from Notre Dame, William B. Dreux earned a law degree f
2 CONC rom Tulane University and practiced law in the New Orleans firm Hurley & D
2 CONC reux.  A captain in the Office of Strategic Services during World War I
2 CONC I, he authored the book No Bridges Blown, which was published by Notre D
2 CONC ame Press in 1972 [actually 1971].  He was a member of Notre Dame's Adv
2 CONC isory Council for the College of Arts and Letters from 1959 until his d
2 CONC eath in 1983.
2 CONT
2 CONT In May 1937, A.J.Waechter and William Behan Dreux, a Tulane [c]lassmate
2 CONC , joined Joseph M. Jones as associates in the practice of law. The name o
2 CONC f the firm later became Jones, Flanders & Waechter...World War II broug
2 CONC ht great change to the law firm.  Early in 1942, William Dreux entered t
2 CONC he Army and distinguished himself as a member of the Office of Strategi
2 CONC c Services.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT E-mail from Lynda Moreau, dated 23 Sep 2013:
2 CONT
2 CONT [William Behan Dreux] graduated from Notre Dame and Tulane University, w
2 CONC here he took a law degree.  He was in WWII, special forces – quite a ch
2 CONC aracter.  When the Army learned he spoke fluent French (he was born the
2 CONC re but moved back to the US after WWI) they asked if he wanted to volun
2 CONC teer to parachute behind enemy lines in France.  He did so, several tim
2 CONC es.  He wrote a book about his adventures entitled “No Bridges Blown”, p
2 CONC ublished by the Notre Dame Press – you can get a used copy of it on Ama
2 CONC zon.
2 CONT  
2 CONT After the service, he had a very successful career as an attorney in Ne
2 CONC w Orleans, and was an early partner in the famed Jones, Walker firm – o
2 CONC ne of the largest firms in the South – they still have their main offic
2 CONC e here.
2 CONT  
2 CONT Mr. Dreux’s parents were Andre Dreux, born in France and a professor at T
2 CONC ulane University, and Katherine Behan.  Katherine was the daughter of W
2 CONC illiam J. Behan and Catherine “Katie” Walker, the subjects of the portr
2 CONC ait we are having restored [to hang in Confederate Memorial Hall in New O
2 CONC rleans].  W.J. Behan was Mayor of New Orleans during the 1880’s – his o
2 CONC ther daughter, Elizabeth (or “Bessie” as she was known) was Queen of Ca
2 CONC rnival in 1891 – a great honor.  They lived at 1207 Jackson Avenue – un
2 CONC fortunately, a condo building now occupies the site.  Sadly, today ther
2 CONC e are no living descendents.
2 CONT  
2 CONT Bill and Nancy’s papers are at Tulane University.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Email from Roxanne Merritt, Director of the JFK Special Warfare Museum, d
2 CONC ated 21 Mar 2020:
2 CONT
2 CONT The artifacts associated with [William Behan Dreux's] WWII career as a J
2 CONC edburgh with the OSS Jedburgh team Gavin in Brittany was donated by his w
2 CONC ife to the JFK Special Warfare Museum, Fort Bragg, NC in 1986, the regi
2 CONC mental museum for the US Army Special Forces.
