1 SEX M
1 _UID D5F973A81A4B54419353958F4C71564D4D5D
1 CHAN
2 DATE 13 AUG 2018
1 NOTE Whitney, Carrie Westlake, <i>Kansas City, Missouri: Its History and Its P
2 CONC eople</i>, <i>1808-1908</i>, Volume III, Chicago, S. J. Clarke Pub. Co.
2 CONC , 1908, pp. 200-201
2 CONT (obtained from http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~wynkoop/webdocs
2 CONC /kiersted.htm):
2 CONT
2 CONT WYNKOOP KIERSTED.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wynkoop Kiersted is a civil engineer of Kansas City, whose business int
2 CONC erests have led him into various sections of the country, where his sup
2 CONC erior ability and knowledge in the line of his profession are recognize
2 CONC d. So carefully has he studied his subject and so expert has he become t
2 CONC hat his opinions are largely received as authority on the subject of th
2 CONC e building of waterworks and sewerage systems. A native of the Empire S
2 CONC tate, his life record began in Mongaup Valley, Sullivan county, New Yor
2 CONC k, February 9, 1857. His parents, Wynkoop and Jane A. (Swan) Kiersted, w
2 CONC ere, also natives of the Empire State. The paternal ancestors came to t
2 CONC his country at a very early day from Holland. Wynkoop Kiersted, Sr., sp
2 CONC ent his entire life in Sullivan county, New York, and was there engaged i
2 CONC n the tanning business. He had an uncle who was a veteran of the Revolu
2 CONC tionary war, serving with official rank in the struggle for independenc
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wynkoop Kiersted of this review was reared at home, acquiring his educa
2 CONC tion in the public schools and in the Eureka high school, which was fou
2 CONC nded and largely supported by his father. His preliminary education bei
2 CONC ng completed, he next entered the Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute at T
2 CONC roy, New York, from which he was graduated in the class of 1880. The sa
2 CONC me year he came west and accepted a position with the Missouri River Im
2 CONC provement Commission. He was identified with this work for two or three y
2 CONC ears and for two years afterward was engaged in the construction of wat
2 CONC erworks. He made the surveys for the sewerage system of Elgin, Illinois
2 CONC , and built the waterworks at Fairfield, Iowa. In 1880 he opened an off
2 CONC ice in Omaha, Nebraska, as a civil engineer and there remained until th
2 CONC e fall of 1887, when he came to Kansas City, where he established and h
2 CONC as since maintained an office. In the intervening years for a considera
2 CONC ble period he was chief engineer of the Kansas City waterworks and intr
2 CONC oduced the present system of water clarification, and also designed the s
2 CONC ewer system of Kansas City, Kansas. For two years he was chief engineer o
2 CONC f the city of Galveston, Texas, in the construction of their new waterw
2 CONC orks system. He was engaged in the appraisal of the waterworks property o
2 CONC f Los Angeles and Oakland, California; of Dubuque, Iowa; of Pittsburg, P
2 CONC ennsylvania; of Eau Claire and Beloit, Wisconsin; of Baton Rouge, Louis
2 CONC iana; of Denison, Texas; of Pittsburg, Kansas; of North Platte and Kear
2 CONC ney, Nebraska; of Meridian, Mississippi; and of Council Bluffs, Iowa, t
2 CONC ogether with various smaller cities, and during this time designed some f
2 CONC orty or fifty waterworks systems. He has served as consulting engineer f
2 CONC or various other cities and has come to be a recognized authority throu
2 CONC ghout the country on the subject of building waterworks. He is the seni
2 CONC or author of a volume on The Management and Maintenance of Waterworks w
2 CONC hich was published by John Wiley, of New York city, has received much f
2 CONC avorable press notice and is accepted as authority on civil engineering
2 CONC . He is also author of a work on Sewage Disposal, which is also recogni
2 CONC zed as a valuable contribution to knowledge of this subject. He belongs t
2 CONC o the American Society of Engineers and to the Rensselaer Society of En
2 CONC gineers, of Troy, New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1888 Mr. Kiersted was married to Miss Medora R. Smith, of St. Joseph
2 CONC , Missouri, a daughter of Dr. J. D. Smith and a granddaughter of Hon. A
2 CONC biel Leonard, of Fayette, Missouri, who for many years was a judge of t
2 CONC he supreme court of this state. Mr. and Mrs. Kiersted have become the p
2 CONC arents of four children: Martha, who is attending National Park Seminar
2 CONC y near Washington, D. C.; Jeanette, Wynkoop, Jr., and Louise, all of wh
2 CONC om are attending school, and are with their parents. In 1903 Mr. Kierst
2 CONC ed established the family residence at Liberty, Missouri, where he now m
2 CONC akes his home, but continues his business headquarters at Kansas City. H
2 CONC e is a republican where national issues are involved but casts an indep
2 CONC endent ballot at local elections. His family are members of the Episcop
2 CONC al church. His rapid and substantial rise in his profession is attribut
2 CONC able to the thoroughness with which he has mastered everything bearing u
2 CONC pon the subject of civil engineering, combined with his unwearied indus
2 CONC try and his professional integrity. He is now one of the ablest civil e
2 CONC ngineers of the state and his fame has spread abroad, winning him a wid
2 CONC e and gratifying patronage.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT John Wanamaker and WFT 52 # 944 didn't have the "Jr.", and this biograp
2 CONC hy refers to his son as "Jr." rather than "III", so I don't know what's r
2 CONC ight.
