1 _UID AB2C5FC579E13645A5759FFAD3B98B6D0748
1 CHAN
2 DATE 11 JUN 2020
1 NOTE This is the biggest hole in the Parks manuscript.  We know that there w
2 CONC as a son named John from the wills of Walter and Martha Davis, but John i
2 CONC s never mentioned by Parks.  It's hard to imagine how this could have h
2 CONC appened.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first thought that comes to mind is that John could have been a sor
2 CONC t-of "black sheep" of the family, whom nobody wanted to talk about, but t
2 CONC hat doesn't seem to fit with the fact that he was named an executor in h
2 CONC is mother's will.
2 CONT
2 CONT The only other possibility that I can think of is that Thomas Davis Par
2 CONC ks was never around John much, so he just forgot about him.  Even if Pa
2 CONC rks didn't know him personally, though, you'd think that others would h
2 CONC ave spoken of him.  At the beginning of his write-up, Parks says that m
2 CONC uch of his knowledge of the family is from conversations with his mothe
2 CONC r and aunts, and you would think that they would have mentioned their b
2 CONC rother John occasionally.  Unless, possibly, he was on the outs with th
2 CONC e rest of the family for some reason.
2 CONT
2 CONT A number of family trees (see, for example, http://wc.rootsweb.ancestry
2 CONC .com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=mlysell&id=I10375) show a John Davis bor
2 CONC n in 1765 (or about 1765), who married Sarah (Sallie) Ewing, and died i
2 CONC n 1818 in Washington County, Missouri.  Note that John Davis's sister S
2 CONC arah (Sallie) Davis married John Ewin/Ewing!  (The family tree site jus
2 CONC t mentioned shows John and Sarah Ewing as siblings as well as John and S
2 CONC arah Davis being siblings.)  As discussed in the notes to Walter Davis, t
2 CONC hings seem most consistent if John was the youngest child, but that doe
2 CONC s not fit with a birth about 1765.  William Cunningham Davis was born i
2 CONC n 1765 (some sources say January 1766), and he is discussed extensively b
2 CONC y Parks.  However, Parks knew William well since William was still livi
2 CONC ng in Augusta County when Parks was growing up there, and Parks even me
2 CONC ntions living with William for a time.  In addition, Parks later lived n
2 CONC ear William’s son William Caldwell Davis in Saline County, Missouri.  F
2 CONC rom Martha (Cunningham) Davis's will, it is clear that John was still i
2 CONC n Augusta County in March 1806 (when the will was proved).  Parks was j
2 CONC ust six years old at that time.  If John was only in Augusta County for a f
2 CONC ew more years after that, the omission of John from the Parks manuscrip
2 CONC t could possibly make sense if Parks didn’t know him well then because o
2 CONC f being so young.  Parks might not have seen him after that, either, si
2 CONC nce John moved to Washington County, Missouri, which is not close to Sa
2 CONC line County.  A possible hole in this theory, though, is that some of t
2 CONC he information about the John Davis who married Sallie Ewing indicates t
2 CONC hat he spent time in Christian County, Kentucky, before moving on to Mi
2 CONC ssouri, and you would think that his name would have come up in the sto
2 CONC ries about the family members living there--though John wouldn't yet ha
2 CONC ve been there during the darkest period, after the deaths of James Davi
2 CONC s, Brewer Reeves, John Ewin, and Sallie (Davis) Ewin.  Parks says that h
2 CONC is family moved to Christian County in 1816, but possibly John had move
2 CONC d on to Missouri by then.
2 CONT
2 CONT For now, I am going to hold off including the info on marriage and desc
2 CONC endants of John Davis and Sarah Ewing, but I would like to find out if t
2 CONC here is any good information tying that John Davis to Walter Davis's fa
2 CONC mily.
