1 NOTE "History of Todd County, Kentucky", ed. J. H. Battle, F. A. Battey Publ
2 CONC ishing Co., 1884, p. 269:
2 CONT
2 CONT JAMES H. HALL was born September 28, 1848, in Todd County, Ky., where h
2 CONC e has always resided.  His father, John E. Hall, was born in 1824, in W
2 CONC est Virginia, and died in Texas in 1879; and mother, Mary E., daughter o
2 CONC f Mrs. S. A. Tandy, of Todd County, Ky., was born February 29, 1828, an
2 CONC d died May 18, 1849.  To them was born one child, our subject, who is e
2 CONC ngaged in farming, successfully cultivating corn, wheat, tobacco, and r
2 CONC aising and dealing in stock.  Subject was married, June 28, 1871, to Mi
2 CONC ss Henrietta, daughter of Maj. Levi and Martha A. (Layne) Darnall, of T
2 CONC odd County, and to them have been born Annie and William Davis.  Maj. D
2 CONC arnall was a soldier in the war of 1812.  He was born September 20, 179
2 CONC 5, and was the descendant of an old and wealthy English family, who emi
2 CONC grated from the mother country with their kinsman, Lord Baltimore, in 1
2 CONC 629, bringing with them the material for the erection of their ancestra
2 CONC l halls, built at what was then known as the Old Wood Yard, on the bank
2 CONC s of the Potomac, where they remained in good preservation till 1862, w
2 CONC hen they were destroyed by fire.  After having amassed quite a fortune, h
2 CONC e moved to Todd County--then Christian--in 1847, where he married and r
2 CONC esided till his death, September 23, 1873.  John E. Hall's father was E
2 CONC dward Hall, an early settler in Todd County.  Subject is a Baptist and h
2 CONC is wife a Presbyterian.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Todd County, Kentucky, Family Histories", by the Todd County Historica
2 CONC l Society, Turner Publishing Co., 1995, p. 199 (obtained from Google Bo
2 CONC oks):
2 CONT
2 CONT [From the biography of Edward Stuart Hall]  Their son [of John E. Hall a
2 CONC nd Mary Ann Elizabeth Tandy], James Henry Hall, was born on Sept. 28, 1
2 CONC 848.  James Henry's mother died on May 18, 1849, leaving an eight month
2 CONC s old infant.  He was taken by his grandmother, Sarah Ann Davis Tandy (
2 CONC 1809-1887), and reared on the farm now known as the Tandy-Hall Farm.  T
2 CONC he nucleus of this farm, now consisting of 298 acres, was acquired in 1
2 CONC 825 by John Davis (1778-1826) and his wife, Mary Pendleton (1775-1844), p
2 CONC arents of Sarah Ann Davis Tandy (1809-1887).  This land has been owned b
2 CONC y some member of the family since.
