1 _UID 1B8F3C59E6FB074390427CB34B503B05465C
1 CHAN
2 DATE 6 APR 2019
1 NOTE "Todd County, Kentucky, Family Histories", by the Todd County Historica
2 CONC l Society, Turner Publishing Co., 1995, p. 199 (obtained from Google Bo
2 CONC oks):
2 CONT
2 CONT JAMES DARNALL HALL - FAY MCLAIN PETRIE - Henrietta Darnall (1850-1904) a
2 CONC nd her husband, James Henry Hall (1848-1911), were both Todd County bir
2 CONC th.  Henrietta's father, Levi Darnall (1796-1873), was born in Montgome
2 CONC ry County, MD, served in War of 1812, following which he lived for a ti
2 CONC me in West Feliciana Parish, LA, before moving, as a bachelor, to Todd C
2 CONC ounty in 1847.  There he married Martha Ann McReynolds Layne (1815-1863
2 CONC ), 20 years his junior.  She was the daughter of Todd County pioneers, J
2 CONC ohn Layne (1777-1856) and Sarah McReynolds (1789-1854).  John Layne arr
2 CONC ived in the Fairview district of Todd County from Campbell County, VA, i
2 CONC n 1821.  He soon purchased a farm about a mile east of Fairview on what i
2 CONC s now Highway 68.  He lived there the remainder of his life, having at l
2 CONC east 13 children, among whom was his daughter Martha Ann.  John was a d
2 CONC escendant of John Layne (c1685-1755) and Elizabeth (?)Ayres (da/f 1755) o
2 CONC f Goochland County, VA.  The line of descent was Charles Layne (1721-c1
2 CONC 821) and his wife Priscilla (c1720-c1824), Jesse Layne (died 1833), and J
2 CONC ohn Layne (1777-1865).  John fell on hard times in Virginia and for thi
2 CONC s reason emigrated to the new county of Todd.  In earlier times there w
2 CONC ere many descendants of John Layne in Todd and Christian County.  Follo
2 CONC wing John's death in 1856, his farm, about a mile east of Fairview, pas
2 CONC sed out of the family and in more recent years has been known as the Ga
2 CONC be Payne place.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Layne's (1777-1856) wife, Sarah McReynolds, was the daughter of Wi
2 CONC lliam McReynolds (c1759-1796) and Martha Wilson, natives of Campbell Co
2 CONC unty, VA.  William McReynolds was a descendant of John McReynolds (1665
2 CONC -1760) of Keppoch, Scotland, and Elizabeth Shepherd.  John emigrated to I
2 CONC reland where he remained the rest of his life.  Elizabeth, his wife, so
2 CONC me years before John's death, along with their four children, emigrated t
2 CONC o America.  John's son, Joseph McReynolds (1715-1805), married Sarah Di
2 CONC xon about 1736 in Ireland and came with his mother to Pennsylvania.  Th
2 CONC ey became the parents of William McReynolds (c1759-1796).  Other descen
2 CONC dants of the original John McReynolds (1665-1760) settled in Trigg Coun
2 CONC ty, KY, from where Dr. James Alexander McReynolds and Dr. John Oliver M
2 CONC cReynolds emigrated to Elkton and became prominent physicians there for m
2 CONC any years.  Dr. John McReynolds was the father of Justice James Clark M
2 CONC cReynolds, Justice of the United States Supreme Court.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Henry Hall (1848-1911) and Henrietta Darnall had three children: A
2 CONC nnie Hall (Mrs. Charles Buford Fulcher) (1872-1901); William Davis Hall (
2 CONC 1874-1950) (wife, Hyde Wood), and James Darnall Hall (1889-1972).
2 CONT
2 CONT James Darnall Hall, a life long resident of Todd County, was born at th
2 CONC e old Tandy Hall Farm, about 2 1/2 miles southeast of Fairview on July 4
2 CONC , 1889.  He attended the neighborhood schools and upon the death of his f
2 CONC ather, he and his brother William Davis bought the homestead.  After a s
2 CONC hort time "Will", as he was commonly known, sold his half to his brothe
2 CONC r.  James Darnall lived there until 1935.  On Aprill 11, 1916, James D. m
2 CONC arried Fay McLain Petrie (1887-1971), daughter of Dr. William Sobieski P
2 CONC etrie, a long time physician in Fairview, and his wife, Susie Lenora Mc
2 CONC Lain.  James D. or "Darnall" as he was commonly known, and Fay became t
2 CONC he parents of three children: Marjorie Darnall Hall (Mrs. William Archi
2 CONC e Cowherd), William Petrie Hall, M.D. (wife - Jane Vivian Jones of Rich
2 CONC mond, KY), and Suzanne Hall (Mrs. Robert Garland "Bob" Foster).
2 CONT
2 CONT Darnall devoted most of his life to the operation of the old Tandy Hall f
2 CONC arm, but prior to 1935, there was only a mud road leading to the home.  T
2 CONC his became intolerable with the more common use of the automobile.  In t
2 CONC hat year Darnall purchased the Arch O'Daniel farm about a mile east of F
2 CONC airview on Highway 68.  He and Fay spent the remainder of their lives t
2 CONC here.  In later years Darnall devoted some of his time to financing use
2 CONC d automobiles, a venture that day in time that banks avoided.  Darnall e
2 CONC njoyed hunting as a young man, but with the more intensive use of farm l
2 CONC and and the increasing scarcity of quail, he gave this up; however, he l
2 CONC oved fishing and enjoyed this outing until near the end of his life.  F
2 CONC ay was very family oriented and enjoyed providing all sorts of tasty fo
2 CONC od for her family.
2 CONT
2 CONT The old Tandy Hall Farm remains in the family, now owned by daughter Ma
2 CONC rjorie.  The O'Daniel farm became property of Suzanne upon the death of h
2 CONC er parents.  She continues to live nearby.  Unfortunately the family ho
2 CONC me on Highway 68 burned a few years ago.  <i>Submitted by Will Pete Hal
2 CONC l</i>
