1 NOTE From the Hillis/Abney/Franklin/Smith web site:
2 CONT
2 CONT The wedding of Sarah Abney Thompson and George Murrell described by T. C
2 CONC . Rainey in his 1914 book, "Along the Old Trail: Pioneer Sketches of Ar
2 CONC row Rock and Vicinity"
2 CONT
2 CONT A SENSIBLE BUT UNUSUAL WEDDING CEREMONY
2 CONT ONE WOMAN WHO KEPT A SECRET, YEARS AGO
2 CONT
2 CONT One winter Sunday morning-to be exact, on January 2, 1878-at the close o
2 CONC f the services in the Methodist Church in Arrow Rock, the minister anno
2 CONC unced that on the following Wednesday morning, at 10 o'clock, a marriag
2 CONC e ceremony would be celebrated in that Church, and all who chose to com
2 CONC e would be welcome. No explanation was made, and of course, this caused a m
2 CONC ild sensation in a village of eight hundred people, every one personall
2 CONC y known to the other. All sorts of conjectures were proposed, the most e
2 CONC nlightened and skillful gossips were consulted; but every suggestion, a
2 CONC fter being discussed was pronounced improbable. The whole village ackno
2 CONC wledged itself incapable of forming any solution of the puzzle, and the m
2 CONC inister was dumb. Not a spoons couple who were "sweet" on each other wa
2 CONC s known to exist. Time, that old Justice of the Peace, who settles all o
2 CONC ur difficulties, would have to decide it; but even the old scythe-beare
2 CONC r mowed his swath slowly until Wednesday morning finally consented to a
2 CONC rrive.
2 CONT
2 CONT George A. Murrell, a prosperous farmer, whose wife had died some years p
2 CONC reviously, lived seven miles from town, on his farm near Jonesborough. H
2 CONC e came into my store early Wednesday morning, and announced that he was g
2 CONC oing to make a trip to St. Louis, and that he would go to Boonville, th
2 CONC e nearest railroad station, in a coach, which would leave about noon. H
2 CONC e was considerably dressed up, for George, but as he was going away on a v
2 CONC isit, that caused no surprise to any of us. We supposed he was going on t
2 CONC he mail coach, which carried passengers. It was quite a cold morning, a
2 CONC nd we sat around the stove and chatted. George seemed to be particularl
2 CONC y jolly frame of mind, and we made many remarks about the wedding annou
2 CONC nced to occur in the church that morning; but no one had even a guess a
2 CONC s to who the parties might be. I was not much concerned about the affai
2 CONC r myself, and 10 o'clock had almost arrived when George said to me, "To
2 CONC m, let's go up to the church and see who is going to get married."
2 CONT
2 CONT I put on my hat and we walked together, talking of indifferent subjects o
2 CONC n the, way, and took seats in a pew about the middle of the church, Geo
2 CONC rge sitting next the aisle. The church was already half filled, and pre
2 CONC tty soon about every seat was taken. Mrs. John C. Thompson, Sr., widow, a m
2 CONC ember of the church, dressed in full black, a tall and stately lady, ca
2 CONC me in late, walking alone, and took her usual seat in a pew next the fr
2 CONC ont one. The audience sat and stared suspiciously at each other, but st
2 CONC ill no hint of the candidates for future happiness.
2 CONT
2 CONT The minister, after a dramatic pause, arose, and in a very deliberate t
2 CONC one said, "The parties who propose to enter into the holy bonds of matr
2 CONC imony will now please present themselves before the altar," at the same t
2 CONC ime coming down from his pulpit and standing inside, the railing.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was a silence so deep that it reverberated. You could feel your p
2 CONC ulses beat, for almost a minute, before any movement was made. I began w
2 CONC hispering into George's ear, "To be or not to be?" when he said to me, "
2 CONC Tom, I believe if no one goings is going up I'll go myself;" and then h
2 CONC e arose and walked very deliberately up the aisle. When he reached the s
2 CONC eat where Mrs. Thompson was silting she stood up, dropping a long black v
2 CONC eil over the back of her pew, and took the proffered arm of Mr. Murrell
2 CONC . The ceremony was soon over, and I think there were more pop-eyed peop
2 CONC le gazing at, them during its progress than were ever seen before in th
2 CONC at church. At its close every soul present rushed forward and offered s
2 CONC incere congratulations. Both were very well known to their auditors, bo
2 CONC th were members of the Methodist Church; but even Mrs. Grundy admitted s
2 CONC he had been outwitted, for although this marriage was universally conce
2 CONC ded to be a very appropriate union in every particular, not a soul in t
2 CONC he town had a thought of either in connection with the unusual occurren
2 CONC ce.
2 CONT
2 CONT A carriage was in waiting outside, and the happy pair, highly pleased w
2 CONC ith the success of their cunning plans, started on their journey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Further inquiry developed that Mr. and Mrs. Murrell were old acquaintan
2 CONC ces; that before either had been married they had been on very friendly t
2 CONC erms, if not sweethearts. It also was discovered that they had correspo
2 CONC nded, and met together in such an apparently casual manner that none of t
2 CONC heir friends had suspected them. The marriage proved a happy one to bot
2 CONC h, and was only terminated by the sad death of Mrs. Murrell, several ye
2 CONC ars previous to that of Mr. Murrell, which occurred last year, at the r
2 CONC ipe age of 84.
2 CONT
2 CONT It has always seemed to me that Mr. and Mrs. Murrell chose a very sensi
2 CONC ble plan for beginning life, together. Mrs. Thompson was a childless wi
2 CONC dow, living alone in her dwelling in the village, and Mr. Murrell was l
2 CONC iving on his farm with his two sons, the only white members of his fami
2 CONC ly. Their friends witnessed their wedding, and no one was put to any tr
2 CONC ouble in preparing for it. It was an ingenious, and at the same time a d
2 CONC ramatic entrance into the wedded life. But I, the unsuspecting groomsma
2 CONC n, was cut off from the full performance of my office.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Old "Lochinvar had come out of the West,"
2 CONT Had walked up the aisle while the people were dumb,
2 CONT And his groomsman sat twiddling his fingers and thumb?
2 CONT My friend George had jilted me.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that the marriage date given in the story does not agree with the d
2 CONC ate of 2 Jan 1879 from the Hillis/Abney/Franklin/Smith web site.  The l
2 CONC atter date is confirmed by the "Saline County Missouri Marriage Index 1
2 CONC 820-1881" on Ancestry.com.  (2 Jan 1879 was a Thursday, by the way.)
