1 NOTE "The Leverett Memorial" by Charles Edward Leverett; Crosby, Nichols & C
2 CONC o., 1856, pp. 154-55 (obtained from Google Books):
2 CONT
2 CONT John,(13) eldest son of Knight, was born in Boston, Jan. 28, 1726-7.  M
2 CONC arried Mary Greenleaf, Dec. 19, 1757.  By papers in our hands, we find h
2 CONC im a commissioned officer as early as 1758, "in the Regiment whereof Jo
2 CONC hn Phillips, Esq., is Colonel," and after sustaining various grades, ap
2 CONC pointed Lieut.-Colonel, and subsequently Colonel of the Boston Regiment
2 CONC . ... In 1762, he is made one of his Majesty's Justices of the Peace, a
2 CONC nd was Overseer of the Poor, &c.  Col. Leverett was a merchant, importe
2 CONC r of British goods, and his warehouse, with its valuable contents, was d
2 CONC estroyed by the King's troops when they took possession of Boston in th
2 CONC e Revolutionary war.  Notwithstanding this serious loss, and under such c
2 CONC ircumstances, he would not feel exonerated from the debt, and, with a j
2 CONC ust but very uncommon magnanimity, paid the full value to his English c
2 CONC reditors.  His justice or generosity di not, we are pleased to say, pre
2 CONC vent his leaving, at his decease, a respectable fortune to his family.  S
2 CONC ome relics and traditionary reminiscences show that he lived in much st
2 CONC yle and luxury, not common in that century.  He was a gentleman of such e
2 CONC xcellent spirit as to be ready and willing to serve his town in any cap
2 CONC acity,--thinking it just as honorable to be the plain soldier or civili
2 CONC an, as the titled leader or magistrate.  Col. Leverett is represented t
2 CONC o have been of high temper, but of great integrity, capacity and energy
2 CONC .  At the invasion of the English, he removed his family to Middletown, C
2 CONC onn., where he died, June 10, 1777, aged 51.  His widow died at Windsor
2 CONC , Vt., July 8, 1795.
