1 NOTE Full name is from Find A Grave, listing for Henry Archibal Tandy.  They h
2 CONC ave a photo of the gravestone, with that spelling for his name.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hereford Brand, 30 Jan 1919 (obtained from Find A Grave):
2 CONT
2 CONT TANDY WAS SHAVING; SHOT THROUGH NECK
2 CONT
2 CONT Reached Into Bag After A New Blade And Discharged Pistol
2 CONT
2 CONT BULLET SEVERED JUGULAR ARTERY
2 CONT
2 CONT Small Son Sees His Father Fall To Floor And Expire Without Uttering A S
2 CONC ingle Word
2 CONT
2 CONT Some details of the unfortunate accident through which Henry A. Tandy, w
2 CONC ell known cattleman, lost his life at Van Horn, Texas, Wednesday of las
2 CONC t week have been received here.  Mr. Tandy left a large circle of sorro
2 CONC wing friends in this community, and several of them were at the funeral s
2 CONC ervices held at the Tandy home in Amarillo last Saturday, following whi
2 CONC ch the body was shipped to Woodward, Oklahoma, for interment.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Brand has been informed that little Henry Jr., and Glenn Sullivan w
2 CONC ere in the room with Tandy at the time of the accident.  He was shaving a
2 CONC nd talking to them at the same time.  He had gone over his face once, a
2 CONC nd remarked that he thought he had another rayzor blade in his travelin
2 CONC g bag, which was open by his side, that was better than the one he was u
2 CONC sing.  He reached into the bag with one hand and rummaged around for th
2 CONC e blade.  His automatic revolver was in the bag, together with a lot of o
2 CONC ther personal effects, and the safety must have been opened on the gun, f
2 CONC or his hand struck it and the weapon was discharged, the bullet strikin
2 CONC g him in the neck, severing the jugular vein, passing entirely through h
2 CONC is neck and lodging in the wall of the room.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tandy pitched forward on the floor and expired in just a few seconds, w
2 CONC ithout being able to say anything.  Mrs. Tandy and a young lady visitor w
2 CONC ere in the house, and the stricken wife held the head of the dead man i
2 CONC n her lap for hours until a physician could be summoned in hopes that t
2 CONC here was a chance for his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT A long delay was encountered in securing the services of a undertaker f
2 CONC rom El Paso, and it was Saturday before the ceremony could be held at t
2 CONC he home in Amarillo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. and Mrs. Geo. Barber of Hereford took charge of these services at t
2 CONC he Tandy home.  Besides these two, Nestor Gass, Miss Belle Woodburn, Ed J
2 CONC ohnson, Walter Lupton, Truman Hines and A. F. Luse were present from He
2 CONC reford.
2 CONT
2 CONT The body was shipped to Woodward, Oklahoma, to the home of Tandy's fath
2 CONC er, Saturday evening.  Arrangements had been made for interment Sunday.  A d
2 CONC ouble portion of sorrow was meted out to the Tandy family at this stage b
2 CONC y the death of a brother-in-law of Henry Tandy, and a double funeral wa
2 CONC s held at Woodward Sunday.
2 CONT
2 CONT Besides his parents, wife and small boy, Mr. Tandy is survived by four s
2 CONC isters and three brothers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Nestor Gass of Hereford was raised with Henry Tandy in Haskell coun
2 CONC ty, and was much affected by the accident.  The sympathy of the entire c
2 CONC ommunity is with the bereaved wife in her sorrow.
