2 DATE 16 APR 2019
1 NOTE Foreword to "Memoirs of Lincoln" by Herring Chrisman (William Herring C
2 CONC hrisman, 1930; obtained from https://archive.org/details/memoirsoflinco
2 CONC ln00ilchri):
2 CONT
2 CONT These articles from the pen of my father, Herring Chrisman, are submitt
2 CONC ed to all those interested in Abraham Lincoln, his forbears, and his co
2 CONC ntemporaries.  They were written in the year 1900 as a family record an
2 CONC d my principle motive in republishing them is to establish in the line o
2 CONC f Lincoln ancestry the position of Bathsheba Herring, about whom there h
2 CONC as been much controversy and doubt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Herring Chrisman's great-grandfather, William Herring, and Abraham Linc
2 CONC oln's grandmother, Bathsheba Herring, were brother and sister.
2 CONT
2 CONT Herring Chrisman was born September 16, 1823, in Rockingham County, Vir
2 CONC ginia, where his father was a large planter and slave owner, and where m
2 CONC any of Lincoln's forbears lived.  The youthful Herring had a Negro boy o
2 CONC f his own age as his personal slave as long as he remained at home.  He w
2 CONC as educated at Washington and Lee University, and became Commonwealth A
2 CONC ttorney for Rockingham at the age of twenty-one years.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the menacing days of secession he became a member of the Chicago B
2 CONC ar, and worked to keep the Union together without force of arms.  The r
2 CONC ebellion was to him a war between brothers as his two younger brothers e
2 CONC ntered the Confederate army.  During the time that he was in Chicago th
2 CONC e city was enjoying a boom and he dealt to a considerable degree in rea
2 CONC l estate.  In these transactions he was financed by his old friends in V
2 CONC irginia, and for them and himself he made some nice profits.  He next m
2 CONC oved to Knox County, Illinois, where he took up a homestead and bought o
2 CONC ne thousand acres of adjoining land.  Here he fattened a thousand head o
2 CONC f cattle on dollar corn to help feed the Union army, while at the same t
2 CONC ime his brother William was in the commissary department of the Confede
2 CONC rate army buying large droves of cattle to feed the Southern army.  As a
2 CONC n active business man during those days he lived on his farm part of th
2 CONC e time and practiced law in Galesburg and Monmouth, the county seats of K
2 CONC nox and Warren Counties.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Illinois he followed his oldest son to Pottawattamie County, Iowa, w
2 CONC here the family purchased 1,040 acres of prairie land.  From there he r
2 CONC ode on horseback to Monona County, Iowa, where, in a comparatively shor
2 CONC t time, he acquired , at an average cost of $5.60 per acre, 7,000 acres o
2 CONC f untouched land near Mapleton, on which the native blue-stem swayed wa
2 CONC ve-like before the wind and the scrub oak grew unmolested.  Still finan
2 CONC ced by stalwart partners who had confidence in his ability and judgment
2 CONC , he proceeded to break, fence, and improve these lands and in the mean
2 CONC time moved his family to Mapleton, from Abingdon, Illinois.
2 CONT
2 CONT His remaining years were spent at Mapleton, where he divided his activi
2 CONC ties between his land, cattle, and law business.  As a resident of Mono
2 CONC na County he was elected County Attorney by a landslide of votes, and a
2 CONC lso was honored by being elected many times president of the Monona Cou
2 CONC nty Bar Association.  His death occurred in Mapleton at the ripe old ag
2 CONC e of 87, on August 14, 1911.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since Herring Chrisman personally knew Lincoln, the practicing attorney
2 CONC , in Illinois, and then lived on through the years in which Lincoln's n
2 CONC ame became immortal, he naturally was well fitted to express the though
2 CONC t of the times in regard to Lincoln.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is hoped that, with the ever growing reverence for Abraham Lincoln a
2 CONC nd the increasing demand for information concerning his life and family
2 CONC , this contribution may add its bit to the already brilliant luster of h
2 CONC is undying name.
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIAM HERRING CHRISMAN.
2 CONT Mapleton, Iowa
