2 DATE 16 APR 2019
1 NOTE Middle initial is from the 1870 census.  Most sources do not show a mid
2 CONC dle initial.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From Hardesty's Encyclopedia (1884; obtained from Find A Grave, listing f
2 CONC or Maj George Chrisman):
2 CONT
2 CONT MAJOR GEORGE CHRISMAN--is of a family whose genealogy is thus traced fo
2 CONC r five generations:  George Chrisman, his great grandfather, founder of t
2 CONC he family in America and Virginia, came to America about 1740, from Ger
2 CONC many, a native of Suabia, and settled in Pennsylvania.  A few years lat
2 CONC er he came to what is now Rockingham county, and settled on Linville cr
2 CONC eek.  Here he raised three sons: Hugh, the eldest became a pioneer sett
2 CONC ler of Kentucky; Charles, second son, lived and died in this county; Jo
2 CONC hn, third son, grandfather of Major Chrisman, married Nancy Harrison of R
2 CONC ockingham county, and they had issue as follows: Joseph, born November 1
2 CONC 1, 1797, settled in Fayette county, Missouri, in 1837; George H., fathe
2 CONC r of Major George, born September 23, 1799; Margaret, born January 16, 1
2 CONC 801.  George H. was born on Linville creek, near Edom, and married Mart
2 CONC ha Davis Herring, who was born in this county.  She died January 19, 18
2 CONC 66, and his death was on the 15th of September 1870.  Their children we
2 CONC re: Herring, born September 16, 1823; Burke, born September 5, 1827; Ma
2 CONC rgaret A., born October 24, 1829; George, June 2 1832; William J., born J
2 CONC une 11, 1834; infant, born May 20, 1836, lived only six days; twins, bo
2 CONC rn and died July 31, 1838; Martha G., born December 31, 1840.  Martha D
2 CONC ., Major Chrisman's mother, was a granddaughter of William Herring, who w
2 CONC as born in 1743, and in 1766 married Elizabeth Stephenson, born in June
2 CONC , 1744.  They had eight children, Alexander, Sarah, Elizabeth, Abagail, R
2 CONC euben, Edith, Margaret and William.  Alexander was born February 25, 17
2 CONC 67, and March 14, 1793, he married Margaret Reed Smith, who was born Ap
2 CONC ril 23, 1777.  Their children were: John S., born July 6, 1794, died at L
2 CONC exington, Kentucky, January 9, 1825; Elizabeth S., born September 17, 1
2 CONC 796; Martha D., April 2, 1799; William, September 4, 1801; Alexander, N
2 CONC ovember 28, 1804; Daniel S., December 6, 1807, died at St. Augustine, F
2 CONC lorida, June 22, 1836; Margaret D., born August 9, 1810; David S., born A
2 CONC pril 30, 1814, died June 9, 1838, on board steamer "Chillicothe," near C
2 CONC incinnati; Rebecca H., born February, 1817; Ann H., October, 1819.  The s
2 CONC ubject of this sketch in 1860 organized a volunteer company, of which h
2 CONC e was made captain, known as "Chrisman's Infantry."  When the 1861 war w
2 CONC as inaugurated, the company marched to Harpers Ferry, and was mustered i
2 CONC nto the Confederate States service as Company H, 10th Virginia Infantry
2 CONC .  (The roll of the company will be found in this work.)  Captain Chris
2 CONC man continued in command until 1862, when he entered McClanahan's batte
2 CONC ry, with which he served eighteen months.  He was then made captain of a c
2 CONC avalry company attached to Davis' battalion, Imboden's brigade, and to m
2 CONC ajor in August, 1864.  In the Piedmont battle, June 5, 1864, he was sev
2 CONC erely wounded in the right hand.  He served four years and two months i
2 CONC n all, and was paroled at Harrisonburg, June 15, 1865.  His brother Wil
2 CONC liam J. entered the service at Harrisonburg in October, 1861, as second l
2 CONC ieutenant Company E, 4th Virginia Infantry, and was made first lieutena
2 CONC nt in November, 1862.  He was later discharged for disability, and assi
2 CONC gned to duty in the quartermaster's department, where the close of war f
2 CONC ound him faithfully serving.  He was at home at time of surrender and n
2 CONC ever paroled.  Since the war, Major Chrisman has served four years as s
2 CONC upervisor of his county.  In Harrisonburg, November 13, 1867, he marrie
2 CONC d Lucy Gilmer Grattan, Rev. John R. Bowman of the Presbyterian Church o
2 CONC fficiating clergyman.  July 27, 1873, their daughter, Mattie Grattan, w
2 CONC as born, and she died August 11, 1874.  The wife of Major Chrisman was b
2 CONC orn at her father's seat, "Contentment," in Rockingham county, August 9
2 CONC , 1835, daughter of Robert and Martha Divers (Minor) Grattan.  Her fath
2 CONC er was born at "Contentment," March 31, 1800, and her mother, born at R
2 CONC idgeway, Albemarle county, November, 1810, came here at her marriage.  R
2 CONC obert Grattan died August 10, 1856, and his widow survived him thirteen y
2 CONC ears, dying in 1869 (see sketch of Captain George Grattan elsewhere).  M
2 CONC ajor Chrisman devotes himself to the pursuits of agriculture, making a s
2 CONC pecialty of breeding short-horn cattle.  He may be addressed at Chrisma
2 CONC n P. O., Rockingham county, Virginia.
