2 CONT William Chrisman.
2 CONT [Special to the Times-Dispatch]
2 CONT Harrisonburg, Va.  May 27.-- William Chrisman, seventy-eight years old, i
2 CONC s dead near Elkton, Rockingham county.  He was a former deputy sheriff.  F
2 CONC or two years he had been totally blind.  He was twice married.  His fir
2 CONC st wife was Miss Jennie Giles, of Nelson county, of which union four ch
2 CONC ildren survive, including Miss Mattie Chrisman, of Baltimore, and Mrs. O
2 CONC live Kirkman, of Greensboro, N. C., who was married the day before her f
2 CONC ather's death.  His second wife was Miss Jennie Gay, of Harrisonburg, w
2 CONC ho left two children: Mrs. Frank, of Rockingham, and Dr. William G. Chr
2 CONC isman, State veterinarian, of Raleigh, N. C.  He leaves two brothers [s
2 CONC ic]: Major George Chrisman, of Amberley, and Mrs. Margaret Williams, of H
2 CONC arrisonburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The bracketed note at the beginning  is in the original.  There is a lo
2 CONC t of confusion about his wives because of the similarity of their names
2 CONC , and this death notice helps to clear things up.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From Hardesty's Encyclopedia (see the notes for brother George):
2 CONT
2 CONT His brother William J. entered the service at Harrisonburg in October, 1
2 CONC 861, as second lieutenant Company E, 4th Virginia Infantry, and was mad
2 CONC e first lieutenant in November, 1862.  He was later discharged for disa
2 CONC bility, and assigned to duty in the quartermaster's department, where t
2 CONC he close of war found him faithfully serving.  He was at home at time o
2 CONC f surrender and never paroled.
