1 NOTE Rockingham Register, 1 Sep 1899 (obtained from Find A Grave, listing fo
2 CONC r Margaret Davis Smith Effinger):
2 CONT
2 CONT Death of Mrs. Margaret Effinger.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Born April 14, 1810; died August 25, 1899," are the words which will r
2 CONC ecord on her tomb the longevity of the estimable lady whose name heads t
2 CONC his article.  Nearly a century -- her birth almost coeval with that of t
2 CONC he republic; her life contemporaneous with its vicissitudes and glories
2 CONC !  Still, these many years but evidence a remarkable vitality.  It is t
2 CONC he manner in which they were passed that attracts our interest.
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret Smith was the daughter of Daniel Smith, one of Rockingham's mo
2 CONC st distinguished lawyers and judges.  Her mother was Fannie Duff, daugh
2 CONC ter of James Duff, whose mother was a Miss Strother.  The grandparents w
2 CONC ere both natives of Culpeper, but located in Harrisonburg when it was o
2 CONC nly a straggling village.  It was here, at the old "Duff Tavern," that M
2 CONC rs. Effinger's father and mother were married; and near Dayton, at "Wav
2 CONC erly"--a place now owned by Peter Paul--they spent most of their marrie
2 CONC d life.  It was at "Waverly" that Mrs. Effinger was born.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the admirable historical sketch of Rockingham County prepared by Jud
2 CONC ge John Paul, among the names of Rockingham men mentioned with commenda
2 CONC tion, that of Judge Smith stands second to none in professional eminenc
2 CONC e, and his moral attributes were as attractive as his judicial ability w
2 CONC as distinguished.  Under the eye of such a mentor and the loving watchf
2 CONC ulness of a devoted mother, in the midst of the honest, quiet people of R
2 CONC ockingham in these primitive days, the character of the girl was formed
2 CONC .  Possessing a discriminating judgment, a vivacious, cheerful temperam
2 CONC ent, and an affectionate disposition, under such a tutelage and surroun
2 CONC dings she ripened into the flower of womanhood, charming all who came w
2 CONC ithin the sphere of her influence.  Bright and joyous, tender and true, s
2 CONC he maintained to her dying day full sway over the affections of her fri
2 CONC ends.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Effinger's tenacious memory had stored away many pleasant reminisc
2 CONC ences of the olden time, and stirring events of social, political, and r
2 CONC eligious changes.  With the spice of anecdote and the wisdom of serious r
2 CONC eflection these were recounted to the new generations of relatives and f
2 CONC riends who were wont to seek information and pleasure in her society, u
2 CONC ntil she turned her face upon the world's probations and went to rest.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Effinger was twice married--in 1834 to John Craig, a lawyer, of St
2 CONC aunton, by whom she had one son, Robert Craig; and in 1845 to M. Harvey E
2 CONC ffinger, of Harrisonburg, a merchant and banker of rare business capaci
2 CONC ty.  By her second marriage she leaves one son, J. Fred. Effinger, at w
2 CONC hose home in Staunton she spent the latter years of her life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Effinger was a member of the Presbyterian church, but her religion w
2 CONC as above and superior to mere denominational rules, resting on the beau
2 CONC tiful precepts of Love as taught by the Master.
2 CONT
2 CONT The near relatives who survive her are her son, J. Fred. Effinger; her n
2 CONC iece, Mrs. John Kinney; her sister, Mrs. Tams; the children of her dece
2 CONC ased brother, Judge J. W. Green Smith; her nephew, John Kenny; her brot
2 CONC her-in-law, G. M. Effinger, and others who reside in Harrisonburg.  The f
2 CONC riends who mourn her demise include all who were even brought within th
2 CONC e magnetic influence of her charming personality.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral took place last Sunday afternoon from the First Presbyteria
2 CONC n church in Staunton.  The gentlemen who acted as pall-bearers were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Active--Major S. M. Yost, Capt. H. M. McIlhany, Capt. Thos. D. Ranson, C
2 CONC apt. Charles A. Holt, Hon. Edward Echols, Arista Hoge, Charles Catlett, a
2 CONC nd Henry A. Walker.
2 CONT
2 CONT Honorary-- D. A. Kayser, H. D. Peck, Isaac Witz, P. H. Trout, R. P. Bel
2 CONC l, and Capt. T. C. Morton.
2 CONT
2 CONT The interment was in Thornrose Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT
