1 _UID E733E6275C1E48D299969220726F35B769EC
1 CHAN
2 DATE 4 JUN 2020
1 NOTE Note that J. Fred Effinger and Frances Strother Smith were first cousin
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From Find A Grave (listing for Lieut John Frederick Effinger):
2 CONT
2 CONT Residence was not listed;
2 CONT Enlisted as a Private (date unknown).
2 CONT He was listed as:
2 CONT On rolls New Market, VA (date not stated) (Fought with infantry)
2 CONT Detailed 4/1/1864 (place not stated)
2 CONT (Detailed to Gen Echols to 04/29/64)
2 CONT Promotions:
2 CONT 2nd Lieut (Also aide on Gen Echols staff)
2 CONT He also had service in:
2 CONT VA Infantry (Also served)
2 CONT VA Mosby Cavalry (Served for several months)
2 CONT "F(2nd)" Co. VA 14th Cavalry
2 CONT born 5/13/1846 in Harrisonburg, VA
2 CONT died 3/9/1932 in Bronxville, NY
2 CONT
2 CONT At Virginia Military Institute 3 months over 2 years (March 19-May 26, 1
2 CONC 862, having been dismissed for general neglect and disorderly conduct;
2 CONT
2 CONT reinstated on July 19, 1862, but resigned Aug. 18, 1862 for "reasons sa
2 CONC tisfactory to the Superintendent."
2 CONT
2 CONT Military Record:
2 CONT According to an unofficial source, he ran off and joined Colonel J.S. M
2 CONC osby's Regiment of Virginia Cavalry (43d Battalion, Virginia Cavalry) a
2 CONC nd then at his father's request was transferred to be orderly for Briga
2 CONC dier General J.W. Echols (VMI Class of 1843);
2 CONT
2 CONT same souce indicates that he refused to clean Echols' boots, ran off, a
2 CONC nd joined infantry; bad health led to his transfer to cavalry and event
2 CONC ually back to Echols' staff;
2 CONT
2 CONT official records show that he was eleced 2d Lieutenant, Company G, 23d V
2 CONC irginia Battalion on either May 2, 9, or 20, 1864; Wounded in Action (n
2 CONC o location or date given but probably during September, 1864); spent Se
2 CONC p.-Oct. 1864 in a Harrisonburg hospital; reccomended for light duty by m
2 CONC edical board in Oct., 1864;
2 CONT
2 CONT Late 1864-early 1865 rolls show him on duty with Echols; paroled at Sta
2 CONC unton on May 16, 1865 as 2d Lieutenant, Aide-de-Camp to Echols.
2 CONT Post War Career:
2 CONT at the University of Virginia 1867-68; at Washington College 1869; work
2 CONC ed as a lawyer and coal operator.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: http://www.civilwardata.com/active/hdsquery.dll?SoldierHistory
2 CONC ?C&396832
2 CONT http://www.vmi.edu/archives/archiverosters/Details.asp?ID=2692&rform=se
2 CONC arch
2 CONT --
2 CONT While reading an article entitled 'Building the Rend and South Branches a
2 CONC nd South Side Mainline In New River Gorge, Part 1' by Wendell McChord, i
2 CONC n the Jul/Aug 2014 issue of the Cheaspeake & Ohio (Railway) Historical S
2 CONC ociety magazine, pages 6-24. Reference is made to J Fred Effinger (1846
2 CONC -1932) becoming president of the New River Consolidated Coal Company, w
2 CONC hich was formed to combat shrewd business actions by the Chesapeake & O
2 CONC hio Railway Coal Agency, managed by George H Ingalls, after his father M
2 CONC elville Ezra Ingalls was named preident of the Chesapeake & Ohio Railwa
2 CONC y Company by J P Morgan in 1888.
