1 NOTE From Find A Grave (listing for Capt John William Tobin):
2 CONT
2 CONT CAPTAIN JOHN WILLIAM TOBIN, was born on April 21, 1827, in Clarke count
2 CONC y, Ala.; the son of JOHN TOBIN and EMILY ELIZABETH PHELPS.  His father'
2 CONC s grandfather came from Dublin and settled in Barnwell district, South C
2 CONC arolina.  His mother's family originally came from England and settled i
2 CONC n Georgia.  Both families were loyal to the Colonies and took prominent p
2 CONC art in the early affairs of these colonies.  CAPTAIN TOBIN'S father fir
2 CONC st lived in Georgia and finally settled in Alabama, where he engaged in c
2 CONC otton planting.  He was successful, dying early and leaving an immense f
2 CONC ortune, which was dissipated after his death by Misfortune and disaster
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, young JOHN W. TOBIN had been thoroughly educated at Oxford, O
2 CONC ., in the full curriculum, thereby acquiring a love for literature, esp
2 CONC ecially history.  Upon his leaving college, in 1846, young TOBIN return
2 CONC ed to Mobile, enlisted as lieutenant in the Gaines Rifles for service i
2 CONC n the Mexican war, but, before the regiment reached Mexico, peace was d
2 CONC eclared.  Upon his return, he decided to settle in New Orleans, and, in 1
2 CONC 847, at the age of 20 years, he began steamboating, which was then in i
2 CONC ts infancy.  He was not successful and lost all he possessed.  In 1848 h
2 CONC e went to California, by way of Panama, to seek gold.  He was fairly su
2 CONC ccessful, and returned to New Orleans in 1853, where he settled permane
2 CONC ntly, and again essayed steamboating, buying a boat and operating her i
2 CONC n Ouachita and Mississippi rivers.  He was successful and, when the Civ
2 CONC il war began, had made quite a snug fortune, which was swept away by th
2 CONC e war.
2 CONT
2 CONT He served through the Civil war as a member of the Confederate army, fi
2 CONC rst in Virginia, being present at the Battle of Manassas; second, in th
2 CONC e naval warfare in the Mississippi and Yazoo rivers, and finally in the s
2 CONC ecret service in Mississippi, under GEN. FORREST.  During the war nearl
2 CONC y all the steamboats were either burned or destroyed to prevent their f
2 CONC alling into the hands of the Federals.  CAPT. TOBIN was always proud of h
2 CONC aving spurned an offer from the Federals of $300,000 cash for his boat, t
2 CONC he J. F. PARGOUD, preferring poverty to treachery; his reply was his ow
2 CONC n order "to burn his boat to prevent her falling into the hands of the e
2 CONC nemy."
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war, CAPT. TOBIN again returned to the river and engaged succ
2 CONC essfully for over 20 years in steamboating, up to the time of his death
2 CONC , on Sept. 13, 1888.  In those days, when there were few, if any, railr
2 CONC oads, steamboats were popular and river travel was delightful; when the r
2 CONC ailroads came the boats began to dwindle; now, there are few left.  Dur
2 CONC ing his career, CAPT. TOBIN was widely known, universally admired and g
2 CONC enerally recognized as a master mind, bold and fearless as he was kind h
2 CONC earted and generous.  He was a man of polish and refinement, of fine se
2 CONC nsibilities and dignified reserve, keen wit and humor.  He was universa
2 CONC lly loved and respected by every one who knew him.  He had lofty ideals a
2 CONC nd bold conceptions, which he seldom failed to execute.  He had a fine p
2 CONC hysique, and to him fear was unknown; he was generally recognized as a l
2 CONC eader of men-, a captain of industry.  During his life, he owned, or ma
2 CONC naged, over 60 steamboats, and only upon one occasion was a single life l
2 CONC ost upon any boat of his.  His boats were floating palaces, as fine and a
2 CONC s fast as money could produce.  He never stinted but gave bountifully; h
2 CONC is cuisine and appointments were perfect.  He prided himself upon havin
2 CONC g the largest, finest and fastest boat upon the Mississippi river, the f
2 CONC amous J M. WHITE, the climax and queen of the great river craft.  Among o
2 CONC ther boats owned by him were the 3 Pargouds, Vicksburg, R. W McRae, Wad
2 CONC e Hampton, Katie, Thompson Dean, Ed Richardson and others.  Ministers, p
2 CONC riests and sisters of charity were always welcome and carried free on h
2 CONC is boats.  He was the first to install electricity for general lighting o
2 CONC f boats.  Besides the boat business, CAPT. TOBIN was a pioneer in the m
2 CONC anufacture of cotton and cotton seed products.  He helped to found the f
2 CONC irst cotton factory in New Orleans, and was the principal founder and f
2 CONC irst president of the Planters oil mill, the pioneer cotton seed oil pl
2 CONC ant in this state.  In politics, he was a loyal democrat.  He never hel
2 CONC d an office, but was often urged for congress.
2 CONT
2 CONT In religion, was a Protestant.  On July 18, 1854, he married MISS MARY F
2 CONC RANCES SCOTT, the young and beautiful daughter of Judge C. C. SCOTT, of t
2 CONC he supreme court of Arkansas, and his talented wife, ELIZABETH SMITH, b
2 CONC oth of whose ancestry hold places in the colonial, revolutionary and ju
2 CONC dicial fame of this country.  MRS. JOHN W. TOBIN (1914) still lives and i
2 CONC s universally beloved and admired for her rare charms and lovable quali
2 CONC ties that endear her to all.  Their union was blessed with 7 children, o
2 CONC f whom 2 died in infancy 5 living, who are: (1) MARY GAILLARD TOBIN wid
2 CONC ow of CHARLES P. McCAN, since remarried; (2) FANNY JOHN TOBIN, wife of C
2 CONC APT.. THOMAS H. UNDERWOOD; (3) MAUDE EMILY ELIZABETH TOBIN, wife of LEO
2 CONC N G. GILBERT; (4) ELLEN VIRGINIA TOBIN, wife of ALBERT SIDNEY WHITE; (5
2 CONC ) JOHN FRANCIS TOBIN, who married MISS ELISKA PROVOSTY.
2 CONT
2 CONT CAPT. TOBIN was a man of truth and highest integrity; consecrated to du
2 CONC ty and fair dealing.  He was a lover of his home, his family and his fl
2 CONC owers.  While he was one of the founders of the Rex and. Proteus carniv
2 CONC al societies and a member of the Boston club, the Southern Yacht club a
2 CONC nd other clubs, he did not care for club life.  In his younger days he w
2 CONC as a lover of horses; was a charter member of the celebratedMetairie an
2 CONC d Louisiana race course clubs.  He was one of the founders of the Fair G
2 CONC rounds and the Cotton Centennial exposition.  He was always for general g
2 CONC ood.  He contributed liberally to the rebuilding of several churches.  H
2 CONC is charities were unbounded; he had a heart that always responded.
1 BIRT
2 DATE 21 APR 1827
2 PLAC Clarke Co., AL
