1 NOTE From the SpenReeve family tree site:
2 CONT
2 CONT <b>From the Kirksville Daily Express, Kirksville, Missouri, January 4, 1
2 CONC 993:</b>  Owen Wood Reeves, 81, of Brashear, Missouri, passed away Sund
2 CONC ay (January 3, 1993) at a Kirksville hospital.  He was born on October 2
2 CONC 6, 1911 in Howard County, Missouri, the son of Charles Payne and Nancy C
2 CONC atherine Ewens Reeves.  On October 28, 1930 in Colorado Springs, Colora
2 CONC do, he married Laura Lillian Hotchkiss and she preceded him in death on M
2 CONC arch 31, 1962.  On November 10, 1965 in Chillicothe, Missouri he marrie
2 CONC d Mary Beerbower and she survives.  Also surviving are eight sons; Owen R
2 CONC eeves and his wife Nita of Belleville, Illinois, Harold Reeves of Brash
2 CONC ear, Charles Reeves and his wife Paulette of Brashear, Tom Reeves and h
2 CONC is wife Carolyn of Shepherdsville, Kentucky, Jerry Reeves and his wife J
2 CONC anice of Kirksville, David Reeves of Kansas City, Missouri, Matt Reeves a
2 CONC nd his wife Cheryl of Hurdland; Mark Reeves and his wife Suzi of Brashe
2 CONC ar, seven daughters; Mrs. Bill (Doris) Hettinger of Brashear, Mary Stan
2 CONC sberry of Kirksville, Mrs. Larry (Naomi) Wagner of St. Charles, Missour
2 CONC i, Mrs. Charles (Bertha) Spencer of Tipton Missouri, Mrs. Garland (Sall
2 CONC y) Robinson of LaPlata, Missouri.  Mrs. Leo (Nancy) Hunolt of Brashear, M
2 CONC rs. Francis (Dolly) Tonkinson of Hurdland, two step sons; Danny Shirley o
2 CONC f Hurdland, Mike Shirley and his wife Carol of Brashear, one step daugh
2 CONC ter Mrs. Jerry (Sandy) Muir of Kirksville, 50 grandchildren, 38 great-g
2 CONC randchildren, one great-great-grandchild, one brother Woodrow Reeves, o
2 CONC f Centralia and several nieces and nephews.  He was preceded in death b
2 CONC y his parents, one son Stanley Reeves, three granddaughters, four broth
2 CONC ers, and three sisters.  Owen was raised in Howard County, where he att
2 CONC ended schools, and had also attended high school in Kirksville.  He wor
2 CONC ked for some time in the coal mines in Colorado, and during World War I
2 CONC I he worked on the pipe line in the Yukon territory.  Owen was engaged i
2 CONC n farming in the Brashear and Hurdland communities since 1937, from 195
2 CONC 0 to 1956 he worked for the Miller Trucking in Brashear, and for over 3
2 CONC 9 years had been employed by the United States Postal Service as a rura
2 CONC l mail carrier retiring August 1, 1992.  He was a member of the Rural C
2 CONC arriers Association and a member of the United Methodist Church in Bras
2 CONC hear, Missouri.  Funeral services will be held Tuesday (January 5, 1993
2 CONC ) at 2:00 P.M. in the Hudson-Rimer Park View Chapel in Kirksville.  The R
2 CONC everend Virgil Clow of Brashear will officiate the services assisted by t
2 CONC he Reverend Beryl Cragg of Kahoka, Missouri, and the Reverend Orville S
2 CONC hriver of LaPlata, Missouri.  Burial will be in Park View Memorial Gard
2 CONC ens in Kirksville.  Music will be provided by Margie Barker, Barbara Be
2 CONC erbower and Donna Sorenson.  Selections will be "One Day at a Time", an
2 CONC d "Sheltered in the Arms of God".  Casket bearers will be Raymond Salte
2 CONC r, Robert Magruder, James Helton, Charles Baker, Donald Beach and Howar
2 CONC d Hughes.  Honorary bearers will be Richard Hughes, Wesley Spiker, Robe
2 CONC rt Wilson, Roger Thompson, and Daren Miller.  Visitation will be from 2
2 CONC :00 to 9:00 P.M. Monday and until time of services on Tuesday with the f
2 CONC amily receiving friends from 7:00 to 9:00 P.M. Monday evening at Hudson
2 CONC -Rimer funeral Homes Park View Chapel, Kirksville, Missouri.  An expres
2 CONC sion of sympathy in memory of Mr. Reeves may be made to the Brashear Un
2 CONC ited Methodist Church.  The memorials may be left at or mailed to the H
2 CONC udson-Rimer park View Chapel, Route 4, Box 2, Kirksville, Missouri 6350
2 CONC 1.
