2 DATE 30 SEP 2019
1 NOTE Paducah Sun, 22 Mar 1906 (obtained from Find A Grave, listing for Rev J
2 CONC ohn Simon Cheek):
2 CONT
2 CONT REV. JOHN. S. CHEEK DIES IN WACO, TEX.
2 CONT
2 CONT Popular Pastor of First Baptist Church Expired at 11:40 p. m.
2 CONT
2 CONT Had Been Suffering From Nervous Breakdown When Pneumonia Developed.
2 CONT
2 CONT BURIAL AT MADISONVILLE
2 CONT
2 CONT Rev. John Simon Cheek, D. D., pastor of the First Baptist church, died a
2 CONC t 11:40 o'clock last night at Waco, Texas, from pneumonia.  The sad new
2 CONC s was received early this morning and was a shock to his hundreds of fr
2 CONC iends as well as to the members of his congregation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Cheek had been ill for several weeks.  Soon after the revival began a
2 CONC t his church last November his health appeared to be failing, and final
2 CONC ly he suffered a nervous breakdown which necessitated his abandoning hi
2 CONC s work temporarily and going to Hot Springs, Ark.  He seemed to improve a
2 CONC nd returned in about two weeks.  Members of his congregation noticed a g
2 CONC reat change in him, and it was rumored that the work and excitement had m
2 CONC ade inroads on his mind as well as health.  It was decided to send him b
2 CONC ack to Hot Springs, and he was accompanied this time by his wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reports from there from time to time indicated his improvement, as he w
2 CONC as suffering only from a nervous breakdown, and from Hot Springs he and h
2 CONC is wife went to Waco, Tex., where they had relatives.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reports of his condition continued to be encouraging, but last Thursday
2 CONC , while taking an outing at Corpus Christi, he had a chill, and was tak
2 CONC en back to Waco Friday.  Pneumonia developed and his condition grew wor
2 CONC se.  Last night a report was received that he was dying, and news of hi
2 CONC s death reached the city early this morning.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Cheek was born August 26th, 1863, at Burksville, Campbell [sic] cou
2 CONC nty, Kentucky.  When 19 [?] years old he went to Lebanon, O., to attend c
2 CONC ollege, and later went to Madisonville, Ky., and taught school.  He was m
2 CONC arried there 21 years ago, July 21st, to Miss Minnie Lee Wells, and aft
2 CONC erwards taught school in Dixon and Kuttawa, Ky.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was ordained a minister of the gospel when 23 years old, and had bee
2 CONC n preaching ever since.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his marriage he attended Bethel college, at Russellville, and his f
2 CONC irst pulpit was at Earlington, Ky., then to Carrollton, Ky.
2 CONT
2 CONT He went to Louisville and attended the theological seminary for two yea
2 CONC rs.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Louisville he went to Salem church, in South Christian, 12 miles f
2 CONC rom Hopkinsville.  He was in charge of the church there for five years, a
2 CONC nd then took charge of the Baptist church at Russellville, Ky., from wh
2 CONC ich place he came to Paducah last April.  The degree of doctor of divin
2 CONC ity was conferred on him in 1904.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Cheek was a very popular man as well as minister.  He was distincti
2 CONC vely a self-made man and had worked hard and long to achieve the positi
2 CONC on and success he enjoyed in the church.  He was never discouraged, and y
2 CONC ear after year added to his popularity and success, and the past year w
2 CONC as the greatest of all.  His death is made all the sadder because he wa
2 CONC s in the prime of life, and had such a promising career before him.
2 CONT
2 CONT He leaves a wife and five children, four boys and one daughter.  The ch
2 CONC ildren are: John Frank, aged 20, Major Oswald aged 17, Oscar Henry, age
2 CONC d 12, Lawrence McKenzie, aged 8, and Mary Wells, aged 1 year.
2 CONT
2 CONT His wife was the only member of the family with him in Texas.
2 CONT
2 CONT He leaves two brothers in Cumberland county, Ky., J. O. and W. H. Cheek
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Cheek was a Mason and had his membership transferred to Paducah whe
2 CONC n he came here.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second telegram was received this morning stating that the body lef
2 CONC t Waco at 10 o'clock this morning and would arrive tomorrow at noon.  T
2 CONC he funeral services will be held in Paducah Saturday morning, interment S
2 CONC unday morning at Madisonville.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Paducah Sun, 23 Mar 1906 (obtained from Find A Grave, listing for Rev J
2 CONC ohn Simon Cheek):
2 CONT
2 CONT The remains of the late Rev. John S. Cheek, pastor of the First Baptist
2 CONC s church, arrived in the city over the Illinois Central this afternoon a
2 CONC t 12:20 o'clock from Waco, Texas, where he died Wednesday night from pn
2 CONC eumonia, and were taken to the Baptist parsonage on North Fifth street.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Cheek accompanied the body, and has stood the trip fairly well.  I
2 CONC t was intended to hold the funeral this afternoon, but it was decided a
2 CONC fter consulting the family to hold it tomorrow morning at 10 o'clock at t
2 CONC he First Baptist church, services by Rev. George Cates and Rev. Gordon H
2 CONC ill, of Louisville.
2 CONT
2 CONT The burial will be in Paducah, at Oak Grove cemetery, instead of at Mad
2 CONC isonville, as at first announced.
