1 _UID 013D74424CEC49E09647A05DC7DD469AB3CE
1 CHAN
2 DATE 9 APR 2019
1 NOTE From the SpenReeve family tree site:
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Kirksville Daily Express:  Dr. William Thomas Reeves, 72, of R
2 CONC oute 2, Brashear, was pronounced dead at the scene after he was struck b
2 CONC y lighting while driving a farm tractor yesterday afternoon.  Dr. Reeve
2 CONC s, his wife and her grandson, had been repairing fences on the farm, an
2 CONC d were returning home as it started to rain about 3:30 p.m.  Mrs. Reeve
2 CONC s told authorities that she was trying to shield the 7-year-old boy fro
2 CONC m the rain and did not notice that anything was wrong until the tractor c
2 CONC rossed a small ditch and started up an embankment toward a fence.  She s
2 CONC aid that when she looked up, she noticed that the tractor was running o
2 CONC ut of control, and that her husband's head had fallen backward.  Both M
2 CONC rs. Reeves and the little boy were knocked from the small lift-like box i
2 CONC n which they were riding when the tractor struck the fence.  They were a
2 CONC lmost one-quarter mile from the house and the nearest telephone when th
2 CONC e accident happened, according to Sheriff Leon Coy and Mrs. Reeves ran t
2 CONC he full distance to summon help.  Coy said that he radioed the police d
2 CONC epartment, asking for aid from the highway patrol on his way to the sce
2 CONC ne.  The fire department was also contacted to send a truck and resusci
2 CONC tation equipment to the scene.  Coy said that all the aid arrived withi
2 CONC n ten minutes, but all were to late to help the doctor.  "There is abso
2 CONC lutely no debt that it was lighting," Coy said, explaining the Dr. Reev
2 CONC es' hair was singed and his forehead marked where a lightning bolt had s
2 CONC truck him.  Coy explained also the the rain and mud were probably respo
2 CONC nsible for the lightning striking so thoroughly insulated an object as a t
2 CONC ractor.  "It was raining hard and the tractor tires were quite muddy at t
2 CONC hat time."  Coy pointed out, "and it is possible that his shoe or foot c
2 CONC ould have been touching the muddy tire when the bolt struck."  Mrs. Ree
2 CONC ves said that she had "noticed the terribly bright flash" but that it n
2 CONC ever occurred to her that it had struck anything so close as the tracto
2 CONC r ahead of her.  Coy said that the doctor was apparently killed instant
2 CONC ly.  The body is at the Funeral Home of Davis & Davis.  Funeral arrange
2 CONC ments will be announced later.  The son of Charles and Kitty an (Ewing) R
2 CONC eeves, he was born Jan. 31, 1896, at Centralia.  On Nov 6, 1961 in Miam
2 CONC i, Okla., he married Nellie Nelson who survives.  Also surviving are tw
2 CONC o sons, Dr. G. A. Reeves of Corpus Christi, Tex., and Dr. William N. Re
2 CONC eves of Boulder, Colo.; five step-children, Raymond Lee Phipps of the h
2 CONC ome, Mrs. Stanley Jennings of Kansas City, Mrs. Richard Potuclk and Mrs
2 CONC . George Davis, both of Moberly, and Lester Phipps of Lake Charles, La.
2 CONC ; three sisters, Mrs. Frank Sarlis of Fayette, Mrs. Frank Long of Calga
2 CONC ry, Canada, and Georgia Muzzy of Portland, Ore.; five brothers, Woodrow R
2 CONC eeves and E.A. Reeves, both of Centralia, Owen Reeves of Hurdland, Otha R
2 CONC eeves of Torance, Calif., and Ben Reeves of Hartley, Ia., and 17 grandc
2 CONC hildren.  Dr. Reeves attended public schools in Harrisburg and graduate
2 CONC d from Northeast Missouri State College.  In 1924 he received his degre
2 CONC e from the American School of Osteopathy in Kirksville.  Following his g
2 CONC raduation he practiced in Kirksville and served as public school doctor f
2 CONC rom 1926 until 1949 when he moved to Kansas City.  In 1964 he retired a
2 CONC nd returned to his farm east of Kirksville on Rt. 2, Brashear.  He was a m
2 CONC ember of the First Baptist Church of Kirksville and a former member of t
2 CONC he Masonic lodge.
