1 NOTE In her listing on Find A Grave she is called Fannie, but the obituary i
2 CONC ncluded calls her Nannie and the entry for her father (listing for Chan
2 CONC cellor Benjamin C. Buckley) calls her Nancy.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Lawrence County Press, Monticello, MS, 2 Aug 1900 (obtained from Find A G
2 CONC rave, listing for Fannie E. Buckley Creegan):
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Nannie E. Creegan, of Orange, N. J., who died in a hospital in New Y
2 CONC ork City on the night of the 16th July, was brought here for burial, an
2 CONC d her remains were interred beside those of her illustrious father, Cha
2 CONC ncellor B. C. Buckley, in the town cemetery, on Saturday, the 21st July
2 CONC .  The remains were accompanied on their long journey by Miss Marguerit
2 CONC e Morgan, an only daughter of the deceased, and at Brookhaven she was j
2 CONC oined by Hon. Hiram Cassedy, Jr., a friend of the family and also their f
2 CONC ormer attorney in this section.  Mrs. Creegan was 35 years of age and w
2 CONC as quite prominent in Washington circles, where she lived for many year
2 CONC s.  She was a woman of rare beauty and remarkable talents.  Her father, m
2 CONC ore commonly known as General Buckley, was a jurist of distinction in a
2 CONC ntebellum days and was living in Monticello at the time of his death in 1
2 CONC 868.  Mrs. Creegan was quite young when her mother removed from this pl
2 CONC ace and, of course, there are but very few of the old settlers who reme
2 CONC mber her.  The funeral services were conducted by Rev. J. P. Williams.
