2 CONT Mrs. Elvina Jackson was tried by a jury in Justice S. W. Smith's court a
2 CONC t Westville last Saturday for disturbing the peace of Miss Willie Rutle
2 CONC dge, a school marm, and was fined $1 and costs.  The defendant's fine a
2 CONC nd costs amounted to something over $27, which she made arrangements to p
2 CONC ay.  The trouble arose over Miss Rutledge's attempt to make a daughter o
2 CONC f Mrs. Jackson quit chewing gum in school.  When informed of the "indig
2 CONC nity" offered her daughter, Mrs. Jackson donned her war paint and went t
2 CONC o the school-house with the avowed purpose of giving the teacher a thra
2 CONC shing.  The irate mother's arrest followed with the result given above.
