2 DATE 22 AUG 2020
1 NOTE There is a listing in the 1850 Cumberland County census for M.A. Donley (
2 CONC female) living with Anne and Walter Cole, Mary and John Cheek, and Will
2 CONC iam Donley.  Some family trees have assumed that this is Martha Stockto
2 CONC n Donley, and the others are children of James D. Donley -- though that w
2 CONC ould be a bit odd because James D. Donley was living nearby in the 1850 c
2 CONC ensus, but not with M.A.  The initial M leaves open the possibility, bu
2 CONC t it is fairly certain that Martha was dead by then (see her notes), pl
2 CONC us M.A. Donley is listed as Mary A. Donley in the Slave Schedule for th
2 CONC e 1850 census.  (She owned one slave.  James is also on the slave sched
2 CONC ule, with seven slaves.)
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bailey Famiy Tree (https://worldconnect.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-b
2 CONC in/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=fredexbailey&id=I787) has a note about a deed in C
2 CONC umberland County, KY, Deed Book M, p. 302, dated 23 Mar 1852, for land f
2 CONC rom Oscar J.E. Stewart of Washington County, MS, to Wm. C. Donley and M
2 CONC ary Cheek, wife of John L. Cheek (children of late Mary S. Donley, sist
2 CONC er of said Stewart).  In that instance the S presumably stands for Stew
2 CONC art.  Elsewhere in the note Polly A. Donely and some other Stewarts are r
2 CONC eferred to as heirs of Elizabeth Stuart, dec'd, heir of Peyton J. Stock
2 CONC ton (alias John P.), dec'd, who was an heir of John Stockton dec'd ... l
2 CONC ate of Pittsylvania County, VA.  Note that Polly was commonly a nicknam
2 CONC e for Mary, and also that Martha (Patsey) Stockton was also an heir of J
2 CONC ohn Stockton (see her notes), so apparently Mary Stewart and Martha Sto
2 CONC ckton were related.  Also note that there are spellings of both Stewart a
2 CONC nd Stuart in the above note, and recall that the Parks manuscript says t
2 CONC hat James Donley's brother William married Mary Stuart, who was a niece o
2 CONC f James' wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT The above sounds to me like Mary Stuart Donley had already passed away (
2 CONC "late Mary S. Donley").  The Bailey Family Tree says she died after 185
2 CONC 2 (https://worldconnect.rootsweb.ancestry.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db
2 CONC =fredexbailey&id=I883), but I wonder if that is just a typo.  Bailey ha
2 CONC s another note, from a Family History by Ralph Sevier Stockton, that Ma
2 CONC ry "inherited the family homestead after the death of her mother; it wa
2 CONC s left by Mary Stuart Donnelly to Mrs. Mary Donnelly Cheek Ewing."  Dau
2 CONC ghter Mary didn't marry Matthew Ewing until 1874, but that could just b
2 CONC e a description by the author rather wording from a will or deed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bailey Family Tree has Mary Stuart, sister of Oscar James E. Stuart
2 CONC , granddaughter of John Stockton.  Her parents were James Stuart and El
2 CONC izabeth Stockton, who was a sister of Martha Stockton.  There is anothe
2 CONC r family tree on Ancestry.com (https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/per
2 CONC son/tree/37988997/person/19166912371/facts) that has those relationship
2 CONC s as well, and also shows Mary’s nickname of Polly and her husband as D
2 CONC onley (no first name).  Everything fits for her husband to be William D
2 CONC onley.  This tree lists Mary S. Donley and William C. Donley as childre
2 CONC n of Mary Stuart.  Anne is not listed, possibly because she is not list
2 CONC ed in the 1852 deed.  It seems likely from this omission that Anne had a
2 CONC lready passed away by 1852.  It might also mean that there were no othe
2 CONC r older children still living.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Polly is mentioned in a letter from William L. Reeves to Jeanette (Reev
2 CONC es) Leonard, dated Jun 1846:  "I received a letter yesterday from Aunt P
2 CONC . Donley of Cumberland County.  She writes me they have been a good dea
2 CONC l afflicted with the chills this spring."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT A final note is that I have DNA matches on Ancestry.com with two descen
2 CONC dants of Mary and John Cheek, which bolsters the case that Mary Cheek w
2 CONC as William Donley's daughter (it could also work for her to be James Do
2 CONC nley's daughter, but the other evidence argues against that).  They are w
2 CONC eak DNA matches, but that's not surprising since they would be sixth co
2 CONC usins to me.
