2 DATE 25 OCT 2019
1 NOTE From http://www.hardwoodfloorsdenver.com/History-Evans_Ranch.pdf :
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1868, John Evans, the second territorial governor of Colorado, appoi
2 CONC nted by President Lincoln in 1862, took a camping trip into the mountai
2 CONC ns.  Sam Elbert, who later became Colorado’s sixth territorial governor a
2 CONC s well as the son-in-law of John Evans, was in the party.  Evans and El
2 CONC bert were impressed by the lush grass, thick timber, beautiful vistas a
2 CONC nd abundant game and fish in the Upper Bear Creek area.  They decided t
2 CONC o acquire some 340 acres from a homesteader.  In 1870 John Evans and Sa
2 CONC m Elbert built a large family house and continued to buy adjacent land.  I
2 CONC n all, the Evans-Elbert Ranch grew to over 5,000 acres and covered thre
2 CONC e mountain valleys.  It was a great summer retreat.  It allowed two pol
2 CONC iticians and their families to escape the heat of Denver and the heat o
2 CONC f the politics of the time!
2 CONT
2 CONT The original family summer structure burned to the ground in 1909.  The f
2 CONC amilies and relatives and descendants of Evans and Elbert no longer had a s
2 CONC ummer retreat.  Louise Elbert, Governor Sam Elbert’s niece, and her ent
2 CONC repreneurial husband Leonard Everett, built a family house near the cen
2 CONC ter of the Evans-Elbert Ranch.  This house was called Wind-in-Woven, an
2 CONC d is now the Elbert-Austin Ranch house.  Other Evans family members and d
2 CONC escendants soon built other family retreats on the larger Ranch.  Each h
2 CONC ad a unique western style.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wind-in-Woven was built in the “western rustic” Craftsman style.  Jock S
2 CONC pence was the builder and the Hiwan Homestead Museum in Evergreen pays t
2 CONC ribute to his many buildings in the Evergreen area.  The Elbert-Austin R
2 CONC anch is now on the National Historic Register because of the history of t
2 CONC wo territorial governors and the fine western rustic example of the Cra
2 CONC ftsman style.
