2 CONT U. S. Circuit Court Judge Shackelford Miller Jr., who served on the Cou
2 CONC rt of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit, Cincinnati, from 1945 until his re
2 CONC tirement last November 4, died Wednesday in Louisville, which was his h
2 CONC ome.  He was 73.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Appeals Court, of which Judge Miller became a esnior [sic] judge at h
2 CONC is retirement, handles cases in the states of Ohio, Kentucky, Tennessee a
2 CONC nd Michigan.
2 CONT
2 CONT A native of Louisville, Judge Miller came from a distinguished family o
2 CONC f attorneys and political leaders.  He had been a member of the Federal J
2 CONC udiciary since 1939, when he was appointed a district judge in Louisvil
2 CONC le.
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, Shackelford Miller Sr., was a former chief justice of the K
2 CONC entucky Court of Appeals and his brother, Neville Miller, now a Washing
2 CONC ton, D. C., attorney, was mayor of Louisville at the time of the 1937 f
2 CONC lood.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Judge Miller received his first Federal appointment in 1939, it wa
2 CONC s following his successful management of the re-election campaign of th
2 CONC e late Sen. Alben W. Barkley in the previous year.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was graduated from Princeton University, and received his law degree a
2 CONC t the Harvard Law School in 1917.  The following August he enlisted in t
2 CONC he First Officers' Training School at Ft. Meyers, Virginia, and served o
2 CONC verseas in World War I.  He won promotion to captain in the 325th Field A
2 CONC rtillery.
2 CONT
2 CONT In addition to his brother, Judge Miller leaves a daughter, Mrs. Margar
2 CONC et H. Martin, Louisville.
