1 NOTE The Handbook of Texas Online (article on Haden Edwards):
2 CONT
2 CONT EDWARDS, HADEN (1771-1849).  Haden (or Hayden) Edwards, pioneer settler a
2 CONC nd land speculator, was born in Stafford County, Virginia, on August 12
2 CONC , 1771, the son of John Edwards.  In 1780 the family moved to Bourbon C
2 CONC ounty, Kentucky (at that time part of Virginia), where John Edwards acq
2 CONC uired 23,000 acres of land, worked for statehood, and was elected to th
2 CONC e United States Senate.  Haden was educated for the law but like his fa
2 CONC ther was more interested in land speculation.  In 1820 he married Susan
2 CONC na Beall of Maryland, and they moved to the area of Jackson, Mississipp
2 CONC i, where he and his brother Benjamin W. Edwardsqv acquired a plantation
2 CONC .  He and Susanna eventually had thirteen children.  In Mississippi the E
2 CONC dwardses first heard the news of Moses Austin'sqv plans for colonizatio
2 CONC n in Texas.  In 1823 Edwards traveled to Mexico City, where he joined S
2 CONC tephen F. Austin, Robert Leftwich,qqv and others in a three-year attemp
2 CONC t to persuade various Mexican governments to authorize American settlem
2 CONC ent in Texas.  Because of his wealth Edwards was often called upon to f
2 CONC inance Austin.  Their efforts resulted in the colonization law of 1824 i
2 CONC n Mexico City and of 1825 in Saltillo, which allowed empresarios to int
2 CONC roduce settlers to Texas (see MEXICAN COLONIZATION LAWS, 1821-30).  Edw
2 CONC ards suffered more than he profited from his relationship with Austin, a
2 CONC t least in his own mind, since he believed that Austin claimed the best l
2 CONC ands and tried to push his boundaries in every direction at the expense o
2 CONC f other empresarios (see EMPRESARIO).
2 CONT
2 CONT Edwards received a grant in the vicinity of Nacogdoches where he could l
2 CONC ocate 800 families. Like other empresarios he agreed to honor preexisti
2 CONC ng grants and claims made by Spanish or Mexican officials. Of all empre
2 CONC sarios, Edwards probably had the most such claims, some over a century o
2 CONC ld.  In 1825 he posted notices to inform all potential claimants that t
2 CONC hey must come forward with proof of their claims or he would consider t
2 CONC he land his, subject to sale to new settlers.  This angered the older s
2 CONC ettlers, who opposed Edwards until he was expelled two years later.  He a
2 CONC lso became involved in an election dispute between the representative o
2 CONC f the older settlers, Samuel Norris,qv and Chichester Chaplin,qv Edward
2 CONC s's son-in-law.  As empresario, Edwards certified the election of Chapl
2 CONC in.  Norris then protested to Governor José Antonio Saucedoqv in San An
2 CONC tonio, and Saucedo upheld Norris's claim to office. However, Chaplin co
2 CONC ntinued to hold the position until Norris requested aid from the local m
2 CONC ilitia. Continued complaints from the area caused Edwards to come under s
2 CONC uspicion, and his brother Benjamin, who handled business affairs while H
2 CONC aden was absent from Texas in 1826, addressed such strident corresponde
2 CONC nce to government officials that it resulted in the revocation of the E
2 CONC dwards grant in October of that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edwards was shocked by this turn of events.  He had invested more than $
2 CONC 50,000 to secure and launch the grant, and he did not willingly surrend
2 CONC er it.  Additionally, the cancellation of his grant resulted in the for
2 CONC feiture of the claims of all settlers who had moved onto his lands. Thu
2 CONC s, when the events known as the Fredonian Rebellion,qv which the Edward
2 CONC s brothers eventually headed, began the following month, the Edwards gr
2 CONC antees were most supportive. In November 1826 Edwards was arrested as a r
2 CONC use.  When no one appeared at his trial as an accuser he was freed, but N
2 CONC orris and militia chief José Antonio Sepúlveda were found guilty and ju
2 CONC dged deserving of the death sentence, which was commuted to banishment f
2 CONC rom office by this extralegal tribunal.  News of the uprising reached t
2 CONC he Mexican authorities, who dispatched Lt. Col. Mateo Ahumada to Nacogd
2 CONC oches.  Learning that troops were on their way, Martin Parmerqv and Ben
2 CONC jamin Edwards recruited the Ayish Bayou militia to come to town as well
2 CONC .  They signed articles establishing the Fredonian Republic, with Haden E
2 CONC dwards as its leader.  An alliance was also made with Cherokee Indians l
2 CONC ed by Richard Fields and John Dunn Hunter,qqv who also had grievances a
2 CONC gainst the government. Before an armed clash occurred the Fredonians di
2 CONC spersed, in early February 1827, and Edwards fled to Louisiana for safe
2 CONC ty.  He returned to Texas during the Texas Revolutionqv and made his ho
2 CONC me in Nacogdoches until his death, on August 14, 1849.  Edwards was the f
2 CONC irst worshipful master of Milam Lodge No. 2 when it was organized in 18
2 CONC 37, a fact that indicates his status in the Anglo leadership.  Until hi
2 CONC s death he was engaged in the land business.
