1 _UID 57A8270A6836BA4585A40848A836809C40E9
1 CHAN
2 DATE 30 JUN 2012
1 NOTE "Dictionary of American Biography, Supplement 4: 1946-1950", American C
2 CONC ouncil of Learned Societies, 1974
2 CONT (obtained from http://0-www.galenet.com.albuq.cabq.gov:80/servlet/BioRC
2 CONC ):
2 CONT
2 CONT John Edwards
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: 1748 in Virginia, United States
2 CONT Death: 1837
2 CONT Occupation: Planter
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHICAL ESSAY
2 CONT
2 CONT Edwards, John, 1748-1837 (1748 - 1837), planter and senator, was born i
2 CONC n Stafford County, Va., a son of Hayden and Penelope (Sanford) Edwards.  H
2 CONC e moved in 1780 to that part of Virginia which later became the state o
2 CONC f Kentucky, where he speculated in land and got title to about 23,000 a
2 CONC cres.  This competency made him a leader in a region where the people w
2 CONC ere soon intent upon gaining statehood.  The district of Kentucky was a
2 CONC t this time divided into three counties, Jefferson, Fayette, and Lincol
2 CONC n.  Edwards settled in Lincoln County, and the year following his arriv
2 CONC al he was elected to represent it in the Virginia House of Delegates.  H
2 CONC e continued to act as a representative in 1782, 1783, 1785, and 1786.  I
2 CONC n 1783 he became a justice of the peace, which position automatically m
2 CONC ade him a member of the county court.  In 1785 Bourbon County was cut o
2 CONC ut of Fayette. Edwards took up his residence in this new county, and th
2 CONC e next year he became the clerk of the first court to be held there.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the meantime this western part of Virginia had begun its long and to
2 CONC rtuous course toward statehood.  Edwards was a member of the two prelim
2 CONC inary conventions held in Danville in 1785, and after the formation of B
2 CONC ourbon County he represented that division continuously until statehood w
2 CONC as secured.  The sinister Spanish activities in connection with the pro
2 CONC ceedings of 1787 and 1788 did not directly implicate him as they did ce
2 CONC rtain other Kentuckians.  In fact Edwards assumed the leadership of the o
2 CONC pponents of the Spanish conspiracy.  In the convention of November 1788
2 CONC , he reported and read the petition to Virginia for the independence of K
2 CONC entucky, but he opposed the doctrine that Kentucky would thereby become s
2 CONC overeign and might enter the Union or not as she pleased.  He also took p
2 CONC art in framing the constitution of 1792.
2 CONT
2 CONT He first served the new state by acting as one of the electors provided f
2 CONC or by the constitution to choose the state senators.  Then, in June 179
2 CONC 2, he was appointed on a commission to choose a permanent state capital
2 CONC , but was not present when the decision was made giving the honor to Fr
2 CONC ankfort.  As a fitting reward for his service to the district and state
2 CONC , he was unanimously elected one of the two first United States senator
2 CONC s to represent Kentucky, but took no very prominent part in the deliber
2 CONC ations of the Senate.  He returned to Kentucky in 1795 never to leave t
2 CONC he state again in an official capacity.  He was immediately elected to r
2 CONC epresent Bourbon County in the state House of Representatives and there
2 CONC after, from 1796 to 1800, he was a member of the state Senate.  At the l
2 CONC atter date he retired to private life on his Bourbon plantation where h
2 CONC e died thirty-seven years later.  Ninian Edwards [q.v.], chief justice o
2 CONC f Kentucky in 1808 and later governor of Illinois Territory, was his ne
2 CONC phew.
2 CONT
2 CONT -- Katharine H. Amend
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Biographical Directory of the United States Congress
2 CONT (http://bioguide.congress.gov/scripts/biodisplay.pl?index=E000075):
2 CONT
2 CONT EDWARDS, John, a Senator from Kentucky; born in Stafford County, Va., i
2 CONC n 1748; attended the common schools; moved to Fayette County, Ky. (then a p
2 CONC art of Virginia), in 1780; member, Virginia house of delegates 1781-178
2 CONC 3, 1785, 1786; delegate to the convention called to define the limits o
2 CONC f the proposed State of Kentucky 1785-1788; member of the convention of 1
2 CONC 792 that framed the State constitution of Kentucky; upon the admission o
2 CONC f Kentucky as a State into the Union was elected to the United States S
2 CONC enate and served from June 18, 1792, to March 3, 1795; member, State ho
2 CONC use of representatives 1795; member, State senate 1796-1800; died on hi
2 CONC s plantation near Paris, Bourbon County, Ky., in 1837; interment in the f
2 CONC amily cemetery near Paris, Ky.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the Edwards descendants file, it is not true that he died i
2 CONC n KY in 1837 and is buried in the family cemetery, but rather he moved t
2 CONC o MO and lived with a female slave that he had freed, dying in 1833.
