1 NOTE WFT 137 # 299:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Trewolla was born September 17 or 18, 1769 in Truro, Cornwall Coun
2 CONC ty, England.  He was christened on November 21, 1769 in Truro, Cornwall C
2 CONC ounty, England.  He was married on December 3, 1793 at St. Clement's Pa
2 CONC rish, Truro, Cornwall County, England to Margaret H. Pearce, who was ch
2 CONC ristened on September 10, 1769 in Illogan Parish, Cornwall County, Engl
2 CONC and.  They had ten children.  He was the only son of Henry to go to Ame
2 CONC rica.  John and his brother, Samuel, were in the Army during the Napole
2 CONC nic War preceding 1808 and they were left two-thirds of thier father's e
2 CONC state, because they were not so well established as their brothers.  Jo
2 CONC hn was a tailor and was the first of the Trewollas to follow this trade
2 CONC .  John and Margaret came to America at the age of 70 on the barque Vit
2 CONC toria, 650 tons burden.  The Commander was Moses Simpson.  They sailed A
2 CONC pril 1, 1841 from Maplun on the Truro River for Quebec.  John kept a di
2 CONC ary of his journey.  He died in Ballard County, Kentucky on September 1
2 CONC 7, 1841.  Margaret died on January 20, 1861 in Ballard or McCracken Cou
2 CONC nty, Kentucky. A ten inch column in the Ballard County newspaper was de
2 CONC voted to recalling her early years in England, in praise of her charact
2 CONC er.  In Cornwall, the Trewollas were members of the Church of England, b
2 CONC ut Margaret remembered the preaching of elder John Wesley and his visit
2 CONC s to her home.  She accepted Christianity under his teaching at the age o
2 CONC f 14.  She became an active member of the Methodist Society that then f
2 CONC unctioned within the membership fo St. Mary's Parish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Excerpts from John Trewolla's diary beginning with the April 1, 1841 oc
2 CONC ean crossing:
2 CONT April 1st, went on board the barque Vittoria, Captain Moses Simpson at M
2 CONC alpas, bound to Quebec.  Paid 13 pounds, 10 shillings for passage.
2 CONT April 2nd, sailed from Malpas for Falmouth.
2 CONT April 3rd, Saturday, quit Falmouth about 8 o'clock in the morning, with a f
2 CONC air wind all day.  Lost sight of land in evening.  Past Lilly Light abo
2 CONC ut 12 o'clock at night.
2 CONT April 4th, Sunday night, had a most tremendous storm that I shall not s
2 CONC oon forget.
2 CONT April 5th, Monday, heavy seas and high wind.
2 CONT April 11th, Easter Sunday, beautiful weather, at night going 8 knots.
2 CONT April 12th, Monday, threw a bottle overboard with this note "April 12, 1
2 CONC 841 ship Vittoria, Captain Moses Simpson from Truro to Quebec, Latitude 4
2 CONC 3:48, Longitude 17:20 all well Jo. Trewolla, Truro, Cornwall, passenger
2 CONC ."
2 CONT April 20th, Tuesday, going 7 to 9 knots, past the Lyra Packet, homeward b
2 CONC ound from Brazil.  The men murmur about their meals.
2 CONT April 21st, five vessels in sights.  About 1300 miles from Falmouth.   I
2 CONC n the evening saw a shark alongside.
2 CONT April 24th, 25th, 26th, a hurricane, the sea running mountains high, on
2 CONC e of the boys fell on deck and taken below as dead.
2 CONT May 7th, Friday, saw an iceberg.
2 CONT May 8th, Saturday, got on banks of Newfoundland.  More icebergs passing
2 CONC .
2 CONT May 13th, Thursday, land in sight, 20 days and saw no land.
2 CONT May 15th, plenty of snow on the land of Cape Breton.
2 CONT May 26th, Wednesday, saw the most beautiful sight in the world so many h
2 CONC undreds of vessels in a line past Camel Island, Anticosti, Cape Torment a
2 CONC nd on the Canadian side from Camash to Quebec, some large and handsome h
2 CONC ouse for more than 100 miles.
2 CONT May 27th, Thursday, the vessel visited by the doctor and a clean bill o
2 CONC f health, 40 in number.
2 CONT May 31st, Monday, sailed from Quebec.
2 CONT June 2nd, Wednesday, arrived Montreal.  Lodged at William Laphand's unt
2 CONC il Thursday morning.
2 CONT June 3rd, Thursday, quitted Montreal for Laprairie.  Left about 11 by t
2 CONC he railroad train.  Got to St. John's and went on board a steamer for W
2 CONC hitehall.
2 CONT June 4th, Friday, arrived at Whitehall by 6 o'clock in the morning.  We
2 CONC nt on board a canal towboat for Albany.  Got to Troy by 6 o'clock, Alba
2 CONC ny by 8 o'clock.
2 CONT June 6th, Sunday, got the luggage on thye quay by 4 o'clock for New Yor
2 CONC k, sailed by 5 o'clock.
2 CONT June 7th, Monday, got to New York by half past 4 in the morning.  Found M
2 CONC r. Holt and lodged that night at a house three doors from his.
2 CONT June 8th, Tuesday, wrote to John and Uncle Teava.
2 CONT June 14th, a letter from John Teava and a 20 dollar bill.
2 CONT June 19th, quitted New York for Richmond, all sick at night.
2 CONT July 6th, left Richmond for Oxford, North Carolina by wagons.  Went to J
2 CONC ohn Trewolla's house.
2 CONT
2 CONT September 17, 1846 quitted Young's X-Roads after dinner.  Stopped all n
2 CONC ight at Mr. Robert Downey's about three miles.
2 CONT September 22nd, went past Caswell Court House (Leesburg, North Carolina
2 CONC )
2 CONT September 24th, completed 100 miles.
2 CONT September 26th, Friday, in sight of the Blue Ridge Mountains 45 miles a
2 CONC way.
2 CONT September 27th, Saturday, passed Pilot Mountains, 300 feet high.
2 CONT September 28th, Sunday, go to Mount Airy, North Carolina.
2 CONT September 29th, crossed the Blue Ridge before dinner at a place called C
2 CONC old Spring (Lowgap, North Carolina at the North Carolina and Virginia s
2 CONC tate line)
2 CONT September 30th, go to Graten Court House (Galax, Virginia) crossed a ri
2 CONC ver 300 yards wide (Carroll River)
2 CONT October 3rd, Saturday, to Seven Mile Ford (near Marion, Virginia)
2 CONT October 4th, Sunday, camped at Saltville in a lane 14 miles from Abingd
2 CONC on.  Bought two dozen bundles of fodder cost 33 cents.  At Abingdon got o
2 CONC ne half bushel of meat  25 cents.
2 CONT October 5th, near Abingdon bought one pair shoes $1.00, another pair 75 c
2 CONC ents, bacon 84 cents, oats 33 cents.  Camped at Beaver River at 2 o'clo
2 CONC ck. Then came on bought greens and camped one mile from Blountville, Te
2 CONC nnessee (near Bristol, Virginia)
2 CONT October 6th, Tuesday, in Blountville bought meal 25 cents, two dozen bu
2 CONC ndles of fodder 25 cents, greens 5 cents and corn 16 cents.  By a very c
2 CONC old spring we fix our shafts.
2 CONT October 7th, Wednesday, we came on to the board yard (Kingsport, Tennes
2 CONC see) crossed a bridge, toll $1.00 and bought beef 37 1/2 cents and coff
2 CONC ee 25 cents.
2 CONT October 8th, camped at Beach Springs, two mile from Rogersville, Tennes
2 CONC see
2 CONT October 9th, Friday, saw a road to nowhere near Mooresburg, Tenneessee b
2 CONC ought 14 pounds of bacon 75 cents.  In afternoon came to Bean Station w
2 CONC here Muster Day in progress, and watched a horse race.
2 CONT October 10th, passed through Rutledge, Tennessee did some shopping at B
2 CONC lain's X-Roads (18 miles northeast of Knoxville).  Built a fire of logs 1
2 CONC 6 feet long under a large white oak.
2 CONT October 11th, on road before sunrise, went 10 miles before stopping for b
2 CONC reakfast.  Came upon a dwelling house, separate kitchen and smoke house t
2 CONC hat were burning.  A totoal loss.  Covered 17 1/2 miles.
2 CONT October 12th, come to the Clinch River (Northwest of Knoxville) crossed r
2 CONC iver six times before coming to the westbound wagon road.  Made camp fo
2 CONC ur miles beyound the river.
2 CONT October 13th, bought potatoes for 6 cents and more fodder.  Camped wher
2 CONC e a bridge is about to be built, 6 miles from Montgomery, Tennessee.
2 CONT October 14th, forded Poplar River (New River) paid $1.50 to go through g
2 CONC ap in mountains (Cumberland Mountains).  Evening camp in a cane break h
2 CONC ouses very scarce.  The night was cold.
2 CONT October 15th came to a cabin bought fodder, corn 25 cents, butter 10 ce
2 CONC nts, and potatoes 12 1/2 cents.  Camped in a new ground by the cabin.
2 CONT October 16th, arrived at Jamestown, Tennessee crossed another mountain r
2 CONC ange (Montgomery Mountains)
2 CONT October 17th, came to Beaver River.   Found an assortment of supplies f
2 CONC or sale.  Bought beef 6 cents, potatoes 6 cents and brandy $1.00.
2 CONT October 18th, camped at Brundem's store at noon, camped for the night o
2 CONC ne mile beyond Albany, Kentucky.
2 CONT October 19th, Monday, passed Mr. Brian's store.  Camped on a hill appro
2 CONC aching Burkesville, Kentucky.
2 CONT October 20th, crossed the Cumberland River, came to Burkesville, hand a r
2 CONC ung replaced on a barrel 5 cents, two pounds of coffee 25 cents.  Campe
2 CONC d by Church Creek.
2 CONT October 21st, passed through Marrowbone.  One wheel broke.  Fixed it pa
2 CONC rtly.  Bought supplies soap and oats 12 1/2 cents.
2 CONT October 22nd, same wheel broke.  Located a blacksmith.  Replaced the ir
2 CONC on wheel tire and tightened it for 50 cents.
2 CONT October 23rd, passed through Glasglow, Kentucky.  Bought supplies,  Six m
2 CONC iles beyond Glasglow bought a possum and camped there.
2 CONT October 24th, passed a race track.  Bought more supplies and camped two m
2 CONC iles from Barren River.
2 CONT October 25th, Sunday, crossed Barren River.  Passed through Bowling Gre
2 CONC en, Kentucky.  Camped by a new well at the home of Mr. Vase.
2 CONT October 26th, passed Shakertown and Russelville.  Camped one mile beyon
2 CONC d.
2 CONT October 27th, came to the turnpike paid 25 cents toll crossed a bridge o
2 CONC ver Elk Fork of the Pond River at the town of Elkton and camped seven m
2 CONC iles beyond.
2 CONT October 28th, arrived after one mile travel to Fairview on the Todd-Chr
2 CONC istian County line.  Another toll turnpike.  Made noon camp nine miles f
2 CONC rom Hopkinsville.  Passed through the town of Hopkinsville and camped s
2 CONC ix miles west of town by the side of an old plank fence.
2 CONT October 29th, Thursday, we came to Mr. Hopkin's today and staid there a
2 CONC ll the time till the 31st.
2 CONT
2 CONT Information received from Charles Williams of Knoxville, Tennessee
